window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['blomberg/guyer'] = {"blomberg-s01-001":["All our cognitions are acquired",[]],"blomberg-s01-002":["1): through experience",[]],"blomberg-s01-003":["2): through one's own exercises and 3): through instruction",[]],"blomberg-s01-004":["Through learning there arises the spirit of imitation. This learning happens in childhood. there is indeed some skill that one brings about for oneself through imitation; but he who imitates shows the least degree of capacity.",[]],"blomberg-s01-005":["The first origin of our knowledge is empirical. Many things arise through imitation and many cognitions through *imitatio* [imitation].",[]],"blomberg-s01-006":["We see what others have shown us, and do it after them.",[]],"blomberg-s01-007":["The 2nd way is the exercise of our acquired skill. Through frequent exercises of our insights, and talents we attain to a greater cognition, and to new insights, and these can be still more enlarged through one's own experience.",[]],"blomberg-s01-008":["The last is to attain to a cognition through universal rules, these are derived from reason.",[]],"blomberg-s01-009":["The first method of attaining to cognitions must drop away at once, as soon as the capacities acquire a faculty of cultivating themselves. One must seek to think for oneself, to judge for oneself.",[]],"blomberg-s01-010":["This way of the exercises of the capacities attains precisely the same degree, even if one thinks contrary to what others have thought.",[]],"blomberg-s01-011":["The natural understanding has its own laws, according to which alone it can proceed. These are called natural laws.",[]],"blomberg-s01-012":["Besides the natural laws there are also prescriptions which are for the artificial understanding. The first is to proceed entirely without laws, and to make use neither of the natural [ ... ] laws, nor of the prescriptions. If one thereupon wants to cultivate one's own reason, then one first makes use of the natural laws of reason. This happens when one thinks often, or through exercises, and frequent attempts, when one considers an object according to particular as well as universal laws.",[]],"blomberg-s01-013":["The 2nd use of the understanding happens according to artificial rules, or according to prescriptions, and from this arises the artificial understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-014":["One first learns to walk by being led at first, so one also learns to think, by first of all imitating, thereupon one begins to use one's feet oneself and alone. thus we also begin to judge for ourselves, and to use our understanding through frequent, and self-repeated attempts to judge for ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s01-015":["The merely natural laws are cultivated through experience, but for artificial use I must have other rules. First one must imitate then exercise oneself, and finally one receives prescriptions.",[]],"blomberg-s01-016":["Through imitations we can acquire crafts, but through prescriptions a science. *Versification* can as it were be compared with crafts.",[]],"blomberg-s01-017":["To judge beautifully we learn through rules, but to compose poetry beautifully through frequent exercises.",[]],"blomberg-s01-018":["All cognition happens according to rules: These are either merely natural, or artificial.",[]],"blomberg-s01-019":["The natural rules pertain to the common, and healthy understanding and to healthy reason.",[]],"blomberg-s01-020":["The artificial rules are the rules of learnedness. the first use of which we acquire not through imitation, but through the frequent use of our reason, thereby the common understanding is cultivated.",[]],"blomberg-s01-021":["The common use of the understanding is that with objects of experience.",[]],"blomberg-s01-022":["The artificial is that according to prescriptions and rules.",[]],"blomberg-s01-023":["now one speaks much of healthy understanding, one praises it too highly. Some have believed healthy reason differs from the sciences only according to degree. But this gives no distinct boundary, where the one ceases, and the other begins. Healthy reason, and learnedness are distinguished not merely according to degree, but also according to *species*, they are 2 particular sources of each kind.",[]],"blomberg-s01-024":["A learned man is called one whose learnedness is not grounded on common experience. All understanding is called, according to the common usage of speech, a common understanding, insofar as it is encountered in most in the same degree.",[]],"blomberg-s01-025":["But it is a healthy understanding if it is correct. it is not healthy according to degrees, but this is the correctness, that understanding, and reason judge according to such laws as agree with experience. But if there are no other *principia* than experiences . then a healthy reason still remains a common reason. There is also an instructed understanding, which could not have been acquired through common experiences; and this is a science. there is a *logica* of the common understanding, and of healthy reason and a *logica* of learnedness. it serves to enrich the common, and healthy understanding, and to cultivate this healthy reason up to learnedness.",[]],"blomberg-s01-026":["The *logica* of healthy reason will have for its *principium* one's own experience, and thus be empirical; it will not contain the rules how one ought to think, but it will indicate the rules according to which one commonly thinks. There are 2 sorts of rules A:) the rules according to which one proceeds, these are laws of appearance, and subjective . These subjective laws are the laws of reason according to which it commonly proceeds in judging, and thinking. B:) Objective laws, according to which the understanding ought to proceed. These are called *dogmata*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-027":["The subjective laws present the errors. Healthy reason is extended by attending to the rules according to which the understanding ought to proceed.",[]],"blomberg-s01-028":["The objective laws we extend through doctrines, but in order to cultivate healthy reason , one must give no universal rules, and prescriptions; learned reason is not grounded on empirical *principia*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-029":["The common understanding is the understanding to judge according to laws of experience; but common reason the reason to infer according to laws of experience. one calls this reason common because it is the least degree, with all objects that thus come before us. On the occasion of experience we always have a measuring-rod with which we compare magnitudes with one another. Thus we proceed when we want to find the average magnitude; namely we take that of which we want to find the average magnitude, e.g., man: we count each man's magnitude according to the measuring-rod, add them together, and divide them by the number of men, whom we have never in our life seen, then there comes out the average magnitude that is suited to each man. Thus is the average magnitude of the understanding also brought out ,from the understanding that one knows through common experience. The common understanding is the measure. The common and healthy understanding is a small, but correct understanding, and its cognition has a great usefulness. in a tract of land of peasants there are found by far not so many errors, as in a district of learned men, which is just as large. For the peasant always goes according to the guiding-thread of experience: he judges about little, but about the little correctly. The exercises of common and healthy reason are also easy, all cognition from experience has more life, and more of the intuitive, than that which has arisen from universal concepts. The first advantage of common reason is simplicity. The 2nd is correctness. The 3rd usefulness. The 4th ease and the 5th clarity, and liveliness. For the cognitions arise with the light with which the representations of experience are endowed. Healthy reason will accordingly be sufficient for the beautiful sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s01-030":["Taste actually requires no learnedness at all in order to be quite perfect. Learned taste is in fact already a false, a corrupted taste.",[]],"blomberg-s01-031":["What is beautiful must please universally, and everyone. for the judging of beauty experience is required, and the judgment of the beautiful and ugly is passed in consequence of common and healthy reason. in all learnedness common and healthy reason lies at the ground; it becomes ridiculous if it is not grafted onto this stock. in history one needs no healthy understanding, so it is also the case with many mathematical propositions themselves. but in the philosophical, and all other sciences where not merely an imitation of another understanding is sufficient, but the spirit of *genie* itself is necessary, the common understanding is indispensable.",[]],"blomberg-s01-032":["The use of all cognitions of the learned and the application of them is possible only through healthy reason. indeed someone can also be a learned man without a healthy understanding, but that is a ridiculous pedant. All learnedness is either historical, or rational. Historical is the cognition that is borrowed as to form from common reason, but as to matter from common experiences. Historical learnedness contains history, geography etc. a historically learned man can also make *reflexiones*, but these already belong to rational learnedness. The matter in rational learnedness can also be grasped through common reason and experience. But the form is entirely different. Human beings find more amusement in the faculty of being in a position to make themselves some pleasure, than in the pleasure itself. If I draw the particular from the universal, I use understanding, but if I infer the universal from particular cases, then I need another method. The understanding cognizes everything *a priori*, healthy reason *a posteriori*. we have cognitions that are empirical, since we climb up from below, and in rational ones we climb down from above. Logic, which is an *organon* of rational learnedness, pertains not to historical, but rational cognitions. Logic, which is an *organon*, can prescribe rules , to an already existing learnedness, and then it is called *logica critica* or else rules through which one can attain to learnedness, and then it is called *logica dogmatica* all logics are not dogmatic, but critical. to philosophy belongs more genius, than imitation.",[]],"blomberg-s01-033":["All of nature moves according to rules, thus water moves according to hydraulic rules, nature acts according to rules, even inconstant weathers have their certain rules, although we do not notice them. Animals move according to rules of which they are often not even conscious. The human being acts according to rules, and makes use of the understanding in particular according to certain propositions, and rules. how often do human beings also act according to rules without being conscious of them? E.g., they speak their mother tongue. often an exercise of our power happens without our being conscious of it, and indeed 1 *mo* because this already lies in our nature. 2 it often happens through imitation, by means of an example, which we imitate, in order gradually to use the understanding in such a way as we see that others use it.",[]],"blomberg-s01-034":["3: one's own use. one's own exercise brings us to the facility of using the understanding, without being conscious of its rules.",[]],"blomberg-s01-035":["Just as the human being learns to walk, so he also learns to think. This understanding thus formed *sens commun*. if it is correct, then it is called the healthy understanding, because the facility of using it is subject to a common use. The frequent use of our understanding in objects of experience finally makes us fit to use it correctly. it can thus become correct through mere exercise, and use. The healthy understanding is supposed to be common, the understanding therefore that goes further than its senses teach it in experience is not common, that would be the learned use of the understanding. The healthy understanding thus pertains to experience; it must proceed according to rules of which it is itself conscious. it is thus not undisciplined, only it cognizes these rules not *in abstracto* but *in concreto*. thus to many trades belongs a healthy, alert understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-036":["The causes of things do not strike the senses so much as their effects. one must thus also know the rules according to which one could use the healthy understanding. in many cases one cannot rely on one's own exercise; but the only teacher of the healthy understanding is experience. The healthy understanding, and healthy reason are thus firstly of great necessity, in order to lay them at the ground of all abstract higher cognitions, one commonly opposes healthy reason to the affected learnedness of proud school-wisdom. but this will not do, for learnedness always presupposes the healthy understanding, art presupposes nature. they are indeed *distinctae* but not *oppositae*. but it has become fashionable to praise the healthy understanding to the detriment of the honor of the sciences. indeed it is no reproach if one denies science to someone, but if someone has no healthy understanding, then he is a natural minor, a child. from everyone we thus demand at least healthy understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-037":["There are certain intermediate kinds of perfections, the beauty of a woman e.g. is as it were a merit, ugliness displeases, but tolerable looks are as it were the intermediate thing that one as it were demands of a human being. Beauty, and ugliness distinguish human beings in particular. so it is also with the size of human beings. our eyes make for themselves a certain *ideam* of the average, and what is particular, human beings mostly agree in it. Thus e.g. one speaks of a human being's means. the average between wealth, and poverty, we call the well-to-do we thus in all our concepts always make an average the measuring-rod. Thus the healthy understanding is also the average among the capacities of human beings. Healthy reason ought not to go one step further than experience leads it. The healthy understanding cognizes, and judges not *in abstracto*, but *in concreto*, it draws its judgments from experiences. yet it can also cognize much of what experience teaches us also universally *a priori* and *in abstracto*. The healthy understanding is thus the faculty of judging correctly *in concreto*. often very reasonable people cannot judge *in abstracto* at all, although *in concreto* they think just as often, so it is in moral cases, e.g. through an example in an incident one cognizes things *in concreto*. The understanding that can judge *in abstracto* is called the finer scientific understanding. The natural faculty for judging is the understanding, the faculty of inferring is reason. through experience one becomes prudent, but not learned. prudence does not consist in science, one can be, and become, prudent without any learnedness. The healthy understanding, and reason show their utility particularly *in praxi*. a certain lack of the healthy understanding and of application *in concreto* no science can in the least replace, or *compensate*, although it can otherwise replace much. our universal judgments alone cannot help us. With all rules I must know whether they can be applied to the case",[]],"blomberg-s01-038":["2: how I am then to apply them here so there can be very learned people without any healthy understanding. There is thus as it were in the use of the understanding something that nature has reserved for genius, and which no art can replace.",[]],"blomberg-s01-039":["Through an all too great fullness of learnedness people often become the more insipid, and entirely unfit to judge *in concreto*. to the healthy understanding which is small but correct belongs 1. simplicity. it keeps to the ground of experience, and does not love chimerical ideals. precisely this simplicity makes the understanding the more correct, secure, and reliable, than science. An Academy of Sciences at Paris hatches 1000 times more errors than a village full of peasants. 2. its knowledge is intuitive through examples, and instances. it represents the *objecta in natura* to us, whereas the finer understanding shows only the shadow of things. The healthy understanding is the *controlleur* of learnedness. The common healthy understanding grows without instruction. so the common living languages are distinguished from the learned dead languages.",[]],"blomberg-s01-040":["To give universal rules how one could assess the infallibility of a witness, for this belongs more than mere common and healthy understanding, for this belong art and learnedness. Languages were there before grammars of them, orators before rhetoric, poets before *poesia*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-041":["If I have universal knowledge of the understanding, and also universal rules of it, I must still in particular have understanding for it, in order to judge whether this, or that stands under precisely this rule, or not. All doctrine in the whole world is only a universal sum total of the rules for using the powers of the understanding, but there must still be an understanding there which only nature can give. Persons who possess learnedness, but who lack a healthy understanding, are far more insipid than entirely ignorant ones. But from this arises pedantry. a stubborn dunce one cannot call a pedant. Learnedness must always be there, but the capacity to apply the cognized universal rules *in concreto* to particular cases must be lacking. although to be sure a pedant with all his science always retains a kind of stupidity. So it is e.g. with all introductions of witty notions in company with paying compliments even according to a universal prescription. The Chinese have books of compliments universal rules always testify to the weakness of our understanding. Just as a lexicon of a language of a beginner Much regularity takes place without consciousness of the rules; so it is with the healthy understanding. But the use of the understanding according to rules of which one is conscious is called science. there are cases where many a one can go further without science than another with science, but also where the greatest healthy understanding cannot go as far as it alone. So in mathematics, the art of surveying one cannot get on through mere understanding, always some science must be there. So it is also with the art of medicine. for the judging of morality, of right, and wrong, on the other hand, no science, and learnedness is needed.",[]],"blomberg-s01-042":["The human race would otherwise be very imperfect Here the healthy understanding is the judge of science.",[]],"blomberg-s01-043":["In all sciences a prescription serves well for the development of our capacities for all incidents. But with every instruction in art it is still always necessarily left to the understanding of the subject whether, and in what degree these universal prescriptions are applied in individual cases. Thus one cannot possibly become prudent according to universally valid rules, and prescriptions, for prudence consists properly in this, that one can oneself subsume individual cases in common life under its universal rules.",[]],"blomberg-s01-044":["The discipline which contains the rules of the good use of the understanding, and of reason in general, is called *logica*. it determines no individual cases in all other sciences *logica* is applied. Logic relates to the whole use of the understanding just as *grammatica* to a language Grammar has a source of the correctness of the rules, but the proof lies in experience.",[]],"blomberg-s01-045":["Logic shows the rules of the use of the understanding, and reason, which can themselves be cognized *a priori*, and without experience, since they do not *depend* on it. The understanding here has insight into its own rules, and makes from them a discipline, an instruction, which however can be cognized *a priori*, and hence it is called a doctrine.",[]],"blomberg-s01-046":["In logic reason is properly not only applied, but reason is its *Object*, the object of its consideration *in usu*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-047":["There are many cognitions whose form is not rational, and then it is no doctrine of reason. So for ex. it is with the science of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s01-048":["*Philosophia* is in general the science of reason concerning the qualities of things. Through the former it is distinguished from the historical, but through the other from the mathematical sciences. but it is called a science of reason because it presents cognitions *in abstracto*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-049":["For the establishment of the first science, e.g., as logic is, nothing else is required than the common healthy understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-050":["Logic is called by a particular name and one properly belonging to it the science of reason, because this is its *Object*. The many other *scientias rationales* one should call sciences of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s01-051":["Logic is called a science because its rules can be proved by themselves without any use *a priori*. for this reason neither grammar, nor aesthetics is a science. and there actually is entirely no science of the beautiful properly, but what we ever know of the beautiful is nothing but a *critica*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-052":["*Logica* will thus have no other grounds, and source, than the nature of the human understanding. There are always of every cognition, and thus also of logic, 1. .. a) objective, and b) subjective grounds. In every science one by rights studies the *Object*, what belongs to its greatest perfection, but also ... 2. the subject i.e. the means the *substrata* for attaining to the *Object*. and so in sciences of the understanding too I must first of all study the human being, but since this happens seldom, or by few, there arise from it e.g. the many errors in morals especially, which most moralists, who can indeed speak excellently of virtue, praise, recommend and describe it, but are not in a position to instill in their listeners the desire, and love to exercise it, and to produce it in them, because they have not studied the human being.",[]],"blomberg-s01-053":["*Logica* is a good *philosophia* about the good use of reason, and its rules. it thus considers not only both the nature of human reason alone but also in particular its prescriptions.",[]],"blomberg-s01-054":["The nature of the human understanding develops from experience and treats *psychologia*. It will thus also still have its sources, i.e. we shall have to learn from *psychologia*, and experience many subjective laws of the use of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s01-055":["We can thus 1 *mo* make the subjective laws of our understanding, according to which we actually use the understanding, the *Object* of our consideration.",[]],"blomberg-s01-056":["That science which *examines* the subjective laws of the rules of our use of understanding and reason is called *psychologia*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-057":["Logic does not properly contain the rules according to which the human being actually thinks, but the rules how the human being ought to think. For the human being often uses his understanding, and thinks otherwise than he ought to think, and use his understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-058":["Logic thus contains within itself the objective laws of the understanding, and of reason. So the depiction of a good *republique* is often so opposed to an actually existing *republique* that it contains *accurately* the opposite of it.",[]],"blomberg-s01-059":["We thus have at bottom only 2 objective doctrines, for there are only 2 objective powers of the soul. Cognitive power and faculty of desire. Logic thus as well as morals have after all as their *Object* a *praxin*, an exercise, the use of our understanding, the application of our free choice. But for this reason they are not yet called practical sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s01-060":["We can have a theoretical and also a practical logic and morals. Disciplines are practical when their effects are practical.",[]],"blomberg-s01-061":["Logic is not a mere critique, it is an actual doctrine that can be proved. its rules are taken, and proved, *in abstracto*. but each doctrine is not yet always practical, it can be a pure theory. now if logic is a mere theory of the conditions under which a cognition is perfect, according to laws of the understanding, and of reason, then it is no means of [31a] *execution*; it would be a theory but no *organon*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-062":["There is a logic which is called a *scientia propaedeutica* an introductory science.",[]],"blomberg-s01-063":["and so it will serve us as a means of critique or as an *organon*, but that it is not, it only sharpens the understanding in judging about cognitions",[]],"blomberg-s01-064":["Logic thus contains the rules of the use of our understanding, and of our reason. everything else thus stands under it. it paves the way for all other sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s02-001":["There are many rules of motion, and of the understanding, every ship, every fish directs itself according to the rules of motion. we ourselves are subject to these laws. we observe them in walking and in other motions. Nature has implanted these laws in human beings and animals. but with them we do nothing more than that we fulfill them unknowingly like the animals.",[]],"blomberg-s02-002":["It would be unnecessary if one wanted to spy out these laws of motion, and take no step without reflection. just as the fish in its swimming observes these rules most exactly through exercise, so we too can acquire a facility in observing them merely through exercise. how quickly do we not know how to help ourselves when we are about to fall? But this facility would be lacking to us if we wanted to reflect beforehand which posture we should adopt.",[]],"blomberg-s02-003":["Borellus has written a book on regular motion.",[]],"blomberg-s02-004":["But besides the laws and rules of motion there are also rules and laws of the understanding. all our judgments we arrange according to these laws. Just as a child learns in walking to observe the laws of motion; so it also seeks in judging to form itself according to the laws of the understanding. but all this it does unknowingly, and attains the facility merely through exercise.",[]],"blomberg-s02-005":["For common cognition it is not necessary that we be conscious of these rules, and reflect on them. we would, if we wanted to do that, lose very much. but if our understanding wants to have climbed up to learned cognition, then it must be conscious of its rules, and use them after reflection, because common exercise is not enough for it here. These rules, which the understanding has to observe in learned observation, or cognition, are prescribed to us by logic, which we now want to present.",[]],"blomberg-s02-006":["The author *defines* it quite correctly: it is a science that treats the rules of learned cognition, and of learned presentation. a shorter definition would be: Logic is a science which teaches us the use of the understanding in learned cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s02-007":["It is also called *philosophia instrumentalis*, because it can be regarded as it were as an *instrumentum* of all other philosophical sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s02-008":["Through frequent observations one has spied out the rules of the understanding. Aristotle already established some; but these were nothing other than signposts to wrong paths. it has already cost much effort to forget such false propositions, to give back to the understanding its natural perfection and to investigate its true rules.",[]],"blomberg-s02-009":["A correct logic is like a straight line, so that one must deviate from it neither to the right nor to the left. it would be desirable that logic had already been brought to such correctness, and perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s02-010":["The author indicates to us in this *paragraphus* from what we must derive its rules if we want to make them so perfect.",[]],"blomberg-s02-011":["There can namely serve for this",[]],"blomberg-s02-012":["1. the experiences of the effects of human reason Baco de Verulam (see his life in Formey's Hist. of Philosophy pag. 293 et sequ. and in Gentzkenius's *Historia Philosophica* pag. 156, 157.) first showed the world that all philosophy consists of figments of the brain if it does not rest on experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s02-013":["It would be just as necessary to write an experimental logic as physics, in which one should investigate how the human being can err through prejudices, and precipitancy, as also in other ways, so that rules can be prescribed to him how he is to guard himself against it.",[]],"blomberg-s02-014":["One should furthermore set up observations of how one can judge most correctly, and also prescribe rules about this in *logica*.",[]],"blomberg-s02-015":["2. we must look to the nature of human reason, so that one can set up the rules in conformity with it.",[]],"blomberg-s02-016":["It would be foolish to arrange logic for rational beings in general, or even for angels, and to think thereby that it could also be of use to us. this would be just as if I wanted to give a lame man, who had to walk on crutches, rules and prescriptions how he should walk, and run, as a healthy man. 3. our rules must direct themselves according to the universal fundamental truths of human cognition, of which *ontologia* treats. These fundamental truths are the *principia* of all sciences consequently also of *logica*. we must remind here that we here at the beginning of *philosophia* are not yet in a position to deliver a perfect *logicam*, for this insights are required which one can use only with such listeners to whom the whole of philosophy is known. we shall here only endeavor to give preliminary rules for learned cognition, whereby one will make the learning of the other sciences easier for oneself.",[]],"blomberg-s02-017":["The aim of the doctrine of reason can be divided into the immediate, and mediate. the immediate is the improvement of learned, and the mediate of common cognition. The latter can happen if in daily intercourse one seeks to improve the concepts of human beings, in which, however, one must not come along with scholastic *terminis*, if one does not want to be called a pedant.",[]],"blomberg-s02-018":["As concerns the immediate aim of logic namely the perfection of learned cognition, this concerns both the learned by profession, and also others. Each can improve his learned cognition through it as much as he wants. The learned by profession are distinguished from others by certain *terminos technicos*, and also by this, that learnedness is the principal occupation, whereas by the others e.g. by kings it is mostly pursued only as a side instrument.",[]],"blomberg-s02-019":["It would be fair, since now the multitude of sciences is so great, that each should be assigned his particular field, because it is impossible to make oneself perfect in all sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s02-020":["Among the persons who are not learned by profession one could also count women, who can also occupy themselves with learnedness in accordance with their capacities.",[]],"blomberg-s02-021":["There are sciences for which sharp sense, much reflection, and profundity are required. These are for the male sex. on the other hand there are also such sciences for which wit, and a kind of feeling is required, and these are becoming to women.",[]],"blomberg-s02-022":["Herein philosophy is spoken of. The *Object* of it are the universal qualities and properties of things. but it is to be well noted that philosophy does not look to *quotiens aliquid positum sit?* but only *quod positum sit?* it considers only the qualities, it weighs only what is posited. Mathesis on the other hand investigates how many times the thing is posited, and investigates the *quantitates* of things, how great they are. Philosophy further considers not the entire qualities, not the entire constitution of things, and all, but only those which are encountered in many things, and are therefore called universal.",[]],"blomberg-s02-023":["Philosophy is a science, but one must not therefore think that in philosophy one must always *demonstrate*, and *define*. Mathematics has this in it.",[]],"blomberg-s02-024":["The definition of our author, which he gives of *philosophia*, is the following: philosophy, he says, is a science of the universal properties of things, insofar as they are cognized without belief. One is also otherwise accustomed to say: *Philosophia est Scientia, quae circa rerum rationes versatur* [philosophy is the science that is concerned with the grounds of things]. But since philosophy occupies itself not merely with the grounds of things, but also often with their properties simply; one easily sees that this definition is incorrect. To philosophy belongs a higher use of reason: but it is difficult to determine where philosophy begins and where it ceases; just as the boundaries between poverty and wealth, e.g., are to be determined. A human being is rich, one says, e.g., if he has 50000 or 20000 Reichstaler, but at what number of money does he then cease to be rich, and when does he become rich? – Just as difficult to determine are the boundaries between avarice and thrift. At one point philosophy must separate itself from the common use of reason, poverty from wealth, stinginess from thrift, but where this point may be is difficult to say.",[]],"blomberg-s02-025":["Now we will consider the restrictions of the author; he says: the properties of things must in philosophy be cognized without belief.",[]],"blomberg-s02-026":["The way of our cognition is 3-fold. We cognize something",[]],"blomberg-s02-027":["1. through experience, which takes place with occurrences, and in general with many objects of history.",[]],"blomberg-s02-028":["2. through reason, which abstracts from individual things the universal cognitions, and",[]],"blomberg-s02-029":["3. through belief in another's testimony. In this latter way we know that Paris, London etc. are in the world [43a], although we have seen none of these. We also do not doubt it at all, since we rely on the testimony of others who have been there. In philosophy no belief takes place; here no one must rely on others' testimony; even if someone wanted to swear the immortality of the soul to us, we would still not believe and trust him without proofs, because in philosophy no belief takes place. How then does the name of a *philosophus* befit him who is a blind follower of another? Pythagoras's pupils accepted for only 5 years everything their teacher told them, and these supposed *philosophi* do it their whole life long, and always say αὐτος ἔφα.",[]],"blomberg-s02-030":["If there were simply nothing other than a belief in philosophy, then it could not be possible that one set now this one, now that one on the throne of the sciences, and that such changes of fashion went on in the learned horizon. Just as great lords keep people who are learned for them, who conduct the sciences, give counsels, have a good heart for them etc. etc., while they occupy themselves merely with digestion, so Wolffius and other true philosophers have worked in advance for some supposed philosophers, and laid the ground, and these need nothing more than to make themselves acquainted with it.",[]],"blomberg-s02-031":["If we want to divide *philosophia*, we must presuppose that the actions of our soul consist of cognition, feeling and desire. The science which treats of the use of the understanding is",[]],"blomberg-s02-032":["*Logica*; that which speaks of the universal objects of the understanding is",[]],"blomberg-s02-033":["*Metaphysica*; that which treats of corporeal objects is",[]],"blomberg-s02-034":["*Physica*; that which treats of feeling is",[]],"blomberg-s02-035":["*Aesthetica*; and the science that has to do with our actions and desires is called",[]],"blomberg-s02-036":["morals or *philosophia practica*.",[]],"blomberg-s02-037":["The first *philosophia* began without doubt where the objects could not fall under the senses, and this kind of objects were without doubt the objects of religion.",[]],"blomberg-s02-038":["Up to xxiv 35: Suppl. Excerpt from Formey, Kurzgefassete Histoire der Philosophie (1763), cf. pp. 33-40, 78-71, 174-181.",[]],"blomberg-s02-039":["As regards the history of philosophy, the following is to be noted about it: the Chaldeans had a philosopher in Zoroaster, and among the Persians a Zoroaster is likewise known, who however is entirely different from the Chaldean one. The *philosophia* of the Chaldeans was very obscure and uncertain. (Theology thus first extended philosophy, *ad pag. 44*) it ascends into the highest antiquity, and what one can bring out of it stems from very suspect reports. One must therefore be very cautious if one does not want to mix the doctrines of the ancients with the fabrications spread in later times. The religion of these peoples was a mixture of the most absurd superstition, a gross and abominable idolatry. The priests deceived the stupid people through all sorts of artifices. Soothsayings, enchantments, interpretations of dreams, childish and extravagant ceremonies made up the whole. If indeed something good still lay under this gross crust, it is impossible now to pick it out. This philosophy thus has the look of the most perfect folly, but it must also have been a dangerous folly; for from the first times of the Roman monarchy on the arts of the Chaldeans were banished as suspect and godless. But notwithstanding such obscurity of the Chaldean philosophy, it was nevertheless very famous in all antiquity. Its adherents divided themselves into manifold sects, such were: Hipparenes, Babylonians, Orchenians, Marsypers, Borsippenes etc. Zoroaster is given out as the originator of magic, i.e., as the father of the grossest among all deceptions and of the most shameful superstition. It is said he was killed by fire from heaven. Belus is supposed to have instructed the priests in *astronomia* and *physica*. Berosus, Marmaridius, Zabrates and Teucrus are known to us solely by their name alone.",[]],"blomberg-s02-040":["All philosophies of the most ancient peoples were to be found among the priests. The Egyptians and Persians were probably the first whose understanding overstepped its limitation and began to make *speculationes*. *Astrologia*, *cosmologia* arose earlier than the physical sciences. The very first object of their investigation was the origin of things, for these are without doubt the most important questions, and ones which must have occurred to human beings immediately after they had first provided for the necessities of their body. Yet this first inquiry will certainly have contained more superstition than philosophy. So much is certain: before *philosophia* in a nation had separated itself entirely from the power of the government and the rule of the clergy, no philosophy could be rightly constituted.",[]],"blomberg-s02-041":["The originator of the Persian *philosophia* was Zerdusch or Zoroaster. He lived at the time of the Babylonian captivity; he was the head of religion. He brought together his doctrine in a work called Zendavesta. His oracle is a book foisted in by the new Platonists. The priests of the Persians were called *Magi*, and their head *Archimagus*. The doctrines of their *philosophia* were the following. The supreme God is the intellectual fire: from this, in order to bring forth the world, two mutually opposed fundamental beings or *principia* have proceeded. The foremost being, from which all others proceed, on which also the law of emanations depends, is called Mithra. The 2 fundamental beings are Oromasdes, a very pure, active and spiritual light, and Arimanius, the passive and material darkness, which is born from the limitation of the light and is combined with it as a necessary sequence. From the mixture of these 2 fundamental beings all things beneath the moon have proceeded, and thus must one explain the origin of evil on earth. But a time will come when the darkness will be overcome and annihilated by the light. The soul came, in their opinion, from the gods, and was, like them, immortal. Their moral doctrines recommended chastity, uprightness, equity, flight from voluptuousness. This they expressed thus: You must follow the light, and guard yourselves against the plague of darkness or the begetting of matter.",[]],"blomberg-s02-042":["Among the Chinese we note 3 great philosophers: Confucius, Keilau and Janzu, whom they worshipped under a 3-headed idol image. They held the opinion of Pythagoras about the transmigration of the soul, worship sun, moon and stars, indeed not seldom the devil, so that he will not harm them. They therefore set his image on the fore part of their ships, and wear fearsome heads on their clothes. Among the Chinese all *philosophia* is nothing but morals, some astronomy and mathematics, and a kind of political science and manner of governing. Confucius was a Solon of the Chinese.",[]],"blomberg-s02-043":["Among the Jews we note before the destruction of Jerusalem some sects, as 1. the Sadducees, who sprang from Antigonus Sochaeus. This man, out of vexation at the doctrine of good works, through which one can merit nothing with God, fell into the complete opposite, and said: one must serve God without any hope of future reward. Zadok and Boethos understood this doctrine wrongly; they therefore denied the resurrection of bodies and all rewards after this life.",[]],"blomberg-s02-044":["2: the Karaeans, Karaites or scribes, who rejected all allegorical interpretations of scripture, otherwise accepted all Sadducean propositions. They kept to the school of Chamai.",[]],"blomberg-s02-045":["3: the sect of the Pharisees; it has the name from the Hebrew word parash, which signifies separated people. For they believed themselves to be very different from the people, partly through their science, which however consisted only in false explanations of the law, partly through meritorious works, which however were nothing more than observances of external ceremonies and superstitious actions. Their doctrine was preeminently supported by the school of Hillel.",[]],"blomberg-s02-046":["4: the sect of the Essenes. These departed most from Jewish customs. The persecutions of the Syrian kings, or rather Cambyses' invasion, gave them their origin. Their way of life was very similar to the monastic orders. They served God in a very merely spiritual way; they had set hours in which they attended to certain duties.",[]],"blomberg-s02-047":["5. the sect of the Therapeutae. They flourished in Egypt, and were entirely monk-like.",[]],"blomberg-s02-048":["From the times after the destruction of Jerusalem and of the temple we note the rabbis: Gamaliel, and Jehuda called the Holy, the foremost originators of the Talmudic doctrine. The Cabbalistic doctrine is in the mysterious books Happlijah, Ha-Bahir, and Sohar. The rabbis Akiba and Simeon Sohar's son propagated it, but as a secret doctrine. Finally the *philosophia* of Aristoteles mixed itself in, although all rabbis set themselves against it. Proofs of this are found in the work Cuzzeri. The famous rabbi Moses Maimonides was very practiced therein. This mixed doctrine has very much similarity with positive and moral *theologia*, which was grounded on the explanation of the divine law. Rabbi Saudius brought the former into a doctrinal system. Moses Maimonides gave it a more philosophical turn, and made 13 articles of faith out of it, which he termed roots. Finally the Jews in Egypt accepted the allegorical method, and even the philosophy of this land, namely the concept of emanations, which consisted of the mixture of the opinions of Zoroaster, Pythagoras and others. All this they sought, as well as possible, to combine with their opinions. Among the Therapeutae this doctrine was already in vogue, and Philo followed it. The fear of the Jews in the 2nd century of being scattered moved them to set this doctrine down in writing, and thus they propagated these errors ever further. See more on this in Formey's History of Philosophy pag. 72, 184.",[]],"blomberg-s02-049":["The philosophy of the ancients has the defect in it that they did not at all make observations as now. So it went with the *mathematici*, whose ancient writings are nevertheless so inestimable, only that one did not combine mathematics with observations of nature, which afterwards has nevertheless become a cause of so many discoveries.",[]],"blomberg-s02-050":["Geography the ancients had not at all, or at least very imperfectly.",[]],"blomberg-s02-051":["It was among the Greeks where the philosophers were taught about all the propositions and doctrines and the manner of government and aim of their people, and where they did something for the perfection of these. As raw as philosophy came to the Greeks, so *excolirt* [cultivated] was it again handed over by them to other peoples. The merit of the Greeks in all sciences of the understanding and of taste is thus the greatest. The government of the Greeks was formed by the schools of the philosophers. But among them we note especially the so-called 7 sages. As there are:",[]],"blomberg-s02-052":["*1mo* Thales, who among all deserves to be called a sage on account of his mathematics",[]],"blomberg-s02-053":["*2do* Solon, a fine mind, good poet, skillful general and excellent lawgiver. His motto was: consider the end.",[]],"blomberg-s02-054":["*3tio* Chilon became visible and respectable among the Lacedaemonians through his justice and patience. His motto was: learn not too much, but to cognize yourself.",[]],"blomberg-s02-055":["4. Pittacus of Mytilene, a courageous and good soldier; his motto was: know the time.",[]],"blomberg-s02-056":["5. Bias of Priene in Ionia, an upright, magnanimous, virtuous and prudent man. His *symbolum* was: love as if you could hate.",[]],"blomberg-s02-057":["6. Cleobolus, native of Rhodes. He learned wisdom among the Egyptians. His daughter Cleobolina was the heir of his virtues and sciences. Nothing, he said, is better than moderation.",[]],"blomberg-s02-058":["7. Periander, a prince of the Corinthians.",[]],"blomberg-s02-059":["Democritus deserves to be called the first philosopher. He was the teacher of the great and famous Epicurus, who is among the ancients what Cartesius represents among the moderns, and improved the previous method of philosophizing. In Lucretius one finds propositions of the Epicurean philosophy. Yet since Lucretius, as is known, was at times insane, he may well certainly have disfigured the propositions.",[]],"blomberg-s02-060":["Pyrrho was a man of great insights. He had the saying: *non liquet* [it is not clear], which he constantly called out to the bold sophists to dampen their pride; he was the founder of the sceptics, who also called themselves *Zetetici*. But this sect finally drove doubting so far, and became so extravagant, that it finally began to doubt everything, indeed even the mathematical propositions.",[]],"blomberg-s02-061":["Socrates applied himself to practical philosophy, which he especially demonstrated through his conduct.",[]],"blomberg-s02-062":["Pythagoras made much of numbers; the number 10 he held to be the most perfect, but others the 4th, and therefore thought God had made 4 *elementa*. He wanted to think out everything through numbers. Hence he said among other things: *Animal est Numerus se ipsum movens* [an animal is a number that moves itself].",[]],"blomberg-s02-063":["Plato was very rhetorical and obscure, and so that he often did not understand himself.",[]],"blomberg-s02-064":["Aristoteles acquired a blind trust, and has harmed *philosophia* more than benefited it.",[]],"blomberg-s02-065":["Zeno was the founder of the Stoic sect",[]],"blomberg-s02-066":["The Romans finally brought up no particular chief sects, but followed the Greeks.",[]],"blomberg-s02-067":["The Arabs brought forth again the doctrine of Aristoteles, whom the *scholastici* also followed. In some respects modern times have advantages over the ancients; these lacked cognitions of experience. Thus, e.g., to Aristoteles and all Greeks the *eventus* of nature, of ebb and flow, which was discovered in modern times, was unknown. But there were also not then the means that are now available to attain to it. E.g., to travel so quickly, and through this lack especially the extension of cognition of experience was held up; and these are nevertheless preeminent sources from which reason is to draw.",[]],"blomberg-s02-068":["In general the ancient *philosophi* were all either 1. sceptical philosophers, and these were misologists or reason-haters, or dogmatists, and all these had their term according to their *principia*. But moral philosophy, natural science and knowledge were poor and false among them.",[]],"blomberg-s02-069":["Baco de Verulam, who indicated the importance of the effort that one bring the observations of nature under certain propositions. (and also up to now, to be sure, the outer, but not the inner observations and *phaenomena* of nature have yet been sufficiently extended.)",[]],"blomberg-s02-070":["Cartesius, Malebranche, Leibnitz and Wolffius, the latter of whom by his industriousness brought about a *systema* of philosophy, were in recent times the improvers and real fathers of philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s02-071":["All efforts of our philosophy are",[]],"blomberg-s02-072":["1 dogmatic",[]],"blomberg-s02-073":["2 critical.",[]],"blomberg-s02-074":["Among the critical philosophers Locke deserves the preference. But Wolff, and in general the Germans, have a methodical philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s02-075":["Finally Crusius has become famous, who however for some good has very much that is incorrect, and errs especially in that he wants to prove very many propositions merely from the nature of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s02-076":["As regards *logica*, Aristoteles was the first who expounded it, and also invented the 4 syllogistic figures.",[]],"blomberg-s02-077":["The *logica* of the scholastics consisted of nothing but subtleties.",[]],"blomberg-s02-078":["Locke's book *de intellectu humano* is the ground of all true *logica*",[]],"blomberg-s02-079":["At the time of the Reformation Petrus Ramus wrote a logic in France. He taught his countrymen the French to pronounce *quin*, *quisquis*, *quanquam* not according to the French but the Latin dialect. Over this there now arose between him and the teachers of the Sorbonne a great dispute, so that many were deposed merely on account of the wrong pronunciation, as this matter came before the government. After him Wolff, and also Crusius, wrote a *logica*, the latter of which, however, cannot facilitate the learning of the sciences, but rather is so obscure that yet another logic is required in order to understand this one.",[]],"blomberg-s02-080":["Now finally the critical philosophy revives most, and the English have the greatest merit therein.",[]],"blomberg-s02-081":["For the most part the dogmatic method has fallen out of use almost universally in all sciences; even morals is no longer expounded dogmatically, but more frequently critically.",[]],"blomberg-s02-082":["Logic treats either of the rules of certain or of probable learned cognition; the latter is called *logica probabilium*. In common life we act more according to probability than according to certainty, for which reason the *logica probabilium* would be very useful.",[]],"blomberg-s02-083":["Bernoulli has indeed written one, but it is nothing other than a mathematics that is applied to cases of chance. He shows how one could, according to the rules of probability, e.g. throw 8 times.",[]],"blomberg-s02-084":["The *logica probabilium* has merely examples, and has its use with funeral funds. But that which we name here is to extend to the experiencing of all human beings, and such a one is not yet available.",[]],"blomberg-s02-085":["Crusius's *logica* of the probable is held to be good by many; but it contains merely universal considerations, which have come into esteem through the learned tone.",[]],"blomberg-s02-086":["Just as a circle is easier to make than an ellipse, the rules for virtue easier to give than those for inconstancy, so likewise one can also find the rules for certain cognition more easily than those for probable. The grounds for certainty are determined, but those for probability not. But now it is to be shown how great they are to be if something is to be probable, which is difficult to determine.",[]],"blomberg-s02-087":["*Logica* can be divided",[]],"blomberg-s02-088":["*1mo* into theoretical",[]],"blomberg-s02-089":["*2do* into practical *logica*",[]],"blomberg-s02-090":["Theoretical *logica* shows us only the rules of learned cognition, but the practical applies these rules to particular cases.",[]],"blomberg-s02-091":["In this *paragraphus* the author speaks of the use of the doctrine of reason. It promotes *1mo* the learning of the sciences in general. But this is to be understood only of logic in the objective sense, namely of that which is actually so constituted as it rightly ought to be. But a *logica* that is as obscure and excessively refined as Crusius's cannot have this use.",[]],"blomberg-s02-092":["It improves *2do* the understanding and reason, just as he who understands a language according to the rules cannot so easily err as he who has learned it only *ex usu* [from use]. Hence he is also not as certain of his matter as the former, and hardly knows how to help himself in what has not yet come before him. Whereas another can arrange everything according to the rules that the language prescribes to him, so also he who has before his eyes the rules according to which he is to form his understanding, and duly applies them, will not err so easily as he who knows nothing at all of these rules.",[]],"blomberg-s02-093":["Logic promotes, e.g., virtue. In doubtful matters one can in fact proceed according to the rules of logic, and judge whether the matter is actually good or not. This use it has *directe* for virtue; *indirecte* it promotes, like all speculative sciences and cognitions, virtue, in that, when the human being has acquired a taste for it, it draws him away from sensible charms and at the same time imparts to him a kind of propriety. If the human being wants to occupy himself with speculations, he must be calm, proper, and content with the things outside him, which then can finally become a habit for him with the help of logic.",[]],"blomberg-s03-001":["The first section Of learned cognition in general universally",[]],"blomberg-s03-002":["The author has wanted to give in this *paragraphus* a definition of representation, but because he has not been able to do it, he has helped himself out with a rhetorical flourish, as usually tends to happen when one can say nothing certain. To that end he says that a representation is related like an image which the painterly skill of the soul draws in its interior. What representation is cannot really be explained at all. It is one of the simple concepts that we must necessarily have. Every human being knows immediately what representation is. Cognitions and representations are the same. But concepts differ somewhat from them, as we will see in what follows. Cognition and representation are taken in logic as the same. Every representation is something in us, which however relates itself to something else, which is the object. Certain things represent something, but we represent things to ourselves. Logic does not teach us at all how we are to represent something to ourselves through consciousness, but rather it already presupposes the consciousness of something as a matter of psychology.",[]],"blomberg-s03-003":["In every cognition we find something material. But also something formal.",[]],"blomberg-s03-004":["The object that we represent to ourselves is the object, but the way of representation is called the formal. If I represent virtue to someone, e.g., I can thereby look partly to what I represent to myself, partly to how I represent it to myself; this is the formal, but that the material of the *repraesentatio*. But logic has for the most part to do with the formal of cognition. But cognitions can have different form in one *materia*. There are entire sciences whose philosophy is different from the common understanding not according to matter, but only according to form, to distinctness; so it is, e.g., with morals",[]],"blomberg-s03-005":["If we distinguish a representation and its object, to which it pertains, from other representations, then we are conscious of the representation. Consciousness accompanies each of our states; it is as it were the intuiting of ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s03-006":["Yet we are not conscious of most, of the mightiest and most forceful representations. Who should represent that to himself? Only to the representations of which we are conscious can we prescribe rules. Consciousness cannot be spoken of here in more detail. The doctrine of it properly belongs in *metaphysica*. And in order to have a correct concept of it, much from *metaphysica* is laid as the ground.",[]],"blomberg-s03-007":["Herein confused and distinct representations are spoken of. If in a composite representation I distinguish from one another its parts, of which it consists, then that is a distinct representation. But if I do not distinguish its partial representations from one another, then it is a confused representation. E.g., if I see a cheese mite, my representation of it is at first confused. But if I take a *microscopium* in hand and become aware in it of a maw full of teeth, 2 rows of feet, 2 black eyes, then I have also represented all that to myself before. But I have not been able to distinguish these partial representations of the individual limbs from the whole body. But through the *microscopium* my cognition becomes distinct, in that I thereby represent to myself the individual parts of a cheese mite and *distinguire* them from its whole body. Likewise, if I consider the Milky Way with bare eyes, I distinguish nothing therein from one another. I am conscious of no parts in the *repraesentatio*. But if I consider the Milky Way through a *tubus*, I see that it consists of fixed stars. These fixed stars I represent to myself as parts of the Milky Way, and distinguish the representation of one fixed star from the representation of the other, and therefore have a distinct representation, because just for such a one it is required that I distinguish the partial representations from one another, and the partial representations of the fixed stars are partial representations of the Milky Way.",[]],"blomberg-s03-008":["A simple representation and simple cognitions cannot be and become confused, nor distinct. Confusion and distinctness thus take place only with composite cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s03-009":["A chief property of logic will thus be to set apart a confused cognition and make it distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s03-010":["To distinctness indistinctness is opposed, but to order confusion. Something can be confused although I am conscious of the partial representations, and it may well have distinctness. A distinct representation is not yet always orderly. The more we bring our representations to simple ones, the more we abolish confusion.",[]],"blomberg-s03-011":["In the distinct representation the material is just as in the confused one. In one case we represent to ourselves just as much as in the other. In the confused representation we have the partial representations as well as in the distinct one. For if we did not have them, then we would also not have the total.",[]],"blomberg-s03-012":["But in the confused one we do not distinguish the partial representations. A cognition can be distinct only insofar as it is a total representation. Likewise also only a total representation can be confused. A simple representation is neither confused nor distinct. For in it I can distinguish no partial representation from another. The distinct as well as the confused takes place only with total and composite representations.",[]],"blomberg-s03-013":["Distinctness can take place, namely,",[]],"blomberg-s03-014":["*1mo* in intuiting, when we can well distinguish the mark of what we intuit. That is distinctness in intuition.",[]],"blomberg-s03-015":["2. in thinking, when we combine clear concepts and representations with intuiting. Often one can intuit something distinctly without thereby thinking anything distinctly.",[]],"blomberg-s03-016":["To distinctness of intuition we attain through more attentiveness *per synthesin*; but to distinctness of concepts belongs the analysis of what I think, what I already actually *concipire* in thoughts, i.e., the distinctness of the understanding *per analysin*. The whole of morals has nothing but concepts of the understanding. Empirical distinctness is acquired *a posteriori* through attentiveness to the objects of experience. But distinctness of reason is acquired *a priori* through attentiveness to the actions of my understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s03-017":["A ground is that from which something can be cognized, and a sequence is what can be cognized from the ground. With this definition we can make do here. But in *metaphysica* we will not be satisfied with it. A ground from which everything in the sequence can be understood, which thus lacks nothing, is a sufficient one, and one such that only something is cognized is an insufficient ground. E.g., if we say that the moon has inhabitants because mountains and valleys are found in it, then this is an insufficient ground. One sees from it only that it is possible and probable that there are inhabitants in the moon. But if, e.g., a merchant has 100000 Reichstaler in property, and one says: 50000 Reichstaler he has acquired through trade, and 50000 Reichstaler he has inherited, then this is a sufficient ground. The relation of the ground and the sequences is the interconnection; e.g., the tree hangs together with its fruits, the tree is the ground, and the fruits are the sequence.",[]],"blomberg-s03-018":["Every truth has its ground, i.e., that from which one can distinguish it from the false and take it to be true. This is here in *logica*. Of sufficient grounds one properly speaks in *metaphysica*.",[]],"blomberg-s03-019":["If I merely the ground of a sequence, the cognition is not therefore yet rational. E.g., if one gives saltpeter as the ground of the thunderstorm. But if one distinctly has insight into how the sequence arises from the ground, e.g., how the storm comes from the saltpeter, then our cognition is rational. The author here already speaks of rational cognition, without however first having spoken of the distinct. He should have called it a cognition of reason, which arises from reason itself.",[]],"blomberg-s03-020":["All perfections of cognition are",[]],"blomberg-s03-021":["*1mo* aesthetic, and consist in agreement with the subjective laws and conditions.",[]],"blomberg-s03-022":["*2do* logical, and consist in agreement with the objective laws and conditions.",[]],"blomberg-s03-023":["But all *requisita* of these perfections of cognition in general are:",[]],"blomberg-s03-024":["*1mo* sensation, how I am *afficiret* by the presence of the object.",[]],"blomberg-s03-025":["2. power of judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s03-026":["3. spirit",[]],"blomberg-s03-027":["4. taste.",[]],"blomberg-s03-028":["A cognition agrees with the subject when it gives us much to think and sets our capacity in play. And to this also belongs especially ease, intuition; to this belong *similia*, examples, instances. But sensation has only the 2nd rank, intuition however the 1st. For through sensation one cannot judge, but one can through intuition, and just for this reason the former has the least rank among what makes up aesthetic perfection, but the latter the highest.",[]],"blomberg-s03-029":["In cognition there are 2 kinds of perfections: 1. that it agrees with the constitution of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s03-030":["*2do* that it has an effect on our feeling and our taste. The former is a logical, the latter an aesthetic, but both a formal perfection. We have a logic which makes, and can make, our cognitions logically perfect, and another which makes our cognition aesthetically perfect. The former teaches us to make representations that are in accordance with the constitution of the thing, which the logic does that we now want to treat. But the latter, which aesthetics contains, must treat of the representations that have effect on our feeling. To logical perfection belongs the distinctness of the representation, and also that it must be rational. Of the latter it is treated in §14.",[]],"blomberg-s03-031":["We can say that that cognition or representation is rational in which reason is applied, in which I cognize the grounds; but especially to the logical perfection of a representation belongs truth. Another means to objective perfection is distinctness. Thus, e.g., a demonstration is logically perfect if the proofs adduced are secure, distinct, certain, and agree with the properties of the thing. But a proof can also be easy, although this property does not properly relate to the object, but rather to the subject. Hence one and the same object can be, for different subjects, difficult to comprehend for one and in turn easy for the other.",[]],"blomberg-s03-032":["One must ordinarily sacrifice something of logical perfection if one wants to attain an aesthetic one, and expend something of the aesthetic if one wants to attain a logical perfection. Something can never be quite distinct without being somewhat difficult, and never quite lively without being somewhat indistinct, *et vice versa*.",[]],"blomberg-s03-033":["Never yet has anyone been able to combine, determine, and discover accurately the right measure of aesthetic perfection with the logical. For that much delicacy is required. That which favors our life, i.e., what as it were sets our activity in play, that pleases. Something becomes easy for us when it is in order. Order is thus a means to the agreement of our cognition with the object to which it is directed. Further, the new pleases us especially, no less the incomprehensible, precisely because it is incomprehensible, if only it is nevertheless true as well. In general everything marvelous. Strangeness always has something agreeable for sensibility, but also something displeasing for the understanding. Hence one so gladly hears the most marvelous stories. An aesthetic perfection is a perfection according to laws of sensibility. One makes something sensible when one makes the object arouse a sensation, and move, and when I make something capable of intuition. The greater art of taste consists in this, that I now make sensible what I first presented dryly, clothe it in objects of sensibility, but in such a way that the understanding nevertheless loses nothing thereby. A perfection receives its worth through this, that it can be communicated. In all that concerns taste a sociability lies at the ground, and thereby it is much elevated; he who chooses only that which pleases him but no one else has no taste at all. Taste can thus not possibly be separately peculiar. The judgment about taste is thus never a private judgment, from this we thus see",[]],"blomberg-s03-034":["1 *mo* Taste must refer to the judgment of all. Taste is a judgment of the most universal approval.",[]],"blomberg-s03-035":["2 *do* Taste has something sociable, social about it.",[]],"blomberg-s03-036":["Sociability, however, gives to life a certain taste that it otherwise lacks, and this taste itself is social. If that which pleases me sensibly must also directly please others, then it is said: I have taste. There are some human beings who have nothing social, although they can otherwise be honest people.",[]],"blomberg-s03-037":["*Solitarii*, eccentrics, all have no taste. In the human soul there is present a certain *principium* that would deserve very much to be studied, namely that our mind is communicable, and sympathetic, so that the human being both gladly communicates and also lets himself be communicated to. Hence human beings so very gladly communicate with one another, and the human being seldom feels a real gratification if he cannot communicate himself, and his thoughts, or inclinations, to someone. Our knowing is nothing if others do not know that we know it. Hence it comes that the human being tests his judgment against the judgment of others, and it at once pleases him if the same agrees with his own. It is thus wholly wrong to say: taste is a certain private judgment of a human being about that which gratifies him, which pleases him. Such a human being has altogether no taste. Taste also has universally valid laws, but can these laws be cognized *in abstracto a priori*? No. Rather *in concreto*; because the laws of taste are properly not laws of the understanding but universal laws of sensibility. Taste itself is an object about which we can rationalize a good deal. This rationalizing, however, does not constitute taste, but only increases the same. The judgment of taste is: one judges of this or that thing through and according to the look",[]],"blomberg-s03-038":["If a human being has taste, then he sees what he chooses. That pleases almost all, and throughout",[]],"blomberg-s03-039":["The norm, the guideline (how something ought to be) of taste, this does not lie in the universal rules of reason, but it is encountered nowhere else than in the actual works of taste. There must thus be lasting models of taste, otherwise taste will soon cease. Likewise there must be lasting models of style in a dead (learned) language, for these alone are unalterable, i.e., the Latin and Greek languages. *A priori* much can be explained, and cognized, and yet afterwards not be exercised at all.",[]],"blomberg-s03-040":["The human being is polished, smoothed not through instruction but merely through social intercourse, and especially through intercourse with well-mannered and well-brought-up women.",[]],"blomberg-s03-041":["Genius and taste are very much to be distinguished from one another. Genius works so to speak in the rough, yet with excellent things. It would be to be wished that school instruction took place so",[]],"blomberg-s03-042":["1.) to work in the rough but sublime",[]],"blomberg-s03-043":["2.) and then to polish.",[]],"blomberg-s03-044":["But there everything must be polished at once, and become so, and more attention is paid to trifles and incidentals than to the main things, and even compulsion is used therein. Genius, on the other hand, is seldom cultivated. The harm, however, that such school methods cause thereby is very great. E.g., with elegant Latin. One never looks to the thoughts, but always only to the expression, to the words.",[]],"blomberg-s03-045":["Only through social intercourse and society is the human being polished. Models, models worthy of imitation, are necessary in order to form and to maintain taste. The archetype of all perfections, however, is always only in thought. It is an *archetypon*, and merely an ideal. True beauty must be as it were projected through a sensibility. Morals contains the *archetypon* of moral perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s03-046":["If, however, reason is not especially applied with a representation, then the same is no rational cognition is for that reason also no irrational one. E.g., if I know into what sorts of lands Europe is divided, then I do not use reason for that, but only memory, and for that reason this is no rational cognition. But what is not a rational one is not yet irrational, but this holds only of that which is contrary to reason. E.g., if I have made myself acquainted with the motion of the planets without using reason, then this is no rational cognition that I have of it, but also no irrational one.",[]],"blomberg-s03-047":["A cognition that is not rational, where I use not reason but only memory, in which I do not see how something comes from its ground, is a historical cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s03-048":["A perfect historical cognition, says the author in §19, is a *cognitio pulchra sive aesthetica*. But it is false that he takes beautiful and aesthetic for one and the same, for to the aesthetic belongs not only the beautiful but also the sublime. A historical cognition is one that is not rational. Hence when the author speaks of a perfect historical cognition, and takes it for an aesthetic one, then he can understand by it nothing other than a cognition which, although it is not rational",[]],"blomberg-s03-049":["can nevertheless still have a perfection. Thereby, however, he has explained himself very badly. For who can see from this what sort of perfection this is supposed to be. This is just as much as if I wanted to say: the peruke is that which, although it is not a hat, nor a cap, can nevertheless just as well be put on the head.",[]],"blomberg-s03-050":["We have already spoken at first of the aesthetic perfection of a cognition, and said that it consists in the effect on our feeling. From this we can easily gather what an aesthetic cognition is, namely one that acts on our feeling (through pleasure or displeasure). A cognition that is not rational is the historical. E.g., if someone has great acquaintance with natural history, and he does not cognize it in interconnection with its grounds, then he has no rational but only a historical cognition. Otherwise a historical cognition signifies one that is directed only at individual things, and is opposed to the dogmatic. In this sense, however, it is not taken here. The author takes every perfect historical cognition for a beautiful one. But a cognition can be historical without its being beautiful. We can know from Homer everything mythological, also all the travels of Ulysses, etc., and more, so that we have a quite perfect historical cognition, and nevertheless it will not be beautiful if it has no effect on our feeling, if we look at everything coldly and feel nothing therein. Such a cognition, as we have already said above, is only beautiful and aesthetic which has an influence on our feeling of pleasure or displeasure. The beautiful in a cognition is diminished when the rational and distinct in it is promoted. Just as the beauty of a woman vanishes when one considers her through a *microscopium*. The best expressions, which move us most, lose their force when one dissects them and cognizes them more distinctly through the understanding. Our understanding often rejects something, e.g., various kinds of clothes, which is nevertheless according to our taste and moves our feeling. Hungarian clothes are much more suited to the understanding than French ones, they would be better, more comfortable, etc. Likewise it is more suited to the understanding that the door not stand in the middle of the house and thereby make small rooms: but our feeling necessarily requires that everything be symmetrical, and the door, e.g., not stand at the corner of the house. Feeling is moved through confused cognition, for which reason it is very difficult to observe, so that in general a science of it, i.e., *aesthetica*, has very many difficulties. Baumgarten first made a science of it.",[]],"blomberg-s03-051":["Rational cognition has advantages over the historical: in that it requires understanding and reason. Sometimes, however, the historical has advantages over the rational. Historical cognition must lie at the ground before all rational. The brooder who shuts himself in his room and wants to attain many rational cognitions will not get far if he has not first brought about for himself a historical cognition through experience from books, or also from social intercourse, from society with other people. Historical cognition is given to me *a posteriori*, it already contains everything in itself that belongs to the rational. It shows how something is, the rational, however, shows how something ought to be (so it is, for ex., in morals.) It only makes distinct what the historical indicates. It alters only the formal. Without historical cognition reason has no *materialia* that it is to make distinct. In our education it is a great error that we occupy ourselves too early with rational cognition without our yet having a sufficient historical cognition. Rational cognition, however, is not in all relations, not in all cases more perfect than the historical. E.g., if someone is to be moved, then rational cognition will have little or no effect therein.",[]],"blomberg-s03-052":["In regard to common life historical cognition likewise has more use, just as the peasants in a state are far more useful than all *mathematici* and *astronomi*. –",[]],"blomberg-s03-053":["There is no human being who does not have metaphysical judgments. A science in general will consist of the cognitions that make up a whole.",[]],"blomberg-s03-054":["Philosophical cognition has the same objects that the historical and rational has. Only the form is different. It is undetermined where philosophical cognition begins and the rational ceases.",[]],"blomberg-s03-055":["Just so, for ex., the degree with the affects is undetermined. A greater degree of the sensible desires is an affect. But how great this degree must be is uncertain.",[]],"blomberg-s03-056":["In philosophical cognition one now seeks from their properties in things to have insight into their interconnection of grounds and consequences. Some think they have philosophy, although they actually lack it, and others actually have it without thinking so. Those who learn by heart the *definitiones* from Wolff and other philosophers think they have philosophy. They have only a mere historical cognition, and actually cannot philosophize at all, and think and judge about objects themselves. The skill of judging philosophically about a thing at the least is lacking to them.",[]],"blomberg-s03-057":["In this *paragraphus* the author speaks of the perfections of cognition. It is also defined by him what perfection is. The concept of perfection, however, properly belongs in *metaphysica*, and is brought in here in the wrong place. All perfection, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s03-058":["1 *mo* practical",[]],"blomberg-s03-059":["2 *do* theoretical",[]],"blomberg-s03-060":["Theoretical perfection is properly directed at the quantity of the same, as, e.g., extensiveness, fruitfulness. Practical fruitfulness, however, on the other hand, is more a perfection according to quality, as, for ex., the importance and the beauty of the same, and hence it is in turn",[]],"blomberg-s03-061":["1 *mo* logical",[]],"blomberg-s03-062":["2 aesthetic",[]],"blomberg-s03-063":["Beauty is properly a matter of art. Art, however, presupposes practice.",[]],"blomberg-s03-064":["With all perfection it always depends on this, that one have before one's eyes an aim, a model, an exemplar and archetype. In regard to these one can properly judge, and say whether one, and which, thing is more perfect than the other. So it is constituted already in general with the nature of the human being. Everyone who has seen many makes for himself an archetype, a model of the size, of the beauty of a human being, which holds the mean in all pieces and kinds of the human figure. E.g., among many faulty copies of a human being the original for the most part makes and holds the mean",[]],"blomberg-s03-065":["But from the composition of many partial beauties a perfect beautiful cannot possibly be brought out. Many beautiful different clothes sewn together would look bad. (*Nulla dies sine Linea Apelles.*) The artist, the painter, etc., for ex., cannot possibly compose for himself an archetype of a painting, rather he must create it for himself. Logical perfections are those that are perceived only when one regards the thing distinctly. Aesthetic perfections, however, are those that are felt through confused concepts. The beauty of all good poetic representations, e.g., of Paradise Lost, have an aesthetic perfection. It is felt only with confused concepts, and at once loses its worth as soon as the concept is made distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s03-066":["There are, however, 1 *mo* beautiful objects,",[]],"blomberg-s03-067":["2 *do* beautiful representations of objects.",[]],"blomberg-s03-068":["The ugliest woman can be beautifully painted, i.e., excellently caught, and the most beautiful woman can be painted ugly, i.e., badly caught and disfigured. Beautiful objects can take place only in objects. Of a beautiful cognition, however, properly no judgment at all can be made. For that we have examples, to be sure, but those are no archetypes. Models, however, are examples that come nearest to the archetype.",[]],"blomberg-s03-069":["In mathematics the propositions and *theses* have logical perfections, the concepts therein are distinct. If the poet has made for me the best, most moving, and liveliest representations of spring, if he has spoken of bleating, grazing herds, softly whispering zephyrs, fragrant meadows, etc. etc., then my cognition is aesthetically perfect. It is beautiful, but not distinct. I have thereby attained no distinct insight into the ground and the causes of spring. This, however, the *astronomus* can and must explain to me from the path of the sun. The French for the most part observe only aesthetic perfection. When they investigate grounds of something and want to have distinct concepts of a thing, they let themselves get into the beautiful, moving, and pleasing, and thereby they do great harm to logical perfection. There are scholars who are properly nothing but artists of reason, and such imitative philosophers are commonly very obstinate about retaining their opinions once formed. Other scholars, however, on the other hand, are in turn as it were so to speak the masters and lawgivers of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s03-070":["The learning that is historical according to form but rational according to matter is called a historical cognition. There are very many *Ciceroni* of philosophy, indeed many who do not even know at all what difference takes place between philosophy and the science of philosophizing.",[]],"blomberg-s03-071":["The imperfections of cognition are also in turn either logical or aesthetic. If the imperfections are to be met with in confused concepts, then the same are aesthetic. If they are found, however, in distinct concepts, then they are logical imperfections. E.g., a Swiss poet says that, just as the Rhone runs through Lake Geneva and yet remains clear, so too the truly virtuous are to go uninjured and undefiled right through the throng of the vicious. Here is a logical imperfection. On closer investigation one finds that the Rhone runs slimy through Lake Geneva, so that it becomes clear and pure through it.",[]],"blomberg-s03-072":["A cognition which in aesthetic perfection makes up the beauty is dry, e.g., in mathematical considerations. If, however, a cognition is to be beautiful, but this end is nevertheless not attained, then it is ugly. This, however, takes place with all such writers who want to be witty and yet are not, and cannot be.",[]],"blomberg-s03-073":["Aesthetic imperfections we notice far sooner than the logical. For the former are at once contrary to our feeling. –",[]],"blomberg-s03-074":["A historical cognition is historical either",[]],"blomberg-s03-075":["1 *materialiter*",[]],"blomberg-s03-076":["2. *formaliter*",[]],"blomberg-s03-077":["Further, to imitate the science of another is called copying. There are sciences that can be learned, that are historical according to form but rational according to matter. So are some sciences of reason. So it is, e.g., with mathematics. Thus sciences can be acquired, indeed even communicated in the degree of perfection that is suited to their nature. There are sciences of imitation, of learning, but also other sciences of genius that cannot be learned.",[]],"blomberg-s03-078":["Philosophy, and the art of philosophizing, cannot possibly be learned, but mathematics can So I can teach no human being new thoughts, wit, spirit, naivety in expression as when I teach him to write or to calculate. To be sure, a philosophy would be learned in a certain time if I acquired it for myself in such a way that I copied it off, since it was given to me, then one would do nothing more than philosophize as much as one has learned by heart. But about what has been learned one would certainly not be in a position to philosophize in the least.",[]],"blomberg-s03-079":["The principles of mathematics, on the other hand, are for the most part however intuitive, and the application of the same likewise takes place according to a certain *intuitus*; but so it is impossible with philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s03-080":["The character of a true philosopher is this, that he does nothing but exercise the natural powers and capacities, and indeed through the inquiring investigation of critique.",[]],"blomberg-s03-081":["Never can philosophy be learned, as is nevertheless commonly believed, in half a year. Philosophy properly belongs as a study for men who hold dignities, have experience. Although we think philosophy is far easier than jurisprudence and medicine.",[]],"blomberg-s03-082":["Philosophy cultivates the universal capacities of nature, through reason, and surpasses all effects of these capacities. The investigation surpassing all cognition explores the sources of the concepts of the understanding, and their first origin. And this exploration is one of the most important, and it follows from this necessarily that one does not in the least learn to philosophize if one copies off the philosophy of others and learns it quite exactly. Rather one could far sooner still profit something if one accustoms oneself to, and learns the science of, being able to criticize and judge about the philosophy of others. My philosophy must be grounded in myself, and not in the understanding of others. The same must indeed bind me to no original model. The method of teaching philosophy is thus twofold:",[]],"blomberg-s03-083":["1 *mo* that which cultivates the philosophical spirit, and cannot be learned, nor imitated.",[]],"blomberg-s03-084":["2 *do* or that which faithfully follows a certain *autor*, explains him, and wants to take memory to help in philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s03-085":["This works well enough in mathematics, but not in philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s03-086":["A cognition can be merely learned, or learned and beautiful at the same time. In the former one has merely distinctness as one's aim, and this is dry. That cognition, however, which not only teaches the understanding but also stimulates and entices feeling, is an at once beautiful and learned cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s03-087":["The latter cognition, however, is the most difficult, and far more difficult than the former to obtain. For if one goes only somewhat too far in beauty, one at once does harm to logical perfection. If, on the other hand, one wants really to promote logical perfection, then one becomes dry and loses the beautiful.",[]],"blomberg-s03-088":["To observe the learned and beautiful cognition at the same time in an oral, but still more in a written cognition can thus actually be called the true stone of the learned. But who possesses it?",[]],"blomberg-s03-089":["A perfect historical cognition, if it is to be really beautiful, is perfect not in regard to the material but rather the formal. E.g., an extensive science of geography is historically in itself very useful and perfect, but for that reason not yet beautiful. Rather with the beautiful it always depends on the formal, on the way of cognition. If, therefore, for ex., Herr v. Voltaire presents precisely the same historical cognition as Hübner, then the material of this cognition in these two presentations will be one and the same, but the formal very different, it will be with the former still far otherwise than with the latter, it will namely be beautiful and move feeling.",[]],"blomberg-s03-090":["The material, as e.g. the distinctness and correctness of cognition, can be constituted with the one just as with the other. The formal, however, the moving, delighting, and what flatters our feeling, is different. Distinctness, therefore, and correctness concern the material, and consequently also belong to logical perfection, as a property of the same. Being moved, however, and delight, which pleases at once all at once, concern the formal, and consequently also belong as a property to aesthetic perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s03-091":["With every aesthetic perfection I cognize things confusedly. Much acts on me all at once, and thus puts me into affect. E.g., when on the *theatrum* a hero all at once appears fearsome to the point of terror, and with a great retinue, then the moving storms our soul, the understanding, on the other hand, leaves out much that seems superfluous to it. It rather seeks only to make its cognition distinct as much as ever possible, and precisely for that reason it for the most part passes over all taste, all flattering ornaments, all rouge falls away there, and that which appears aesthetically very beautiful we nevertheless regard as very wretched when we in turn consider it through the understanding alone. The French nation has this peculiarity, that it looks more and most of all to the aesthetic, to outward adornment. The English nation, on the other hand, rather cultivates logical perfection. Hence it comes also that commonly our understanding learns not much or little from French books (yet some are also excepted from this). One knows well enough afterwards, when one has read them, how to remember that one has delighted in them, as in a music; but one also knows at the same time that the understanding has learned nothing from them, which does not often take place with English books.",[]],"blomberg-s03-092":["These two paragraphs contain perfections of cognition in themselves. Extensiveness, among others, through which many objects are cognized, is certainly a perfection, e.g., he who has made progress in true geography, who knows many cities, lands, rivers, etc., by name, and their locations, can have the so-called name-geography perfectly in his possession.",[]],"blomberg-s03-093":["In § 26 the author speaks of the magnitude of cognition as likewise a perfection. With every magnitude there is also always a multitude, but with every multitude there is not yet a magnitude. With each magnitude I can consider the extensive and the intensive. In the first case, when I consider the extensive of a magnitude, I look to the multitude of the units, to the number of them, how many of them there are. In the other case, however, namely when I consider the intensive magnitude, I look especially to the magnitude of each unit. And so it is also with cognition. If I consider, namely, only the multitude of the objects that I cognize, then I look merely to the extensive magnitude of the cognition. If, however, I cognize a single object wholly and quite perfectly, then the cognition is intensively certain. With the latter it properly depends on the degree, how I cognize each unit.",[]],"blomberg-s03-094":["Truth is a perfection that belongs both to the logical and also to the aesthetic perfection of cognition. It is a chief logical perfection on which all others depend. If I cognize the thing as it actually is, then my cognition is true. For aesthetic perfection truth is also required. Hence Milton is censured for having represented death and sin as it were as persons, because such is not suited to their constitution. Yet with the aesthetically perfect not so great a truth is required as with the logical. With the aesthetic something need only be *tolerabiliter* true. In such a way it is aesthetically true that Milton represents the angels in Paradise Lost as fighting, and engaged in combat, for who knows whether this cannot take place? A good fable must always contain an aesthetic truth in itself. So, e.g., in Aesop in the fable of the wolf there is such truth, when he says of the sheep and wolf at the river that the patient lamb excused itself quite meekly against the malicious accusation of the wolf, and endeavored to soothe its anger, but that the bloodthirsty and unjust wolf answered to this, or at least signified by his worthless answer: In short, now I am hungry, I must eat you –.",[]],"blomberg-s03-095":["Distinctness, further, is also a perfection of cognition, and indeed it has this particular about it, that it belongs both to logical and also to aesthetic perfection as a necessary property. An extensive distinctness, which one meets with in the beautiful understanding, is an aesthetically pure one, intensive distinctness, however, which one finds in the deep understanding, this belongs to logical perfection. The poet describes spring to me extensively distinctly. He heaps many marks upon one another. The philosopher, however, describes the same to me intensively distinctly, he namely looks not to the multitude of marks, but rather seeks only to represent some few, indeed if not otherwise, even only a single mark, quite clearly and distinctly.",[]],"blomberg-s03-096":["The beauty of the understanding rests on this, that one has many marks of a thing. The depth of the understanding, however, requires only that some marks be clearly cognized, and also at the same time be distinct and easy to have insight into.",[]],"blomberg-s03-097":["The author here defines certainty: “as the consciousness of the truth of a cognition.” Provisionally this definition of it is enough, and sufficient. We will speak of it further afterwards. Certainty belongs to the logically and aesthetically perfect. The uncertain, the wavering, always displeases. For aesthetic certainty *auctoritas* is often sufficient enough. I.e., it is enough if one appeals therein to the standing of great men who, e.g., have believed the immortality of the soul, or a future world, so one will thereby already be sufficiently aesthetically convinced, although this does not yet make up any logical certainty. What, therefore, great men highly esteemed by the learned world say, that is a ground for aesthetic certainty, but never for logical certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s03-098":["If a cognition is to be aesthetically true, then in this cognition very much must be heaped upon one another, and a multitude of representations brought in all at once. If I want, e.g., to prove the shamefulness of this or that deed, then I will not indicate and demonstrate therein the shameful according to morals, but rather I digress so to speak; I show, e.g., how the rocks, trees, etc. etc. tremble before this deed, how everything quakes, how the heaven wraps itself in gloomy clouds so as not to be a witness of this deed, which is so abominable. He, therefore, who attains this and such a cognition must also be blinded by the confusion of the same. Hence it comes also that the judgments of other people are already sufficient for the certainty of the same.",[]],"blomberg-s03-099":["A practical cognition we call one that has an influence on our will and *per consequentiam* also on our actions (to be ruled by the will).",[]],"blomberg-s03-100":["From the consideration of all perfections of cognitions we have insight that the chief capacities of our cognition are properly the following: 1. understanding, 2. feeling, and 3. desire.",[]],"blomberg-s03-101":["If, therefore, 1. I make my cognition perfect in regard to my understanding, then the same is logically perfect. If 2 *do* I make my cognition perfect in regard to my feeling, then the same is aesthetically perfect",[]],"blomberg-s03-102":["If, finally, 3 *tio* I make my cognition perfect in regard to my desires, then the same is practically perfect, or also moral.",[]],"blomberg-s03-103":["Moral perfection rests on logical and aesthetic perfection, taken together. To logical perfection, however, belong the following 3 pieces:",[]],"blomberg-s03-104":["1 *mo* distinctness",[]],"blomberg-s03-105":["2 truth and",[]],"blomberg-s03-106":["3 *tio* certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s03-107":["Distinctness, however, is the first and foremost property on which it especially depends in a logical perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s03-108":["Truth as well as certainty help little if they are not distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s03-109":["The 3 chief *caractères* of an aesthetic perfection, however, are",[]],"blomberg-s03-110":["1 *mo* truth",[]],"blomberg-s03-111":["2 *do* certainty and especially 3 *tio* extensiveness",[]],"blomberg-s03-112":["For in an aesthetic perfection the cognition is not distinct but confused. Yet truth, to be sure, because of the confused and heaped-up in the cognition, cannot be met with precisely in a high degree in aesthetic perfection. For the same does not properly occupy my will, but rather only my taste, and just so it is also with certainty. Not every sensible certainty is already aesthetically perfect, but properly only that which moves our feeling and flatters it. One can, e.g., have the greatest knowledge of geography, etc., which, although it is perfect, is nevertheless not aesthetically perfect, and indeed because it does not move our feeling.",[]],"blomberg-s03-113":["The author here draws everything together, and says: that we ought to make our cognition at the same time logically and aesthetically perfect. The whole of our cognition that we possess ought to have all possible kinds of perfections. But who can achieve this? In each part of our cognition such can very often not take place at all. If, e.g., I want to make a book logically perfect, then I must not, and cannot, indeed at the same time bring in the aesthetic and practical everywhere. And if I want, on the other hand, to make a book aesthetically or practically perfect, then I cannot therein think everywhere of bringing in the logically perfect. –",[]],"blomberg-s03-114":["Many theological propositions do not, after all, bind our choice straightaway. we can therefore leave out much of the logical, and aesthetic perfection, without the human being suffering any harm from it. Just as a wise teacher conceals much that is beautiful which he knows, divests himself of it, when he knows that the listener their minds are so constituted that they might become accustomed to speculations, and thereby be turned away from the practical, and just so one must in turn in certain cases often abstract from the practical in one, or the other matter. Many matters can be true, and yet harmful to the human being. Not all truth is useful. Meanwhile it is nevertheless also certain that all harmfulness which a truth can perhaps ever have is at all times only contingent. Thus there are certainly recommendations of certain cognitions, and judgments, which can in the end turn into bribery, and again dis-recommendations, contempt, censure of other cognitions, or judgments, without, however, investigating how these cognitions are then constituted. In order to avoid these errors on both sides, therefore, let one take, in every investigation of a cognition, the same entirely alone separated from all foreign questions of utility, or of harm, and especially when this utility, or harm itself interests us, for otherwise partiality straightaway pronounces the judgment, and stupefies all cool reflections of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s03-115":["Opining has at all times been the fruitful source of all errors of the human understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s03-116":["All actions of human beings occur from the drive to perfection. This, however, is attained when our feeling of pleasure, and displeasure is moved. Presupposes for the feeling a rational cognition, then it is a rational pleasure, and feeling.",[]],"blomberg-s03-117":["But if it follows only from a confused cognition, then it is a sensible pleasure, and feeling. according to both kinds of feeling we must make our cognition perfect.",[]],"blomberg-s03-118":["What moves our rational feeling is logically perfect. e.g., when I rejoice over a thorough instruction. but what moves our sensible feeling is aesthetically perfect.",[]],"blomberg-s03-119":["In general the horizon of our cognition determined practically is far narrower than the horizon of cognition determined logically.",[]],"blomberg-s03-120":["Here the author shows how logical perfections can be combined with the aesthetic ones. e.g., logical perfections one finds in Wolff and other writings, but no aesthetic ones. In the beautiful books, however, one finds in turn fewer logical, and on the other hand far more aesthetic perfections. The *autores* of such books, however, have the lowest rank. Everything that charms us, and moves us serves to the detriment of our power of judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s03-121":["Aesthetic perfection, especially emotions and charms are in many respects contrary to the logical, inasmuch as the former concerns sensibility, the latter, however, on the other hand the abstract. in many respects, however, it is required after logical perfection, namely for what belongs to sensible aesthetic intuition.",[]],"blomberg-s03-122":["Whoever wants truly, and lastingly to convince himself of the truth of his propositions must abstain from all sensation, impression, charm, and studied beauty. so that the reader does not believe he merely wanted to persuade him, deceive him, and flatter him. so that he would give assent to his propositions, because he is not in a position to prove them to him.",[]],"blomberg-s03-123":["The entire cognition that we possess should be logical, and aesthetic. if we therefore want to enlarge the logical perfections, then we must not annihilate the aesthetic ones. Further, if we want to promote the beautiful of cognition, then we must not entirely neglect the logical. one should therefore read, if one wants at the same time to cultivate one's understanding with one's taste, not merely comedies, novels, gallant stories etc. and other such books, where there are indeed beautiful and aesthetic cognitions, but where no logical perfections at all are encountered. feeling is indeed moved by this and taste refined, but it is also certain, the understanding suffers harm from it, it is thereby made duller and unusable. With children one should begin from the sensible. Geography could be presented to them in such a way that in it they always sense, and are moved. thus instead of having them learn the names *verbosus* by heart one could rather recount the remarkable things of the sea, the particular customs of foreign nations and peoples etc., and not, as indeed commonly happens, merely show them on the map, without remarking anything about it: that is Paris, that London etc. From history one could in turn present to them the occurrences that have taken place in the liveliest and most moving way, so that their feeling, and desires would thereby be improved. with riper years one could also begin various exercises of the understanding with them. one could have them themselves read skillful, select poems, and good speeches, and reflect upon them.",[]],"blomberg-s03-124":["but one must not, as indeed commonly happens in school, force them, often before they can even think, into an oratory which afterwards is of no use to them at all except that they forget it; for what one has had to learn unwillingly, or even through means of compulsion, one forgets very soon, and of itself. Dry cognitions, which are nevertheless so much favored, such as reciting the names of places, emperors, the dates of years etc., corrupt youth in the worst way. The child learns them with vexation, and does not retain them but seeks only out of fear of punishment to recite its lesson fluently etc. That what it has once learned should always remain in its memory, that is not at all its aim. Even if one tells it that it could otherwise not be, and become happy, it nevertheless pays no heed to that at all, it is still completely disinterested, and content only with its present gratification. abhors therefore everything that would diminish this for it. It will therefore naturally retain, and seek to retain, only what pleases it, and moves its feeling. now if everything is presented in such a way, then it would also be able to retain everything gladly, and with gratification.",[]],"blomberg-s03-125":["To be truthful is the greatest virtue in the world. on which all the others are grounded, and all other virtues are in fact without truthfulness nothing but dissimulations. This virtue therefore should be presented to children first, and at the same time accustomed everywhere, if not yet through words, then at least through facial expressions.",[]],"blomberg-s03-126":["One should above all things endeavor to implant in children a strong disgust at all, and every untruth, under whatever illusion one might excuse it, just as e.g. at a caterpillar or other vermin. They will afterwards gradually accustom themselves in their life to lie as little as to touch a caterpillar. they will always be honest, and hate all possible dissimulation.",[]],"blomberg-s03-127":["Every human being further has a natural, and as it were innate drive to benevolence. just as we rejoice when we can bring another to laughter with our ideas. so we rejoice still more when we have made others happy through us, or only put them into a joy, gratification etc. (only a few malicious minds, which wish the misfortune of their fellow human beings, or even promote the same, are excepted from this) This one would therefore also have to know how to preserve in a child. Let one give e.g. a child something in order to give it to other children who are poorer, but not only then, when these beg for it as beggars, since they are miserable. For thereby one merely accustoms them to compassion, which is still very different from true mercy, but rather one would have to let the child share something with others, in order to give it merely because it is beautiful, and praiseworthy to share what one has to spare; because those deserve it, even if they do not beg for it, or do not have the heart.",[]],"blomberg-s03-128":["Now when one has imparted so much to it through sensation, then this will certainly straightaway improve its moral character. and it will take pleasure in moral stories.",[]],"blomberg-s03-129":["Once one has attained this, then one can teach it to read, then it will itself see what the skill of being able to read helps it. it will itself acquire pleasure in it, it will endeavor to acquire a skill in it the sooner the better, inasmuch as this reading puts it in a position itself to read letters, poems, moral occurrences, and other beautiful books, and henceforth not to need to ask others to tell it something. Now when this has happened, then let one seek to promote its historical cognition. because this must lie at the ground of the rational. Let one by no means burden it with a monstrous multitude of names, of this it naturally sees no use at all, and consequently has no pleasure in it at all.",[]],"blomberg-s03-130":["Let one take the histories morally then it will have a liking for them, and will indeed consider the names only as secondary matters, but nevertheless retain them.",[]],"blomberg-s03-131":["Finally let one also seek to extend, and to promote its rational cognition. let one tell it the grounds when it asks the cause of something, but let one not burden it with remote grounds. Does it ask why no grain grows on this or that field? then let one tell it, because it is sandy etc., but give it no lengthy explanation of fertility, this serves for nothing but to confuse its understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s03-132":["In this § the author says that one must let the small perfections go when greater ones are thereby hindered. To small aesthetic perfections greater logical ones, and the greater aesthetic ones in turn to smaller logical perfections must be preferred.",[]],"blomberg-s03-133":["If one follows this rule, then one will be able to write logically, also aesthetically beautifully, and to express oneself in a lecture.",[]],"blomberg-s03-134":["The opposite of each and every perfection is always twofold, namely either the contradictory opposite, when the perfection is merely lacking, or secondly the real opposite, which entirely cancels the perfection which might perhaps be there from another ground. An example of this of the first would be e.g. such *medicamenta* as help nothing at all, but also do no harm, such as can often be prescribed, is fittingly compared with the contradictory opposite of healing, those medicines, however, which not only are of no use, but even do harm, have the real opposite of healing. And just so it is also with cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s03-135":["The imperfection which is the contradictory opposite is called the lack and the imperfection which is the real opposite of the perfect is called the fault. Thus e.g. ignorance of the immortality of the human soul is the contradictory opposite, whereas error, where one even believes the human soul is not immortal, is the real opposite of the cognition of the immortality of the soul. For ignorance only grounds are lacking, it can therefore easily be remedied. In error, however, there are actual grounds, which are opposed to true cognition as counter-grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s03-136":["The faults: *vitia*, must therefore be avoided far more than lacks, *defectus*. it is surely far worse when I cancel that which is already actually proved from other grounds than when I as yet know nothing; because in the latter case I can sooner be taught than in the former. e.g. he who does not define something at all can already make do with his common cognition, whereas if he makes false *definitiones*, and afterwards applies them, then there arises from this a far greater, and more important harm, which with lack could not have arisen. With the *vitiis*, however, cognition is not only not increased, rather diminished, but even that which is already there is actually cancelled.",[]],"blomberg-s03-137":["Common, and historical cognition can be much more perfect than the learned. they are to be regarded as *heterogenea*, but can nevertheless also be considered against a *focum perfectionis*, e.g. whether they are lively, fruitful and so on etc. By historical cognition we understand not the aesthetic, but merely dry cognition. This dry cognition in a human being e.g. of the circumstances of life, and occurrences of the same, however, is much more fruitful, and extensive than the learned.",[]],"blomberg-s03-138":["A *mathematicus* who otherwise possesses much cognition will nevertheless in *praxi* not get as far with his engineering science as another who has not studied the theory, but has acquired for himself a merely historical cognition of *praxis* itself.",[]],"blomberg-s03-139":["Machiavelli e.g. had a great learned cognition of placing an entire army in battle order, but when he was actually once commissioned to command an army, he brought, instead of ordering it, the entire army into disorder, because he had no historical cognition of the accessory circumstances.",[]],"blomberg-s03-140":["In many respects the learned man actually himself with his solid cognition by far does not accomplish as much as another whose cognition is historical. because the first is not so generally useful as the other.",[]],"blomberg-s03-141":["Rousseau holds that the sciences have brought more harm than utility. we have capacities which are far greater than is necessary for this life. our theoretical capacities are stronger in us than the practical ones. The former we can improve more than the latter: whence then a disproportion, a monstrosity, in which the head *en régard* of the other members of the body is too large. if we had no other life to expect, then learnedness would certainly be more harmful than useful to us. For here we often have for the trouble we take by far not advantages enough. Meanwhile learnedness has nevertheless already created many advantages for us here too. Navigation, the art of government etc. would certainly without it have remained still very obscure, and imperfect. what a great utility is it not, that through it alone as through a light the darkness of superstition has been dispersed, and the same so happily rooted out. Many old women can now grow older with honor, and end their life in peace, than formerly, when they very often came under suspicion, and indeed had to lose their life in a miserable way.",[]],"blomberg-s03-142":["Learned cognition considers the universal, and therefore gives occasion for inventions, and improvements.",[]],"blomberg-s03-143":["When learned cognition is at the same time beautiful, then it is the most useful and most usable, especially in *praxi*. One knows the cognition of the scholastics, these dry philosophers binding themselves to a very constrained method, how they as it were annihilated the moral properties, and capacities of human beings, and degraded them to superstition. An irreplaceable loss! All this, however, could of course not be otherwise, and came all merely from this, that their cognitions, and consequently also their lectures on morals etc. were merely learned, and not also at the same time beautiful, but rather entirely dry. So harmful, therefore, is a beautiful but not learned, and a learned but dry cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s04-001":["The extensiveness of learned cognition is opposed to the poverty of the same. This concerns either the material or the formal. When someone lacks historical cognition, then he is in a complete ignorance. Thus e.g. the ancients were in a complete ignorance of the existence of the new world, they had no historical cognition of it. The common man is still entirely ignorant in regard to many things. But one can also be in ignorance in regard to the grounds of a matter: 1. inasmuch as it is entirely unknown to us, so that one has no historical cognition of it. E.g. when one does not know at all whence the thunderstorm, the rain, snow etc. arises.",[]],"blomberg-s04-002":["2*do* When one does not at all see the *nexum* of the matter with the ground, although the grounds are known to one. e.g. when I indeed know that ebb, and flood have the moon as cause, but do not yet understand, and see how it comes about that ebb, and flood derive from the attraction of the moon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-003":["As regards ignorance, it can be divided",[]],"blomberg-s04-004":["1: into a necessary, and on the other hand",[]],"blomberg-s04-005":["2: into a voluntary ignorance.",[]],"blomberg-s04-006":["Where no *data* are given to us in order to settle something, the ignorance is necessary. e.g. to settle where the human soul will be after the death of the body. *item* of the generative power of animals, and human beings.",[]],"blomberg-s04-007":["Thirdly the place that we have in the world makes ignorance necessary for us. but if on the contrary from certain motives we voluntarily rid ourselves of some things, so that we deliberately do not want to know them, when we see that something is too difficult for us, or without use, then this is a logical ignorance.",[]],"blomberg-s04-008":["Logic is more a doctrine of skill, which belongs to the youth, the doctrine of wisdom, however, belongs to the man.",[]],"blomberg-s04-009":["That circle in which we can see things is called our horizon. The sum total of all things which the human being can cognize in a learned way without detriment to his other perfections is the horizon of learned cognition. what is above the horizon can be seen in the geographical sense. Here, however, it is to signify such matters as one cannot cognize even if one wanted to, the ignorance of which is necessary. of many things we have even an ignorance in historical cognition e.g. of the joys in heaven, of the mysteries, because they perhaps cannot be expressed through our words of some matters we are often in ignorance on account of our weak capacities, of others, however, in regard to the circumstances, the time, and the place.",[]],"blomberg-s04-010":["We determine the horizon of cognition",[]],"blomberg-s04-011":["1. logically merely through the faculty, through the measure of our powers",[]],"blomberg-s04-012":["2. practically according to the ends.",[]],"blomberg-s04-013":["One believes it is always good to know more than one needs, and it is better to know too much than too little. All effort, however, in a greater degree toward an aim is superfluous, and badly applied. It is therefore necessary that all our efforts be commensurate with the ends that we have once set ourselves, otherwise the human being squanders the capital of his powers, which is after all very limited; we say: something goes beyond our horizon, when it goes beyond the capacities of our cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s04-014":["Many more things are above the learned than above the historical horizon. the former is therefore far smaller than the latter. much that I cognize historically I cannot always cognize learnedly, and beautifully. e.g. of the *commercio animae et corporis* I have indeed a historical, but not learned cognition. We must therefore not engage ourselves with all objects, but at all times think of the fact that we have a horizon in cognition. Thus it is also not necessary to engage in inquiry about the state of the soul after death, which is above our horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-015":["It is very difficult to determine what is really above our horizon. Even if something often seems to me as if it were above my horizon, so that I even take it for impossibility, still such can in time through inquiry, and diligence come under my horizon. It is the true philosophy of the lazy when one asserts of all things that they are above our horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-016":["But when someone says: I can indeed cognize something, but I do not need it, it is superfluous to me, or even it is harmful to me, I ought not to cognize it, then that means just as much as: it is above my horizon. According to taste one determines the horizon",[]],"blomberg-s04-017":["1. According to the taste of the human being.",[]],"blomberg-s04-018":["2. According to the taste of the age.",[]],"blomberg-s04-019":["But if it is true that most human beings draw their horizon of cognition only according to taste, then it is only a superficial cognition, in order to seem learned to all, to be able to judge learnedly of everything. This, however, is as it were only the froth of cognition, for which a certain boldness itself is needed.",[]],"blomberg-s04-020":["Our age is almost such a one. but the harm from it is also inevitable. namely, that in the end all sciences are treated only cursorily according to the surface.",[]],"blomberg-s04-021":["The more a science is restricted to a single aim, the greater degree of perfection it can attain. One would almost think in our times that everything was refined and learned. Womenfolk, instead of doing coarse housework as before, now read refined writings. That is called being well-read, wherein they seek a distinction. but just this great extension causes the science to lose its worth. The logical horizon in regard to our capacities is 1.) historical which is, and must be, the most extensive.",[]],"blomberg-s04-022":["2.) Rational, however, it is very narrow.",[]],"blomberg-s04-023":["The historical horizon is to be cultivated especially in our youth, but the aim must also be known according to which I acquire for myself the historical acquaintance.",[]],"blomberg-s04-024":["The polyhistor is the one who possesses all historical cognition of all possible sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s04-025":["Philosophy is the field of all historical cognition, of the theologian, *physicus*",[]],"blomberg-s04-026":["A polyhistor brings the materials to a science, his head is a true *bibliotheca*: to polyhistory, however, there belongs also especially philology, the science of the instruments of learnedness.",[]],"blomberg-s04-027":["The more perfect a science is to become in its kind, the narrower it becomes. Those who possess all historical, and all rational cognition, those are the great, universal heads, of whom there are, however, only very few.",[]],"blomberg-s04-028":["Everyone must think that according to his capacities, be they now great, or small, he has a determinate horizon, suited to the same. E.g. he who knows something of geometry, supposing it is not yet much, must and can well think that finding the quadrature of the circle is raised above his horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-029":["Our horizon of cognition changes with time. what is now above it comes, when I acquire more capacities, and cultivate them properly, and better, in time probably under it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-030":["Commonly a beginner thinks nothing is above, but everything under his horizon, he is completely in a position to cognize, and to settle everything. in time, however, one sees through the deception, and knows how to restrict one's horizon, or indeed falls on the contrary into the thought that everything is difficult for us, or above our horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-031":["How many there are also among the so-called, and merely nominal philosophers, who are vain of their outward glitter, and quite taken with it, indeed even think they are in a position to be teachers of philosophy. while such is nevertheless very far removed above their horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-032":["Under the horizon of learned cognition things are degraded then, when they are unworthy of our learned cognition. Some absurd opinions of the ancient philosophers are not even worthy of our historical cognition, that we should acquaint ourselves with them.",[]],"blomberg-s04-033":["In and about nature, however, not the least thing is unworthy of our historical cognition, unless one wanted to apply oneself to it so much that one thereby neglected more important things.",[]],"blomberg-s04-034":["Delusion and the methods have also brought very much under the horizon of our cognition. e.g. the examination of urine is now on account of the delusion of human beings held almost to be indecent, while it nevertheless serves very much for distinguishing diseases.",[]],"blomberg-s04-035":["Matters are outside the horizon of our learned cognition which are excluded because they bring it about that we thereby neglect more important things. With an unlimited understanding nothing can be outside the horizon, but this takes place only with a limited one.",[]],"blomberg-s04-036":["We must deal sparingly with our capacities, so that we can also apply them to important things.",[]],"blomberg-s04-037":["What therefore does not belong to that science which we have really chosen properly to cultivate, that is already outside our horizon, be it now above, or below it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-038":["To occupy oneself principally with foreign matters, and with neglect of more important ones, is what it really means to overstep one's horizon. As far as is possible one can make the historical horizon. Very seldom is a matter outside the historical horizon, but rather sooner outside the learned.",[]],"blomberg-s04-039":["Now as the understanding grows, so also ever more and more objects come under our horizon, and the same extends little by little.",[]],"blomberg-s04-040":["Rousseau says quite rightly that all who have written on education, and so too most parents, want to make perfect men out of children, while they should first form a perfect child out of them. They bring under the horizon of the child what really belongs under the horizon of the man.",[]],"blomberg-s04-041":["Within our horizon is that which is not under, or above, or outside it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-042":["The determination of the learned horizon is too difficult to be able to be made by a youth.",[]],"blomberg-s04-043":["People of insight, who have themselves erred in their way, can best determine the horizon. They will indicate to the apprentice what is necessary, and what is unnecessary, and how one can very soon err here.",[]],"blomberg-s04-044":["It is better if one first seeks to have insight into very many things than if one wants to learn only few, and these entirely thoroughly, because this is much easier, and more charming for a youth. Let one seek to make for oneself a principal concept of many sciences, afterwards let one first take up one science after another in order. This would have the use that, when one had already resolved to treat this, or that single science entirely thoroughly, one could see the *nexum* with other sciences, and in each bring in an elucidation with the other. It is therefore very perverse when parents want to determine for their children already in the cradle their horizon, and their way of life. when e.g. from the fact that it makes a serious face, and can bravely shout, and scold, they want to conclude from this that it will be well suited for the pulpit, and because another in its youth would like to investigate, dissect, and take apart all sorts of things exactly, conjecture from this that he must one day become a skillful *anatomus*.",[]],"blomberg-s04-045":["Ignorance is in every case an imperfection, and can therefore also certainly never be praiseworthy, but it can well be blameless, and indeed then, when one gives up certain objects in order not thereby to neglect more important cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s04-046":["Leibniz could have had a blameless ignorance in many things. e.g. in chemistry, in antiquities, and in general in very many other matters which overburden a great genius all too much.",[]],"blomberg-s04-047":["One can divide ignorance into the common, and into the learned. For a learned man to see rightly for the first time that he knows nothing, for that a great learnedness is required.",[]],"blomberg-s04-048":["*Quantum est quod nescimus!* but no one is cleverer in his thoughts than a beginner. for this one does not yet understand at all what it means to know little or much or nothing. also he himself does not know whether he himself knows something, or not. in time, however, one learns indeed to estimate one's insights, and to see that they still have very great lacks, and imperfections.",[]],"blomberg-s04-049":["Socrates held it only to be his greatest learnedness that he saw he knew nothing. – A beginner believes he can define, and demonstrate everything, but does not at all see what a labyrinth so many materials are for him, but such he learns to recognize when he already possesses more learnedness. The learned man is in many things just as ignorant as the common man. but he is nevertheless in a position to philosophize about this ignorance itself, and to be able to indicate the ground, and the degree of the same.",[]],"blomberg-s04-050":["When one has already undertaken many investigations, learned much, inquired much, then one first learns to recognize one's ignorance. Who indeed imagines that of substances we as yet know only a mere outer, and no other determination at all? Only he who has already sufficiently wandered through the fields of learnedness knows this, and is in a position to see how small, how deficient, and imperfect all his cognitions are.",[]],"blomberg-s04-051":["A cognition that is *extensive* great, or that comprehends very many objects in itself, the author calls a polyhistory. Those who study out of gallantry are commonly accustomed to apply themselves to this polyhistory. Their cognition resembles a great land which contains in itself many white places, and by far cannot be as fruitful as that land which is indeed smaller, but on the other hand better cultivated.",[]],"blomberg-s04-052":["Exhaustive, and complete a cognition becomes then, when it is sufficient for a certain end. Precise it is when it contains no more in itself than is required for the final end which this cognition has before it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-053":["For the office of schoolmaster e.g. many a preacher can have cognition enough, although not for the office of preacher. he would certainly have become an excellent schoolteacher, now, however, he is a miserable preacher.",[]],"blomberg-s04-054":["On the other hand many a schoolteacher, who now can administer his office only poorly, would have become a better preacher than he now is a schoolmaster.",[]],"blomberg-s04-055":["He who only runs through learnedness, who acquaints himself with everything only historically, so that he knows how to talk of everything in society, has only a meager learnedness, and as it were nothing but a skeleton of it",[]],"blomberg-s04-056":["Such a one is in the same degree worthy of contempt as he who makes great show with clothes, but in himself on account of a shriveled stomach, or other diseases, and miserable circumstances feels the greatest torment.",[]],"blomberg-s04-057":["He who possesses a meager cognition can never satisfy himself, and sees well that it is only a delusion. by himself alone he cannot be content and gratified. himself alone he is not in a position to amuse. he must seek others, go among others, and takes a gratification in others, perhaps ignorant ones, at least taking him for learned.",[]],"blomberg-s04-058":["One must at all times be at pains to extend one's learned cognition. If we do not come forward, then we certainly always come backward.",[]],"blomberg-s04-059":["One must have an immediate inclination to learnedness if one wants to enlarge it constantly, and never let it come into decline. but one thereby commonly acquires only as much as one needs in order to have bread, and to be able to live.",[]],"blomberg-s04-060":["Certainly, we would never overload our head with learnedness, and do ourselves harm, if we observe this properly, and take the easy before the difficult.",[]],"blomberg-s04-061":["It would be better that one might first present all sciences quite easily; so that one would not need much beyond the common use of the understanding for it. now had one presented the sciences thus, then one could present them then in their whole perfection, and acuteness, which commonly happens at the beginning,",[]],"blomberg-s04-062":["while this perfection is nevertheless after all not necessary to all, and from it also often much harm arises.",[]],"blomberg-s04-063":["Many would be content with a preliminary presentation of the sciences at the beginning e.g. according to *Gesneri Isagoge*, than with a more lengthy presentation of the same, which thereby becomes obscure, and difficult",[]],"blomberg-s04-064":["Hippocrates says wisely: *Ars longa, vita brevis.* Negligent people commonly complain about the length of the time in which they are still to live. That time, however, which they have already spent entirely inactive, and dreaming, seems to them to have been very fleeting, and appears to them very short, because they did nothing in it, and consequently can remember nothing at all of importance that occurred in it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-065":["Many sciences are of such a kind that with the duration of time through their domain the human capacities are yet exceeded.",[]],"blomberg-s04-066":["Thus e.g. history is already now very extensive with time and its length now still more happens. ever more and more occurrences still take place in the world. These all accrue to history, and this science will in the end become so extensive, and increase, that in the end our memory will by far not be large enough. since it is already now so difficult.",[]],"blomberg-s04-067":["With mathematics it will perhaps go just the same way. For that which it now contains is already very great enough for our slight capacities. perhaps it will with the length of time become so great, that our descendants will no longer at all presume to venture upon it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-068":["Perhaps with time the mathematical writings will stand like the Egyptian pyramids, which no one imitates, but only remembers those who in former time have gone before.",[]],"blomberg-s04-069":["But although learnedness is so very extensive, our life is still long enough for us to look about properly in it, if we wanted to apply all our time to it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-070":["To earn bread is an ignoble, an unworthy end for learnedness. Great insights are not at all serviceable for bread-earning. A slight measure of learnedness, boldness (*Hardiesse*),",[]],"blomberg-s04-071":["the art of disputation, shamelessness in elevating oneself, and in belittling other great minds, are at present the most suited for bread-earning: to offices anyone at all can come, only the worthy excepted",[]],"blomberg-s04-072":["To forestall forgetfulness, says the author, one must learn much, so that one still knows something, even when one has in time forgotten something, so that one is not all at once stripped of all cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s04-073":["Best of all, however, is probably when one has once acquired the skill to be able to philosophize about the objects themselves, and to cultivate (*excoliren*) the sciences. whoever has this, resembles a *Musicus*, who has indeed already forgotten his learned hand pieces, but can play off everything put before him, and also compose himself.",[]],"blomberg-s04-074":["A learned man who brings his science fairly much into social intercourse, one calls a pedant. in order to be this, however, someone must actually possess a skill, which he however seeks to bring in everywhere, supposing it often also is not fitting. Wholly stupid, and ignorant, however, he must not be. there can be pedants in all estates, even outside the learned. Thus there are e.g. among noblemen hunting-pedants, who talk of nothing but of their hunt, hounds, foxes that have played tricks on them, hares, roes, stags etc.",[]],"blomberg-s04-075":["Thus there are finery-pedants, who cannot be vexed enough, and know and can find no end of complaining, when a cuff, or a curl, or a ribbon of theirs is spoiled, or tangled. in companies one calls someone a pedant, when in his conversations he brings in unnecessary investigations, and *distinctiones*, which would be fitting far sooner, and better for the lecture-chair than for a pleasure-party.",[]],"blomberg-s04-076":["In learnedness one becomes a pedant, when one always wants to define, and make unnecessary scruples. The methods which one makes use of nowadays to instruct the female sex, are ways to make them into pedants.",[]],"blomberg-s04-077":["To charlatanry belongs this, that one thinks oneself great with one's small insight, acts proud, and even elevates oneself above others.",[]],"blomberg-s04-078":["The mathematical method, with which one still in former times made a great show, and wanted to be thorough, was nothing but a kind of charlatanry. to play, or to boast, with one's demonstrations, or with one's wit, is also charlatanry.",[]],"blomberg-s04-079":["To avoid this properly let one acquire a thorough learnedness. if this has now happened, then one will at once become aware, and see, how much we lack, and how little cause we have to make a great show with our plain sciences, and pieces of knowledge, but rather how great cause we have to cry out with humility: *Quantum est, quod nescimus*.",[]],"blomberg-s05-001":["In the preceding second section we have spoken of the extensiveness of learned cognition, but this belongs properly to the magnitude of cognition. The author has thus done wrong, in treating of the extensiveness as well as magnitude of it in particular sections.",[]],"blomberg-s05-002":["In the preceding section we have properly considered the extensive of cognition, and now there remains still the intensive of it to be weighed. The author, however, makes here an actually wholly unnecessary distinction.",[]],"blomberg-s05-003":["The degree of dignity (*Dignitaet*), how we see into things, depends particularly, and first, on our choosing correct things for ourselves. To this belongs a correct taste, and a good feeling, by virtue of which one is capable of choosing important objects, which have either many, or few, but yet important consequences.",[]],"blomberg-s05-004":["The concepts of politeness and inconstancy have indeed not great, but yet many consequences in common life, and are therefore of very great importance.",[]],"blomberg-s05-005":["The multitude of consequences often very well replaces that which a single, or some consequences lack in importance.",[]],"blomberg-s05-006":["The improvement of error, of the conscience, on the other hand has single but great consequences.",[]],"blomberg-s05-007":["Further it belongs to the dignity of our insights, that we do not apply too much effort to one thing, where it is properly not so necessary, and on the other hand withdraw our diligence from other objects. There belongs a particularly good feeling, and true correctness of the imagination to the art of properly estimating the importance of cognitions. The consequences I can well easily show to another, but to feel the magnitude of these consequences one cannot teach him at all.",[]],"blomberg-s05-008":["Of *Metaphysica* I can tell him that he will obtain enlightened concepts of his soul of providence, of the highest being, of the world. But if he is unfeeling with regard to this great use, all description will not help him.",[]],"blomberg-s05-009":["A cognition is great insofar as it has a universal use, but all these cognitions are so disadvantageously constituted, that indeed their use is great, and one cognizes many things through them, but, that one cognizes all the less in these many things themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s05-010":["I think little of many objects.",[]],"blomberg-s05-011":["There is, however, yet another cognition, through which one cognizes much in few things. The preceding fault arises for the most part from this, that one always thinks *in abstracto*, and from this arise many others. Baumgarten says: beauty is a *perfectio phaenomenon*, perfection in appearance. but can one from this definition well actually already notice beauty in objects, in cognitions? Through this, that a cognition comprehends much under itself, it comprehends all the less in itself. The lack of a cognition is not always blameworthy, if only it does not contradict a proposed end. But as soon as this is so, then from this lack arises an error. From this it follows then of itself, that those who promise to give a definition of a thing, entangle themselves in the danger that one at once imputes to them even the least mark merely missing, which one would yet not have done to them, if they had indicated, how they properly do not want to develop the thing wholly, but only to define it in accordance with a certain consideration. Just so it is with the *demonstrationes*, and the proofs.",[]],"blomberg-s05-012":["The boundaries which we set to our desire for knowledge, are so necessary, that without them we hardly cognize properly another science. We must thus, in order to get to the point that we are in a position to cognize a cognition completely, impose on ourselves voluntarily as it were an arbitrary lack with regard to other, and cognition perhaps opposed to this cognition, i.e. a kind of ignorance of it, so that we apply all diligence to this one, and can see into it quite perfectly.",[]],"blomberg-s05-013":["The lacks *defectus* are nothing other than the boundaries of a thing which is to begin. they are thus the *termini*, the limitations of it, and the lack actually very well subsists with the perfection of a beginning.",[]],"blomberg-s05-014":["To the estimation of cognitions belongs not at all acuteness, but only feeling. The *mathematici* can be very acute, but nevertheless have no taste for *Metaphysica*, or for some other logical, or moral investigation.",[]],"blomberg-s05-015":["Often cognitions seem to us dry, and to be of no use. in the sequel, when we first wander through them, and penetrate into their interior, then their advantages soon disclose themselves. Thus e.g. electricity was nothing but a fine plaything; in the sequel however its use has shown itself so, that it may now serve for the healing of many a disease. Perhaps one will even by the help of electricity in time be able to drive away thunderstorms.",[]],"blomberg-s05-016":["Euclid discovered many properties of the circle therewith, and only now in time did one see the extensive advantages of it. Many matters have become despised, and despicable only through misuse. E.g. the Wolffians talked so long and so much of the monads, until they were mocked by merry poets.",[]],"blomberg-s05-017":["The expressions of the monads, of the best world, of sufficient, and insufficient ground have been so dishonored by the learned rabble, that one now actually already hesitates to make use of them. Much that is in fact possible, and good, can also very soon be made ridiculous, and represented as absurd.",[]],"blomberg-s05-018":["Thus e.g. Voltaire made ridiculous the thought of Maupertuis, when this latter is of the opinion that the Egyptians, in order to make an immortal name for themselves, should rather have buried books in the earth instead of the pyramids, so that he becomes wholly ridiculous, although he was perhaps one of the greatest men of his time.",[]],"blomberg-s05-019":["Let each one meanwhile not apply his learnedness to useless objects, nor yet take it along into common companies . just as e.g. it went with the doctrine of the best world, which one at last applied to everything possible.",[]],"blomberg-s05-020":["Finally it is to be noted, that much is passed off as useless brooding, because one is too weak for it, and not in a position to be able to see into, and cognize it.",[]],"blomberg-s05-021":["»Just as many a one often despises a beautiful woman, because he cannot obtain her at his pleasure, and just as the fox decries the grapes as unripe when he cannot snatch them. –«",[]],"blomberg-s05-022":["One can never, or yet very rarely, see into and know in advance all the use of this, or that cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s05-023":["Where did Euclid, when he invented his propositions, think of this, that from his propositions one would find out the distance of the heavenly bodies?",[]],"blomberg-s05-024":["The author says: that the intermediate truths must not be neglected, since they are often very necessary with the principal truths. because a cognition is difficult, it is not for that very reason yet to be estimated highly.",[]],"blomberg-s05-025":["Because something is imposing, it sometimes pleases, even if it is of no use. To this belong e.g. the sophistical questions of the scholastics, the fallacies, the 4 figures of inferences. One of the scholastic learned men is said to have studied himself so thin over the sophistical proposition which they called the Liar, that he finally had to wear leaden soles, so that he would at least have a weight, and a strong wind would not knock and throw him down from the bridge.",[]],"blomberg-s05-026":["If many trifles come together, they finally make up a great cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s05-027":["A cognition can be great on account of its many consequences, even if these consequences are not very great and correct. Thus it is constituted e.g. with the art of reckoning.",[]],"blomberg-s05-028":["For one that is great which for the other is small. The Latin language, as also metaphysical questions and investigations are important and great to learned men.",[]],"blomberg-s05-029":["For the female sex, however, they are unnecessary. So too not all cognitions are necessary to people of rank, princes etc., but they can in many sciences and in various pieces rely on the learned; just as someone who does not understand this or that single craft relies on the craftsman, when he commissions him to make a thing. For such lords as princes etc. it would be unnecessary to engage far in *Metaphysica*, and its investigations.",[]],"blomberg-s05-030":["On the other hand one should instill in them the true learnedness which is necessary to them, that is, one should teach them preeminently, and first of all, to distinguish justice, the worth of the human being, of their subjects, hatred for flatterers, and flattery, true provision for the citizens of the land, and the true advantages of the state from the false, and properly imprint on them the knowledge of all this; then so many monarchs now ill disposed will be full of noble magnanimity, and of exalted dispositions, worthy of their estate. we will have happy princes, subjects, and lands. A great mind occupies itself according to its feeling, not with trifles, it sees what is important and chooses it for its object.",[]],"blomberg-s06-001":["The universal supreme question of one who wants to learn *Logica* is probably naturally: What is truth?",[]],"blomberg-s06-002":["Now to answer this question, which is so suited to the understanding of the human being, and especially of a beginner, will cause more difficulties than one should well think, and there have already been people who have well seen that one is properly not in a position to give a distinct complete precise concept of it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-003":["So it has always gone, none of the old Roman jurists e.g. has properly been able to say what is right, and not right (*quid sit jus*) and nonetheless the concept which one has of it is complete for use, and adequately good enough.",[]],"blomberg-s06-004":["In order to see the difficulties, however, that can occur with the concept of truth, we note the following: First, if a mark *criterium* of truth is to be specified, then a judgment a rule will have to be given of it. The supreme *criterium* thus cannot be specified.",[]],"blomberg-s06-005":["If a cognition does not agree with the constitution of the thing which we represent to ourselves, and want to cognize, then it is false, since it cannot subsist with truth. If on the other hand a cognition is in accordance with the constitution of that thing which we represent to ourselves, then it is true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-006":["The skeptic however on the other hand said: Something is true, if it agrees with the object. but now I cannot see this, without first considering, and cognizing the object. It is thus nothing other than that truth will consist in this, that the cognition of the object agrees with the cognition of the object. But this is indeed *identitas idem per idem*, nothing, said the skeptics, is a circle in the definition of truth. This argument was called *Diallele*, and since the skeptics inferred thus, they finally derived from it the principal conclusion that all distinction of the true and false was nothing.",[]],"blomberg-s06-007":["In false cognition I cognize, and comprehend not the thing itself which I want to represent to myself, but another. e.g. if I represent the polyp to myself as a worm, then I have a false cognition of a polyp.",[]],"blomberg-s06-008":["False learned cognitions are such as are supposed to be learned, or else also seem learned, but yet in fact are not. This however happens always, when one clothes the indistinct common cognitions in *terminos occultos*, through which one yet actually obtains no correct concept of the thing, but merely *idem per idem* is defined. e.g. if one asked after the cause of the propagation, and multiplication of the human, and animal understanding, and wanted to have a distinct concept of it, how such came about? then the ancients said with an affected learned mien: the cause of the propagation of human beings and of animals is the *vis plastica*, power of propagation.",[]],"blomberg-s06-009":["One then imagines oneself to have given an actual ground of the thing, and yet says nothing more than that which one has actually asked, and wants to know, but yet in other words.",[]],"blomberg-s06-010":["One clothes the concept of such a thing somewhat differently, sets on it, so to speak, a plume of feathers, and then estimates it exceedingly highly.",[]],"blomberg-s06-011":["In the writings of the ancient philosophers one finds such *qualitates occultas* very frequently, and in the writings of Crusius they are as frequent as they could ever have been with the ancients. He says e.g.: something is true because no one can regard, and hold it other than as true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-012":["The one means just as much as the other, this is thus a *qualitas occulta*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-013":["Now if one does not want to let oneself be asked further about the cause of this or that, then one often specifies a ground which yet is none. e.g. if I ask why the one yawns and looks at that one, then the ancients said earnestly, the ground of it is sympathy. This word however indicates nothing other than a suffering-with. They thus said nothing other with it than that it came from this, because the one must have suffering-with with the other. but how is this applied to yawning? Whereby they thus explain nothing.",[]],"blomberg-s06-014":["With the truth of learned cognition the formal also matters. The cognition itself must actually be not a common, but rather a learned cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s06-015":["The material in a cognition can very often be true, the cognition can be in accordance with the constitution of the thing, and thus as a common cognition have truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-016":["But if the same cognition were supposed to be learned, without yet its actually being so, then it will through this become a false learned cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s06-017":["To spread *qualitates occultas* is the grave of philosophy. one then believes oneself to possess an actual learned cognition, while it is yet nothing other than a mere common one.",[]],"blomberg-s06-018":["That which Crusius says of probability in his writings pleases very many quite well. it is however in fact nothing other than what one already knows through the merely common cognition. only that it is presented with a certain pomp which is always very peculiar to Crusius.",[]],"blomberg-s06-019":["Hence it follows then naturally that it carries with it indeed the illusion, but not the essence of a learnedness, and of a learned cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s06-020":["The first in every matter, cognition, science, art etc. causes great difficulty.",[]],"blomberg-s06-021":["Truth, and error never lie in concepts, but merely in judgments. Whoever thus never judges, will also never err, but he will also never say a truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-022":["The concept is always the *datum* about which I am to judge. Concepts are the materials for judging.",[]],"blomberg-s06-023":["There are methods for avoiding all error, but these are so simple that one for that very reason does not heed them. They consist however briefly in this: do not judge so much, do not decide often, indeed very rarely, except in certain cases where it is also necessary. Do not always, and very rarely settle whether something is true, or whether it is false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-024":["There is thus, and it is necessary, in order to avoid error, a certain moderation in thinking, and in judging.",[]],"blomberg-s06-025":["But how many philosophers of our times like always to decide (*decidiren*).",[]],"blomberg-s06-026":["To this bold assertion of opinions once accepted or hatched by oneself, to these decisive judgments about propositions of reason, customary already in old times, Pyrrho cried out against: »*Non liquet*«. – Many *autores* entangle themselves in such doubtful circumstances through the decisive judgments and side-thoughts, which are often taken (*capirt*) as errors.",[]],"blomberg-s06-027":["A household remedy for errors is: to distinguish nothing, to judge little, hence it comes then that one finds, a village full of peasants has by far not so many errors as an academy of sciences, thoughtlessness preserves them from errors.",[]],"blomberg-s06-028":["Yet shall we therefore, in order to want to avoid all errors, be permitted to resolve to leave many unweighed.",[]],"blomberg-s06-029":["This tells us, and indicates only, which are the limitations of our judgments, and it is just about these limitations that one must judge. but if one had exceedingly important ends, so that each, even the smallest error would be dangerous, then it is of course better to remain in one place, and merely to judge about that which concerns our affairs, thus it is e.g. in morals.",[]],"blomberg-s06-030":["Ignorance is an empty space in our head and for that reason does not yet hinder any true knowledge, if he does not spread it. Error on the other hand however is a hindrance of truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-031":["So long as our cognitions are only speculations, we can well still venture to err, but as soon as an error becomes disadvantageous, and this happens as soon as it bears on the practical, then it is very dangerous to retain it",[]],"blomberg-s06-032":["Judgments are actions [207a] of the understanding, and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-033":["The senses however do not judge not all concepts arise from the understanding, but some from the senses, all judgments however come merely from the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-034":["Often human beings take a certain judgment of their understanding for a sensation, and representation of the senses, while it is yet nothing less than that.",[]],"blomberg-s06-035":["About a sensation there is no disputing. This is a *datum*, and not yet an error. often however such a sensation is properly the judgment of the understanding on the occasion of such sensations.",[]],"blomberg-s06-036":["The appeals to such feelings, and sensations are very much the fashion at present, often some want to feel, and sense right, and wrong masterfully, and indeed through an inner moral feeling peculiar to them alone. Ridiculous. In this the understanding must necessarily be in play.",[]],"blomberg-s06-037":["Truth is nothing other than the agreement of a cognition with the laws of the understanding, and of reason and on the other hand the conflict of a cognition against the laws of the understanding, and of reason is nothing other than falsehood.",[]],"blomberg-s06-038":["No power in nature deviates in its actions from its laws, or conditions under which alone it can act, the understanding taken alone thus cannot possibly err.",[]],"blomberg-s06-039":["All judging agrees with the laws of the understanding, all judgments of the understanding are thus true. even in our erroneous judgments the understanding must always have done something, and thus in them everything can yet never be false, but there must still always be something true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-040":["It is wholly impossible that a human being should err entirely, when he judges.",[]],"blomberg-s06-041":["In error there is thus still truth. but we judge there in a mixed way, and mingle the effects of the remaining powers of the mind in between and from this arises the erroneous, which is so very contrary to the understanding, and of which we yet falsely think, and often believe quite certainly, it is an effect of the understanding. While it is yet in fact nothing other than a bastard as it were of sensibility, and of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-042":["Now since, as we have already shown, each judgment does not take place without an *ingrediens* of truth, it follows necessarily from this, we will have greatly to moderate our judgment about the *Critica* of truth, and about the judgments or also errors of others. For it is a certain *principium*: One can never come to truth if one always quarrels, if always one conflicts with the other.",[]],"blomberg-s06-043":["Every discovery of an in fact gross and manifest error is very sad. let one thus rather not make such sad discoveries, help one another jointly and full of friendship, support each other, and not always act (*agiren*) one against the other.",[]],"blomberg-s06-044":["Instead of refuting a matter, then, one will merely have to investigate it, whether there does not actually lie a truth in it, what is lacking for supplementing, and so to act jointly everywhere, and then to make comprehensible to the one erring in a way least biting, but loving, how it is not at all to be wondered at, since he could very easily, and readily have erred.",[]],"blomberg-s06-045":["This idle way of judging, this so well applied good-heartedness of dispositions is just as necessary for the attainment of upright cognition, as it may itself always be in common life.",[]],"blomberg-s06-046":["There is, however, naturally therefore no total error. Otherwise the understanding would have to contradict itself and its precepts, and act contrary to them.",[]],"blomberg-s06-047":["Errors, however great and important they may seem, are yet all only particular.",[]],"blomberg-s06-048":["One can teach no one except only through the remainder of understanding which is still in him. One can improve no one himself, only through the remainder of good which is still in him.",[]],"blomberg-s06-049":["In the teaching of the understanding, and improvement of the will I must certainly still always presuppose something true and something good.",[]],"blomberg-s06-050":["The judgment of every other human being is yet always a judgment of one of those human beings, whose judgment of all taken together is my judge, and the greatest judge of all my products of understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-051":["A contradiction is of course nothing other than an occurrence where the one says: yes, the other however on the other hand: No.",[]],"blomberg-s06-052":["It follows thus from this that it must to a certain extent naturally disquiet every rational one. It is however a universal duty of a philosopher, in such a case always to take up the cause of humanity in general, and to think affably thus: these opinions, seeming so bizarre, and absurd, are perhaps not at all so badly thought, not so absurd, as it well seems.",[]],"blomberg-s06-053":["Everything that makes human beings united and compatible actually contributes very much to promoting the perfection of the whole human race. Conflict however produces nothing. It holds everywhere and so also in the whole world, among the learned:",[]],"blomberg-s06-054":["*Concordia res parvae crescunt*",[]],"blomberg-s06-055":["*Discordia dilabuntur*",[]],"blomberg-s06-056":["Further the limitations i.e. the narrow boundaries by which the human understanding is surrounded, are not in the least, as so many supposed philosophers yet hold, to be regarded as sources of errors; indeed they are in fact causes of the ignorance of the human being in many pieces, but not of error, as soon however as with this ignorance a self-conceit a presumption a learned arrogance to judge more than one knows, and is in a position to, combines, then error can arise from this.",[]],"blomberg-s06-057":["The sole cause of error is thus the ill-applied presumption to overstep the limitations, and boundaries of one's own understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-058":["The understanding alone does not err. The senses alone also do not err, they are passive, and do not judge at all.",[]],"blomberg-s06-059":["The human understanding alone judges.",[]],"blomberg-s06-060":["There must thus also be certain *materialia*, materials for judging.",[]],"blomberg-s06-061":["The understanding must necessarily combine with sensibility in order to be able to judge, and this does not depend (*dependiret*) in the least on choice",[]],"blomberg-s06-062":["There are thus naturally also subjective conditions of our understanding, and thinking. and these we count all to sensibility.",[]],"blomberg-s06-063":["Sometimes the understanding is as it were in a certain way at a loss. Its horizon is wholly befogged, but since the concepts must always be furnished (*fourniret*) to the understanding, and laid before it for choice, we well see, sensibility must always assist (*assistiren*) the understanding. only this often does not happen sufficiently. [217a]",[]],"blomberg-s06-064":["To all distinguishing of the true from the false belongs the cognition of inner sense i.e. I must be, and become conscious of what properly lies in my concept, and what I think.",[]],"blomberg-s06-065":["Inner sense is often dull, and its horizon befogged, and gives us not enough help.",[]],"blomberg-s06-066":["But since inner sense yet also belongs to sensibility, while without it the understanding cannot judge, the understanding must judge by the help of sensibility, and just this combination, and mingling of the understanding with sensibility is the source of all errors, namely when the effects of the understanding are taken for effects of sensibility.",[]],"blomberg-s06-067":["As we thus see, the understanding, and sensibility alone cannot err. thus we see how an error is possible.",[]],"blomberg-s06-068":["All errors are so to speak: curved lines, which we determine, in that we are driven on the one side by the understanding, on the other side however by error.",[]],"blomberg-s06-069":["We see before us the play of the things of the world, but we do not for that reason yet have a complete concept of their motion.",[]],"blomberg-s06-070":["The senses do not judge. Inner sense, which alone teaches us, whereby we become aware whether we have all *requisita* of cognition, or not, does not do us the right service continually, and from this then arises the semblance as if the objects themselves lacked something for their cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s06-071":["One can e.g. have correct rules for reckoning, and nevertheless for that reason fail in carrying them out. There is commonly a fault of inner sense, this happens through overlooking.",[]],"blomberg-s06-072":["The sole means for avoiding error will thus be that I find out certain *criteria*: through which I can in each judgment properly distinguish that which stems from the understanding from that which outer or inner senses supply (*suppeditiren*) to us.",[]],"blomberg-s06-073":["The understanding however, abandoned by inner sense, judges at once, come out what may, if inner sense offers it either something wrong, or nothing at all",[]],"blomberg-s06-074":["All *criteria* of truth however are either",[]],"blomberg-s06-075":["A: inner or B. outer",[]],"blomberg-s06-076":["The former are objective, which contain the ground why properly something is true, or false. The others however subjective which contain certain circumstances by which one is in a position to presume the truth or the falsehood in a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s06-077":["That whereby the true cognitions are distinguished from the false is the mark of truth",[]],"blomberg-s06-078":["The proposition of identity is the formal mark of truth in affirmative, and the *principium contradictionis* in negative judgments.",[]],"blomberg-s06-079":["Material marks are the *notae intermediae*, whereby I prove a judgment, the *principia materialia*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-080":["In a simple, and wholly simple *idea* is naturally neither truth nor falsehood to be met with. but it takes place only in comparison.",[]],"blomberg-s06-081":["If I attribute to the thing such a mark as either contradicts it, or is even wholly contrary to it, or deny in it such a mark as is yet identical in it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-082":["That however whereby I cognize that the comparison with a thing is correct, or not, is called the *nota intermedia* (*principium materiale*) e.g. if I want to prove that a body is divisible, then I take a *notam intermediam*, that it is composite, and infer in the following way: everything that is composite is divisible, *atqui* every body is composite, *ergo* every body is divisible.",[]],"blomberg-s06-083":["Some propositions are so constituted that they have no material mark of truth whatsoever in themselves, but merely carry the formal with them. These are properly the immediate judgments that we have of a thing, and can prove, and derive from nothing further. E.g. that a body is composite.",[]],"blomberg-s06-084":["Simple concepts have in themselves the mark that they are at all times true. e.g. if I represent the moon to myself as round, then this is a true concept because I actually think it so.",[]],"blomberg-s06-085":["But if I compare this representation of the moon with the representation of other planets, if I doubt whether the moon is round, or oval, then I can very easily err in this, and my cognition can be true as well as false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-086":["Among all marks of truth the first inner characteristic, or principal mark of it is inner possibility.",[]],"blomberg-s06-087":["Through experience we are taught of the possibility of many things, which one would otherwise actually not have held to be possible.",[]],"blomberg-s06-088":["Possibility is nevertheless still far from a sufficient ground of truth: because something is possible, and if we can well represent it to ourselves and think it through our reason, it is not yet actually true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-089":["Yet possibility is at least a ground of probability, if something is possible, and can be thought by us, then we at least believe, and hope, that it could be true, supposing that we are also not in a position to assert it with certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s06-090":["Impossibility however is on the contrary an always sufficient, and *sufficient* ground for the infallible proof of the untruth, or falsehood of a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s06-091":["If we see that something is wholly impossible, then we commonly also at once hold it to be false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-092":["Yet it is still to be remarked here that we can err in both cases. we very often take something to be useful, and *per Consequentiam* also to be true, and correct, which yet, if we examine it more closely, is actually entirely impossible, and false. and on the contrary we in turn take many a thing to be Impossible, and to be False, which is yet actually possible, and true at the same time, or if not true, yet at least useful.",[]],"blomberg-s06-093":["If the consequences of the cognition are true, then the cognitions themselves are also true. E.g. It is true, and settled for all, that there are Fire-spewing mountains, warm baths etc. Now just as this is true, so it can also always be true that there is a Central Fire in the earth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-094":["With some ground we can, to be sure, always infer from this to the Central-Fire of the earth, for these Fire-spewing mountains, warm baths etc. can with much conjecture, indeed even probability, be regarded as consequences of such a Central-Fire, and from the consequences one can very often correctly infer to the ground.",[]],"blomberg-s06-095":["If all consequences of all cognition without exception are true, then the cognition is also certainly true. For were even just some falsehood to be met with in the cognition, then falsehood would also already have to be lodged in the consequences. But if this is not so, then nothing in the cognition can be false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-096":["If for *Ex.* a fortunate engagement is announced to us in the newspapers, and the victory of a hero is made known; if the *Publication* of it even takes place from the pulpit, if solemn arrangements are made, festivals of thanksgiving are held over it, in short, if all consequences of it are true, then the engagement itself must also be true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-097":["Sometimes, however, we cannot cognize all consequences of a thing, and then among them some False ones can also very well many a time have been mixed in, and be present. even though we take the cognition itself to be true. e.g. if we assume the Central-Fire to be met with in the earth, then from this it will in turn follow that the layers of the earth that stood not very far from this Central-Fire would be entirely dried up, and spoiled, from all too great, and violent heat, and would finally even have to collapse into one another.",[]],"blomberg-s06-098":["But since, as one indeed sees, this consequence is false, so it is also quite easy to notice that the Central-Fire too cannot be taken to be entirely undoubtedly certain, and reliably asserted.",[]],"blomberg-s06-099":["If a cognition is to be possible in interconnection, then it must have correct grounds and important as well as true consequences.",[]],"blomberg-s06-100":["The cognition of the possibility of a thing arises from experience, reason cannot teach us this. e.g. that the magnet draws iron to itself.",[]],"blomberg-s06-101":["The possibility of composite bodies we can have insight into through reason, if we are convinced of the possibility of simple effects.",[]],"blomberg-s06-102":["But the first possibility of causes, and effects is evident solely through the senses from experience. Hence reason also gives no objection to the impossibility of a thing whose possibility, or actual *Existence* we clearly see through experience. Experience namely teaches us through the actuality of a thing naturally also about the possibility of the same.",[]],"blomberg-s06-103":["Reason, however, can derive from these examples of experience certain laws of the possibility of this, or that thing, but the first *Principia Data*, and *Materialia* of the possibility must yet necessarily be given to us from experience, and arise from it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-104":["But we must not take something to be impossible because we cannot think it, or Represent it through the senses: e.g. the one born blind cannot make for himself through reason any representation of colors, because experience denies him the *Data* for it nonetheless he can and may still by no means infer to the impossibility of them. if we assume the former, then all philosophy is abolished.",[]],"blomberg-s06-105":["There are logical truths. These are those which relate merely to understanding, and reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-106":["From the other side, however, we can also think an Aesthetic and a practical truth. the former namely relates to the relation of taste, the latter on the contrary to the rules of free will.",[]],"blomberg-s06-107":["Logical truth, however, is not always aesthetically true, nor does it always bring practical perfection with it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-108":["Every truth is always in itself a logical perfection, or a perfect cognition, according to the rules of the understanding, and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-109":["Much can be Aesthetically true without its being logically true, it need only charm, and please. e.g. so it is with novels.",[]],"blomberg-s06-110":["Even practical truth need not always be grounded on the understanding and on reason. E.g. if I slander someone to another, then I can thereby excite his *affecten*, and in such a way his will, although this slander is not logically true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-111":["Logical truth is distinguished from Aesthetic truth by this, that, if something delights, and moves me, I can give up something of the truth of it. Logical truth can thus very often yield to the Aesthetic.",[]],"blomberg-s06-112":["Truth often does not please us most, but even the lie. we are far happier in fables than in logical truths. whoever e.g. takes nature as the basis in painting does not always agree with delight, charm, and pleasure. *Fiction* often moves much more.",[]],"blomberg-s06-113":["Our wishes often belong among the *fictiones*. they often aim at something better than nature properly offers us. So e.g. enthusiasm in friendship pleases us, we are moved by the Earthly shepherd's life. It is indeed of course true, in Aesthetic truth too there must always be a certain logical truth, but the more we look to the Aesthetically True, the less logical truth can be found in the *Subject* that we want to represent to ourselves. So e.g. it is Aesthetically true that the human being, when he is dead, will not rise again. Although this runs directly contrary to logical (and to say nothing of moral) truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-114":["What all human beings say is true, according to the rules of taste, and thus Aesthetically true. for a universal *Consensus* is sufficient for this. meanwhile this is yet very often false, according to the rules of reason, what is true according to the rules of taste",[]],"blomberg-s06-115":["Human beings often think what they hope, or at least wish, as possible, and actual, because they would gladly have it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-116":["All fables contain an Aesthetic but seldom a logical truth in themselves. e.g. the speaking of animals. Here there must of course actually be something true: but aesthetic truth has precedence over the Logically True. one represents something as possible therein. It also involves no absurdity that animals should not also be able to speak.",[]],"blomberg-s06-117":["Under a certain *Hypothesi*, which can be invented, a certain degree of logical truth can prevail even in a fable.",[]],"blomberg-s06-118":["But the degree of truth that must be found therein, this no scholar has so far been able to determine precisely, and the greatest Aestheticians have not yet succeeded in indicating it. Objective marks would often not be adequate for us. One can here only appeal either to the agreement of our cognitions among themselves, or with the cognitions of others, as happens e.g. in calculating.",[]],"blomberg-s06-119":["We always test a proposition against the reason of others, or against ours, at different times, and occasions, and in different situations of our reason. but very often we also rely on illusion i.e. on the way it first appears to us. we want only to obtain the approval of others.",[]],"blomberg-s06-120":["We gladly *publiciren* our judgments if we have, or believe we can obtain, a merit thereby.",[]],"blomberg-s06-121":["The Human understanding, since it has a natural law to extend its cognitions as much as ever possible, so the means must also not be forbidden it through which alone it is yet able to distinguish the true from the false, and not only to enrich its cognitions but also to correct them.",[]],"blomberg-s06-122":["One must thus set no hindrances against the making known of judgments, that is the exposing of them to the insights of all. That is indeed the universal right of every human being, and the only sure path to arrive at truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-123":["Even *Mathematic* borrows the proposition, and the triumphant and invincible certainty in its Apodictic proofs, and convictions, not so much from the objective, but rather from Subjective *Criterien* of truth, because in this science there is something which agrees with every reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-124":["But where cognitions indeed rise very high, but soon are again cast down all the deeper, there the suspicion is very great that the universal approval which was attributed to them at the beginning and all at once was grounded only on the particular heads, and thus cannot be lasting.",[]],"blomberg-s06-125":["A cognition is either entirely true, or either in part true, or in part false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-126":["But a cognition can never be entirely and wholly false, although it can often be wholly true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-127":["Every judgment arises from the understanding, hence it must also to some extent agree with the laws of the understanding, and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-128":["It is therefore impossible that a human being should pass an entirely false judgment, and even accept the same as entirely true",[]],"blomberg-s06-129":["For otherwise the judgment would have to contradict the laws of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-130":["A cognition, therefore, that is even given out as false throughout, will nevertheless always be only in part false, and always contain a certain degree of truth. Indeed even in the judgments of an Insane human being (however strange this remark may also seem) upon closer examination at least a partial truth will always be met with.",[]],"blomberg-s06-131":["If we were to assume that some judgments could be entirely, and wholly false, then it would yet have to be possible that the understanding deviates from all laws, and in this way under this *Supposition* the entire authority of our Doctrine of Reason would fall, and vanish, we would on the contrary be able to trust it very little.",[]],"blomberg-s06-132":["If another were capable of judging wholly falsely, then it would indeed also be possible in just the same way as well with us as with him. we would thus in the entire use of our understanding and of our reason be nothing other than very unsure.",[]],"blomberg-s06-133":["If we thus believe we meet with falsehood in the judgment of another, then we must also by that very fact be convinced that some partial truth must prevail therein, however hidden it may also be in it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-134":["In this Principle, however, much fairness also actually prevails. such a way of thinking is moral, and sympathetic, so that one does not straightaway deny others all truth in their cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s06-135":["But as exclusive self-love lets one promote only one's own welfare, without taking the least care for the good of others, and contributing something to it, so also the Exclusive judgment, where one ascribes to oneself alone the possession of all true cognitions, but on the other hand entirely denies them to others, and invents nothing but false ones, is nothing but a pernicious self-conceit, where one esteems oneself alone highly, and despises all others beside oneself.",[]],"blomberg-s06-136":["Such heads, taken in by this self-conceit, think a Goshen in their understanding alone, while everything else is veiled in Egyptian darknesses.",[]],"blomberg-s06-137":["Many judgments and concepts are true in regard to their essential pieces, but on the other hand false in regard to their *accidental* pieces.",[]],"blomberg-s06-138":["In theory we can often judge falsely. Notwithstanding this, however, our judgments can nevertheless be true in regard to *Praxis*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-139":["Even in natural theology there can actually be many erroneous ideas and *Speculationes* which are yet true *in der Praxi*. e.g. with the omnipresence of God one can represent, and think it as well as nothing, as also as an expansion through the whole of space.",[]],"blomberg-s06-140":["Now this concept is of course false, notwithstanding this, however, it is yet true in regard to *Praxis*. God may now be Omnipresent how and in whatever way he will, enough, he actually is so, and we must therefore stand in awe of his omnipresence, in whatever it may consist.",[]],"blomberg-s06-141":["If a *Speculation* is actually erroneous, but yet this error therefore has no influence on *Praxin*, i.e. on the morality of mores, then it is indifferent in regard to mores, and not so abominable as if it also corrupted mores.",[]],"blomberg-s06-142":["If therefore our judgments are in conflict with the judgments of others, then, in order to be able to compare these judgments with, and against one another, we must make use of the following rules: A knowledge of truth is: If our judgments agree with the judgments of others who can have science of it, or what is the same, if they are universally valid.",[]],"blomberg-s06-143":["If therefore something is to be found in my judgment which is contrary to the judgments of others, then I am not very sure with respect to the truth of my judgment, and I must therefore first take pains to seek out some posited, even hidden and far-fetched agreement of my judgment with the judgment of others, and this agreement will also actually, at least in most cases, be very easy to find. For in our agreeing judgments more agreement is present than we suppose.",[]],"blomberg-s06-144":["It is only necessary that we understand the judgment of others, and do not misinterpret it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-145":["There is also no *Systema* of universal truths of reason that would be entirely and wholly true, except *Mathematic*, just as no cognition can be entirely false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-146":["A cognition can be *partialiter* true in that it is indeed entirely true, but does not concern the whole *Object*, but only a part of it. nonetheless such cognitions are always certain.",[]],"blomberg-s06-147":["Whoever thus straightaway, all at once and without thinking much about it, confidently gives the approval of truth to a whole Philosophical *Systema* of another, of him one can always correctly infer that he does not yet understand it, and is not able to distinguish the true from the False rightly. For it is certain: no *Systema* can be entirely true, there is always a falsehood posited in it, even if it does not concern the essential of the System. With Philosophical truths errors, and mistakes are always so mixed up with truth that one always finds something in them to improve",[]],"blomberg-s06-148":["A cognition is a precise one which has enough truth in itself as is only required for the attainment of the main end, but on the other hand crude, if with it one cannot attain the main end. (Precision is a cognition, but cannot suitably be opposed to the crudeness of it, but the crudeness of it must be *opponiret* to the unity of it.)",[]],"blomberg-s06-149":["*Praecision*, punctiliousness is: if I also indicate no more truth of a cognition than is necessary to have insight into it. The transgression of this rule becomes a great mistake. That is called crude in a cognition for which only a small attention, that is called subtle, for whose clarity a greater attention is required.",[]],"blomberg-s06-150":["Very often it is necessary to be subtle. because from the omission of *Subtilitaet* such great confusions arise. although *Subtilitaet* too, in that it requires an all too great attention, is often censured.",[]],"blomberg-s06-151":["In Regard to practical cognitions one can very much appeal to, and rely on, healthy understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-152":["A rule for correctly testing oneself, and one's science of some cognition, is this: if I myself perfectly understand, and have insight into, a thing, then I must be able to impart, and represent it to another human being who has only a healthy understanding, myself so clearly, and so distinctly, that he has insight into it just as perfectly as I. But if I cannot do this, then it is a sure sign I do not myself yet rightly understand it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-153":["Every cognition is subject to a partial untruth, but this untruth concerns merely the more, or less precise. and it can nevertheless still always be precise enough to reach its aim. e.g. if I measure a mountain, then I will never be able to measure precisely, and infallibly, so that I should not miss at least, supposing it were even only a few inches, notwithstanding this, however, the *Quantum* that has come out through my measurement is adequate, and precise enough, to determine the whole height of the mountain. many of our judgments of reason are passed only according to the measure of the eye, and can therefore of course not well be precise enough according to the finer reason, and erudition, notwithstanding this they are entirely precise for their aim.",[]],"blomberg-s06-154":["The highest, and most laborious precision, however, which must be reached with much *Accurateße*, and erudition, but with respect to the main end is yet of no use, is called pedantry. In *Mathematic*, however, it is entirely otherwise. There a precision must always take place. nothing must be lacking in it there.",[]],"blomberg-s06-155":["If a cognition considered Historically is correct, then it can nevertheless considered *formaliter* be incorrect. e.g. the soul is immortal, this proposition is *materialiter* true, and correct. The form, however, when one represents this, can often be entirely false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-156":["A cognition, however, is *materialiter* true if we have a Material science of it. But *Formaliter* true if the grounds for proving, and deriving it are correct.",[]],"blomberg-s06-157":["Much that is undoubtedly certain in regard to *Praxis* is false in regard to reason. e.g. a part is smaller than the whole, or: the world must have a beginning.",[]],"blomberg-s06-158":["A cognition can be *materialiter* true, and *Formaliter* false, but not conversely thus, it is then a formal error This occurs in philosophy more frequently than one believes.",[]],"blomberg-s06-159":["Thus there is also lodged no material, but a Formal error in the *qualitatibus occultis* which one gives as the cause of a thing as a ground, and answers the given question with the question itself.",[]],"blomberg-s06-160":["All Definitions that explain *idem per idem* are *formaliter* erroneous.",[]],"blomberg-s06-161":["These formal errors where one grants the Philosopher the proof in a cognition, in that one is already taken in by it *anticipando*, and even takes the proof to be dispensable.",[]],"blomberg-s06-162":["Where my healthy understanding already precedes the cognition of truth, there I am not in the least painstaking, and also need not be, to examine the rigor of the proof. This rigor, which one lets befall certain propositions, also often comes from this, that they must be regarded not only as very true, but also as very necessary.",[]],"blomberg-s06-163":["So it is with the two Articles of Faith of natural religion",[]],"blomberg-s06-164":["1: there is a God.",[]],"blomberg-s06-165":["2: there is another world.",[]],"blomberg-s06-166":["Those who want to examine such shallow proofs of such necessary propositions, and make some fine *Distinctiones*, these straightaway fall into ill repute, because one believes they may well be enemies of the propositions themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s06-167":["These are the *piae Fraudes*, which are so suited to the human being, so very peculiar to him.",[]],"blomberg-s06-168":["One thinks, and believes one is right in this, if one believes one does not deceive someone if one imparts to him something good under all sorts of pretext dissimulation, etc., indeed often one seeks a merit in it, but without looking to the Moral side of this deception, which yet always remains, it is already in itself reprehensible; the recommendation of virtue must be virtuous. –",[]],"blomberg-s06-169":["Much is often cognized as true without any marks. Such truths, irrefutable in themselves, are called indemonstrable, i.e. because one cannot doubt them. otherwise it is not yet necessary to prove them particularly.",[]],"blomberg-s06-170":["A cognition whose consequences are all true is also true. but if only one consequence is false, then the cognition is also false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-171":["From a false cognition nothing true can arise. For a false cognition is the true Nothing. but the consequences of a cognition that is partly true, partly false, can be true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-172":["One will thus, in the examination of a cognition, be compelled to look not so much to its true as to its false consequences.",[]],"blomberg-s06-173":["In general it is of course true that the so-called Apagogic proofs, or the *Demonstrationes ad absurdum contrarium*, where namely in order to assert and to strengthen one's opinion one makes it distinct, and demonstrates, how it would be ridiculous, and entirely absurd, if one assumed the opposite of it, that these proofs are very easy, but they yet do not give me enough light in regard to the sources of truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-174":["The interconnection of cognitions with their grounds is thus indeed the surest, and best mark of the truth of a cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s06-175":["Historical cognitions are distinguished from rational ones according to the form of reason of the cognition",[]],"blomberg-s06-176":["A cognition is Historical if it does not agree with the form of reason, rational if it agrees with the same, and indeed without regard to the *Object*. Here, however, we must also reflect on the *Object* if we want to fix the distinction between Dogmatic, and Historical cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s06-177":["*1. mo* The Dogmatic is a universal cognition which arises *a priori* from reason",[]],"blomberg-s06-178":["*2. do* The Historical, however, is not always universal, and rests on the testimonies of things that have happened, on the testimony of others, it thus arises *a posteriori*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-179":["All Morals is Dogmatic, one teaches, censures, and indicates there not what is commonly abhorred by human beings, but what ought to be censured.",[]],"blomberg-s06-180":["*Physica*, however, is not Dogmatic. a part of it would be pure *Physic*, where we derive the properties of bodies from universal Rules, and concepts of them.",[]],"blomberg-s06-181":["The weight of bodies, however, already belongs to experience and is a Historical cognition *a posteriori*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-182":["All *Dogmata* should properly first be grounded on reason, but they can also often be Historical.",[]],"blomberg-s06-183":["The *Object* of *Dogmatum* are not single things, but universal properties, and constitutions of things. The *Object* of *Historie*, however, are single things.",[]],"blomberg-s06-184":["But although *Dogmata* have universal properties of things as their object, they can yet often be cognized Historically, although they are rational according to their nature.",[]],"blomberg-s06-185":["All *Dogmata* are according to their nature *Objective* rational, though they can be *Subjective* Historical.",[]],"blomberg-s06-186":["Now we must also speak of doctrinal edifices. The *Autor* restricts the significance of this word all too much, in that he *appliciret* it merely, and solely to Dogmatic truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-187":["A better Definition Of a System will thus be the following:",[]],"blomberg-s06-188":["A System is a multitude or manifoldness of many simple and combined-together cognitions, and truths, such that these taken together make up a Whole. every System must thus carry a unity with it, yet this unity can have as its ground either *Coordination*, as with Historical cognitions, or *Subordination*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-189":["A *Systema*, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s06-190":["A. Historical",[]],"blomberg-s06-191":["B. rational",[]],"blomberg-s06-192":["The form of a doctrinal edifice is in most cases regarded as arbitrary.",[]],"blomberg-s06-193":["Human beings have a different understanding, the one who gladly goes to the parts is Subtle. The one who goes to the whole is the great one.",[]],"blomberg-s06-194":["A proper doctrinal edifice must be built only by a single one. it cannot possibly be a patchwork to which one adds this, the other that.",[]],"blomberg-s06-195":["This capacity to form a *Systema* is so excellent because it is so rare, and at the same time has so great a use.",[]],"blomberg-s06-196":["A Kind of *Systema* one must always have, for every one of our cognitions must have a Logical place.",[]],"blomberg-s06-197":["The first *Systemata* are naturally astonishingly defective. Gradually they become larger, and more complete.",[]],"blomberg-s06-198":["We have already above fixed the distinction between Logical and Aesthetic truths.",[]],"blomberg-s06-199":["Much can be logically true without on that account having an Aesthetic truth in itself, and on the other hand much can be Aesthetically true without on that account having Logical truth in itself.",[]],"blomberg-s06-200":["To this belong all *Fictiones*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-201":["To this belong all Aesthetic truths which are true according to the Rules of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s06-202":["In these cognitions there actually prevails a kind of Fashion. To this belong e.g. proverbs, which are actually subject to a kind of Fashion; although they are actually not even always such propositions as are according to taste.",[]],"blomberg-s06-203":["Aesthetically true is everything that is in conformity with the Rules of taste, and agrees with the Rules of appearance.",[]],"blomberg-s06-204":["Poets who want to write merely Aesthetically true one surely does not test according to the gold-scale of Logic, one surely does them great injustice thereby. they are the poets, and not Moralists and Logicians, and always remain in their worth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-205":["In order to be Aesthetically true, a degree of untruth can also often take place. which, however, must nevertheless not be entirely contrary to truth. e.g. when a Philosopher there cried out: My dear friends, there are no friends, then this is aesthetically true, and a good thought, because good friends are very seldom, and a complete agreement of minds is very rare.",[]],"blomberg-s06-206":["We come now to a very important doctrine of the whole of Logic, namely to the doctrine of the origin of error. Does error arise from the lack of reason?",[]],"blomberg-s06-207":["Answer: No. from the lack of reason nothing arises but ignorance. Ignorance, however, is very much distinguished from error.",[]],"blomberg-s06-208":["He who knows little can yet judge entirely correctly of this little.",[]],"blomberg-s06-209":["The other question is:",[]],"blomberg-s06-210":["Does error not also arise from the lack of cognition, combined with the desire to judge?",[]],"blomberg-s06-211":["Answer: The one who certainly knows he is ignorant will certainly not presume to judge about something that he does not understand.",[]],"blomberg-s06-212":["But supposing he did not feel himself incapable either, and thought he knew much, thus wanted to judge about much, then nothing more would yet arise from this, he would employ his effort to judge In vain.",[]],"blomberg-s06-213":["But that the understanding still seems very difficult to have insight into, how can that come about? That the understanding in judging deviates from its laws?",[]],"blomberg-s06-214":["No force can of itself conflict with its own laws. E.g. if a body is left to its weight, then it falls downward according to the laws of weight, and one is actually able to calculate the speed of its fall.",[]],"blomberg-s06-215":["If thus in our judgments through the understanding no force were active, no force mixed itself in, then we would actually never err, but only often be ignorant.",[]],"blomberg-s06-216":["No error thus arises properly from the understanding, but another force must at the same time be active in the understanding if an error is to come about. All Forces of nature deviate from their laws only then when foreign laws mix themselves in among the given laws.",[]],"blomberg-s06-217":["If we thus had nothing but understanding, then we would indeed judge only little, but this all little truly.",[]],"blomberg-s06-218":["If we further had an [274a] entirely pure will, then we would, supposing we did even only very little good, yet never be able to sin.",[]],"blomberg-s06-219":["The deviation from the Rules of the pure will makes up moral evil, and this arises merely then, and thereby, because other effects of other forces mix themselves with the otherwise pure laws of the will. E.g.: The inclinations, and *affecten*. In just this way there arises of course, when foreign forces mix themselves with the correct laws of the understanding, a mixed effect, and error arises from the conflict of our judgments from the laws of the understanding, and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-220":["Every error is a *Phaenomenon*, a riddle with respect to the concept of the possibility of the same.",[]],"blomberg-s06-221":["It is as it were an entirely unusual, unnatural appearance, which contradicts the laws of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-222":["Everything that is contrary to the laws of nature is for us no *Object* of examination, but indeed that which seldom occurs, and is contrary to the laws of nature.",[]],"blomberg-s06-223":["Error indeed often occurs, but it conflicts with the laws of the understanding, and of reason, and thus it is worthy to be an *Object* of our examination.",[]],"blomberg-s06-224":["Error stems merely from the understanding, and reason, because foreign forces mix themselves into them. never, however, from the senses, and fantasy.",[]],"blomberg-s06-225":["Since our understanding, and reason judges *Objective*, there are yet still other *Subjective* grounds of our judgment: which, however, do not accord with the understanding, and reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-226":["All error arises when we take *Subjective* grounds of our judgments to be objective. [276a]",[]],"blomberg-s06-227":["The whole story of ghosts is grounded not on the judgment of the understanding, but in the *Imagination*, which in the observation of an *Object* associates with itself other Similar *Objecte*, although they do not *Existiren*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-228":["The understanding combines concepts with a thing, in conformity with its laws, and with the thing, the *Object* itself.",[]],"blomberg-s06-229":["The imagination, however, brings in other images, which relate not to the *Object*, but to the *Subject*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-230":["From this mixture of the imagination with the understanding arise such effects [276b] as do not perfectly agree with the Rules of the understanding, and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-231":["Many errors arise from habit, others merely because a change has taken place in the *Subject*. e.g. when we make a spot on the wall.",[]],"blomberg-s06-232":["Many errors arise because one always *renoviret* in the mind the images once had, and therefore regards everything similar as the thing itself. e.g. if a youth has been sharply *tractiret* by his teacher, so that he therefore hates him, and this one afterwards sees a human being who is similar to him, then he is very often inclined to hate him too merely [276c] because of the similarity with his taskmaster.",[]],"blomberg-s06-233":["One can often reject something through the understanding, and through the effect of another force accept the same again. all this arises merely from the mixture of forces, which, even if they are not contrary to one another, are nevertheless of different kind.",[]],"blomberg-s06-234":["We cognize the magnitude of things in no other way than through the judgment with respect to the distances, e.g. the difference of a fly and an elephant.",[]],"blomberg-s06-235":["We notice a fly well up close, an elephant etc., however, also at a distance, and hence we infer [276d] just only that the latter must be larger than the former.",[]],"blomberg-s06-236":["The imagination, however, can very much alter these judgments, and bring about in our soul other impressions, other representations of the thing than we yet ought to make of it for ourselves if we have insight into its true properties.",[]],"blomberg-s06-237":["Therefore abolish the imagination, the sensible wit, from the human being, and the judgment of the understanding will always be true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-238":["The understanding mixed with other forces errs, and this error we must in turn have insight into through the understanding itself. From this [276e] it is evident that we must have insight into and cognize them through the same force whose mistakes we want to notice.",[]],"blomberg-s06-239":["No error can arise from the understanding, and from reason alone, for no force can act contrary to its own laws.",[]],"blomberg-s06-240":["That ignorance in itself cannot in the least be an origin of an error has already been indicated more fully in the previous *Paragrapho*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-241":["Error is either avoidable or unavoidable.",[]],"blomberg-s06-242":["If one does not judge at all, then one can of course also avoid all errors. he who does not judge also does not err. also an error can be prevented through ignorance. This ignorance, however, consists not in the ignorance of lack, but rather in the ignorance of intention, and this latter is actually a kind of wise ignorance. Many *speculationes* are nothing other than a way to the greatest errors. Many errors, however, can be avoided if one has the intention of settling nothing, deciding nothing. Many things, however, are of the kind that they cannot possibly remain undecided. e.g., if a judge is to settle disputes, supposing therefore that he also errs in these matters and cases, then the error is unavoidable with respect to him",[]],"blomberg-s06-243":["One can opine something, and indeed with ground without erring, but if one passes off this same mere opinion and deficient conjecture as a certain truth, then one often errs.",[]],"blomberg-s06-244":["The propensity to decide is thus the surest way to error, and a certain (to many so-called philosophers customary) dogmatic pride, but if one does not yet trust the marks of truth which one has cognized hitherto, and therefore does not yet pass a judgment, but rather often holds it back, then one actually avoids many errors.",[]],"blomberg-s06-245":["A cognition is",[]],"blomberg-s06-246":["1. manifestly or 2. in a hidden way false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-247":["This division of false cognition is relative.",[]],"blomberg-s06-248":["We have a kind of average understanding, which is abstracted from the understanding of all human beings, and is as it were a standard, i.e., an ideal.",[]],"blomberg-s06-249":["If we now take an error which can subsist with this average degree of the understanding, then the error is still tolerable, but if it cannot subsist even with it, then it is no longer to be endured, and then it is an absurdity.",[]],"blomberg-s06-250":["Absurdity, namely, presupposes not only a false cognition, but also that the common understanding (*le sens commun*) can see it to be false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-251":["One can make every hidden false cognition manifestly false, and then one brings the cognition *ad absurdum*, so that he who hitherto has had that cognition is not, to be sure, absolutely absurd, but rather that he would be manifestly absurd, and would act contrary to all reason, if after all the grounds which have been laid before him concerning the falsehood of his cognition he were still to want to take it to be true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-252":["Foolish it is, therefore, when scholars call themselves absurd, and yet afterwards want to dispute with one another whether what they have written is true or false. For by calling one another absurd, they indeed thereby deny each other all true cognition at once, and if I suppose this, that 282] someone possesses wholly and entirely no cognition, and is capable of possessing any, how then can he see my grounds concerning the falsehood of his cognition, or even boast of a true cognition, and believe himself to possess it? but how am I then capable of disputing?",[]],"blomberg-s06-253":["In the 114th *paragraphus* the author treats of the necessary, and of the contingent, and contingent truths, about which, however, we have nothing more to remark.",[]],"blomberg-s07-001":["All grounds of cognition are either inner or outer. The former are determinations in the thing itself, through which it can be cognized already without comparison with other things. Through the latter, however, I obtain only a cognition of a thing insofar as I compare it with other things. and these outer grounds of cognition are called marks or characteristics.",[]],"blomberg-s07-002":["The inner cognitions, however, cannot even properly be called mark or characteristic of a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-003":["In the comparison of one thing with another we can always represent to ourselves two things, either the sameness, the *identity*, or the difference, *diversity*. e.g., marks of sameness in human beings and animals are that they both have a corruptible body. Marks of difference, however, are that the former are endowed with a rational soul, but the latter have no reason at all.",[]],"blomberg-s07-004":["In most cases one uses the marks of *identity*, but in many also the marks of *diversity*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-005":["All *genera* and *species*, e.g., arise from the marks of *identity*, but not of difference.",[]],"blomberg-s07-006":["If, however, one wants, e.g., to distinguish the metals from one another, then one must on the contrary again have *notas diversitatis*, e.g., that gold is heavier than all metals. For this is the foremost distinguishing mark of gold from silver, copper, etc., and the remaining metals.",[]],"blomberg-s07-007":["A mark is thus not merely a *ratio disjunctionis*, as some have defined it, but also a *ratio identitatis*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-008":["A mark in general is called a *nota*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-009":["All marks in general, however, are either",[]],"blomberg-s07-010":["1 Inner",[]],"blomberg-s07-011":["2 outer",[]],"blomberg-s07-012":["*Sunt vel externae, vel internae omnes notae.* [All marks are either external or internal.]",[]],"blomberg-s07-013":["1: The marks which I cognize of a thing are inner if I consider the thing alone merely in itself.",[]],"blomberg-s07-014":["2: The marks which I have of a thing are outer if I compare the thing with other things. and such marks are called *characteres*, grounds of distinction, characteristics in the proper sense.",[]],"blomberg-s07-015":["The use of all marks will thus naturally be able to be, likewise",[]],"blomberg-s07-016":["1: Inner",[]],"blomberg-s07-017":["2 outer",[]],"blomberg-s07-018":["If I cognize the inner marks which taken together make up the whole concept, then these marks are complete, and sufficient to cognize all that in a thing which can be cognized in it *absolute*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-019":["The absolute pieces of knowledge of things, however, are far more difficult than the cognitions which one acquires through comparisons. For these latter are far easier, and more natural to us, because it lies as it were already in our nature that in comparison with others we far more easily become aware of the agreement, or the difference, in *objects*, and are capable of noticing it. Thus, e.g., ugliness *en comparaison* with a beauty, and a true beauty, strikes the eye right away when it finds itself among many ugly persons, and the human being himself, when he undertakes a judgment of himself, mostly asks not how good he is, and how much he still lacks, but rather whether he is not as good as, or even better than, others. Hence, however, it comes that Herr von Wolff, and even our author, take marks and characteristics to be the same.",[]],"blomberg-s07-020":["In the beginning it is mostly thus, that most things are held, if not wholly the same, then yet only very little different; with time the differences then indeed gradually disclose themselves, and as one attends ever more to the things, so too ever more and more of the marks are found. But to this gradual search for the marks of a thing there also belongs much fineness of understanding, just as for being able, among things where the greatest differences seem to be, or also actually are there, nevertheless to come upon a certain similarity, a sameness, and agreement.",[]],"blomberg-s07-021":["In this *§phus* the author speaks of mediate and immediate marks. If a thing can be cognized through a certain mark without the mediation of a mark that is different from this, then such is an immediate mark. A mediate mark, on the other hand, is a mark of a mark: e.g., corruptibility is a mark of the human being. but an immediate one, for corruptibility is a mark of a mortal being, i.e., of a body, but the human being is mortal, and has a body.",[]],"blomberg-s07-022":["There are thus degrees of remote marks, and thus there are degrees of mediate marks. The first degree is: if a thing can be cognized only through one *notam intermediam*. Among all marks, however, of which one is the mark of the other, a relation of *subordination* is always to be met with. There is, however, also a relation of *coordination*, and this takes place with immediate marks, where each particular mark is a new ground of cognition of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-023":["Through experience we can become aware of, and cognize, no other than *coordinated*, i.e., juxtaposed, marks of a thing. Reason, however, is alone capable of *suppeditating* to us *subordinated* marks of a thing, i.e., of presenting to us series of marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-024":["With the *subordination* of marks I have nothing more than a single immediate mark, and the remaining *notae* are marks of marks. With the *coordinatio notarum*, however, all marks are immediate.",[]],"blomberg-s07-025":["In the series of *subordinated*, i.e., one another subordinated, marks there is always a first, i.e., the concepts are limited.",[]],"blomberg-s07-026":["In *coordination*, however, there is no limitation.",[]],"blomberg-s07-027":["There are innumerable marks which belong to a thing immediately, and our understanding is not capable of seeing all properties, and of determining them exactly.",[]],"blomberg-s07-028":["With *subordination* the immediate mark is called *nota infima*, and the last mark of all marks is called the *nota summa*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-029":["Through the *subordination* of marks I obtain a deep, i.e., *intensive* magnitude. through the *coordination* of marks *notarum*, however, one attains an *extensive* magnitude, i.e., cognition worked out according to the ground and extensive.",[]],"blomberg-s07-030":["The series of marks *coordinated* with one another is as it were a line without boundaries, which is infinite; for it is indeed still always possible, indeed to be conjectured, that posterity will with time still always be able to find out more immediate marks of a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-031":["The series of marks *subordinated* to one another, however, is bounded, and has its limitations, and indeed often very soon, when in it no mark of a mark can any longer be given.",[]],"blomberg-s07-032":["*Metaphysica* is that science by means of which we search for the *terminum a priori* of the *subordinated* marks. Through it we obtain simple and indissoluble concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s07-033":["Too much beauty also harms, and actually arouses suspicion. Beautiful simplicity is often found through nature. The greatest art, however, consists in so hiding the art actually applied somewhere that it is not at all noticeable, but rather seems to be pure nature.",[]],"blomberg-s07-034":["Profundity is in a certain way opposed to beauty, cognition becomes dry the more is abstracted in it. Merely abstract cognitions in general cannot be fully seen into unless I present a case *in concreto*, and see into it. The dry, however, also often falls into the droll, and for this art is often required: thus e.g., with sallies in company. and one is accustomed to call this simplicity.",[]],"blomberg-s07-035":["With all description one seeks more to understand the thing than to see into it. With all explanation, however, one seeks on the contrary again more to see into the thing than to understand it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-036":["The multitude of marks *coordinated* with one another increases experience.",[]],"blomberg-s07-037":["The *extensive* magnitude of a cognition is as it were the *volumen*, the *intensive* magnitude, however, is its density. In certain cases the *extensive* magnitude of cognition is more useful, in others, however, the *intensive*. In morals, e.g., the *intensive* magnitude of a cognition is more needful, in *physica*, however, and medicine, the *extensive* magnitude of cognition is very often more indispensable.",[]],"blomberg-s07-038":["Dry cognitions are not at all suited to sensibility, and thus also not to beauty. Thorough, deep, solid cognitions, however, can and must without reproach be dry. This dryness, however, one then makes up for when one considers the abstract *in concreto* in a certain case. (*Analysis* goes *ascendendo*. *synthesis*, however, *descendendo*.)",[]],"blomberg-s07-039":["Through negating marks of a thing I think to myself something which is not actually to be met with in the thing. Through affirming marks, however, I think to myself that which is in fact present in the thing. Negating marks, however, are possible only through one's first representing to oneself the marks opposed to them, which affirm. thus, e.g., I cannot know what darkness is unless I first have a clear *ideam* of light.",[]],"blomberg-s07-040":["Negating marks, however, serve only to avoid errors. E.g., animals have no rational soul. affirming marks, however, serve to obtain an extensive unworked-out cognition of a thing. e.g., God is omniscient etc. Gold loses nothing of its *massa* and weight in fire, is ductile.",[]],"blomberg-s07-041":["Besides ignorance there is another still more dangerous ill in the understanding, and this is error. Through affirming marks we avoid ignorance, through negating marks, however, error. This is the end of both kinds of marks. Through negating marks one of course gets out no new cognition at all. nonetheless they are yet certainly very needful, and important, for one thereby guards oneself against erroneous cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s07-042":["The fewer means we have at hand to attain true cognitions, the more negating marks we will need in order not to err in the cognition of the thing. Thus, e.g., in no science is there a greater multitude of negating marks than in rational divinity, in natural *theologia*. For in it we have no means at all at hand to attain extended cognitions. Thus, e.g., we are every moment inclined to err in the concept, *idea*, and representation of the divine being and in general of God himself, because we are very fond of comparing him sensibly with the things which we become aware of on earth, but these can be no standard at all of God. God indeed cannot be cognized by us through the senses, and consequently our whole knowledge of him will only be able to be very much limited.",[]],"blomberg-s07-043":["If we therefore consider God in *theologia naturali*, and take away all negating marks through which we speak of him, then nothing more will remain to us than the *idea* of the all-sufficient and all-perfect being.",[]],"blomberg-s07-044":["Negating judgments are actually often foolish, because what is negated is often already in itself impossible. e.g.: the human being has reason, therefore he is no stone. It is true, that is a settled truth which no one will doubt. but because it is yet also manifest that no one will take it into his head in earnest to take a human being for a stone, this judgment is futile.",[]],"blomberg-s07-045":["Every mark is a ground of cognition, which is estimated by its sequences. Accordingly as the sequences are of worth, with respect to reason or to our perfection and happiness, so too will the marks as grounds of cognition be of worth.",[]],"blomberg-s07-046":["From the first sequences the logical, from the other sequences, however, the *practical* worth of the marks will be derived.",[]],"blomberg-s07-047":["If from a ground many sequences are to be derived, then it is fruitful, or it has an *extensive* magnitude, e.g., from the sun many sequences flow with respect to the earth. Thus the rules of morals are very fruitful, in that they have an influence on our life, i.e., on our conduct, which is very great.",[]],"blomberg-s07-048":["A mark is *extensive* great if I can cognize many sequences of a thing. *intensive*, however, a mark is great when I can cognize not so much many as great and important sequences from a thing. the former mark is called fruitful, the latter, however, correct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-049":["Empty speculations have a logical worth. They are often of many sequences with respect to reason, although of course they have no influence on the happiness of human beings, e.g., that the moon is inhabited etc. This proposition has no influence on the will, on the state etc. No one becomes, with respect to his outer understanding, more perfect, happier, or better through this cognition, although he does become somewhat more perfect with respect to his reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-050":["Other cognitions are *aesthetically* correct, because they stimulate our taste, and gratify, e.g., poetry delights because it is in accord with our taste. All importance of cognition, however, exists only when it is considered alone by itself without comparison with other cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s07-051":["The marks either suffice for our ends, and then they are *comparative sufficientes*. or to distinguish a thing from all others, thus for all logical ends, and then they are *notae absolute sufficientes*",[]],"blomberg-s07-052":["All experiences have only a relative *sufficientiam*. e.g., if I consider a pear tree closely, then I can easily distinguish its leaves from the leaves of all other trees in the forest. This cognition is thus sufficient in relation to other cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s07-053":["The *sufficientia relativa*, or *comparativa*, suffices to distinguish a thing from such things as we merely know hitherto, but not from all things that are possible, that can only ever *exist*. E.g., gold distinguishes itself from all remaining metals through its weight, but one has also already found out a metal, called platina, which is of white color, but has the same weight as gold. Consequently this mark and ground of distinction of gold from all remaining metals is not sufficient enough",[]],"blomberg-s07-054":["Things which fall into our senses can be cognized and distinguished only through marks which are relatively sufficient, but not by means of such marks as suffice *absolute*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-055":["The *sufficientia notarum*, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s07-056":["1: *Externa*",[]],"blomberg-s07-057":["2. *interna*",[]],"blomberg-s07-058":["The former consists in this, when the marks of a thing are sufficient to distinguish it from other things through comparison. The latter, however, consists in this, that a mark of a thing suffices to derive from it all remaining determinations of the same. From this *sufficientia* arises the division of grounds of cognition into",[]],"blomberg-s07-059":["A: Inner and B: Outer",[]],"blomberg-s07-060":["With the latter one can derive nothing from them, e.g., gold has a yellow color, the human being is an animal that laughs. The former, however, are fruitful, so that much flows from them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-061":["Outer grounds of cognition can often be in themselves very important, but with respect to inner *sufficiency* unfruitful and useless.",[]],"blomberg-s07-062":["Necessary marks are such *ad essentiam pertinentia*, without which the thing can wholly and entirely not be thought. *Pertinent autem ad essentiam 1: vel solum ut causae aut 2: ut causata* [But they pertain to the essence 1: either only as causes or 2: as caused]",[]],"blomberg-s07-063":["*Omnia autem ad essentiam pertinentia A: essentialia* [But all things pertaining to the essence A: essentials] e.g., the",[]],"blomberg-s07-064":["extension B. *attributa*. E.g., the divisibility of bodies. contingent marks, however, are *extra essentialia*, without which the thing can nevertheless be thought, e.g., learnedness in a human being.",[]],"blomberg-s07-065":["If the *essentialia* are marks, then they are not *sub-* but rather *coordinated* to one another, the *attributa*, however, are not *coordinated* to the *essentialibus*, but rather *subordinated*, for they follow from one another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-066":["The *extra essentialia* marks are either",[]],"blomberg-s07-067":["A. Inner or B. outer.",[]],"blomberg-s07-068":["The latter are also called relations. The inner, however, are again",[]],"blomberg-s07-069":["1: Necessary",[]],"blomberg-s07-070":["2: contingent",[]],"blomberg-s07-071":["The former are again",[]],"blomberg-s07-072":["1. Complete, or marks which are sufficient to derive from them everything that is contained in the thing, or",[]],"blomberg-s07-073":["2: incomplete. When the marks do not suffice to derive from them, and to cognize, everything that belongs to the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-074":["The inner contingent relations are called *modi*. The *modification* of the outer relations does not *modify* the human being himself, although among the things of nature a change of *relation* mostly seems also to be combined at the same time with a change of *modification*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-075":["Summer and winter are properly no changes in the sun itself, but rather only changed *relationes* and relations of the sun to our earth.",[]],"blomberg-s07-076":["*Modi* are nothing other than inner determinations and changes which can be thought in a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-077":["*Relationes*, however, are: with a thing in relation to others. and changes occurring in reference to other things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-078":["We now raise the question, however, whether with the *completudine notarum, necessaria interna*, a *praecision* cannot also take place, so that superfluous marks are met with therein which are no longer needful to distinguish the thing from all others, and to derive from them all proper determinations, so that all that is left out which, even if it is lacking, is yet so constituted that even without it the concept will nevertheless be complete?",[]],"blomberg-s07-079":["*Responsio* Yes. It is often possible. The *nota,e rei necessariae internae sufficientes* make up the *praecision*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-080":["If the marks contain no less than is needful for the distinguishing of the thing, then this is a *praecision*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-081":["If finally the marks of a thing are wholly adequate to it, then they are *notae adaequatae*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-082":["The *adequate* concept of a thing, insofar as it can no longer be derived from any other, is the concept of the essence *essentia*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-083":["The essence of a thing is the first sufficient fundamental concept, which suffices to derive everything that belongs to the former.",[]],"blomberg-s07-084":["The *notae necessariae sunt*",[]],"blomberg-s07-085":["A: *Primitivae* or B: *Derivativae*",[]],"blomberg-s07-086":["The former make up the *essentialia*, the latter, however, the *attributa*. e.g., with the triangle the 3 angles are just as needful and indispensable as the 3 sides. the former, however, one derives in turn from the latter, and they are therefore *attributa*, but the sides themselves are fundamental concepts *essentialia*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-087":["In order to cognize the essential of a thing, the following is needful: In order to cognize the *attributa* I must cognize the inner necessary marks, which, however, are accompanied by others. In order, however, to cognize the *essentialia*, I must see that the marks can no longer be derived from any other, in order to cognize the *essentiam* itself, I must know that the marks, and *essentialia*, are complete, and taken together are sufficient for the cognition of the whole.",[]],"blomberg-s07-088":["The *coordination* of marks which is complete makes up the *completudinem*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-089":["The fundamental concept of all that which is necessary to a thing is its essence. To investigate the essence of things, this is the business and the end of philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s07-090":["The marks which taken together make up the essence of a thing are the *essentialia*. The sequences, the *rationata sive consectaria*, however, from the essence, those are the *attributa*. *Modi sunt notae internae, quarum possibilitas tantum per essentiam determinatur.* [*Modi* are internal marks whose possibility only is determined through the essence.]",[]],"blomberg-s07-091":["The *modi* are thus not *rationata essentiae* with regard to their truth, but rather only with regard to their possibility. *Modi* are thus nothing other than contingent marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-092":["*Modi* as well as *relationes* do not belong at all to the essence, *ad essentiam non pertinent*, neither as *constitutiva* nor as *attributa*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-093":["All marks of a thing either belong to the essence of a thing, *essentialia*, or not *essentialia*: *ad essentiam pertinentia sunt vel essentialia, vel attributa. Extraessentialia autem sunt vel notae, sive notae internae, vel relationes, sive notae externae, quarum possibilitas tantum per essentiam determinatur.* [things pertaining to the essence are either essentials or attributes. But extra-essentials are either marks, or internal marks, or relations, or external marks, whose possibility only is determined through the essence.]",[]],"blomberg-s07-094":["The essence is divided",[]],"blomberg-s07-095":["1: *mo* into the logical",[]],"blomberg-s07-096":["2 *do* into the real essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-097":["The complete fundamental concept of a thing is in general its essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-098":["The first ground of all that, however, which is thought by me in the concept of the thing is the logical essence. The first fundamental concept, however, of all that which belongs to the thing very actually and in fact is the real essence",[]],"blomberg-s07-099":["From this it naturally becomes evident that the logical and the real essence must necessarily be very different from one another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-100":["If I pronounce words, and combine with them a certain concept, then that which I think to myself with this word and expression is the logical essence. E.g., if I pronounce the word matter, then all that which is inseparable from the concept which I combine with the expression matter is the logical essence of matter. The logical essence one can always represent to oneself, and think, by itself alone. Thus, e.g., with matter I always think to myself an extension, an impenetrability, a certain constant inertia and lifelessness, so that it is not capable of changing its place, or of moving by itself, but rather only through the addition of another foreign force.",[]],"blomberg-s07-101":["All these are the *essentialia* of the word matter, and consequently taken together make up its logical essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-102":["The *subjective* fundamental concept is that which is contained in an inner concept of the thing. This, however, does not yet prove that in the *object* still more marks can be there. but rather I combine with the thing only that which I represent to myself. Hence, e.g., with the word matter still more other properties can take place.",[]],"blomberg-s07-103":["The logical essence is the *subjective* fundamental concept. This concept, however, is valid merely for me, and not for others. Another can represent to himself more or fewer marks with the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-104":["The essence of our concept is not always, indeed seldom, the essence of the thing itself.",[]],"blomberg-s07-105":["The logical essence is far smaller than the real essence. The logical essence is a *concentrated* fundamental concept of a thing. it comprehends in itself only that which we represent to ourselves in the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-106":["This contracting, *combining* of concepts is actually very suited to the human understanding, and quite needful. it must deal sparingly with its powers; such a *compendium* is nonetheless just as good as an extended concept of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-107":["The real essence comprehends in itself in general all that which can ever actually belong to a thing. This *eduction* is the only means of making our concepts really distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-108":["Distinctness, however, is one of the most essential logical perfections. Through this my cognition becomes far more fit for use, through the real essence, however, it becomes more extended.",[]],"blomberg-s07-109":["The logical essence, however, must not be mixed up with the real essence. For in my concept which I have of the thing I do not yet think to myself everything that lies in the thing, and belongs to it, or can lie in it, and belong to it. E.g., if we take the concept of matter, then newer *philosophi* have discovered that in the essence of matter there further lies the force of attraction; this property I would certainly never have thought to myself straightaway with the word matter, and there can perhaps belong to matter still far more marks which are not yet discovered, and which only a philosopher will cognize.",[]],"blomberg-s07-110":["From this it becomes evident that in the real essence infinitely more is contained than in the logical essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-111":["Of no single object of experience or also of the senses can we properly cognize the real or *objective* essence. at most it is possible for us really to see into its logical or *subjective* essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-112":["That which we think to ourselves with the word of a thing is very small, and slight, indeed often very little, but all that which actually *revera* belongs to a thing is often immeasurable, and cannot be determined at all.",[]],"blomberg-s07-113":["To search for the real essence of empirical concepts and objects is thus a wholly futile effort, but one can indeed represent the logical essence of concepts of reason, and clear it up for oneself properly.",[]],"blomberg-s07-114":["Our empirical concepts are very mutable, because our experience is daily extended more and more. The logical essence is therefore also very changeable, and mutable, because with the concept of a thing one with time ever more determinations",[]],"blomberg-s07-115":["thinks to oneself. all moral concepts, however, are pure concepts of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s07-116":["There are, namely, such pure concepts of the understanding as have their origin not from experience, but rather merely from pure reason. He who wants to find the real essence must know all marks which belong constantly to the thing. Then he must further search for their ground, and be concerned to investigate it, and this is then the real essence. e.g., a body which grows through inner unfolding belongs to the logical essence of a plant. The fundamental concept, however, of its constant determination is the real essence, whereto also that can belong, that plants generate their like.",[]],"blomberg-s07-117":["The concept that water is a fluid *elementum*, without smell and taste, 14 times lighter than quicksilver, etc., is the logical essence of water, for if I am in some measure equal to physical knowledge, then I think to myself, as soon as I name the word water, all this. From this I can indeed by far not yet derive all remaining properties which, determined for water, belong to it, or can belong to it, and perhaps are not yet all discovered, although we do not at all times think them to ourselves therewith, consequently it is not yet the real essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-118":["Of individual things we can never give the real essence, but indeed of the *generibus*, e.g., of body in general.",[]],"blomberg-s07-119":["Nevertheless we do not know all concepts and determinations of all things which can constantly belong to them, and then we are indeed also not at all times capable of straightaway giving the true ground of all these determinations, we can consequently only very seldom indicate their real essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-120":["Herein clear and obscure cognition is spoken of. The author first treated of confused and distinct concepts in the first section in the *logica*, without first thinking of clear and obscure cognition. how is it then that he now begins to speak of it?",[]],"blomberg-s07-121":["*Responsio* the concept of clear and obscure cognition is at the beginning of logic something such that it can be assumed without proof, and thus must be presupposed, and laid at the ground. Here, however, one can already derive it again from what has been treated beforehand.",[]],"blomberg-s07-122":["It is thus here exactly reversed. A representation, however, is obscure (to return to our matter) of which one is not immediately conscious. Mediately, however, one can indeed become conscious of this cognition, namely by means of reason, and thus a way still remains of nevertheless making an otherwise obscure cognition [329 (339)] clear, distinct for oneself and thus making its insight easier. e.g., with the naked eye I glimpse, when I glimpse the Milky Way, nothing else in it than a white streak, but if I take the *tubus* to help, then I soon become aware of its individual parts as individual stars, and then judge straightaway by means of reason that these must be the very same stars which with naked eyes I have seen only as a mere white streak. Of this representation I am thus mediately conscious, but not immediately: thus it is only at first obscure, but afterwards *mediate* distinct, or clear.",[]],"blomberg-s07-123":["A cognition is either in and for itself obscure, so that notwithstanding all effort applied it can nevertheless not become clear at all. or it is relatively obscure, so that in certain circumstances it can become clear, but under others not clear.",[]],"blomberg-s07-124":["The first, in itself obscure cognition, however, is according to its description wholly impossible, for everything can become clear, however difficult it may seem to us. and what seems to us to be most obscure is, when we investigate it, always only relatively obscure.",[]],"blomberg-s07-125":["Logic prescribes to us the rules which we are to apply in order to know how we are to proceed with cognitions of which we are conscious that we actually possess them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-126":["All clarity is the right way to attain distinctness. Clarity itself, however, is not yet a logical perfection, which distinctness nonetheless is. Logic thus already presupposes clarity, it *supposes* it, and does not itself produce it, it deals with cognitions and representations already made clear.",[]],"blomberg-s07-127":["Distinctness is thus also not the proper object of its occupation. Distinctness, *disjunctio*, is to be well distinguished",[]],"blomberg-s07-128":["from evidentness *dispicuity*. Insofar as clarity of marks takes place in a cognition, insofar is the cognition distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-129":["A cognition is brought *in perspicuum*, however, only when it is so bright and clear that even the slightest understanding sees into it, whereas a cognition is *imperspicua*, or *non perspicua*, when it is too great for the whole horizon of the whole human race, when it can be called incomprehensible. *Perspicuity*, on the other hand, signifies nothing other than comprehensibility.",[]],"blomberg-s07-130":["often a cognition ceases to be comprehensible in the degree in which it should become really distinct. This, however, cannot be avoided at all, except by not speaking of the thing at all.",[]],"blomberg-s07-131":["If we now go back to the distinction of clear and obscure cognition, then we have further to remark: all obscure cognitions, however, can be",[]],"blomberg-s07-132":["a) *Objective*",[]],"blomberg-s07-133":["B) *Subjective* obscure; if we are to say it properly, then the former does not occur, but only the latter.",[]],"blomberg-s07-134":["But if the relation of a thing to the limitations of human cognition is so constituted that it cannot be cognized clearly enough, but always remains obscure, then one for the most part ascribes all the blame not to him who does not cognize the thing clearly enough, but to the thing itself or to him who presents it. Although it often lies rather in the former than in the latter. One then calls this cognition, which the former does not have insight into, and cannot have insight into, *objective* obscure. *Subjective*, however, a cognition is obscure when the blame for the thing's remaining obscure despite all effort applied lies not in the thing, and the cognition itself, but in him who wants to cognize something, namely because he either possesses a great ignorance, and hence is naturally not in a position to cognize the thing, or also because he perhaps does not apply to it sufficiently the diligence required for it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-135":["This latter obscurity, however, is properly not grounded at all in the nature of the cognition itself, or even, as some take it, of all human beings, and of their understanding. For if this were to occur, then the cognition can surely be clearly cognized for not a single human being, but it lies merely either in the incapacity, and ignorance, or in the negligence of this or that individual subject.",[]],"blomberg-s07-136":["Hence it comes about, then, that the same cognition which is clear to one subject can be obscure to another, and on the contrary precisely the cognition that is obscure to one subject can be clear to another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-137":["Few human beings even have a right concept of what it means to have a right clear concept of a thing, or cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s07-138":["No human being can, so to speak, understand himself rightly any more, he always judges that all that he says is completely clear to him, and precisely hence it comes about, then, that he is also kept from investigating rightly whether he then understands himself rightly, or not.",[]],"blomberg-s07-139":["*Metaphysica*, and morals, these 2 pure sciences of philosophy, are of the kind, and so constituted, that the human being is never in a position to represent distinctly to himself the least concept in them, unless he can first acquire beforehand a *complete*, and exhaustive concept of the whole, i.e., of the whole science.",[]],"blomberg-s07-140":["Either someone who wants to study *Metaphysicam* has a quite pure, a quite distinct concept of it, or he knows nothing whatsoever of it. There are only these two ways. there is no middle in this at all.",[]],"blomberg-s07-141":["On the other hand someone can be very graspable, especially if he still knows nothing, or at least no errors, and he who hears him goes away again just as wise as he came to him, this is very easy, and such a presentation, which does no harm, but also is of no use, can also be very graspable and comprehensible.",[]],"blomberg-s07-142":["It is therefore not possible in some cases to combine an easy graspability with a *complete* distinctness, and those certainly judge wrongly who take the easiest presentation for the best, and estimate a somewhat more difficult, less childish presentation, arranged for reflecting, as obscure, unintelligible, indeed exaggerated.",[]],"blomberg-s07-143":["To make the obscure clear is so difficult that no human being has yet invented a universally valid means for it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-144":["Exhaustiveness is the *sufficiency* of a cognition for a certain aim. The sequel, if it serves to pursue the aim, and to judge it, is called the end.",[]],"blomberg-s07-145":["With all *completudine* we therefore compare the cognition with a certain aim. This *completudo*, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s07-146":["a) *Externa*, which appears in the comparison of the cognition with others.",[]],"blomberg-s07-147":["B) *interna*, which occurs in itself, and lies in the cognition itself.",[]],"blomberg-s07-148":["The *completudo comparativa*, however, can often be so very useful to us that we do not need the *completudinis absolutae*, or *internae*, at all over and above it, and can do without it. Exhaustiveness, however, rests solely and alone on the multitude of marks *coordinated* with one another, or posited next to one another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-149":["*Philosophia* has the aim of instructing us how we should learn to understand ourselves ever better.",[]],"blomberg-s07-150":["All new cognitions that deviate greatly from the usual way of judging are at first entirely obscure, and are not understood by the best head. and indeed this often happens not because the cognitions are obscure in themselves, but because it is commonly very difficult to deviate from the usual way of judging, and to observe this or that thing from another side. whoever, e.g., has learned to dance with a bad master, and afterwards is to begin to learn with a skilled one, finds it really very hard to break himself again of the old steps and placements of the feet once grown habitual, and to follow the newer better method. Just so it goes also with cognition, everything is hard to understand, and obscure, that departs from the old once-established way, of cognition, of presentation, of style of writing etc.",[]],"blomberg-s07-151":["All inventors of new methods in all sciences are therefore at the beginning obscure, indeed for precisely this reason, because one loves the old all too much, and does not want to depart from it, they are perhaps even often despised; so long, until one has also accommodated oneself to this new method with time; whereupon one then experiences that it is often far better than the old.",[]],"blomberg-s07-152":["Locke, and Newton experienced this fate, that their writings were at the beginning exceedingly obscure, and unintelligible, or at least seemed to be so, until one afterwards considered them more closely, and discovered their advantages.",[]],"blomberg-s07-153":["The doctrine of obscure cognitions is not at all logical, but only metaphysical. *Logica* is not a science about the nature of the subject, of the human soul, in order to cognize what properly lies hidden therein. but it already presupposes clear concepts, and treats of the use of our understanding, and of our reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-154":["In our soul all cognitions already actually lie hidden, and properly nothing more is necessary than only to develop these cognitions, and to bring them into a brighter light.",[]],"blomberg-s07-155":["Rational philosophy teaches us nothing new, but it only seeks to make distinct to us what we already know and to impart to us a consciousness of it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-156":["Empirical philosophy, however, on the other hand occupies itself with imparting to us new cognitions that we had not known until then.",[]],"blomberg-s07-157":["In this *Paragrapho* the author speaks of exhaustive clarity.",[]],"blomberg-s07-158":["The *completudo*, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s07-159":["1) *Externa*",[]],"blomberg-s07-160":["2) *interna*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-161":["The former consists in this, that the marks which are attributed to a thing suffice to distinguish it from all other things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-162":["The latter, however, in this, that the marks of a thing are sufficient to derive from them all possible remaining determinations of it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-163":["The *Completudo externa* always presupposes the *Completudinem internam*. where the latter is not, there the former is certainly not either, and conversely the former, namely the *Completudo externa*, can be attained by means of the *completudinis internae*. Immediately I cannot know that a thing, and its marks, are sufficient enough to distinguish the thing from all remaining things, but this must happen through the *completudinem internam*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-164":["The inner *Completudo* is therefore not only a means to outer exhaustiveness, but the latter also cannot be attained, and *acquired*, without the former.",[]],"blomberg-s07-165":["No empirical concept can therefore become inwardly *complete*. Inner exhaustiveness therefore does not occur with objects of experience. From this it follows at the same time that outer exhaustiveness is also not to be met with in the *Objectis* of experience. For the inner is always the ground of outer exhaustiveness. E.g., with metals, herbs, plants etc. I cannot cognize the thing according to all determinations, consequently also cannot yet always distinguish it from all other things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-166":["One finds in many books that treat of physics *Definitiones* of objects of experience. It is, however, a certain proposition that these never are, nor can be, right, and sufficient. For every *Definitio* must be *complete*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-167":["No one can therefore obtain a *complete* concept of objects of experience that should serve to distinguish it from all remaining merely possible, and actual things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-168":["Hence Locke took pains to show that no one, however learned he may be, could be in a position to give a correct definition of the human being. From this, however, it is evident that all our concepts of objects of experience can never be *absolute complete*, but a *Completudo comparativa* can indeed occur with them, namely, where the marks of a thing suffice to distinguish the thing from all that which we have cognized in experience up to now.",[]],"blomberg-s07-169":["Pure concepts of reason, however, can be inwardly as well as outwardly *complete*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-170":["With empirical concepts things outside us, the judgments, are the *Exemplaria*, and our concepts are the *Exemplata*. With pure concepts, however, the concepts themselves are the *Exemplaria*, and those of which we have the concepts the *Exemplata*, as e.g. the concept of virtue, of right, and wrong, of kindness, of the rightfulness, and wrongfulness of actions, of the simple and of the composite, and of the contingent, and necessary.",[]],"blomberg-s07-171":["These concepts do not arise at all from the objects, hence I also cannot represent to myself the determination of them only in part, but they are arbitrary.",[]],"blomberg-s07-172":["Reason is the creatress of these concepts and consequently the thing also has no other determination than reason has attributed to it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-173":["*Mathematica* is of this kind, it has nothing but pure concepts of reason, which hence can also be completely inwardly, and outwardly *complete*. E.g., the *Mathematicer* think of a *Conum*, cone, or a right-angled triangle, which turns around its *Cathetum*, or one of its sides. Here he thinks of everything that suffices to distinguish the thing from all others, for the cone is not a thing outside him, which he has cognized in part according to certain determinations, but a thing in his pure reason, which he arbitrarily thinks himself and in conformity with it attributes certain determinations, whereby he wills that the thing should be able to be distinguished from all other things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-174":["Exhaustive, therefore, are only pure concepts of reason, but not *Objecta* of experience, which is distinctly evident from what has been adduced so far.",[]],"blomberg-s07-175":["No one must therefore presume to define empirical concepts, but one can indeed have correct *Definitiones* of pure concepts of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-176":["Herein the author speaks of cognition that is wholly or in part clear, wholly, or in part distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-177":["Representations are wholly clear when the thing that I think is composed of no more determinations than those I represent to myself therewith. Representations, however, are in part clear when I think not each and every determination of the thing, but only some determinations of it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-178":["Some of those ancient philosophers have hence distinguished concepts into *Rationes Archetypas*, and ἔκτυπα *Ectypos*. the former are to be regarded as archetypes, and *Originaria*, the latter as copies, and *Exemplata*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-179":["To the former belong the pure concepts of reason, to the latter on the other hand the concepts of experience. The former can be wholly clear, the latter on the contrary are never wholly, but always only in part clear. If, e.g., I see a house before me, and cannot go around it, then the house is only in part clear to me, namely with regard to the side that I have seen; the rest is wholly obscure.",[]],"blomberg-s07-180":["With concepts of experience I always cognize only those determinations of the thing which stir our senses, and indeed so that we are conscious of them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-181":["Liveliness, and strength of cognition are not *opposita*, but both of them are combined with one another. Liveliness is an aesthetic perfection. Distinctness, however, *Rationabilitas*, or rather deep distinctness, is a logical perfection. The deeper the distinctness of a cognition is, the more the liveliness in it dwindles. but the livelier in turn a cognition is, the shallower it is.",[]],"blomberg-s07-182":["Liveliness in a cognition, however, is attained by help of a great combination. Distinctness in a cognition, however, one attains when one seeks to produce it by means of a great separation.",[]],"blomberg-s07-183":["Through *coordinated* marks we make a cognition lively, through *subordinated* marks, however, deeply distinct. Extensive distinctness is combined with the liveliness of a cognition, the deep distinctness of a cognition, however, is on the other hand combined with its dryness. E.g., in describing spring I represent it lively through a multitude of marks coordinated with one another. So does the poet. he shows, e.g., the sprouting flowers, the young green of the woods, the leaping herds, the renewed rays of the sun, the beautiful pleasant air, the reviving of the whole of nature.",[]],"blomberg-s07-184":["In all eloquence, and poetry, one seeks to adduce the marks coordinated with one another, of which one becomes immediately aware in the thing to be described, in order to make the concept of the thing lively.",[]],"blomberg-s07-185":["Hereby one attains aesthetic perfection in a cognition. The philosopher, however, who represents a thing to himself through a deep distinctness, singles out only one mark of the thing, and leaves out the rest, but seeks out marks, and the grounds of the marks, which he thinks of the thing. His reason, therefore, is chiefly occupied herein.",[]],"blomberg-s07-186":["The form of cognition is therefore wholly different when one represents the thing to oneself through subordinated marks than when one represents it to oneself through coordinated marks. Aesthetics occupies itself only with painting the thing, and making it distinct through *coordinated* marks. Reason, however, ascends from one mark to the other, and therefore leaves out many *coordinated* marks, but the cognition thereby becomes dry.",[]],"blomberg-s07-187":["The poet, and the orator cognize much in few things. The philosopher, however, on the other hand considers many objects, and cognizes little in many objects. his cognitions are therefore universal, they do not pertain merely to single *Objecta*, but to whole genera of them. He does represent to himself only some marks, but through a deep distinctness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-188":["Distinctness of reason is a distinctness *a priori*, which I cognize through marks *a priori*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-189":["Through extensive distinctness we cognize *Objecta* without needing the use of reason, without rationalizing. For deep distinctness, however, reason is necessarily required.",[]],"blomberg-s07-190":["For lively cognition sensibility is required. Sensibility is the perfection of cognition where we represent a thing to ourselves just as objects of the senses.",[]],"blomberg-s07-191":["*Rationabilitas* belongs to things insofar as we think them through universal concepts. Sensibility, however, on the other hand insofar as we represent a thing to ourselves through singular concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s07-192":["All sensibility brings about liveliness. Conformity to reason, however, produces dryness. Our concepts of reason obtain a sensibility when we represent universal judgments to ourselves *in Concreto*. This happens, for ex., through examples, and *Similia*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-193":["The sensibility of a cognition is not at all an imperfection of it. we think therewith the universal, which we have already had insight into in abstract cognitions, *in concreto* in those cases in which it actually occurs.",[]],"blomberg-s07-194":["The extensive clarity of cognition combined with sensibility produces liveliness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-195":["Our understanding here always wavers, as it were, between 2 cliffs. if we seek to cognize something aesthetically perfectly, then we soon lack the correct grounds, the solid in cognition, and we fall into the shallow. but if in turn we cognize something through a logical perfection, then we very easily lack aesthetic liveliness etc. and we lapse into the dry. indeed it is really quite very difficult to hit the right mean between these two dangerous byways.",[]],"blomberg-s07-196":["Nevertheless it is also possible to unite both perfections with one another in a high degree.",[]],"blomberg-s07-197":["The clarity of cognition is, just as the completeness, *completudo*, of it",[]],"blomberg-s07-198":["A:) *Extensiva*",[]],"blomberg-s07-199":["B ) *intensiva*",[]],"blomberg-s07-200":["*Claritas extensiva* rests on a multitude of *coordinated* marks, which are immediately cognized in a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-201":["It is the right way to liveliness, in that it brings much sensibility with it, for this consists precisely in *coordinatione* of the marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-202":["The consummation of the perfections of all our cognitions is: finally to give them sensibility, so that one represents the universal in particular circumstances and cases, and thinks the *Abstractum in Concreto* in a single individual sensible case. E.g., when I think friendship, true love, and the readiness to serve one another flowing from it, in Damon, and Phintias. Here I therefore think the universal in single cases. Hereby, however, my cognition becomes lively, e.g., when I think *Patriotismum* in Cato.",[]],"blomberg-s07-203":["Without sensibility our pure cognition of reason is only very poor indeed.",[]],"blomberg-s07-204":["For extensive clarity, and liveliness, only understanding is required, because with *coordinated* marks I do nothing but judge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-205":["*Claritas intensiva*, however, rests on *subordinated* marks and for this reason is necessarily required, because we therein infer, and derive one mark from the other.",[]],"blomberg-s07-206":["*Claritas extensiva* serves for the description of an object. *Claritas intensiva*, however, investigates the grounds of the thing. it seeks from a mark belonging to the thing that another mark must also belong to the thing. it therefore seeks to find out the cause of things, and always asks after the why? why this, why also that, indeed why it is not otherwise?",[]],"blomberg-s07-207":["The senses give us a lively, but not completely distinct cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s07-208":["He who can relate completely and in order all that he has ever seen possesses much liveliness of cognition indeed he can also often bring about a lively cognition in others through this relating.",[]],"blomberg-s07-209":["Sensible clarity is nothing other than liveliness, in intuition the clarity, however, is sensible clarity. Clarity through universal concepts is a logical clarity.",[]],"blomberg-s07-210":["Mathematics is the only science that is in a position to judge according to its concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s07-211":["He who is in a position to combine in his cognition both liveliness and also deep distinctness has in fact already attained the greatest degree of perfection of human cognition, this, however, is so difficult that it seems to be almost impossible for anyone.",[]],"blomberg-s07-212":["Through sensibility the perfection of reason is not lost in the least, but it rather only obtains a greater liveliness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-213":["From this, therefore, we have quite distinct insight that sensibility does not conflict at all with the perfection of reason but rather promotes it in certain cases, indeed often brings about for it a greater correctness, in that *in abstracto* we often leave out, and must leave out, such marks of things as actually belong to the nature of the thing, which, however, can be *restored* when one weighs the thing *in concreto*. Orators and poets can hence often be very helpful to the philosopher.",[]],"blomberg-s07-214":["It is a most universal rule of a good, and especially learned presentation: combine with a presentation, and give your instruction sensibility.",[]],"blomberg-s07-215":["Logical clarity, however, rests on the *subordination*, sensible clarity rests in turn on the *coordination* of the marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-216":["Just so we can also have A: a sensible distinctness according to aesthetic laws.",[]],"blomberg-s07-217":["and B: a logical distinctness according to logical laws",[]],"blomberg-s07-218":["We must often abstract, but this also becomes really very difficult for us.",[]],"blomberg-s07-219":["1: *mo* Sensible distinctness according to laws of aesthetics is nothing other than the distinctness of intuition.",[]],"blomberg-s07-220":["2 *do* Logical distinctness, on the other hand, according to laws of the understanding, and of reason, or of logic, however, is nothing other than the distinctness of *reflection*",[]],"blomberg-s07-221":["Something can be very lively, but all the less distinct. again something can be skillful, colorful, and lively, but therewith nonetheless little distinct, and confused.",[]],"blomberg-s07-222":["That our cognition, as the author asserts, becomes distinct solely through dissection is false.",[]],"blomberg-s07-223":["Our cognition can be made distinct in two ways",[]],"blomberg-s07-224":["a) *per Synthesin*",[]],"blomberg-s07-225":["B) *per Analysin*",[]],"blomberg-s07-226":["We must, however, herein distinguish well the science of making a distinct cognition from the science of making distinct a cognition that was previously obscure. we namely either make a distinct concept, and this happens *per Synthesin*, or we make a concept that was previously confused distinct, and this happens *per Analysin*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-227":["In *Synthesi* we generate, and create as it were, a concept that previously was not yet there at all, wholly new both *quoad materiam* and also *quoad formam*, and make it distinct at the same time.",[]],"blomberg-s07-228":["All concepts of the *Mathematicer* are of this kind, e.g., the concepts of the triangle, square, circles etc.",[]],"blomberg-s07-229":["All concepts *feigned* by reason are at the same time distinct, but only *per Synthesin*. If a concept is to be made distinct *per Analysin*, then it must already be given, one therefore occupies oneself with making the confused and obscure in this given concept clear, and distinct, developing it, setting it apart, and so illuminating it. Hither belong, e.g., each and every concept of metaphysics.",[]],"blomberg-s07-230":["With these one is to become conscious merely solely and alone of the marks which belong to the thing. E.g., with the concepts of virtue, of vice, and we hence need nothing more than to *analyze*, to set apart, and to dissect, to make the confused concepts distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-231":["The matter, therefore, is already there, we are only to give the thing a form. By means of analytic distinctness we cognize in a thing no more than we have already thought in it beforehand, but we cognize only that which we have actually already known, better, i.e., more distinctly, more clearly, and with more consciousness. E.g., with the concept of perfection I will first lead someone to the cases in which he makes use of the expression perfection, in order thereby to teach him what he then properly understands by perfection? what concept he makes for himself of it, and what he thinks when he utters the word perfection, and ascribes it to a thing. Here he will now naturally find that he calls much perfect that is actually very imperfect, or perhaps even vicious. E.g., the voluptuary takes his *Vagam Libidinem* for the greatest possible perfection. For if this were not so, he would surely be unable to cling to it so strongly, and to find in it so very great a gratification, obscuring everything else for him. Dissection therefore properly pertains to the making distinct of the confused. The composition of cognition, however, or *synthesis*, is solely and alone serviceable, and useful, for producing something new, and also making it distinct at once at the same time.",[]],"blomberg-s07-232":["In general all concepts of the human understanding are either",[]],"blomberg-s07-233":["1: *mo conceptus dati* or 2 *do Conceptus facti*",[]],"blomberg-s07-234":["A *conceptus datus*, a given concept, is that which is produced either through the nature of our understanding, or also through experience.",[]],"blomberg-s07-235":["*Conceptus Facti*, made concepts, are such as are arbitrarily generated, or fabricated, by us without having previously been given.",[]],"blomberg-s07-236":["All these fabricated concepts are produced at the same time with distinctness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-237":["One namely *feigns* something there arbitrarily, and indeed at the same time with the consciousness of it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-238":["Such *Conceptus facti* there are many in aesthetics, and in mathematics. in *Philosophia*, on the other hand, there are *Conceptus dati*",[]],"blomberg-s07-239":["Through dissection alone a cognition does not become distinct, for the action whereby a cognition becomes distinct is indeed one and the same as that through which its marks become clear.",[]],"blomberg-s07-240":["A cognition can receive a distinctness of reason *per analysin*, but also a distinctness of understanding *per Analysin*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-241":["Through dissection I merely bring the marks of a cognition under more universal marks, which I previously already had insight into *per Analysin a priori*. *per Synthesin*, however, I learn new marks of a cognition in addition *a posteriori*, e.g., with the concept of the essence of gold.",[]],"blomberg-s07-242":["This is empirical distinctness: that, however, is distinctness of understanding, which has its origin in my reason and happens by means of dissection. E.g., morals is nothing more than the setting apart of our moral representations, of virtue, of the good, and of evil. Socrates said: I am the midwife of the thoughts of my listeners.",[]],"blomberg-s07-243":["To make a cognition means to fabricate",[]],"blomberg-s07-244":["We will now briefly indicate the degrees of the representations of all cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s07-245":["1: *mo* »to represent something in general is the most universal, and most usual, also easiest cognition of a thing. But to represent something with which a consciousness is also combined is different from representing in general, whereby we are often not even conscious to what these representations properly refer.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-246":["*Sibi aliquid repraesentare id est cognoscere, Repraesentatio.*",[]],"blomberg-s07-247":["But if with such a representation there is also combined the capacity to bring that which I represent under a universal concept, and therefore to be able to know to what my representation properly refers, then this is the",[]],"blomberg-s07-248":["2nd degree, namely to be acquainted with, »I am then acquainted with that which I represent.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-249":["3.) »the following degree is to understand something, i.e., to be acquainted with through the understanding, or to be acquainted with something distinctly through a distinct concept.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-250":["Nature hands us many things which we are indeed in a position to be acquainted with, but not on that account properly to understand. Further",[]],"blomberg-s07-251":["4: To have insight. much »that is found in nature we in turn indeed understand, but we do not yet have insight into it. E.g., the growth of plants.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-252":["To have insight means to cognize something through and by means of reason, that is, *a priori*. »Herein the thing therefore need not even be given to me. Human beings often believe they already have insight into something when they are only in a position to give an explanation of it.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-253":["To make, *facere*, a cognition means to fabricate it, *Fingere*",[]],"blomberg-s07-254":["A *Conceptus Factitius* therefore is also at the same time a *Conceptus Fictitius*. To make a cognition arbitrarily and with consciousness therefore means to make a distinct cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s07-255":["All mathematical *Definitiones* are, e.g., merely fabricated, and are therefore nothing other than made arbitrary distinct concepts of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-256":["5: »To comprehend something is finally the last, and highest degree, namely: it is to have insight into something through reason, yet so that it is sufficient for a certain aim, that is *comparative* insight.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-257":["but if I have insight into something sufficient for each and every aim, then this is *absolute* comprehending without qualification.",[]],"blomberg-s07-258":["Much that I actually understand, am acquainted with, and have insight into, I do not on that account yet comprehend. and in turn much that I comprehend *comparative* I do not on that account yet comprehend *absolute*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-259":["*Absolute*, that is sufficient for every aim, we have insight into nothing whatsoever in the whole of nature.",[]],"blomberg-s07-260":["*Die Sufficientia Notarum rei est Completudo, et est*",[]],"blomberg-s07-261":["a:) *externa*, i.e., when the marks that I have of a thing are complete, and sufficient enough to distinguish this thing from all others. the whole concept of it must therefore be known.",[]],"blomberg-s07-262":["Thus an outwardly sufficient concept is always also always inwardly sufficient. Further conversely, an inwardly sufficient concept is always also outwardly sufficient. and a concept that is not inwardly sufficient is also not outwardly sufficient, and that which is not so outwardly is in turn also not so inwardly",[]],"blomberg-s07-263":["*Philosophia est vel pura, vel applicata*",[]],"blomberg-s07-264":["In *Philosophia pura* each and every concept is given through the understanding, and through reason. E.g., the concepts of the possible, and in turn of the impossible, of the necessary, and of the contingent.",[]],"blomberg-s07-265":["*Omnes conceptus sunt vel*",[]],"blomberg-s07-266":["1: *pure rationales*",[]],"blomberg-s07-267":["2 *Empirici*",[]],"blomberg-s07-268":["All concepts are made distinct through dissection, that is *per analysin*, since they were previously obscure, and confused. Through it one namely learns to have insight into the partial concepts of a whole concept.",[]],"blomberg-s07-269":["A distinct concept, however, arises *per Synthesin*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-270":["In *Analysi* the concept becomes either *completely* distinct, or not.",[]],"blomberg-s07-271":["All my concepts of reason can become *completely* distinct, but empirical concepts are excluded from this, and always remain *incompletely* distinct",[]],"blomberg-s07-272":["Herein in this *Paragrapho* the author speaks of the comprehensible, and of the incomprehensible. in order to obtain an all the better *Ideam* of it, we will once more first present the degrees of human cognition. This cannot be described any further, nor defined.",[]],"blomberg-s07-273":["The 1st degree is to know something, that is, to represent something with consciousness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-274":["The 2nd degree is to be acquainted with something, i.e., to be able to cognize something in distinction from other things through comparison with them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-275":["The 3rd degree is to understand something, i.e., to cognize distinctly through the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s07-276":["The 4th degree finally is to have insight into something, or to cognize through reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-277":["When I have insight into something, I cognize it through mediate marks, I therefore infer and therein seek out *Notam notae*, a mark of the mark.",[]],"blomberg-s07-278":["If I am to understand something, then the understanding must be occupied therein, I must cognize a thing through immediate marks, but therein also judge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-279":["To understand something is far easier than to have insight into something, for in the first case it is only necessary that I *coordinate* the marks of a thing, in the other case, however, I must *subordinate* them. To understand what gold is, I need nothing more than to know the properties of this metal, that it is, e.g., ductile, yellow, heavier than others etc., that it does not rust. But in order to have insight into what gold is I must investigate one mark of it in particular, and separate out the ground of it. E.g., why it does not rust, why it is ductile, heavier than others.",[]],"blomberg-s07-280":["The fifth, and highest degree of our cognition finally is called to comprehend something, that is to have sufficient insight into something, or in the degree that is necessary for a certain insight.",[]],"blomberg-s07-281":["One can comprehend something either *Absolute*, or *Totaliter*, entirely, or also *partialiter*, i.e., *relative*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-282":["*Absolute* I comprehend something when I have insight into it as is required for this, or that end.",[]],"blomberg-s07-283":["We never comprehend something *totaliter*, or *absolute*. The most learned among us himself can yet not boast that he has insight into something in such a way that it should suffice for all aims. The common man comprehends with regard to religion, if he only has a healthy understanding, always just as much as the greatest *Theologus*, as is necessary for him to know in order to be able to live righteously, and arrange all his actions according to the laws of God.",[]],"blomberg-s07-284":["This, however, will nevertheless never be sufficient for all his ends.",[]],"blomberg-s07-285":["In regard to the practical perfection of cognition we therefore all have equal insights, although we are distinguished according to insights with regard to logical perfection, whereby, however, he too who has greater insights, and more elaborated cognitions, comprehends nothing *absolute*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-286":["The author translates the word »begreifen« falsely by *concipere*, whereas *concipere* properly means nothing other than »einsehen«.",[]],"blomberg-s07-287":["The 6 degrees of our cognition, of which we have already spoken more at length above, are rather called",[]],"blomberg-s07-288":["1. *repraesentare*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-289":["represent",[]],"blomberg-s07-290":["2. *Scire*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-291":["know",[]],"blomberg-s07-292":["3. *Noscere* be acquainted with.",[]],"blomberg-s07-293":["4. *intelligere* understand",[]],"blomberg-s07-294":["5. *Concipere* have insight",[]],"blomberg-s07-295":["6. *Comprehendere* comprehend",[]],"blomberg-s07-296":["To one human being something is often comprehensible which in turn is incomprehensible to another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-297":["Something is not comprehensible to us at the beginning which can yet very easily be comprehended by us in the sequel of time. Yet one can often err in this, that one believes, and fully holds, that others will comprehend very well what one does not comprehend oneself, *et vice versa*, when one holds that what one comprehends well is incomprehensible to others.",[]],"blomberg-s07-298":["If I am to make the concepts of a thing distinct to myself, then my representation of it must first of all be clear. Then I must pay attention to the various marks of the thing, after this I must take many marks together in one thing, or *compare*, hold them against one another, and compare them with one another. In this *comparation* there takes place not only a mere *collection* or gathering of the marks but rather also a placing side by side, a *coordination* of them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-299":["Finally, however, there is added the *abstractio notarum*, or that action in which I leave out of attention all such marks of a thing as could hinder and disturb me in the making distinct of the representation, or at least are of no use to me and are thus superfluous for it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-300":["In this *§pho* the author shows the difference of distinct cognition into *cognitionem totaliter* and *partialiter distinctam*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-301":["One often cognizes an *object* distinctly according to one part, but indistinctly according to the other.",[]],"blomberg-s07-302":["Much is very hidden from us in experience, but in logic more distinctness is already to be met with.",[]],"blomberg-s07-303":["Indistinctness actually arises from the lack of attention to the parts of a whole concept.",[]],"blomberg-s07-304":["The fault of this indistinctness one commonly is accustomed to ascribe either to the thing itself, of which one has a distinct cognition, or to the person and the *subject* in which the indistinctness of the cognition is found. If a thing has all too many marks, so that one is not in a position to survey them at once, then the fault for its indistinctness necessarily lies in the thing itself.",[]],"blomberg-s07-305":["With *empirical* concepts there always prevails more confusion than with concepts of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-306":["The concepts of experience namely arise through the affection of our senses, but these cannot have insight into all the determinations of things, but rather many of them still remain hidden and unknown to us.",[]],"blomberg-s07-307":["Our cognitions are either",[]],"blomberg-s07-308":["1: *adaequat*, i.e., complete, or",[]],"blomberg-s07-309":["2) incomplete and not *adaequat*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-310":["The author in this *paragrapho*, following Baumgarten, and Baumgarten in turn following Wolff, has called a certain kind of distinct cognitions complete.",[]],"blomberg-s07-311":["If the marks of a thing are cognized completely distinctly, then this is the intensive clarity of cognition of the first degree; but if I cognize the immediate marks of a thing distinctly, but derive from them other mediate marks, then this distinctness of cognition is the intensive clarity of the 2nd degree, and according to the expressions of Wolff, the completeness of cognition, e.g., if I say: vice is an aptitude for sinning.",[]],"blomberg-s07-312":["Now an aptitude, however, is a facility in acting. But sinning means nothing other than to live contrary to the *moral* rules and laws. Vice is thus an aptitude to *edit* actions that are contrary to the moral laws.",[]],"blomberg-s07-313":["This 2nd degree of the intensive distinctness of cognition, however, cannot possibly be completeness, for otherwise the third degree of distinctness would have to be still more complete. and this would go on ever further. here all *comparation* falls away, and if something is already *absolute* complete, then nothing can be met with that would be still more complete.",[]],"blomberg-s07-314":["The author, however, has in an improper way called a complete cognition *adaequatam cognitionem*, and in turn incomplete cognitions *cognitiones inadaequatae*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-315":["Completeness itself, however, consists in nothing other than in *completudine*, and in *praecision*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-316":["A cognition is thus complete (*cognitio rei adaequata*) if the representation of a thing is properly in conformity and commensurate with it, so that it contains nothing more, but also nothing less, than actually lies in the thing: *Si repraesentatio rei de re quadam nec supra, nec infra rem est.*",[]],"blomberg-s07-317":["All distinctness through *subordination* of marks is of different degrees, and hence one cannot call a cognition complete before its marks are no longer *subordinated* to any other concept.",[]],"blomberg-s07-318":["*Completudo*, or exhaustiveness, we have already considered above, and of *praecision* we will speak at greater length afterward in what follows.",[]],"blomberg-s07-319":["*Completudo* consists in nothing other than in the *sufficiency*, or sufficingness, of a distinct concept of a thing, in order to derive from it all the remaining marks of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-320":["*Omnis cognitio sufficiens est*",[]],"blomberg-s07-321":["a) *extensiva*, that is, exhaustiveness",[]],"blomberg-s07-322":["B: *intensiva*, that is, thoroughness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-323":["For thoroughness, then, profundity is required, to this, then, belongs proving, and it is attained through ascending up to the highest mark of many marks subordinated to one another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-324":["Some complete thoroughness one requires in regard to morals.",[]],"blomberg-s07-325":["Nevertheless one also errs very often here. So, e.g., it is with a certain *casu necessitatis* such as a necessary lie, a Christian deception etc.",[]],"blomberg-s07-326":["Exhaustiveness actually consists in nothing other than this, that nothing is lacking, that the concept can be whole.",[]],"blomberg-s07-327":["Nevertheless, however, even with this exhaustiveness an error can take place. namely, if my concept contains too much, than it might contain, in order accordingly to be complete.",[]],"blomberg-s07-328":["There is thus added yet another perfection, which aims at this, that neither more nor less than is needed be contained therein. and this perfection is called measuredness, *praecision*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-329":["A cognition is thus measured if it has neither too many nor too few marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-330":["*Cognitio est praecisa, quando non abundat notis.*",[]],"blomberg-s07-331":["*Praecision*, however, is thus the determinate degree of the marks, according to which they contain no more than is needed in order to derive from them all the remaining determinations of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-332":["It requires more effort to make a cognition measured than to bring it to an *abundant* exhaustiveness, i.e., to make it more than *complete*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-333":["*Praecision* is actually a great and difficult art, this one has insight into more easily in all cases, even by means of experience; thus the otherwise so *praecise* mathematician can nevertheless, e.g., in the quadrature of the circle find no *praecision*. The calculations that have hitherto been made of it are all so constituted that the number arrived at has been either too large or too small, and has not yet been able to be hit correctly.",[]],"blomberg-s07-334":["But to be in a position to be able to observe such *praecision* in regard to *philosophical* concepts; for this there certainly belongs still far more art and skill. a *complete* and *praecise* concept taken together, however, is commensurate with the thing, and then such a concept is called a *conceptus adaequatus*. *Cognitio rei adaequata.*",[]],"blomberg-s07-335":["It is actually the case here with marks just as with a measure. A measure is commensurate with the thing if it contains neither too much nor too little than is necessary for the magnitude to be measured. A mark is also commensurate with the thing of which it is supposed to be a mark if it contains neither too much nor too little than lies in the concept of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-336":["With *completudine* I take care that there be nothing too little in the cognition, but with *praecision* we see to it that there be nothing too much in it. *Completudo* is, as is easy to have insight into, always far more necessary than *praecisio*, for superfluity in cognition, although it is also an error, which one must endeavor to avoid as much as possible can nevertheless be endured far sooner and more easily than lack in cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s07-337":["*Praecision* is actually only merely relatively a perfection, because it merely serves for this, that one deal sparingly with one's knowledge, and not apply one's powers all too superfluously to one thing, and thus as it were waste them where it is not needed, so that one afterward has no capacity for other more useful cognitions. *Praecision* is thus nothing other than a rule of economy, which for that very reason also has a certain inner beauty: It is to be met with especially in *mathematics*, in geometry, and in *mechanics*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-338":["The superfluous, the *abundans* in a concept comes merely from this, when one concept already lies in the other, and this superfluity is always a sequel of the lack *in praecisione*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-339":["One says that which one could have expressed and said through some words, through many and more words than are requisite for it. Just so, if in *arithmetica* one expresses a fraction in very large and lengthy figures, which one would yet be in a position to express just as well and correctly with few and small numbers",[]],"blomberg-s07-340":["There is also a certain kind of *praecision* in proofs, according to which not a single proposition is too much, nor too little, than serves for conviction. Such excellent *praecision* in proofs one likewise finds in *mathematica*. This beautiful science is thus, as we see, a model in many respects, and one has from time immemorial always endeavored to bring these its properties, and among them also especially *praecision*, into philosophy as well.",[]],"blomberg-s07-341":["With *systematic* exposition and cognition *praecision* is a chief perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s07-342":["With beautiful, i.e., *aesthetic* cognitions, however, on the other hand *praecision* is in turn a dispensable perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s07-343":["In eloquence and poetry one is not sparing in expressions, but rather often even all too wasteful. one says a thing very often more than once, and yet only with other expressions, and under other images. *Praecision* is thus merely a rule of economy and of prudence, but not at all of taste and of *aesthetics*. *Praecision* is only a rule of prudence, it is thus also difficult to attain, and that *praecision* where one is so measured and sparing in one's expressions that another thereby attains a determinate degree of the clarity of the concept is a particular talent, and hence also a work of genius, and through mere art also only very difficult, indeed almost impossible, to attain. each and every aphorism is of this kind, all proverbs have a certain kind of *praecision*, or commensurateness. One says through them in few expressions a whole wisdom. *Praecision* thus belongs merely to *logical* perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s07-344":["The author in the 151st *paragrapho* calls the *praecision* of cognition a determinate cognition. This expression, however, is entirely false.",[]],"blomberg-s07-345":["Indeterminate marks are such as contain too little, and our author says in turn indeterminate marks are such marks as contain too much, or more. Something can be *complete* without having any *praecision* in itself. now we will speak a little more about profound cognitions. Intensive distinctness has in a cognition, as we have already heard above, different degrees.",[]],"blomberg-s07-346":["A greater degree of the distinctness of cognition through *subordinated* marks is deep distinctness. A higher degree of the distinctness of cognition, on the other hand, through *coordinated* marks is extended distinctness. To the depth of cognition I cannot attain otherwise than if I go from one mark to the other, and in this way mediately cognize one mark of the thing through the other.",[]],"blomberg-s07-347":["Profundity is nothing other than the faculty for deep distinctness, or for a great series of marks by means of, or mediately through, ascending. Here I go from the experiences that I have already had up to the first grounds, or from the first grounds to the first experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s07-348":["The first, from which I begin here, can indeed be either the first, or however also, if I end with it, the last.",[]],"blomberg-s07-350":["In this *paragrapho* the author teaches us nothing further than a mere name. He does not show us here how one can and should make concepts bright and clear, but rather merely teaches us to know a name for a head that has clear concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s07-351":["Some human beings are of such a kind that they foolishly imagine that in their head alone and solely it is bright. Whereas other people had nothing but dark and confused concepts. \"In their head alone there is a Goshen, while on the contrary everything else is covered with an Egyptian night.\"",[]],"blomberg-s07-352":["A scholar, however, must endeavor very especially to attain clear and distinct concepts of the things that are, or can be, objects of his consideration.",[]],"blomberg-s08-001":["Certainty is nothing other than the *subjective* necessity in the *quality* of judgments. The opposite of judgments is the negation of that which is affirmed in the judgments, or also the affirmation of that which is negated in the judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-002":["Many judgments are so constituted, the opposite of them seems to me to have to be entirely impossible, and thereby it is *subjective* necessary",[]],"blomberg-s08-003":["Everything that is true is by that very fact at the same time *subjective* certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-004":["*Objective* necessity is thus actually truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-005":["In a multitude of cases we find that we judge much to be true that we yet *subjective* in certain cases always find to be uncertain, indeed even its opposite to be possible.",[]],"blomberg-s08-006":["It is thus clear that the uncertainty of a cognition rests merely on its *objective* falsehood, but the certainty of a cognition, on the other hand, in turn on its *objective* truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-007":["If something is true, then it is also at the same time certain, if something is false, it is also always at the same time without doubt uncertain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-008":["*Objective* the true cannot be false, that is, about the constitution of a thing, or a true property, it cannot be uncertain whether it be true or false.",[]],"blomberg-s08-009":["All uncertainty is thus grounded in the *subject*, and is *subjective*, i.e., it is grounded in him who is not in a position either to have insight into and to cognize something as true, or as false.",[]],"blomberg-s08-010":["Every ground for truth is a ground of taking to be true, every ground of cognition, supposing that it were also insufficient, is nevertheless a ground of taking to be true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-011":["All taking to be true from grounds about which we do not investigate whether there be in them a great or a small degree of truth is plausibility. if the degree of truth is greater than the degree of the grounds of the opposite, then the cognition is probable. There thus takes place in it not in the least any settled truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-012":["With probability there is actually a sufficient ground present, this ground for truth, however, is yet greater than the grounds of the opposite outweighs them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-013":["Taking to be true for the sake of the illusion of cognition is called persuasion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-014":["Here one thus accepts any degree of truth whatsoever in order to be able to assent to a cognition, without investigating whether the grounds of the opposite have a greater degree of truth or not? The judgment according to the measure of probability is not persuasion but rather the judgment from mere plausibility. Persuasion is actually a kind of blindness;",[]],"blomberg-s08-015":["for one always considers in it only the one side, without yet *reflecting* in the least on the opposite side, which yet is highly harmful.",[]],"blomberg-s08-016":["The consciousness of a cognition, however, through the sufficient ground of truth, is called conviction This arises merely from the consciousness of reason of the necessary of the cognition. Where a contingent cognition, which can be thus, but also otherwise than it actually is, still takes place, there no conviction is present. One can suitably represent conviction as composed of many *moments*, or *elements*, i.e., the small degrees of persuasion, and of taking to be true, and assume all *moments* or degrees of taking to be true and of persuasion to be of the same kind.",[]],"blomberg-s08-017":["If the *moments* of taking to be true make up just half of the sufficient ground, then a cognition is uncertain, doubtful, *ambigua ejus modi cognitio*. The mind thus always remains undecided in it, it hovers as it were between fear and hope. There are on the one side exactly as many grounds as on the other, which hold one another in equilibrium.",[]],"blomberg-s08-018":["But if there is even only one degree of truth more on the side of the insufficient ground than on the side of the opposite, then the cognition is no longer *ambigua*, but rather already probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-019":["But if on the contrary there is in turn one degree of truth less on the side of the insufficient ground than on the side of the opposite, then the cognition is not only uncertain, also not probable, but rather even improbable. Nevertheless, however, the cognition still always remains plausible. it can therefore not yet be rejected at once, for for plausibility merely the least degree of truth is required.",[]],"blomberg-s08-020":["All certainty is to be regarded as a unity, and as a complete whole, and is thus the measure of all our remaining taking to be true, and of each and every degree of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-021":["Certainty, however, arises from nothing other than from the relation of equality between the grounds of cognition that I have, and between the whole sufficient ground itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-022":["If the grounds of cognition that I have are not equal to the whole sufficient ground, then my cognition is uncertain. It is true, there is a certain heterogeneity in objects and concepts, nevertheless concepts can become homogeneous if one brings the manifold under a universal concept.",[]],"blomberg-s08-023":["This production of homogeneity arises merely from comparison. One *compares*, or holds the grounds of truth against the grounds of falsehood: sees then whether the grounds of truth have a greater degree in themselves than the grounds of the opposite, and then after riper reflection passes a more correct and more apt judgment on the probability and improbability of a cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-024":["If we had no cognition of the sufficient ground of certainty, then we would not at all be able to pass any judgment on the probability or improbability of a thing. All these namely arise from the relation of our grounds of cognition to certainty. But one is always accustomed to assent first to that of which one has, and sees before one, more and convincing grounds of truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-025":["The mere sufficient ground is *objective*, the insufficient ground, however, is *subjective*. The former determines in the thing what is to be held of it, the latter, however, is on the other hand very doubtful, for it is surely easy to have insight into the fact that one can have today more, tomorrow in turn fewer grounds of truth of a cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-026":["The sufficient ground, however, is that whose opposite cannot possibly be thought and represented as true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-027":["It thus naturally actually contains all that which is found in the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-028":["The insufficient grounds, however, on the other hand *enumerate* merely that which we think and represent to ourselves of the thing, and cognize of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-029":["Certainty thus, as we have just said, and then the sufficient ground are the standard of measure. All judgments are not probable, but rather plausible, if no relation of the grounds of cognition to certainty takes place in them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-030":["This plausibility, however, can change very greatly.",[]],"blomberg-s08-031":["Probability and improbability are *objective* grounds. Plausibility, however, is a *subjective* ground that is: probability and improbability lie in the *object* itself, in the thing which is to be cognized: plausibility, on the other hand, lies in him who has no cognition of a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-032":["In a *subject* the state is the same, whether the cognition which it has be only a persuasion, or a certain truth, a conviction, or not? it is thereby not at all in the least changed externally.",[]],"blomberg-s08-033":["It is, however, in general not possible to state an infallible, secure, and universally valid mark of conviction in distinction from persuasion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-034":["Since it is now impossible to state properly and securely which grounds of judgment and of persuasion lie in the *object*, and which in the *subject*, we make here the following important general remark: namely \"all error actually arises only from this, that we *confuse* the causes of assent which lie in the *object* itself with [445a] the grounds that lie in the *subject*.\"",[]],"blomberg-s08-035":["But in order to investigate whether something that we accept is properly a mere plausible and invalid persuasion, or an actual secure conviction, one first of all weighs the grounds of the opposite especially.",[]],"blomberg-s08-036":["If I now find that I meet with just as many great and just as weighty grounds for the opposite of my cognition as for that to which I have actually assented, then I easily have insight from this that my whole cognition must not in the least have been a conviction, but rather merely a persuasion, a *phaenomenon* of conviction.",[]],"blomberg-s08-037":["All our representations together are such *phaenomena*, e.g., with the rainbow it seems as if it rested on the earth; it is thus a mere appearance, on closer investigation namely we have insight, however, that this is not so.",[]],"blomberg-s08-038":["Experience teaches us that the strength of persuasion about a thing often can be, and is, just as great as the conviction of its opposite ever is afterward.",[]],"blomberg-s08-039":["A means thus, in order to sense what is properly needed and requisite for true conviction, is thus: to study diligently such sciences as pave the surest way to it, i.e., which actually convince us of certain truths. e.g., *mathematics* Further, one should apply oneself carefully to such sciences as confirm the grounds of reason through experience, as, e.g., *experimental physica*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-040":["Since we thus lack a universally valid mark to distinguish the *subjective* grounds of taking to be true from the *objective*, the *phaenomenon*",[]],"blomberg-s08-041":["of conviction from true certainty and conviction itself correctly and infallibly, we must be content with this, that in individual cases we endeavor to convince ourselves of the certainty of this or that thing, so that we can at least always be in a position to distinguish the false from the true. But in order to be able carefully to avoid error in this or that cognition, one must heed well and observe the following:",[]],"blomberg-s08-042":["*1mo* \"One must compare one's cognition of this or that *object* with the cognitions of other people about one and the very same *object*, about one and the very same object, and carefully hold them against one another, since it is not to be supposed that every human being should have one and the very same motives to take one and the very same thing to be true, but rather there is the greatest probability that that which the one considers from one side, another, who thinks something different, and makes different concepts of the whole thing, would also here turn toward it from the other side.\"",[]],"blomberg-s08-043":["*2do* \"One must assume and *suppose* the opposite of the cognition which one has, put oneself as it were in the place of one's opponent and disputer of the truth of my cognition and then see whether it can be thought as true, or even only as probable. If this takes place even only somewhat, then one already always has cause to place some mistrust in the truth of one's cognition held. But if this does not take place in the least, and I can have insight impartially, also assert, that the opposite of the cognition which I have would be *absurd*, impossible, and, if one wanted to accept it, foolish, then it is evident from this that my cognition must be true and correct.\"",[]],"blomberg-s08-044":["etc. etc. etc. There are, however, with respect to certainty three different kinds, or degrees, of taking to be true",[]],"blomberg-s08-045":["1.) Knowing",[]],"blomberg-s08-046":["2) Opining",[]],"blomberg-s08-047":["3 ) Believing",[]],"blomberg-s08-048":["*1mo* Knowing means to judge something with certainty, and to take it to be true. there thus belongs much to knowing something. I can never say: I know that there are inhabitants in the moon, I have not been there, I have not seen them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-049":["*2do* Opining means to judge something incompletely, and occurs when the grounds that we have to take something to be true are insufficient, and do not have the preponderance against the opposite, and that is then an *opinion*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-050":["In these opinions, however, the grounds, and thus thereby also the degrees of taking to be true, can grow and increase; but it is also to be noted that in opining one always remains undecided",[]],"blomberg-s08-051":["But in order to opine something *practically*, there belongs to this",[]],"blomberg-s08-052":["3.) Believing. Here I am always decided without however finding it in myself as necessary to be decided.",[]],"blomberg-s08-053":["Opinions are thus nothing other than *subjectively* incomplete grounds of taking to be true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-054":["The human being opines at all times.",[]],"blomberg-s08-055":["To accept something without a *subjective* necessity according to logical concepts is called believing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-056":["A logical necessity, however, is nothing other than the necessity of taking to be true according to logical laws of the understanding and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-057":["The necessity, however, of accepting something according to *practical* laws is always *subjective*. Believing thus means nothing other than to accept something of which I am not yet logically certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-058":["Belief is further also a *practically* sufficient taking to be true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-059":["Whereas certainty is a taking to be true sufficient also theoretically. [454a]",[]],"blomberg-s08-060":["In believing I am not even minded to listen to the grounds of my opponent. For my grounds of taking to be true are in it always *practically* sufficient. in believing there also occurs a certain *direction* of our judgment by the will.",[]],"blomberg-s08-061":["If I accept something, then I do not on that account yet exactly take it to be certain, and just so, if I concede something, then I surely do not on that account by a long way yet take it to be true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-062":["A *practical* certainty would be firm belief, and this will be, both according to its conviction and according to the power to dispel all doubts, just as strong as belief according to logical laws can ever be. This belief is often stronger than many an *apodictic* certainty of a cognition. This, however, takes place merely with the universal pieces of knowledge",[]],"blomberg-s08-063":["a) of reason",[]],"blomberg-s08-064":["B) of experience",[]],"blomberg-s08-065":["We can also believe something without thereby attaining a *practical* certainty of it, but rather merely at most a greater degree of taking to be true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-066":["There is also a *practical* taking to be true, and a certain believing, whose cancellation would at the same time cancel and annihilate all universal and necessary laws of the *practical* will, of the whole of morals, doctrine of morals, theology, religion etc. and whatever sublime can be devised, and this is all then a firm belief.",[]],"blomberg-s08-067":["Of such propositions we have 2:",[]],"blomberg-s08-068":["1:) There is a God.",[]],"blomberg-s08-069":["2) There is another world.",[]],"blomberg-s08-070":["These 2 propositions are the sole ground of all religion, morality etc. These 2 propositions are the true *conditio sine qua non*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-071":["The belief of these 2 propositions is not historical, but rather rational, derived from *practical* rules of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-072":["This belief is not an opinion, but rather a certainty, which, however, is so constituted that, although it is not actually *apodictic* and irrefutable according to laws of logic, it nevertheless none the less by far outweighs even all taking to be true from logical grounds, and retains precedence over them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-073":["*Apodictic* certainty, which comes and arises merely from the understanding, actually has, when it comes to the decision of all our welfare, our fortune and misfortune, however convincing it otherwise is, not the inner force to compel the *subject* always to adhere to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-074":["Even with *mathematical* propositions and *demonstrations*, which yet otherwise have the very greatest and surest certainty, it goes thus. However certain it also is that a circle has 360°, however *apodictic* the Platonic theorems in geometry are, yet no one would want to die for their infallibility and irrefutability.",[]],"blomberg-s08-075":["Belief according to *practical*, but especially according to moral laws, however, has the complete power to strengthen the *subject* that has it with a complete confidence, and to bring it to this, that in spite of all dangers and misfortune even then, when it concerns the decision of all that which touches its weal or woe nevertheless always to adhere to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-076":["One has indeed seen people die for moral propositions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-077":["The resolve, thus, not to let oneself be turned aside by anything at all, is indeed more a resolve of *practical* and moral belief than an effect of *apodictic* certainty, and it is far more unshakable, indeed it in fact always carries with it a certain kind of merit.",[]],"blomberg-s08-078":["*Objective* a cognition is certain if it contains those grounds according to which all human beings must agree with respect to it. *Subjective*, however, a cognition is certain if it contains the way in which human beings actually agree with respect to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-079":["Evidentness is the *intuitive*, the intuiting in certainty, *evidence*. Thus, e.g., *mathematical* propositions are *evident*. we are, however, certain of many things without nevertheless having an *evidence* of them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-080":["We have 2 methods of the grounds of taking to be true. The concurrence of other human beings with our opinions, and the testing of our thoughts according to the dispositions of other human beings, is actually a very excellent logical experiment of our understanding on the understanding of others. The human being very much needs this sharing, this *communication* of his cognitions, in order to be able to judge them properly.",[]],"blomberg-s08-081":["Human beings have a natural inclination to share with others the judgments that their understanding has passed, and merely from this *originates* the writing of books, whose cause other censurers of the human race, who like to interpret everything in the worst way, otherwise also place in vanity, in ambition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-082":["Human beings who separate themselves from all human society find at last, when they begin to investigate their state and the causes of their misanthropy, necessarily that they themselves do not have enough means to distinguish the true from the false.",[]],"blomberg-s08-083":["The freedom of sharing one's thoughts, judgments, knowledge, however, is certainly the sole surest means to test one's knowledge properly and to *verify* it. and whoever takes away this freedom is to be regarded as the worst enemy of the spread of human cognitions, indeed of human beings themselves. For he thereby takes away from human beings the one true means that they still possess ever to uncover, to become aware of, and to correct the frequent deception of their own understanding and its missteps.",[]],"blomberg-s08-084":["The deprivation of the freedom to think unconstrained, and to bring these one's thoughts to light, is actually a deprivation of the first rights of the greatest advantages of the human race, and especially of the human understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-085":["Human beings are as it were called to use their reason in common and to avail themselves of it. Just so the temporal goods of this life.",[]],"blomberg-s08-086":["From this it thus follows quite naturally that everyone who has the *principium* of self-conceit thinks foreign judgments to be entirely dispensable to him for the use of his own reason and for the cognition of truth, very poorly, and worthy of censure.",[]],"blomberg-s08-087":["This, however, is actually logical *egoism*, which then admittedly also could not and would not need at all to share its own judgments with others. This logical so-called *egoism* thus consists in nothing other than in the supposed but often false self-sufficiency of our understanding existing entirely for itself, and so to speak: *isolated*, where one believes oneself alone to know sufficiently for oneself, and all one's judgments to be infallibly correct and incorrigible. and one easily sees that this conceited way of thinking is not only entirely ridiculous, but rather even highly contrary to actual humanity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-088":["Sensible certainty is to be well distinguished by us from the certainty of the senses.",[]],"blomberg-s08-089":["Through observations, experiments, and a frequent, also often repeated experience one attains a certainty of the senses.",[]],"blomberg-s08-090":["The sensible certainty of a cognition, however, arises not properly through the actual intuition of *objects* by means of our senses, not through the immediate sensible representation of objects, but rather merely through this, that one makes cognitions distinct, convincing, and capable of being had insight into with easier, or lesser, effort through images and examples.",[]],"blomberg-s08-091":["A cognition can properly be called rational in a twofold way, namely",[]],"blomberg-s08-092":["*1mo* \"insofar as it can be had insight into through reason, and also agrees with it of this kind are all true well-grounded demonstrable cognitions.\" or",[]],"blomberg-s08-093":["*2do* \"insofar as it arises merely from reason. of this kind are the logical cognitions for the most part all, which for this reason can also be called *praecise* and in the most proper sense cognitions of reason.\"",[]],"blomberg-s08-094":["With the certainty of a cognition one can look either to logical and rational certainty or to *aesthetic* and sensible certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-095":["The latter cognition is indeed, to be sure, subject to very many errors, but nevertheless it is also, on the other hand, very easy to have insight into.",[]],"blomberg-s08-096":["A cognition which is perfect according to the rules of coordinated concepts is called, according to the common manner of speaking, complete. Since one could, however, sooner and more correctly call it an extended cognition, because the coordinated concepts are all set up at the same time, and thus, so to speak, as it were extended over the object.",[]],"blomberg-s08-097":["Those grounds to which others are subordinated are properly not at all the *complementa* of these latter to the sufficient ground, but rather they are their origin. in the series of grounds coordinated with one another, however, one concept is indeed quite often a *complementum* of another.",[]],"blomberg-s08-098":["In the highest degree of certainty of reason I look to the series of marks subordinated to one another, in order to come to the first ground. in this way I seek to discover the intensive clarity of conviction. In the highest degree of sensible certainty, however, I always look merely to the series of marks coordinated with one another, in order thereby to be able to bring about a certain strict and accurate completeness according to the rules of extensive clarity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-099":["A cognition, however, can always be certain",[]],"blomberg-s08-100":["1.) Mathematically but also 2 ) Philosophically.",[]],"blomberg-s08-101":["What is peculiar to mathematical certainty is this, that, although it arises from reason just as the philosophical does, one nevertheless with it synthetically, that is, that in the series of grounds subordinated to one another one ascends through composition from the lowest and simplest to the uppermost mark.",[]],"blomberg-s08-102":["In philosophical certainty, on the contrary, one does indeed, to be sure, likewise also go through the series of concepts subordinated to one another, i.e., placed under one another, but this happens not synthetically, but analytically, i.e., by means of dissection. and one goes down from the composite and uppermost to the simplest and lowest concept.",[]],"blomberg-s08-103":["The mathematical certainty of cognition is always very distinct, and intuitive, so that one can have insight into it very easily. This, however, arises from this, because all mathematical concepts are synthetic, and arise through arbitrary composition. for one can most easily be conscious of that which one has oneself invented. In philosophy, on the other hand, all concepts are analytic, they are not arbitrary, as in mathematics, so that one can assume and fix something according to one's own pleasure, but are already given confusedly, and are only to be made distinct and easy [473/474] by philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s08-104":["Since, then, the concepts in philosophy are already given in, the philosopher cannot so easily be certain that he has touched on all the marks that belong to a thing, and has insight into them quite perfectly. but many *notae* can still belong to the thing of which he knows nothing at all.",[]],"blomberg-s08-105":["But no marks of a thing can escape or slip away from the mathematician, nothing can escape his acute eye. For he has himself arbitrarily, if not invented them, yet assumed them, and arbitrarily ascribed them to it, consequently he must surely also be perfectly conscious of them all.",[]],"blomberg-s08-106":["The author speaks in this *paragraphus* of shallow cognitions. In our language, and in our expressions, we very often borrow analogies.",[]],"blomberg-s08-107":["We make use, namely, of certain images, in order by their help to give sensibility to cognitions abstract in themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s08-108":["In just the same way we also here in logic call certain cognitions (as otherwise kinds of understanding, lectures, etc. etc.) shallow, *ad analogiam* of water.",[]],"blomberg-s08-109":["A water is, according to physical principles, as is well known, called shallow when its depth is already to be met with not far beneath the surface.",[]],"blomberg-s08-110":["A cognition, however, is shallow *ad analogiam* when it is grounded on mere experiences and on no investigation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-111":["To the shallowness of cognition is opposed or *contradistinguished* its thoroughness, this latter is namely the *terminus* of certainty",[]],"blomberg-s08-112":["A cognition, however, is on the other hand superficial when one is already content with mere illusion, i.e., when one lets it be enough and rest with that cognition which one has already been able to attain at the mere first glance at it. in a superficial cognition one thus cognizes nothing at all more than what immediately strikes one's eyes at once. Thus one has, e.g., a superficial cognition of geography when one knows nothing further than how to enumerate readily the names of the countries, cities, rivers, etc. Thus one has a superficial cognition of history when one knows some striking stories of the states, without looking to their origin.",[]],"blomberg-s08-113":["Through and by means of a superficial cognition one sees, so to speak, as it were the straws indeed floating up on the surface of the water, but the pearls which lie on the bottom of the sea one does not become aware of.",[]],"blomberg-s08-114":["Superficial cognitions, which concern themselves only with the surface of a matter, and its cognitions, are most nearly related to shallowness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-115":["A cognition, however, is on the other hand thorough when one goes to the ground in it, i.e., when one goes as far as one can only go in order to attain to complete certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-116":["A cognition must be deep in order that it become thorough. When the series of grounds subordinated to one another is very long before one comes to complete certainty, then the cognition is profound.",[]],"blomberg-s08-117":["All cognitions, the empirical as well as the rational, can become thorough.",[]],"blomberg-s08-118":["But the thorough experiential, empirical cognitions are not of the kind that they can become profound, but only the rational ones. The ground of this is, because in empirical cognitions no series of subordinated, but only of coordinated grounds obtains.",[]],"blomberg-s08-119":["Empirical cognitions, however, are thorough when they contain in themselves as many coordinated concepts as are necessary for distinctness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-120":["A thorough historical cognition, e.g., one has when one goes back to the *autores* who first wrote of a matter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-121":["A cognition can be thorough even if it is very limited in regard to extensiveness and extension. But thorough cognitions must always be preferred to extended and extensive cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-122":["Extensiveness, namely, without thoroughness is nothing but an empty shadow, it resembles a null, or zero, a cipher in which there is no real being.",[]],"blomberg-s08-123":["Herein the author speaks of three different kinds of actions which we must undertake in order to be certain of a cognition, or to reject it. these 3 actions are namely nothing other than our different conduct in respect of the assent which we believe we either owe or do not owe to cognitions, as there is:",[]],"blomberg-s08-124":["1: *mo* the giving of assent",[]],"blomberg-s08-125":["2 *do* the withdrawal of assent, and",[]],"blomberg-s08-126":["3 *tio* the withholding of assent.",[]],"blomberg-s08-127":["The first is a positive, the second is a privative, but the third is no action at all. In the first case, namely, I take something as true",[]],"blomberg-s08-128":["In the second case I take something as false In the third case, however, I do not judge at all, but I suspend my *judicium*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-129":["With every cognition we can think either a",[]],"blomberg-s08-130":["plus or a minus, or a",[]],"blomberg-s08-131":["zero.",[]],"blomberg-s08-132":["Affirmative judgments increase our cognitions, and are thus a plus",[]],"blomberg-s08-133":["Negative judgments do not increase our cognition, and are thus a minus. For they seek only to diminish what is false in our cognitions, to put us in a state that we accept nothing but what is true, they serve thus, in short, only to avoid error",[]],"blomberg-s08-134":["But when finally in any cognition we neither affirm nor negate anything, then this is the state of zero.",[]],"blomberg-s08-135":["Just as we, with regard to our outer humor, are in the state of plus in gratification, in the state of minus in sadness, but in the state of zero in complete indifference and insensibility.",[]],"blomberg-s08-136":["The withdrawal of assent indeed brings no increase to our cognitions, but nevertheless keeps us from error.",[]],"blomberg-s08-137":["But when we give our assent to a cognition, then our knowledge receives an *accrementum*",[]],"blomberg-s08-138":["The withholding of assent, however, brings about ignorance. Although it can be rather useful in many cases. Here, however, we must necessarily still discuss the following question. Whether, namely, our free choice has an influence on our giving our assent to a cognition, or withdrawing it from it. or also perhaps withholding it?",[]],"blomberg-s08-139":["Answer In most cases such a procedure of giving our assent, or withdrawing it, or withholding it, does not rest at all on our free choice, but is rather properly *necessitated* through and by the laws of our understanding and our reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-140":["Thus it goes, for example, in *mathematica*. A merchant, e.g., who sees well from his accounts that he owes much, and more than he has to hope for and possesses, will indeed, to be sure, not be able to withdraw his assent and *consensus* from this cognition, which is so evident, however gladly he would want to, he will, supposing he also *positive* does not esteem it much, nevertheless not be able *negative* to regard the sum of the debt as still available existing capital, since he is all too much and evidently convinced of the correctness of arithmetic in this point, and the account of debts has all too great an evidence in itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-141":["This much is certain, if we were in a position, and it pleased us to give assent, now to accept something arbitrary at pleasure, or also now to reject it, then we would certainly thereby be put in a position to promote, to bring about, and to increase very much our gratification, our comfort, indeed even our happiness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-142":["On the other hand, we would to just the same degree also be capable of always removing at pleasure all that which could bring about sadness for us and cause vexation. but then, namely, in such arbitrary cognitions either no sufficient grounds for asserting the opposite of them would have to be there, or, supposing they were present, we would have to have no need at all to reflect upon them. The debtor would in this way easily be able to take himself for a *creditor*, the fallen one for a favorite of his prince etc., he who receives the most distressing tidings for a happy one, or at least remain *in suspenso* (although, considered from another side, hovering between fear and hope is far more burdensome and anxious, indeed more unbearable, than the consciousness of one's misfortune) and then even, if a complete ruin of his station, honor etc. and of his entire fortune stood before him inevitably and in a short time, then nevertheless be in a position with contented soul, and undisturbed, or moved mind, still always to be able to sleep well and peacefully, also to eat and drink well.",[]],"blomberg-s08-143":["This free *arbitrium* in respect of assent, however, might perhaps belong among the felicities of the ancient poets. it falls away entirely, and in the presence of certain degrees of grounds it is always very difficult, indeed altogether impossible, to withhold one's assent.",[]],"blomberg-s08-144":["One can therefore, in very many cases, not only count upon, but even insist upon, the assent which someone must give to another, that is: one can extort and compel it from him.",[]],"blomberg-s08-145":["The mathematician, whose propositions are resplendent with sufficient grounds and are proved, can always speak imperiously, he can with right demand of me assent to his propositions, and he who notwithstanding would still remain *in suspenso*, and still always want to doubt whether he should accept these propositions etc., or on the other side reject them, would always make himself ridiculous, and show that he does not even know what mathematics and its properties are.",[]],"blomberg-s08-146":["Notwithstanding this, however, although assent does not thus at all depend, or *depend*, *immediate* immediately on the choice of human beings, it nevertheless often does *depend* on it *indirecte*, mediately, *mediate*, since one does, according to one's free pleasure, seek out those grounds which could in some way and manner bring about an assent for this or that cognition which cannot insist on the *consensum aliorum debitum*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-147":["When assent does not arise immediately through the nature of the human understanding and of human reason, then it still always requires a closer *direction* of choice, of pleasure, or also in general of our free will, upon the grounds of proof.",[]],"blomberg-s08-148":["To be sure, it is certain that in the human soul an arbitrary *direction* of the powers of the mind takes place upon that which we like to see and wish for.",[]],"blomberg-s08-149":["We give our assent at once to a cognition that is very pleasing and agreeable to us, without our even taking the trouble to investigate more closely the proper and true grounds of taking to be true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-150":["When something is very considerable for us, and of very great importance, so that a great, indeed the greatest part of our peace of mind and of our outer well-being and happiness depends on it, then the mind is in this already not free enough to consider the matter indifferently and impartially also from both sides, the grounds on one side, and the grounds of the opposite on the other",[]],"blomberg-s08-151":["side to weigh, to hold the importance of all these two sorts of grounds properly against one another, and to make the right pronouncement of preference; but the mind, indeed even often our understanding, are as it were fettered and *restricted*, so that we at once easily *approve* that, and confer our assent on that, which is advantageous to us. and on the other hand soon reject and *disapprove* that which could redound to our harm or to our mortification.",[]],"blomberg-s08-152":["In the *suspensio judicii*, finally, that is, in the withholding of our assent, one properly undertakes, as we have already noted above, altogether no action at all.",[]],"blomberg-s08-153":["A judgment is dogmatic when one either in fact accepts, or affirms, a cognition put forward, or on the other side rejects and denies it. but when on the contrary one does not pass one's judgment, but withholds it, and thus neither affirms anything nor denies anything, then this judgment is skeptical.",[]],"blomberg-s08-154":["The skeptical in judging, where one passes no *decision* at all, settles nothing, but leaves everything unsettled, and harbors doubt about all that of which one can assert nothing certainly, until [496/7] one has proper grounds of proof either to accept or to reject a cognition. This cautious and careful procedure in judging, where one takes great care to affirm straightaway something of which one is not quite reliably convinced, and to deny nothing whose impossibility, or not-being, one does not quite distinctly have insight into, this procedure is actually most commensurate with distinct truth, and most advantageous. provided only one does not have in it the constant resolve never to distinguish anything, to doubt everything forever, and to leave everything undecided. Hence it comes, then, that a true philosopher is very much *inclined* not to squander his assent unnecessarily everywhere, but always to withhold it, until he has sufficient and fully sufficient grounds of the truth. but as soon as he has adequate grounds of the truth of a cognition, and himself has insight into its validity, he no longer asks at all, rather it is his habit then at once to accede to the cognition, and to recognize it as true, indeed to assert it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-155":["The insatiable desire, prevailing in many a so-called philosopher or *dogmaticus*, to decide each and every cognition at once at first glance without the least investigation, that is, either to accept or to reject it, has misled so many, and is always the surest way to endless errors.",[]],"blomberg-s08-156":["One of the foremost causes, however, which very often lead human beings to the passing of a false judgment, or also to an error, are further the *affects*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-157":["These perverters of the human being, which cause so much disorder in common life, are no less in a position to confuse the understanding of the human being and to lead it to errors. These affects of the human being, however, steer the grounds of certainty of a matter merely to the one side, and do not permit him also to consider the other side of it. Hence we can indeed blame someone who has given assent to a false cognition; when, namely, the fault actually lies in himself, namely that he rejects those grounds which could have convinced him of the object of the cognition which he has, and thus freed him from his error.",[]],"blomberg-s08-158":["Mathematics, and pure and immediate experience, are alone of the kind that they leave us no grounds for the opposite.",[]],"blomberg-s08-159":["Each and every other cognition, however, is on the other hand of the kind that it very often shows us just as great and important grounds of proof for asserting the opposite of a matter as it offers us proofs for accepting the matter. It is therefore certainly an actual prudence to know how to withhold one's assent in most cases so long until one has sufficient grounds for the matter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-160":["Deception is general in almost all matters, hence this is in fact an actually secure, indeed in many cases infallible way to avoid many delusions of reason, and to be free of error. Nevertheless, however, this withholding of assent must indeed not be so constituted that one is at the same time inclined never to let oneself be convinced of the matter, and always to refuse it one's assent, for in this way we indeed thus never settle anything, thus remain ignorant in all things.",[]],"blomberg-s08-161":["The withholding of assent, namely with the named inclination never to settle anything, is actually nothing other than a lazy doubt, a lazy addiction to doubt, or at least the way to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-162":["The *suspensio* is to serve only to accept nothing so long until one has sufficient grounds, but not on that account at once to reject altogether each and every hope of ever being able to attain to complete certainty of a matter or of a cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-163":["Reflecting is distinguished from investigating, and investigation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-164":["To reflect means to compare something with the laws of the understanding. To investigate, however, properly means to reflect mediately. of many things we can well cognize without investigation what is true and what is false. Reflection, however, is on the other hand always necessary for every judgment, and indispensable for the distinguishing of the true from the false, be it in general or in a cognition etc., in all individual cases.",[]],"blomberg-s08-165":["The word *Pr aejudicium* has a double, or twofold meaning, namely",[]],"blomberg-s08-166":["1 *mo* it signifies a provisional judgment, which, however, is not yet an actual prejudice at all.",[]],"blomberg-s08-167":["2 *do* It signifies an actual prejudice. e.g., when one already esteems the writings of a man, before one has even seen them, far more highly than the writings of another, then this is not yet a proper prejudice, but only a provisional judgment, where reason concludes something from certain grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-168":["It thus properly decides nothing at all here as yet, but it judges only merely from the few grounds which it has of the matter, according to these grounds it thinks, and represents something to itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-169":["This judgment is thus by no means dogmatic, but it is merely still always combined with a certain postponement.",[]],"blomberg-s08-170":["This provisional judgment, namely, is no ground for already holding something certain, not even in the least. meanwhile, however, this much is certain, that in the present state more grounds are present for accepting the matter than for rejecting it and for asserting the opposite of this matter. One therefore easily praises a book which is supposed to derive from a learned, famous, and well-known man, even before one has first read it, merely because one represents to oneself that a quite famous man must write nothing but things worthy of his fame, so this book too will likewise be well written.",[]],"blomberg-s08-171":["There has never been an inventor in the world, and there has been none who invented something, who should not at the same time have passed a provisional judgment about his invention and the invented thing. He was not certain of the matter, but the judgment paved the way for him to attempt and to experiment. e.g., miners judge, from the strata in the earth, where they are to find the metals, and keep conjecturing so long until they have obtained certain knowledge of the metal mines, which no longer deceives them, and in accordance with which they reach their end.",[]],"blomberg-s08-172":["Cognitions, however, arise in us either through the cognition of the object, or through and by means of the properties which belong to the subject, these are subjective grounds of cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-173":["A subjective ground of taking to be true prior to all reflection, as the action of the understanding necessary for it and exceedingly useful, can be called prejudice.",[]],"blomberg-s08-174":["Inclination is a *disposition* of the mind to love something, and to be taken with it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-175":["Inclinations are very seductive; to overcome them steadfastly is certainly the greatest art, indeed almost impossible, and so it is also in logic both with the *argumenta ab utili* and also with the *argumenta ab odio*. and indeed this obtains especially when this or that matter entangles itself in an interest of human beings, be it near or remote, general or particular, and has a particular share in it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-176":["Those prejudices which arise from inclination are manifold.",[]],"blomberg-s08-177":["A further subjective ground of taking to be true is also imitation, to which the human being is accordingly thus very inclined by nature, and the judgments arising from it extend almost over the entire life of human beings.",[]],"blomberg-s08-178":["The spirit of imitation is, among all that can only harm and be contrary to the philosophical spirit, always actually the worst. Imitation is the formation of one's understanding, of one's will, indeed of one's choice, according to the example and *exemplum* of others; when, namely, one is not skilled at thinking for oneself, then one takes one's refuge in them, and one copies them quite faithfully, as the painter the original, except that this copy often becomes very unfitting, indeed comical.",[]],"blomberg-s08-179":["One thing, then, is very often contrary to the spirit of taking cognitions to be true which does not want to err, habit, which very often conflicts with the understanding, but when it is once rooted in, is only with difficulty to be eradicated.",[]],"blomberg-s08-180":["Those people, however, are very precipitate who blindly confuse a provisional judgment with a deciding judgment. A certain *suspensio judicii* is therefore very necessary and useful here, so that one is not precipitate. This, namely, teaches us merely how we are to regard apparent and still far from sufficient grounds, which we still far from hold to be very convincing grounds, and it consequently keeps us in many cases from many wrong paths and errors in cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-181":["The so dangerous enemy of human beings, of which we have already spoken above, is one of the greatest causes of overheating. A calm human being, who is not brought into disorder, confused, heated etc. by any violent emotion, will not be so quickly and so easily precipitate as one who is in *affect* and is irresistibly carried away by it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-182":["Meditation, however, is an important, very great, and secure way, if not to extinguish the affects completely, yet to still them, to prevent their dangerous consequences, and thus to avoid errors. one allows and takes, namely, the time in this to investigate, and to inquire well, whether something be true or false, or not, whether one could accept the opposite of it, or not. and by this way, where one calmly meditates on the matter, one thus very soon becomes convinced of the truth of a matter or cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-183":["Philosophy likewise also strives very much to tame and to eradicate the affects as much as possible. Hence learned people and philosophers can then actually keep their affects very much in rein, so that they do not easily let themselves be taken with a matter without first having sufficient grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-184":["They rather weigh everything which they choose for themselves as the *objectum* of their considerations with cold blood, that is, with calm mind. further consider carefully in every matter both the one and also the other side and in this way come best, most easily, and most securely to the right cognition of the truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-185":["Through provisional judgments we seek to help ourselves to closer conviction, but we judge already before we have considered more closely and cognized the matter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-186":["One does not meet with the truth without seeking it out, but even without seeking it one must already pass judgments about that way on which one thinks to meet with it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-187":["We never attain to complete certainty other than by means of investigation; but before all investigation a provisional judgment must still precede.",[]],"blomberg-s08-188":["Between a provisional judgment and prejudice there seems to obtain, and actually to be, only a small difference, since both judgments are such judgments as always still precede the investigation of a matter or of a cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-189":["The provisional judgment, however, as a means to investigation, connected with the consciousness and the resolve thereby to meet with the ways to investigation, not only befits our understanding in the highest degree, but is also a secure, indeed infallible way to attain to the truth of a cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-190":["Without it actually no important, no invention of consequence has taken place.",[]],"blomberg-s08-191":["But when one judges and accepts something before investigation, with the resolve to undertake no closer investigation at all of the whole matter any more, but already to let it rest there altogether, then such is an in fact culpable prejudice, which is actually not only not conducive to the cognition of the truth, but even obstructive and harmful.",[]],"blomberg-s08-192":["The former judgment, namely a cautious, provisional judgment, yet with the resolve to investigate the cognition more closely in order after all to attain to the truth, is thus actually useful to the truth, and also afterwards *directs* the investigation which one then actually undertakes of the cognition. The essential of all prejudices, however, is that they are universal rules for judging.",[]],"blomberg-s08-193":["It is after all no prejudice at all when one already accepts a proposition without extensive investigation, but one accepts this proposition through a prejudice. One therefore has the two states and particular properties of the human understanding:",[]],"blomberg-s08-194":["1 *mo* To have a prejudice or to be occupied with it, and then",[]],"blomberg-s08-195":["2 *do* Not to have a prejudice, but to accept something through it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-196":["Almost everyone, be he who he will, considers the fashions and customs of his land, or fatherland, the best and most fitting. among us, e.g., we bare our heads, among the Turks, however, not, we therefore hold these to be rude, although it is not yet settled whether it be a rudeness to cover the head or to bare it. The Turks perhaps in turn hold us to be impolite. This, however, is actually not a prejudice itself, but only something which is accepted through a prejudice.",[]],"blomberg-s08-197":["All prejudices are not grounded on reflection. This is lacking to them, for otherwise they would be actual judgments of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-198":["Reflection, of which we have already spoken above, is nothing other than the comparison of a cognition with the laws of the understanding and of reason. we are, however, not merely *directed* by the laws of the understanding and of reason, but also very often by the inclinations, or by our taste. in the inclinations no reflection at all obtains.",[]],"blomberg-s08-199":["Prejudices, however, have very many and various sources.",[]],"blomberg-s08-200":["Habit is likewise one of the causes and grounds for judging without reflection. Without one's having an inclination to something, one nevertheless very often already takes it as true from mere habit. Hence when one wants to cleanse someone of his errors, one must not only convince him of the opposite of what he believes, but also slowly and gradually with time accustom him to it, and leave him time to accept the truth. With the most convincing, most evident, and most secure proofs, he who has long hovered in error and been taken with doubt will nevertheless not be able at once and all at once to be brought so far, and thus convinced, and rightly overcome by the irrefutability of the proofs, that he would at once let all doubt go, and resolve and be minded to concur with the truth at once without hesitation, but for this a long time is very often required.",[]],"blomberg-s08-201":["There are, however, the following 3 sources of prejudices, which are the most usual and greatest",[]],"blomberg-s08-202":["1) Imitation",[]],"blomberg-s08-203":["2) Habit",[]],"blomberg-s08-204":["3) Inclination",[]],"blomberg-s08-205":["In youth one does not yet have any facility in judging, hence one then easily lets oneself be carried away by imitation, and soon accepts as certain and undoubted that which others, either in whom we place a particular trust, or who stand in particular esteem with us, assert, or also what the general *votum* of many prescribes to us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-206":["If now to this imitation there is further added the habit of judging thus and nothing else, then this prejudice becomes all the stronger, and gains more and more the upper hand.",[]],"blomberg-s08-207":["A child, e.g., certainly always accepts the first impressions which occur upon its mind for the most part very easily, and as the greatest and most important truths, indeed it also retains them the very longest.",[]],"blomberg-s08-208":["According to the given rules of the laws of *imaginatio*, such prejudices are indeed, to be sure, not easily to be got out of the soul once they are rooted in it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-209":["Here, then, properly no reflection at all obtains as yet, but one accepts straightaway what others have asserted before us, without reflecting oneself whether it be rational to accept it, or not.",[]],"blomberg-s08-210":["Hence it comes that when false concepts, images, representations etc. have been instilled in children in their tender youth, then these are afterwards not at all to be eradicated from their *imaginatio*, or imagination, and one thus has great cause to proceed very cautiously in this, and to take care that one not corrupt the understanding of children before the time, and in a certain way make it unusable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-211":["Habit is further as it were a very productive source of many pernicious prejudices. when one has often undertaken an action, has, so to speak, fared well with it, and has thus once become accustomed to it, then there arises [526a] finally with the sequence of time from it a certain kind of necessity to act thus, and not to proceed otherwise.",[]],"blomberg-s08-212":["To dress fashionably (yet according to one's station) is praiseworthy and good, for it is after all always still more tolerable, and better, to be a fool within than one outside of fashion. But to judge, to infer, to think, to write in turn as it were according to fashion is always silly, and a proof of no reflection at all. Ceremonies, ways of dressing, compliments, yes, but not the understanding, let themselves be brought under a fashion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-213":["Further, inclination is also a cause and ground of many judgments, and indeed one judges in this, and infers that something is good, agreeable, and perfect, because it charms us, because it is agreeable to us and commensurate with our taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-214":["Still more this inclination also occasions that we always undertake examinations and investigations only from one side, and indeed only from the side where we wish that it were so and not otherwise, and thereby leave the other side, which might perhaps put grounds of the opposite into our hands, wholly uninvestigated. we indeed even seek thereby to find grounds by the help of which we can demonstrate the opposite of what we wish. Here, likewise, the laws of the understanding and of reason are as it were absent and not at home, there is no reflection of one's own there at all, but rather only certain sensations take place thereby.",[]],"blomberg-s08-215":["All prejudices arise from imitation, habit, or inclination, and without these three sources no prejudices would ever take place in the world, which are nonetheless so very often such a hindrance to truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-216":["Everything that is immediately certain needs no investigation at all. All cognitions must be reflected upon, that is, they must be compared with the laws of the understanding and of reason, and held up against one another. but not each and every cognition may be investigated, for to investigate means nothing other than to compare something mediately with the laws of the understanding and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-217":["All cognitions whose agreement with the laws of our understanding and of reason takes place immediately are indemonstrable: these can be accepted without investigation, they may not be proved, indeed one cannot even undertake an investigation of them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-218":["One is in the proper sense not in a position to do it. A prejudice, however, is nothing other than a certain universal ground for judging without any reflection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-219":["A cognition, however, that is accepted merely by means of a prejudice is surely not yet in the least a prejudice itself: if we want to speak properly, then we must understand it thus, there are properly",[]],"blomberg-s08-220":["actually only a few prejudices, but on the other hand infinitely many errors arising from these existing prejudices.",[]],"blomberg-s08-221":["A prejudice always arises when one judges without reflection, i.e., when one already judges about a thing or its cognition without, however, having previously compared this cognition with the laws of the understanding or of reason. Prejudices, therefore, can properly not lie at all in the defects or faults of our understanding and of our reason, but rather they must arise from other powers.",[]],"blomberg-s08-222":["The understanding and reason themselves, left to themselves alone, never err, so much is undoubtedly settled and certain, for this would surely be an evident *contradictio in adjecto*, as we have also already remarked above, they would surely then have to conflict with their own powers, and surely no power can conflict with itself, but rather it comes merely from this, other foreign powers mix themselves with the powers and laws of our understanding and of reason, from which then naturally a mixed effect, and precisely from this in turn an error, arises.",[]],"blomberg-s08-223":["Prejudice can, first of all, not possibly arise from the laws of the understanding, and correct itself, for it is surely a judgment without reflection. That, however, which is accepted by means of a prejudice is not for that reason yet always false as to its matter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-224":["Prejudices, however, are further also not exactly wholly necessary sources of errors, but so much is nonetheless certain, that each and every cognition which is accepted through a prejudice is already in itself always false as to its form.",[]],"blomberg-s08-225":["Through a cognition a certain intended end can often very well be attained, but the method of attaining this end can very often not be the genuine and right one. and so it also goes very often with the actions of human beings.",[]],"blomberg-s08-226":["Any human being can, if only he wills it, very well take revenge on him who has offended him, or has bid too much, that is, on his enemy, and make him pay for the wrong done to him, but the way and manner of taking revenge, and the form of avenging oneself, is wrong and impermissible: One ought not to be one's own judge. He on whom one takes revenge cannot complain in the least, no wrong whatever has been done to him, but rather one has merely paid him in the same coin.",[]],"blomberg-s08-227":["But it is not at all the proper manner of avenging oneself on someone. From it there would only arise such great disorder that it would finally become almost wholly incurable. Rather, divine and human laws prescribe to us the rightful ways by which we can attain to the replacement of our damage, honor, etc., without needing to be ashamed of the way in which we have attained to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-228":["Just so it also goes with prejudice. with regard to the object the judgment and the cognition can very often be very true, but not with regard to the form.",[]],"blomberg-s08-229":["One must therefore take very good care not to reject each and every prejudice immediately and directly, but rather one must first examine them, and investigate well whether there may not perhaps still be something good to be met with in them. one can actually meet in turn with a kind of prejudice against prejudices themselves, namely when one immediately and directly rejects everything that has arisen through prejudices.",[]],"blomberg-s08-230":["In this way one can very often reject the very greatest and most important truths, and thereby innocently deprive of them those who are not capable of and equal to the investigation and inquiry into them. as, e.g., when one accepts what all human beings say, and always holds this to be wholly certain, then this is a prejudice. but it is nonetheless now actually so constituted that very many truths rest merely on the saying of human beings, and have no other ground at all, or can have none, which nonetheless on closer investigation are actually secure truths.",[]],"blomberg-s08-231":["If one now wanted to reject all these truths directly, then many human beings would thereby be deprived of quite good cognitions, who do not possess sufficient capacity and have not enough strength of mind to investigate everything according to the laws of the understanding and of reason, and to examine it with proper reflection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-232":["There are some human beings who are of the kind that their mind is hostile to the true, and that therefore prejudices of this sort take root in them first and most easily, human beings who seek to make suspect every truth that is vouched for by others and firmly and earnestly asserted.",[]],"blomberg-s08-233":["Without any doubt very many useless prejudices are to be met with, but whether for that reason our procedure is already fair and rightful, merely for that reason, and from the aim, because to another a prejudice is perhaps useful, beneficial, or even only not harmful in a certain case, therefore in him not only not to suppress the prejudice but even to generate, to produce, to maintain, to increase it. This is another and wholly different question. This is actually a kind of deception, and a breach of fidelity and honesty, with the aim, that prejudices are useful in this or that case, to generate and sustain them in others.",[]],"blomberg-s08-234":["A prejudice considered in itself can in itself indeed produce some utility, but notwithstanding this, such methods, which one makes use of in order to want to procure and bring forth utility by means of them, are not right, not permitted.",[]],"blomberg-s08-235":["Nevertheless, however, it also always remains true that it would be an evident presumption if one wanted to deprive someone who has a prejudice which is either not wholly harmful to him, or even in a certain respect useful and beneficial, of it directly in a deceptive way, and thus put him into unrest and confusion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-236":["But to strengthen a prejudice in someone is just as reprehensible, thus, e.g., when one proceeds irresponsibly in instruction in morals, then it is just so constituted, namely when, in order to keep the human being from one or another vice, one makes use of an often superstitious delusion in order to keep him from it and frighten him off. That is the so-called *fraus pia* [pious fraud].",[]],"blomberg-s08-237":["Everything true, however, that has been brought forth by means of one or another prejudice is *formaliter* [formally] false, although, however, it can be, and is, *materialiter* [materially] correct. how wretched is the education which seeks to ground true moral rules in youths through prejudices and through errors.",[]],"blomberg-s08-238":["Many prejudices are so constituted, and of the kind, that, if one were to venture to want to attack them, to eradicate them and clear them away, they would nonetheless make the mind of the human being all the more embittered, indeed would deafen it even to the hearing of and attention to true doctrines and important *dogmata*, which have great consequences for them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-239":["There are actually quite comfortable prejudices, with which the human being is quite well off, and it would therefore be a great folly to tear from another such prejudices, which would nonetheless be in a position to contribute so very much to his well-being, and often actually are.",[]],"blomberg-s08-240":["It is otherwise, however, with harmful prejudices, which can have an influence not only on a single case or human being, but rather often even on the welfare of a whole republic. These one can and must certainly always strive, as far as possible, with the greatest diligence to root out, because one is thereby harmful as well to him who has this prejudice, as one also thereby serves and is useful to others, indeed to a great multitude of people.",[]],"blomberg-s08-241":["We must therefore also not despise other human beings if they are perhaps, be it now with or without their fault, very much filled with prejudices.",[]],"blomberg-s08-242":["The common man has and harbors very many prejudices, to which he also very often clings very stubbornly; but it is also just as certain, the scholar, even the greatest, always has just as many of them, and far more dangerous ones still. He too at times holds to his *canones* just as much as anyone else, and does not depart from them at all, as the common man does from his old proverbs, inherited from his forebears and confirmed by a long experience. only the difference takes place, that prejudices in a scholar are hidden under the name of *canones*, but in this one, in the common man, under the name of proverbs.",[]],"blomberg-s08-243":["The author here distinguishes prejudices from one another. He calls some prejudices logical. as if he had in mind to indicate thereby that there are still other kinds of prejudices which are not logical.",[]],"blomberg-s08-244":["Otherwise the aesthetic is commonly contradistinguished from the logical, and therefore prejudices will be, and will be able to be, divided",[]],"blomberg-s08-245":["1.) into logical, and",[]],"blomberg-s08-246":["2.) into aesthetic prejudices.",[]],"blomberg-s08-247":["The former consist in, and take place when, our cognitions the rules of the understanding and of reason, the latter however, on the other hand, consist in this, that our cognitions are contrary to the rules of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-248":["Fashion is properly nothing other than a prejudice of taste, according to which one holds something to be beautiful, or to be ugly, through a prejudice. Fashion is thus the source of aesthetic prejudices. Fashions make one represent to oneself that some inner beauty lies in this or that kind of clothing or costume, one thus thinks, and judges also according to the rules of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-249":["Imitation and habit are the greatest sources of aesthetic prejudices. A frequent approving of one or another object makes in the soul as it were an archetype, with which one is not at all in a position to compare others which look different from this original once fixed, and unimprovable as a model.",[]],"blomberg-s08-250":["If each were to clothe himself according to his own liking and taste, then we would finally have the most excellent kinds of clothing, but now the right taste in clothing is actually lacking, and indeed because fashion, which each follows irresistibly and constantly, reigns therein.",[]],"blomberg-s08-251":["In general all fashion is more harmful than useful, for it is contrary as well to the rules of pure reason as also even to the rules of taste. and he who is, or wants to be, fashionable in his judgments of taste, or even of reason, certainly has no reason at all. He shows thereby his very poor talents, and his complete lack of all taste. Nonetheless, however, it is still far more tolerable to be fashionable in taste than to be fashionable in thinking, in judging, and in sentiments; to be fashionable in clothes is tolerable and often good. But to want to be fashionable in sentiments, that is the actual, logical prejudice. The prejudice of taste, however, reigns not only in clothing, but rather also even in style.",[]],"blomberg-s08-252":["Almost every century has a style, or a form of its own",[]],"blomberg-s08-253":["Now there reigns in the manner of writing a great pomp of inflated words. now there reign anxious contortions of wit, now there reigns again a wholly shallow manner of writing, which one is pleased to call natural, because it is easy, costs little or no effort, and can be brought forth without art. Now one again loves in all things such a manner of writing as one was properly accustomed to use in all letters. Now one again loves above all a difficult, dark, riddling, and enigmatic manner of writing, which one prefers to the easy, complete, and distinct one, which is supposed to betray a certain profundity and a deep-thinking erudition of the authors, because one must guess for a long time thereby, and as it were has to solve a riddle, until one comes to guess what the author meant thereby, to be able to discover the proper sense and understanding of the author, and to find what the author thought thereby.",[]],"blomberg-s08-254":["All taste is very much spoiled by imitation, a fruitful source of all prejudices, in that one borrows everything, thinks nothing of a beauty that one might perhaps invent oneself and hit upon, but only what others have already thought up and previously cognized, and has been declared by them to be a beauty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-255":["If therefore each were to strive not always to imitate so much, but rather to strive himself to be an original, then we would certainly soon behold the greatest geniuses, who would be sublime and great in judgments of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-256":["Just so there are also in turn different kinds of prejudices in different fine arts and sciences, as, e.g., in music, in poetry, in painting, in sculpture, etc. Everywhere in every taste prejudices always reign, indeed even in moral taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-257":["Habit and examples do it, and are for that reason the causes why so many prejudices, so many fashions, reign in them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-258":["It has been remarked that the English nation has far more and greater originals than the French and other nations. This, however, also actually comes from the freer and less constrained form of government. For there each is almost his own master, at least of his own genius. he thinks, judges, and acts thus naturally for the most part without being moved in any way by examples, or at least without directing himself according to them, following them, imitating them. Each thus thinks and judges there according to his taste, and therefore there are so few copies there.",[]],"blomberg-s08-259":["Whereas, on the other hand, countries, states, in which a monarchical form of government has been introduced and reigns, are naturally in a position to bring forth no such, or at least not for long so many, *originalia*, for here one commonly directs oneself very much according to examples, which often become the norm in thinking, in acting, and in judging, and indeed usually the magnates of the realm, the great of the realm, and at court, are for the most part the *originalia*, the models, according to which one is accustomed to seek to form oneself and to imitate them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-260":["The author here further divides prejudices into the prejudices 1.) of too great trust, and 2.) of all too great mistrust but both kinds of prejudices are in fact one and the same, and inseparably bound and united with one another.",[]],"blomberg-s08-261":["The cause of the prejudices of all too great confidence, however, is properly nothing other than the desire to extend one's cognitions, but without acuteness and true judgment of that with which one wants to enrich them, and indeed a diligent observation teaches how this for the most part takes place especially in youth, whereas, on the other hand, old age, as that which already has more experience and cognitions than the former, has also already endured more delusions, more often reflects and looks to see whether it is also worthwhile and worth the effort to strive for the attainment of this or that cognition, and whether also it is correct or not. and by means of this thinking to and fro and often repeated doubt it often finally falls into the prejudice of all too great mistrust, and for the most part it thus comes to pass: the older we gradually become, the more faint-hearted and mistrustful we then also become.",[]],"blomberg-s08-262":["We give, however, in the prejudice of all too great mistrust to some persons a certain precedence over us, we must thus attribute to them more prudence, more erudition, etc., than to ourselves, and therefore choose them for ourselves as originals, according to which we strive to form ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s08-263":["People of rank are for the most part of the kind that one is taken in by very many prejudices toward them and with regard to their skills and qualities. one always holds them to be prudent, learned, sublime, noble-minded, etc. even clothing often makes one learned and prudent; a person in braided clothes even awakens respect, he seems at once to possess more understanding than others, although he nevertheless very often sits in the greatest shadow of darkness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-264":["That which a man of rank says is often far sooner perceived than what a common or poor man brings forth. although the latter is very often more prudent than the former. There is further a prejudice of the all too great multitude, where one accepts and persuades oneself that many persons are more and better in a position to judge something than few. This is the common and lowest prejudice of prestige.",[]],"blomberg-s08-265":["Hence many a one is frightened when for the first time he is to give a public speech, lecture, etc., before a great assembly. To speak before 2 or 3 persons he would perhaps not be frightened, but before some 1000 one is afraid. one judges, namely, that what finds approval with all is true, and must actually lie in the *objectum* itself, although such is often very false.",[]],"blomberg-s08-266":["The prejudice of the all too great multitude arises for the most part from the prejudice of imitation. A great number of human beings who all pay attention to me alone, carefully notice me alone, must necessarily have something venerable for me.",[]],"blomberg-s08-267":["With regard, however, to morality the prejudice of the multitude becomes very harmful, detrimental, indeed shameful, hence it comes also that the human being even in his actions which have an influence on morals so gladly follows the great crowd; he hopes as it were, when he does something evil, to hide himself among it, and thus to be able to escape unseen the eye of the eternal heavenly judge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-268":["By a great multitude a like judgment can easily be brought forth; no one has so much the spirit of imitation as a great multitude.",[]],"blomberg-s08-269":["If something is already so constituted that it receives the universal approval of the people all at once, as soon as it only appears, and at first sight, then it is always very often to be feared that it is not worth much; and a rational man must actually not only not be gladdened, but rather be very humbled, by a universal and thoroughgoing approval which he receives all at once. Books which as soon as they have only come to light from the press, are certainly for the most part very shallow.",[]],"blomberg-s08-270":["That which is good and in fact excellent, which brings forth correct but new truths, commonly always finds very great contradiction in the beginning, and this lasts until, after closer examination and investigation, after inquiry into and discovery of the right properties and of the difference from other cognitions, one also discovers the beauties of it, and what is especially preeminent therein above other writings, and begins to esteem them highly.",[]],"blomberg-s08-271":["All prejudice of all too great prestige, however, can suitably be divided into three kinds of it. – – –",[]],"blomberg-s08-272":["namely 1 mo) the judgment of the all too great prestige in which others stand with us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-273":["2 do) the prejudice of the all too great prestige that we posit in ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s08-274":["3.) the prejudice of the all too great prestige that a cognition has in us, on account of some perfections which it carries with it, and from which one infers that still more perfections must be met with in it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-275":["As concerns the first kind of prejudices, we are for the most part inclined to ascribe and to entrust more to others than to ourselves. we believe that another knows better something that we do not know. This prejudice arises not only from the consciousness of our own weakness, for this is no ground for attributing more to others than to ourselves, but rather from a certain respect which we have for the whole human race, since we judge that what we lack will nonetheless be met with in others.",[]],"blomberg-s08-276":["Another prejudice arises from a certain laziness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-277":["There is nothing more comfortable than to imitate, and to draw one's cognitions from others. from this prejudice arise the various and manifold citations in books, where one adduces the judgments of others and their opinions of this or that thing, and presents these *citationes* as proofs.",[]],"blomberg-s08-278":["There is actually a certain kind of laboriousness therein, an assiduity, but no difficulty. One must work long, although without a certain exertion of one's powers. This prejudice of the all too great prestige that others have with us thus very much flatters ease. One need not think for oneself at all thereby, but rather can rely wholly on the genius, on the understanding of the other, and merely imitate others.",[]],"blomberg-s08-279":["Yet another kind of the prejudices of prestige toward others arises from vanity. one thereby properly as it were actually subjects oneself to others, one thereby makes oneself the slave of a great one, and of the understanding, of the power of judgment of another. This, however, often happens also only in order to be able to command much over others, in order to have a great dominion over others who are still lesser than we.",[]],"blomberg-s08-280":["Nowhere, however, does there take place a greater bombast, nowhere a greater pride anywhere, than in polyhistory. One namely in it elevates, from ambition and vanity, other great scholars, in order under this cloak to be elevated oneself, to be able to elevate oneself, and thus as it were to elevate oneself far above the so-called learned rabble. One namely presents to the eye of the learned world a great and glorious scholar, and seeks firmly to persuade all others that, notwithstanding all the diligence, work, and effort only possible which they can apply, they would nonetheless always act in vain, since they would never be able to be in a position to become equal to this great man or to come near him, indeed, since one regards the pronouncements of this great archetype as incontrovertible and unimprovable *oracula*, one rejects outright all their opinions, merely because they contradict, or seem to contradict, the judgment of this great man. one frightens, or as it were seeks to frighten off, all others from ever wanting to strive to become equal to this scholar, as if it would be a vain undertaking to strive after it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-281":["Just so it comes, e.g., from this that most proud people, who do not like to be subject to several and to obey; or who begrudge their fellow citizens one or another power, would most like to have in a state the unlimited form of government of a monarch, and also help to promote it, and indeed in order then to be equal to one another, and not to need to fear any of their fellow citizens, and this is in fact the true origin of all monarchies. But there is actually nothing more harmful to the human race than always to present others as an unattainable example, and as it were to take them for oneself as a model of imitation. Thereby one copies more the faults than the good qualities of the original set before one, because everything in the world is nonetheless imperfect, and so these models too cannot be wholly excluded from it. Holy Scripture itself says: we ought never to choose anyone else for ourselves as a model than God, this being most perfect of all in all respects. but always to direct and form oneself according to others, human beings just as highly imperfect as we, this brings about more harm than utility.",[]],"blomberg-s08-282":["Such prejudices of prestige and of imitation, however, are very much grounded by a bad upbringing and inept instruction, namely when for the confirmation of one's judgments of the understanding one adduces, cites, alleges not one's own reflection or experience, but rather the judgments and opinions of others. and although this method, be it in an oral or written presentation, is very agreeable, and a witness to great reading, it nonetheless often grounds only the prejudice of prestige.",[]],"blomberg-s08-283":["Of the prejudice of prestige which one posits in a great multitude of people we have already spoken above. It is to be presumed that what all people accept is universally valid. There lies already in our nature a particular inclination to communicate our opinion to others, as we have already indicated above, so that, if someone were in a desert, and had to stay there without any society of human beings, all his judgments would seem to him to be for nothing and in vain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-284":["This inclination arises not at all from vanity, but rather from a particular and preeminent communicative constitution of human reason. namely when one judges alone and by oneself, then one is never really sure whether the judgment does not arise from a certain deception which could make us believe something or other. Hence one is then eager to examine and investigate it precisely against the understanding of other human beings. one here makes as it were an experiment, and tests whether what we think is universally valid, whether others accept it, or whether it is not in agreement with reason. one thus thereby, so to speak, as it were whets those judgments which one has, i.e., of which one is the inventor, on the secure touchstone of the understanding and the insights of other human beings.",[]],"blomberg-s08-285":["Opposed to this inclination of human reason to communicate its judgments to others is logical egoism, of which we have also already said something above. where one namely believes, and fully persuades oneself, that in a judgment of the understanding one does not need the help of the judgment of others in the least. It is indeed of course true, the judgment of strangers in matters of the understanding judges nothing. but it is nonetheless not for that reason superfluous at all, not yet dispensable. The understanding of the human being is already from instinct *communicatio*. if it is thus communicative, then it must surely also rightly be participative, and also concern itself with what others judge of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-286":["The prejudice of the prestige in which other people stand with us is further the prejudice of antiquity, or the prejudice of novelty, on which the liveliness of wit depends. The prejudice of antiquity is grounded on high esteem for the old. That which remains from the old up to our times always already has the illusion about it that it is good, for one infers: it would hardly have remained and come down to us if it were not good and were worth nothing. Just as we also hold those buildings to be good which, when they are old, nonetheless still stand without having fallen into decay, whereas others have fallen into decay through the length of time.",[]],"blomberg-s08-287":["No judgment is more universal than that the world gradually grows old. everything in the world deteriorates: animals, horses, etc., become worse when they have lived long, bodies, e.g., clocks, become worse with time. and this very judgment one passes on the whole world, in general on the whole of nature, it deteriorates more and more, it gradually grows old. Hence one admires old and yet healthy, robust people, and says: where does one indeed find such a one nowadays among us? Just so it goes also with books which remain among thousands of others that have been lost, one holds them for that reason, and not without all ground, to be preeminently good, because, in spite of the usual decadence which is wont to befall such writings, they have nonetheless maintained themselves in prestige for so long a time.",[]],"blomberg-s08-288":["Young people for the most part incline to the prejudice of novelty. Old age, however, on the other hand, to the prejudice of antiquity. »Just as youth is for the most part wont to be generous, but old age avaricious.« Nonetheless, however, it is of course also true that the cause of these prejudices can also often be the change of temperament.",[]],"blomberg-s08-289":["To the old belong diligence and assiduity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-290":["To the new, however, genius, liveliness, and wit without steadiness. we see of course the individuals of nature fall apart and pass away, all works of art pass away. Rome itself collapses, rocks split, etc. – The human being passes away and dies at every age. up to the age of 15 years half of human beings die, in the 16½th none, in the 50th one among 100. Further, one holds that the perfections of the world itself gradually decrease in all things. Horace says: »our posterity will bring forth a still worse brood than we are.«",[]],"blomberg-s08-291":["but this prejudice is wholly incorrect. only in more recent times has one reflected more and better on this matter than in old times.",[]],"blomberg-s08-292":["That which nature brings forth has such a great constancy that nothing, no time, no age can change it. If one excepts the time before the universal Flood, then after it everything in the world is equally good, equally strong, propagation and the power of propagation just as good, just as possible, although of course a single nation can have some advantage in this over another, to which then of course the particular circumstances of the *clima*, of the means of life, etc., must contribute something.",[]],"blomberg-s08-293":["Of many books one can already see at the first moment when they appear before the world that they will not long be esteemed and highly regarded. These books are like the butterflies, which appear with all splendor, are admired by everyone, but last no longer than over the summer, and soon thereafter are forgotten. Other writings, however, which contain much thoroughness in themselves, are of the kind that they are propagated from one age to another, and always remain in their authority.",[]],"blomberg-s08-294":["From this then naturally flows that one harbors a kind of high esteem toward them, and holds them to be estimable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-295":["When one considers how time sifts out everything bad in the sciences, then it may well be said: how will it one day go with our present erudition?",[]],"blomberg-s08-296":["A living language always changes, but a dead one never, all *autores* are inexhaustible sources of it. the present German poet will certainly appear bad after 100 years. Dead languages have the advantage that they remain eternally beautiful and self-standing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-297":["Our present languages might well never become dead ones, for only Holy Scripture and religion make a language dead. so the theologians are thus the true preservers of erudition, and with them many languages would cease wholly and entirely.",[]],"blomberg-s08-298":["The *corpus Juris* is certainly the greatest, and surest proof of human profundity. The discovery of the Pandects, however, at Naples in the 11th *Saeculo* is the best find which human beings have ever been able to obtain among books. In general, however, the ancients are always inimitable models in manner of writing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-299":["If one considers the old world as a whole, and all individual creatures upon it, then one easily finds that the longer the series of copies subordinated to one another is, the greater in turn is the deviation from the *Originali*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-300":["The first creatures were very perfect, but through generation, and propagation they have become more, and more at least dissimilar to one another. Notwithstanding this, however, one nevertheless finds that no change at all has taken place in the species, and *generibus* of creatures, but rather that everything is still so constituted, as it was at the creation of the world and of all things. It is therefore always a prejudice, when one esteems the old so exceedingly.",[]],"blomberg-s08-301":["Besides this, the high esteem which we have for the old also arises from a certain kind of illusion of our understanding and our reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-302":["We always esteem learnedness highly, even in ourselves we always feel a certain high esteem, an *Estime* toward ourselves, if we are learned.",[]],"blomberg-s08-303":["To the learning of the old belongs, and is required, learnedness. This is a further ground of the prejudice of antiquity. to cognition of the old there often belongs a great learnedness, hence it comes, then, that we hold the old in high regard, and indeed for this reason, because we are capable of having insight into it only through learnedness. This high esteem of the old, however, also often falls upon old *Autores*, because they have written something for the understanding of which learnedness is required. This is a species of an inner deception. one namely *aestimiret* the old and *per consequentiam* also the ancients.",[]],"blomberg-s08-304":["The language of the ancients itself is already a learnedness, from this it follows, then, that that which is written in such a language already acquires for itself a certain kind of high esteem, because for the science of the language alone a certain learnedness",[]],"blomberg-s08-305":["is required, without which one is not in a position to understand it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-306":["Hence so much already works to the advantage of the ancients, one sometimes does not even notice their faults, indeed one is also not in a position to cognize whether there are faults in an old writing, or not. Yet it seems to be quite fair to esteem the ancients highly, full of gratitude, because we are after all only their pupils. we ourselves hope, after all, that we too will one day be called the dear ancients among our descendants, and then also promise ourselves from them the same high esteem, and the same thanks from them, which we now pay to the ancients. The ancients are for us the original itself, and therefore those who are raised above all judging, and above all criticism. What a prejudice, when an author is so constituted that his language is no longer criticized at all, or still judged, but is already assumed to be in itself perfectly beautiful, and incapable of improvement.",[]],"blomberg-s08-307":["If one encounters twisted turns of phrase in a Cicero, Horace etc. and other such *Autoribus classicis* once assumed to be incapable of improvement, then one will not say: Cicero etc. should not have spoken so, he could have avoided a fault if he had expressed it somewhat differently etc. but rather one approves it, and we are permitted to speak thus too, merely because Cicero etc. did it so wrongly. Just so the old poets have the *Licentiam poeticam* on their side in all their faults. Here one sees what prejudices spring from the authority which one has toward antiquity. Hence there are so many champions of antiquity who, as soon as something is invented in more recent times, at once show that the ancients also already knew all of that. that it is thus properly nothing new, but only something sought out from the writings of the ancients and brought somewhat to light.",[]],"blomberg-s08-308":["Here, however, we must ask whether we do not have cause to pass a certain provisional judgment to the advantage of antiquity and of the ancients concerning their cognitions, and insights?",[]],"blomberg-s08-309":["Answer. certainly, the ancients quite certainly had the advantage over us, that at least they had no models set before them, no *Originalia* which they could have followed, and should have copied. Hence they surely had to furnish originals themselves, and become them, whether they wanted to, or not, because they had no other guidance than nature, and their reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-310":["Nowadays, however, through the all too great multitude of *Originalium* nothing but copies are produced, and many possible new *Originalia* stifled.",[]],"blomberg-s08-311":["Another advantage the ancients had with respect to their thinking from the fact that their realms were for the most part all divided into small, very many territories and republics, which were all very free, and did not have to groan under the pressure of a monarchical and strict dominion (for freedom in thinking originates from the free form of government, as we have already taken occasion to remark above) this free state, but one so very advantageous to the expansion of the arts, and sciences, was found especially in the old land of the Greeks. Here one knew of no monarchy but rather everyone followed his own inclination, and governed himself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-312":["This way of life, this form of the state, and of government naturally brought it about that among them such great, inimitable and excellent geniuses, and heads were found, and showed themselves to the world, in especially the fine sciences, and in particular in painting, an Apelles etc. in eloquence a Demosthenes, in poetry.",[]],"blomberg-s08-313":["Even still nowadays we find that in those lands where the spirit of freedom reigns, much, and far more is invented, many more *Originalia* arise, far fewer copies appear, than where a despotic monarchical form of government is introduced.",[]],"blomberg-s08-314":["England furnishes us an example of this, here one still finds nowadays the most, and the best archetypes and models. each thinks there for himself, speaks, writes for himself etc. without choosing a model for himself, and imitating it, wanting to follow it everywhere.",[]],"blomberg-s08-315":["Lands on the other hand where monarchs and individual princes rule despotically, and one has to command over all, produce nothing but copies, and seldom *Originalia*. The court is here for the most part the original, and everyone strives to imitate that which goes on at court. The manner of writing of the court is the manner of writing of all others, (just as one gladly wears the clothes which are customarily worn at court.) This one sees nowadays e.g. in France, there one seldom encounters an original, but for the most part nothing but copies. everything there is fashionable, in clothes as well as in judgments, and writings.",[]],"blomberg-s08-316":["The manner of writing of the English authors is therefore also very easily distinguished from all others. the one always says something other than the other has said. The French, however, on the other hand always write according to one and the very same manner of writing, the one makes use of almost the same turns of phrase as the other. Hence it comes, then, also that one French author is not easily distinguished from the other, and one very seldom encounters among them anything particular, excellent, new and unusual.",[]],"blomberg-s08-317":["Now we come to the prejudice of novelty. This is grounded partly on the provisional judgment which we for the most part pass in favor, and to the advantage of the new. one has cause to pass favorable judgments for the old, but also often for the new.",[]],"blomberg-s08-318":["All those sciences which presuppose an extensive, historical, and correct cognition become ever more perfect in more recent times, for it is known that the more recent learned strive more and more to expand historical cognitions. in empirical cognitions the new always has an incontestable advantage over the old. in sciences, on the other hand, for which a sustained diligence, an everlasting effort is required, the new likewise has a more favorable judgment over the old, and that indeed not because it contradicts the old, but because it often adds still more to it. if, however, one esteems the new highly because it *contradiciret* the old, then this is actually a very harmful prejudice. partly, however, the prejudice of novelty is also grounded on a certain illusion of our understanding, our reason and our fantasy. The old man loves the old, a young person, however, the new. Age makes the human being very inclined to defend antiquity, youth in turn to speak up for novelty. If youth owes its cognition, and all science to the ancients, and has merely learned it from them, but not invented it itself, then it has no merit of its own, and is therefore also very much inclined to invent new sciences, and to add more to the ancients.",[]],"blomberg-s08-319":["Whole nations are often very *inkliniert* toward the old, and others in turn toward the new. The Spaniard, and Portuguese loves the old, the Frenchman on the other hand the new. Sluggish, slow, melancholy, and reflective minds love the old very much. on the other hand lively, and cheerful ones love the new. The former are enemies, the latter however friends of new fashions. The Frenchman is fashionable in clothes, in systems, in medicaments etc. The Frenchman is the first author, and founder of all, and every fashion, also the only one, which he will always remain. he alone has the flightiness, and lightness necessary and requisite for it. The French are therefore too much given to the prejudice of novelty, which springs",[]],"blomberg-s08-320":["1.) from the fact that the new contradicts the old or 2*do* from the fact, when one loves the new because it first retains the old, but also secondly adds something new besides.",[]],"blomberg-s08-321":["Inclination is also a great source of favorable prejudice. To this belongs insinuation e.g. of outward conduct, of the countenance, of the name e.g. with books the title. “A good title sells its book” and indeed according to the present fashion a French light *Legairer* impertinent title e.g. when Beaumelle publishes a book with the title: *Mes Pensées* etc. Should we not esteem highly the old inhabitants of Greece, since in this favorable clime all learnedness arose, as we have said above?",[]],"blomberg-s08-322":["A kind of recommendation, however, which impels to high esteem toward someone, also originates from this, if we, having despised someone, discover, and become aware of something unexpectedly great, a fine insight, a genius that surpasses all our expectation, then we esteem him all the higher for that, we admire him all the more, because we despised him before. so it is e.g. when we hear a woman, of whose insights and skill we have only very poor concepts, speak with great insight, wit etc., we admire her all the more, and our contempt or disdain is thus soon transformed into reverence. Just so e.g. we learn to esteem quite highly, and revere the great sage Epicurus, of whom we perhaps formed poor concepts at the beginning, when we examine more closely his conduct, his speeches, his judgments, knowledge. etc. whoever is fond of considering everything from the worst side, and strives everywhere to seek out the faults rather than the good properties, certainly has a very malicious heart.",[]],"blomberg-s08-323":["With vexation, and aversion one always reads such books where the fame, and the honor together with the good properties of the human race are undermined. It is already proper to human nature, not corrupted by any evil heart, with every writing, with every action, always to assume, and to presume the best, and one also actually always *profitiret* by it rather than that one would suffer harm from it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-324":["Some time ago a dispute arose among some French learned men, some exalted, and defended the old, others in turn on the other hand the new. The former held the old to be an original, and the new only to be often corrupted copies. The latter, among whom Fontenelle also distinguished himself, asserted that the new can often very much surpass the old. The old may often be in a position to bring forth products superior to the new, but often the new too can by far surpass the old.",[]],"blomberg-s08-325":["In eloquence, poetry, sculpture, in painting, in all fine arts, and sciences, then, the ancients have surpassed us. But whether more recent times could not also bring forth better products than the ancients have brought forth? This is a quite different question. Eloquence was e.g. so *excoliret* among the ancients because everyone was permitted to step forth before a people which was free, and he also actually often stepped forth, and spoke.",[]],"blomberg-s08-326":["Here, then, everyone who was minded to deliver a public address before such a public assembly necessarily had to apply all diligence and effort in order to attain his end, to move the people, and to obtain the approval of his listeners.",[]],"blomberg-s08-327":["Question Would it then ever be possible to eradicate, and root out all and every prejudice in the human being?",[]],"blomberg-s08-328":["Answer. It seems almost to be impossible. The human being is already accustomed to have incentives in his soul which he cannot resist, and which run ahead of the judgments of the understanding, and in this way form a prejudice.",[]],"blomberg-s08-329":["To be sure, the human being can learn to distinguish this, what springs from incentives, and what from the understanding, that is actually already very much, and he can then of course no longer, and not so frequently as hitherto, be deluded, and deceived, although of course the propensity to it is still there every moment, but to rid oneself of it entirely and completely, and to free oneself from it, this seems to be impossible, and would never do. Nevertheless, however, all, and every pious fraud, just as in common life in morals, so also here in logic in the application of the powers of the understanding is quite unpardonable, i.e. the aim, and the effort to want to bring forth something true by help of prejudices, this, philosophy always pursues.",[]],"blomberg-s08-330":["A proposition is no prejudice, be it as false as it may. For a prejudice is indeed nothing other than the mere desire to want to judge, yet without the proper acuity or reflection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-331":["How then do our prejudices spring?",[]],"blomberg-s08-332":["The propensity of the mind to judge according to authority, according to inclination, according to custom, according to fashion, that is prejudice. Proverbs are for the most part false, for many of them are of such a kind that one can use them indifferently.",[]],"blomberg-s08-333":["Children for the most part learn Latin in school with great difficulty. whence does that come? From this, that one demands, and wants of them that they should be able to speak pure Latin, when they have hardly mastered the first elements of this language, and the first grammatical rules. all prejudices of which we have spoken hitherto spring, then, either",[]],"blomberg-s08-334":["1.) from a bad upbringing, or",[]],"blomberg-s08-335":["2.) from examples, and their imitation",[]],"blomberg-s08-336":["The prejudice of all too great confidence which one places in oneself is nothing other than egoistry. Egoism, however, is *vel cosmologicus*, when one holds that there is no other thinking being at all, no world at all outside me, *vel Logicus*, where one holds that one alone judges rationally, but no other is in a position to judge anything, or to be able to have better insight. This latter, namely *Egoismus Logicus*, seems at the beginning actually to be permitted. One of course has no need then to *consultiren* other judgments, when one oneself cognizes something correctly, one need not seek to bring about an agreement of others with the cognition which one has oneself. but on closer examination one surely finds that one cannot be certain whether one has judged rightly, or not, if one does not compare one's judgments with the judgments of others, and test them on the understanding of others. For a cognition is not correct when it agrees with my private understanding, but rather when it agrees with the universal laws of the understanding of all human beings. Hence, then, every human being has, as we have already shown above, an inclination to others",[]],"blomberg-s08-337":["to *communiciren*, and impart his judgments. This is the true friendship of *Sentiments*. The logical egoist therefore robs himself of a great, and exceedingly important advantage, also very conducive, and necessary to the enrichment and improvement of his understanding, of being able correctly to distinguish the true from the false *per Communicationem judiciorum, et mutuam dijudicationem eorum invicem*. Logical egoism, however, also sometimes springs from a certain kind of humility, and faintheartedness, where one trusts oneself and one's insights all too little, and believes that one's judgments were not worthy to become known to others in the whole learned world.",[]],"blomberg-s08-338":["To logical egoistry, however, yet another prejudice is opposed: where namely one builds all one's judgments on the reason of others, judges nothing oneself, but merely imitates, in that one namely raises others above oneself, and trusts oneself in nothing. These are, to put it briefly, the prejudices of the lazy.",[]],"blomberg-s08-339":["There are, however, also certain arts, and sciences which are of such a kind that they can, indeed must, be learned through mere imitation. To these belong e.g. all and every craft, likewise mathematics, in which one need only present certain propositions to someone, and properly demonstrate, and prove them, which he grasps in memory, and thereby is in fact in a position to become an actual *mathematicus*. other sciences, however, on the other hand are in turn of such a kind that they cannot at all be learned through imitation, but rather genius is required for learning them. Hither belongs e.g. the whole of philosophy. one is certainly sure of this, that one will never be able to become a true philosopher [619a] if one does nothing more than merely imitate the philosophers of former times, leaf through their writings perhaps once or twice, or read them through, and take that which they have said to be true, and, so to speak, quite forget oneself in doing so. if one thus does not trust oneself in the least, also passes no judgments at all oneself, but swears *in verba magistri*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-340":["Nothing is more harmful than to imitate in philosophy. nothing is more wretched, and more ruinous for the understanding than e.g. to take up perhaps a *Wolffium*, or a *Crusium*, or others, to learn by heart and impress upon oneself their *Definitiones Strictißime*, and according to the words, and to esteem them as stars of the first magnitude, but oneself as nothing, but rather one must learn to think oneself, to judge oneself, *reflectiren* oneself on objects, and, in order to be able to become and to be a philosopher, learn to philosophize. Philosophy, however, cannot be learned in the least through books, but merely through one's own reflection, and one's own meditating.",[]],"blomberg-s08-341":["We have, however, two sorts of prejudices which relate to learnedness. namely a judgment for, and in turn a prejudice against learnedness, and for common sound understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-342":["The learned, and also other common people have a prejudice for learnedness. they often accept everything that has been said, and also taught, by learned people. The moralists, on the other hand, have a prejudice for common and sound understanding, which *Präjudicium* is also proper to many common people. This latter prejudice comes from this, that no one denies himself sound understanding. There has namely never yet been a human being who would have believed he had no *Sens commun*, *Sensum communem*, common sound understanding. That one has a poor, deficient memory, one surely admits gladly and willingly. another in turn admits that he has no quickness in comprehending, no wit, no witty notions, no genius for this, or that individual science, no taste for poetry. but the *Sensum communem* everyone believes himself to possess. This no human being will so easily deny himself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-343":["Now since the fewest of those who have a sound understanding are learned people, and it is after all a known but true proverb: *ut ars non habeat osorem, nisi imperitum*, it is of course not at all to be wondered at if from this it very easily arises that learnedness is by very many, be it now from ignorance, or from envy, if not despised, yet held in low regard, and on the other hand common sound understanding is infinitely exalted.",[]],"blomberg-s08-344":["The author here further adduces the prejudice of the adopted doctrinal system. In almost all parts of learnedness the prejudice of unity takes place.",[]],"blomberg-s08-345":["A doctrinal system is distinguished by nothing other than by this, that there is a unity in it. Human understanding, however, is so constituted that it approves all that in which a unity can be encountered. and from this then of course arises the prejudice for unity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-346":["We believe at all times that we have made everything perfect, or yet as perfect as possible, if we have been able to bring it so far that we have reduced it to a unity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-347":["Just so unity also has the in a certain way ingratiating and pleasing feature that it seems to contain within itself an economy of the powers of our understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-348":["Further, where unity is to be encountered, there one also necessarily finds order",[]],"blomberg-s08-349":["Also many beginners have a certain prejudice for the excellence, and the advantage of a thing which they have learned with much diligence, and labor, and which has cost them much effort, many a bitter sweat. (so to speak e.g. Someone has had a terrible amount of labor in learning the French language, and has had to apply effort. He was, however, in earnest about learning it, and has finally also attained his end, so he has a great prejudice for the inner worth of this thing.)",[]],"blomberg-s08-350":["The prejudice of lazy trust arises from a certain kind of gross ignorance, and sluggishness to attain to this or that cognition, and from a contempt of this cognition springing therefrom, but very ill-placed, and worthy of contempt, merely because one cannot become a partaker of it. So e.g. as the fox in the fable despises the grapes which, notwithstanding all leaps, and effort, he cannot snatch, and scolds them as sour.",[]],"blomberg-s08-351":["In almost all parts of learnedness the prejudice of unity surely takes place",[]],"blomberg-s08-352":["If very many properties, and determinations of a thing are to be derived from one ground, then one is commonly accustomed for the most part to pass the provisional judgment, and to have the presumption, that perhaps even all the remaining determinations of the same thing will be derivable from it, and that thus this one ground which one has of this thing will be almost the true, and completely sufficient ground of this whole thing. One namely sometimes assumes a ground, and then infers from the sequences that this is the only true ground, notwithstanding that such is very seldom completely certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-353":["E.g. if one harbors one or another presumption that this or that human being is a thief, then one often infers merely from the sequences whether this presumption may well be true, or not, but rather false. If one finds e.g. that this or that one who stands in combination with us at first had and possessed nothing at all, but afterwards suddenly squanders very much money, and that this happens at the same time when this or that money went missing from me, and presumably was stolen, then one surely believes that he must be the thief of our money. although this presumption can very often be false and incorrect, indeed even insulting, and sinful. from this provisional judgment arises the prejudice of unity, when namely one finds a ground of a thing which has very many sequences, and determinations, then one derives all the remaining sequences, and determinations from it. so it went, to give an example of it, e.g. quite at the beginning with electricity. one namely at that time explained all and everything in any degree wondrous from it, indeed there were even some subtle spirits who went so far that they even wanted to assert that electricity had an influence on religion, in that namely they e.g. even wanted to endeavor to explain the healing of the woman with the issue of blood by Christ from the powers of electricity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-354":["In general whoever finds in one ground the source of all wisdom and cognition, or at least believes he finds it, is always very inclined to ascribe to it still further more than is actually to be encountered in it. Another believes he has sound understanding, and wants nevertheless also to amuse, and gratify himself; but he is far more inclined to satisfy his inclinations as much as possible, far rather than to take the path of reflection, and of labor, and in this way one often falsely takes an understanding which is neither *Exercirt*, and *Coliret*, for the merely common understanding. for this understanding one can rightly call, in a good sense, a plain understanding. For although one now connects with this expression a bad concept and harmful interpretation, it is yet known that the dear ancients had the proverb: plain and right, and with that good. and here took the word “plain” in a very good sense.",[]],"blomberg-s08-355":["All and every such prejudice, of which has been spoken hitherto, can however be divided",[]],"blomberg-s08-356":["“1) into prejudices of pride",[]],"blomberg-s08-357":["“2) into prejudices of humility, and all too great",[]],"blomberg-s08-358":["“timidity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-359":["The former, the prejudices of pride, arise then, when one attributes all too much to one's insights, knowledge, and judgments.",[]],"blomberg-s08-360":["The prejudices of humility and of timidity, however, spring from this, that one attributes all too little to one's judgments in general, or powers of understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-361":["Often there springs up in us even a prejudice against, but also against a whole science, and indeed from this, because it has perhaps often erred, and we have exactly noticed, and taken heed of its faults. So it has gone e.g. with *Metaphysica*, and when it so goes with many sciences that they thus often err, then from this springs a kind of skepticism, mania for doubt, which could often spread itself over all sciences. This, however, comes from weak understanding, which does not like to be laughed at, although it is itself not in a position to judge the knowledge, and sciences. Such people commonly believe little or nothing at all. and this prejudice of mistrust, which originates from experience, arises unfortunately only very often among human beings.",[]],"blomberg-s08-362":["Fashions are nothing other than excitations of vanity or recommendations of the use of a certain vanity, and thus arise through the choice of respected, great, and distinguished men. so e.g. it goes for the most part with style, with *musique*, with architecture.",[]],"blomberg-s08-363":["A thing of vanity is that which rests not on true honor, but merely on the effort to please i.e. on fashion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-364":["Much e.g. which actually at present counts as the enduringly beautiful in our manner of writing is nevertheless nothing other than the fashion of our time. Very many learned and fine-thinking minds are actually often more harmful than useful to the learned world in general.",[]],"blomberg-s08-365":["A Young, Klopstock, Gleim etc. e.g. have actually ruined a multitude of weak heads.",[]],"blomberg-s08-366":["The ignorant man for the most part imitates the faults, but not the beauties, of the original which he has before him, only in order to want to become like a person who is in a position to cause so much sensation through this, or that book, poem etc. So it would be e.g. if many a man or many a woman seeks to imitate the lisping of a beautiful person, and thereby becomes ridiculous.",[]],"blomberg-s08-367":["Sometimes a fault helps to let the wit, the talents etc. of a human being, which are otherwise not very considerable, nevertheless shine forth and glimmer.",[]],"blomberg-s08-368":["Fashion introduces clothes, manners, indeed even often morals, and it is in a certain way almost good to follow fashion, if only its customs do not conflict with the eternal, and unalterable laws of morality, for human beings are as it were by help of it in a certain way better united, and made concordant.",[]],"blomberg-s08-369":["The prejudice of shallowness, further, is the same prejudice of which we have already spoken above, namely the prejudice for sound understanding. For this is shallow in the object of learnedness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-370":["Voltaire is certainly one of the greatest defenders of shallowness. he namely seeks to derive almost everything merely from sound reason, and his judgment sometimes also has no sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-371":["Sound reason is not so much to be rejected as one may well think, it is often actually the source of many cognitions",[]],"blomberg-s08-372":["Hence it is then of course also, on the contrary, in turn a prejudice, when one rejects the *Sensum Communem*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-373":["Further there is also yet a certain prejudice against sound human reason, and also a prejudice for sound human reason. in the former prejudice namely one believes one can have insight into nothing at all, in the other case, however, on the other hand into everything by means of human reason. in the former case, then, one trusts human sound reason too little, in the other case, however, one trusts and ascribes too much to sound reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-374":["One must by no means despise the human race, want to hold all its prejudices worthy of nothing, and despise them, but rather leave to it the advantages that are its due. It is true, one cannot have insight into much through reason, (namely through common sound human reason) nonetheless, however, it is surely not on that account to be rejected, but rather always has its worth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-375":["The author further speaks at the end of this *Paragraphi* of the prejudice against antiquity, and also of the prejudice against novelty. but of both these prejudices it has already been spoken above.",[]],"blomberg-s08-376":["The prejudice against the old namely is always combined with the prejudice for the new, and in turn every prejudice against the new is on the contrary always combined, and inseparably united, with a prejudice for the old; and the one kind of prejudices cannot be thought without the other. For whoever loves the old very much cannot esteem highly the new *et vic e versa*",[]],"blomberg-s08-377":["“Every cognition from merely insufficient grounds is uncertain.” If a cognition is cognized from merely insufficient grounds of truth, such that I have insight into far more grounds for than against the thing, then it is apparent. If, however, finally there are more grounds for than against the cognitions, or at least more than half of the grounds for the cognitions (whether I now have insight into these grounds, or not) then it is probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-378":["Apparentness rests merely on the subject",[]],"blomberg-s08-379":["Probability on the object, such that I thereby actually cognize more grounds for the thing than grounds are possible for asserting the opposite of the thing. In apparentness there is actually an error to be encountered, and indeed in the form, since namely one is not conscious of the insufficient grounds. The first rule is therefore: in all, even in true cognitions, let one seek first of all to become conscious of the uncertainty which arises, and could ever arise, from the insufficient grounds, let one thus place oneself as it were in the situation of the opponent, doubter, who believe they have sufficient grounds to assert the opposite of these cognitions; for otherwise one will actually almost always for the most part err oneself, and deceive.",[]],"blomberg-s08-380":["One can never know whether one cognizes more grounds for than against the thing, and cannot learn to have insight into this before one has a cognition of the sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-381":["Hence with all cognitions one must first become acquainted with the sufficient ground, or want to know and cognize how many grounds are actually required for the complete certainty of a matter, so that one can properly distinguish whether something is plausible or probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-382":["If one does not cognize the sufficient ground of a matter, then one also cannot determine how many grounds for the truth are necessary in order merely to make the cognition at first probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-383":["If I subtract the grounds of taking to be true from all possible ones that are necessary and required for certainty, and rightly hold these against each other and weigh them, then the remaining grounds are those of the opposite. Hence I can then quite easily see which grounds, whether the grounds for or the grounds against the cognition, are preponderant. Plausibility can change very much, for its weight is grounded in the subject, but probability always remains unchanged the same, and indeed merely because it is grounded in the object.",[]],"blomberg-s08-384":["The manner of writing is naturally very changeable indeed, and indeed especially because one can today cognize more grounds for, and tomorrow on the other hand again more grounds against, this matter. according as one directs one's thoughts variously upon this matter, and investigates and reflects more upon this or that, and is concerned to seek them out. The ground of assent is always at least the beginning of the sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-385":["It is still a ground, even if it is not complete. An insufficient ground is quite wrongly, indeed even erroneously, held to be no ground at all, it is still a material for further investigation and inquiry. A half-ounce is still always a ground for lifting a pound, although it is not sufficient, for several half-ounces taken together make up a pound.",[]],"blomberg-s08-386":["All our conviction and assurance of any cognition must necessarily go through all such elements. first we only conjecture that something is true. Incompleteness still makes the beginning with each and every matter, whatever they may be. Through inquiry and untiring attention we make something complete.",[]],"blomberg-s08-387":["True *sagacitaet* is always, as it were, a spying out, a scenting of the truth already in advance. it is one of the foremost talents of good minds, and of meditation. so it goes e.g. with the discoveries of the world.",[]],"blomberg-s08-388":["Grounds of conjecture have often been, with these, causes of inquiry, and causes of discoveries. Thus was the new world etc. discovered, and thus can still much more be discovered. If we have before us some such insufficient grounds of truth, then we have either a plausibility, (and this is only the least degree), the *probabilitas*, or probability, the *verisimilitudinem*, as a greater degree. The sufficient ground of taking to be true, the unity, is that through which a matter is posited.",[]],"blomberg-s08-389":["No ground can be more than sufficient, for otherwise it is no ground at all, just as nothing can be more than true, more than false, more than good, more than evil, for otherwise it is nothing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-390":["The sufficient ground already comprehends everything in itself, but the concept of everything also contains at the same time a unity. but more than everything I can never say of a matter. Nothing is more than too true. nothing is all too true. nothing is too certain. nothing is more than sufficient, as one nevertheless often falsely expresses oneself in common life. The insufficient ground of taking to be true produces plausibility.",[]],"blomberg-s08-391":["Probability is a fraction, where the sufficient ground of truth is the denominator, but the insufficient grounds of taking to be true which I have are the numerator.",[]],"blomberg-s08-392":["One can actually calculate mathematically the degree of probability or the degree of improbability of one or another empirical matter. so e.g. in each and every game, lotteries, dying of human beings according to the number of years, and many other *augmenten*, as changes of the world.",[]],"blomberg-s08-393":["With our philosophical truths we cognize nothing other than a plausibility, of this or that matter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-394":["We have certain grounds for the truth of the matter, and cognize fewer for the opposite of it, since there is no one there who is in a position to adduce to us more grounds against than for the truth of the matter, our cognition is thus nothing more than plausible.",[]],"blomberg-s08-395":["One formerly held the moon to be only a small luminous body, which was there solely and only for the sake of our earth. but afterwards one gradually began to measure the magnitude of the moon, and one soon found that it was somewhat smaller than our earth, that mountains and valleys take place in it. again some *astronomi* even conjectured that it has inhabitants, but others on the other hand that it has none.",[]],"blomberg-s08-396":["This cognition of the moon was thus very changeable, and just for that reason not even probable, but only plausible, for the rest, what is probable, can not be mutable at all, but always remains one and the same. since all probability is not subjective, but objective.",[]],"blomberg-s08-397":["All plausible judgments on the other hand change daily, according as one cognizes more or fewer grounds for and against the matter. but what is probable is, so to speak, probable for all eternity. For the grounds of probability are indeed of course still insufficient, but nevertheless already greater than possible grounds of the opposite.",[]],"blomberg-s08-398":["But in order to know whether the grounds for the truth of the matter are greater than the grounds of the opposite, or whether the grounds for the cognition make up more than half of the sufficient ground, one must first of all always endeavor to attain a solid and upright cognition of the sufficient ground, as has already been noted above. Mathematics alone is that science which is of the kind that with it we can cognize the sufficient ground of a thing. But with philosophy this does not work at all, hence it also comes about that all our cognitions in philosophy which are not undoubtedly certain can never be probable, but only always plausible. For with probability we judge not what actually is, but only what is possible under certain conditions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-399":["But this possibility remains always unchangeable under one and the very same conditions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-400":["By means of probability we thus actually cognize the degrees of possibility; now if these degrees are great, then a great, but if they are small, then a small probability takes place.",[]],"blomberg-s08-401":["Doubtful, taken *subjective*, is a cognition when the grounds for the matter which I cognize are equal to the grounds against the matter, i.e. to the grounds of the opposite. But *objective* on the other hand something is again doubtful when the grounds for the possibility of a thing, or of a matter, which I cognize, are wholly equal to the grounds of the opposite of this matter, and thus there are as many grounds for as against the matter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-402":["Something can be *subjective* practically certain, while *objective* it nevertheless contributes only very little to the sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-403":["As regards certainty, there are three particular and wholly different genera of it, namely",[]],"blomberg-s08-404":["1 *mo*) the logical",[]],"blomberg-s08-405":["2 *do*) the practical",[]],"blomberg-s08-406":["3) aesthetic certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-407":["Logical certainty is certainty according to the rules of the understanding and of reason and thus an objective certainty, which is grounded in the matter itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-408":["Aesthetic certainty is a certainty according to the rules of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-409":["Practical certainty finally is a certainty according to the rules of free will.",[]],"blomberg-s08-410":["Someone is practically certain when the grounds of taking to be true of a cognition which he has are sufficient to determine him to actions; but if he nevertheless does not want to act, then these grounds are still far from sufficient enough to make him logically certain. To practical certainty there actually belongs in many cases very little cognition, but in many cases again far more is required for moral or practical certainty than for logical. E.g. when it is a matter of damning someone, condemning him to death etc.",[]],"blomberg-s08-411":["Aesthetically someone can be certain when he holds the opposite of a matter or cognition which he has to be impossible, merely because it does not please him. So e.g. when his cognition is to believe in a divine being, eternal governance, future world, reward of good and punishment of evil actions etc. The certainty rests here merely on feeling, according as something brings someone either gratification or displeasure, so he either accepts something or rejects it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-412":["The human mind is in this way actually subject to very many illusions and deceptions, often merely because something pleases us, we hold it to be certain, and merely because something displeases or vexes us, we hold it to be uncertain. But this certainty or uncertainty is not objective, but rather subjective. It can even often keep us from properly seeking out or investigating the opposite of a matter and the grounds for it",[]],"blomberg-s08-413":["We often find more comfort in taking something to be true, because otherwise we would still have to *incommodiren* ourselves if we wanted to take the trouble to investigate the *contrarium* of the matter. we hold the opposite to be impossible merely because we have no desire to want to investigate it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-414":["Practical certainty finally is a certainty according to the rules of prudence and of morality. But the latter is a moral certainty. There have been some who have believed that the moral is far less than the practical. So e.g. one says: it is morally certain that there are inhabitants in the moon etc. but that is not in the least connected with my actions. but if something is of course already practically certain, then it is for that reason often still far from morally certain, and even if something is morally certain, it is for that reason often still not logically certain. The possibility of a thing can produce in us a practical certainty e.g. if someone even only believes that it is possible that there is a God, then this thought must nevertheless already necessarily have an influence on his life, just as if he were completely assured and convinced of this cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-415":["The plausibility of a matter can also likewise produce a greater degree of certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-416":["In the practical sense something can be completely certain even if in the logical sense it is only plausible, or at most probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-417":["The poet cannot become otherwise than merely morally certain of one or another matter. Histories are of the kind that they are only practically certain. Logical certainty is very often lacking and one can easily endeavor to find grounds for the opposite, and to refute historical reports.",[]],"blomberg-s08-418":["One can easily prove that the *historicus* has actually narrated not true histories, but only fables and *fictiones*. There are also actually many such people who occupy themselves with weakening the complete certainty of history, which also is no art, but goes easily, because that which is necessary and sufficient for practical certainty is not *sufficient* for speculation and logical certainty. A practical certainty is that which with respect to certain actions brings about just the same as a complete certainty. e.g. if something of value disappears in my house, and my servant stays away just at the same time, then my practical certainty is there that this missing servant has stolen what is missing to me. although there is still no logical certainty at all there, and the servant may have gone away, got away, from wholly other causes and in another way.",[]],"blomberg-s08-419":["But practical certainty is twofold",[]],"blomberg-s08-420":["1.) the pragmatic and 2.) moral certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-421":["If a cognition probable in itself is just as secure a ground of actions according to the rules of prudence as if it were completely certain, then such is a pragmatic certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-422":["But if a cognition probable in itself is just as secure a ground of actions according to the rules of morality as if it were completely certain, then such is a moral wisdom.",[]],"blomberg-s08-423":["What is even logically improbable can nevertheless be morally certain e.g. if I demand money from someone, and I do not know whether I have already received it or not, and the other, my supposed debtor, just as little whether he has already paid it to me or not: then according to morals I can demand nothing more from him: although it is logically uncertain whether something has already been paid or not? Also of the existence of God a logical certainty can be attained, although such is very difficult. but all human beings can arrive at moral certainty even without great logical *speculationes*. if one only sharpens the moral judgments in someone, then one can thereby easily bring him to conviction of the existence of God.",[]],"blomberg-s08-424":["A logical certainty of a future life is very difficult to attain, but one can indeed attain a moral certainty of it, if one considers that here on earth happiness is not always a sequel of good conduct, hence yet another world is to be hoped for, in which this will take place.",[]],"blomberg-s08-425":["Of moral certainty or uncertainty there must be more said and treated in morals itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-426":["Most, and almost all, *autores* do not know moral certainty at all, but they hold it to be any probability whatever e.g. It is uncertain whether there are inhabitants in the moon, but nevertheless it is always to some extent probable. This some take for a moral judgment, but this does not take place, for such a *judicium* has no influence at all on a conduct. It is a logical probability, and mere speculation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-427":["Nothing is a moral judgment but that which has a relation to my actions. If someone thus is to and must act, and the grounds are merely probable, then the grounds which have the greatest probability make up practical certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-428":["Every ground of the opposite of a matter of which one is conscious is called a doubt. Every hindrance of taking to be true is a logical difficulty, and this is",[]],"blomberg-s08-429":["1 *mo* objective, i.e. doubt.",[]],"blomberg-s08-430":["2 *do* subjective, i.e. scruple.",[]],"blomberg-s08-431":["The latter, that is the subjective difficulty, is actually incomprehensibility, where I do not reject a cognition, but notice that I cannot get on with it. This subjective hindrance of the understanding is a fine work for a philosopher who wants to study the human being. The study of scruples is of great importance.",[]],"blomberg-s08-432":["The study of doubts is already somewhat easier to find scruples in us, before we can say that we cannot comprehend a cognition, is very difficult. Scruples hinder the human being in taking to be true, and can be resolved neither by that human being who has the scruple nor by another.",[]],"blomberg-s08-433":["But scruples can become doubts [672a], namely when one becomes conscious of them, and this is also most necessary.",[]],"blomberg-s08-434":["One transforms a scruple into a doubt by considering the cognition that is accompanied by the scruple in all sorts of different relations, and in different circumstances, whereupon one will then immediately without any trouble find out and notice which is the side on which we cannot yet be completely decided, and which on the other hand is the side where no scruple remains any longer.",[]],"blomberg-s08-435":["Scruples thus in themselves are not at all to be despised, or straightway to be censured and rejected.",[]],"blomberg-s08-436":["But there are scruples subjective, which lie in him who has one or another cognition, and these one must not heed. But the objective scruples which lie in the cognition itself one must seek to make clear. if someone presents to me nothing but plausible propositions, then I have a scruple, and do not yet immediately give it my assent. e.g. I reflect on the proposition: that all moral judgments are supposed to lie in a particular feeling and sensation, I give it assent, but nevertheless a scruple still remains to me, I cannot refute the proposition, and yet I cannot trust the cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-437":["If I reflect on the scruple more closely, and think about the matter better, then a doubt arises from it. I see that, if I accept this proposition as true, it necessarily follows from it that he who does not have this feeling and this sensation must be in no position to pass moral judgments, which nevertheless seldom, or even never, takes place.",[]],"blomberg-s08-438":["But from this doubt something else must necessarily arise again, namely: that I will then make various objections against the truth of the proposition; and upon these objections there follows finally a complete refutation from certain grounds, which otherwise is also merely called an answer.",[]],"blomberg-s08-439":["If I refute the doubt from grounds which only one person holds and accepts as correct, adequate and sufficient: then these grounds as well as this answer hold merely for a single subject, this is thus then a so-called *responsio, et refutatio ad hominem*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-440":["This already happens with a kind of reluctance, and happens merely in order to get one's opponent off one's neck as soon as possible. but if on the contrary I refute the doubt from grounds which hold for everyone, consequently universally valid, but also so convincing and assuring that no one is any longer in a position to object anything against it, then this is an actual categorical refutation, *refutatio categorica* and this happens to the end of actually bringing the truth to light, and clearing the false cognitions out of the way as much as possible.",[]],"blomberg-s08-441":["Scruples often always remain in a cognition, and cannot be cleared away.",[]],"blomberg-s08-442":["The unfolding of scruples is a very fine science. Through it a scruple is drawn as it were out of the dark, and then it must be formally refuted as a significant objection, after it has been rightly placed in the light.",[]],"blomberg-s08-443":["But there is actually no more miserable state of the human being to be thought than the state which leaves us undecided. The state of indecision but especially when it concerns our interest. This state of indecision is still far more burdensome than the state of speculation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-444":["Everything that holds us up and makes us inactive, puts us in a certain kind of *inaction*, is very much contrary to the essential determinations of the soul. The human being would even rather know a future misfortune in advance than remain uncertain in conjecture of it, and hover between fear and hope.",[]],"blomberg-s08-445":["Physicians are often themselves to blame for the cowardice of their patients, they mostly always feed them with empty words, and conditions, imaginings.",[]],"blomberg-s08-446":["Young people especially are mostly of such a kind that they very much dislike being *in suspenso*, for their way is: they always want to get further. but with all this uncertainty there can nevertheless still always take place a certain kind of certainty, namely in this, that all our cognition must be only very restricted and bounded, and consequently only very imperfect and deficient.",[]],"blomberg-s08-447":["For we can always cognize the grounds of uncertainty with certainty and reliability, that is a certainty of subjective philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s08-448":["Settled truths are those truths against which no further objection can be made.",[]],"blomberg-s08-449":["To these belong e.g. the *res judicatae* that is, matters settled *in foro* by a legally binding judgment e.g. so it is after a completed lawsuit a settled matter that when the judge has made a ruling, according to it this one or that one must pay to that one or this one e.g. an assigned certain sum of money.",[]],"blomberg-s08-450":["Each and every new truth always finds at the beginning very many opponents, very much resistance, a certain time must be necessary and required for it, in order to settle them, after one has investigated them better. So e.g. it went at the beginning with the writings of *Cartesii*. they found at the beginning very much contradiction, and 30 and more years passed without the truth of them being settled.",[]],"blomberg-s08-451":["Through this settling of truth no new cognition is actually added to it, but nothing else happens than that all other human beings cease to judge about it and concur with it. So long as there is thus still dispute about a matter, so long as disputes are still always exchanged from this and from the other side, so long it is not at all settled.",[]],"blomberg-s08-452":["We can often have a complete certainty of this or that matter, but the grounds of the certainty are often so constituted that one notices very much uncertainty in the way of inferring. E.g. The soul is immortal: this is completely certain. but the grounds of philosophy for it are not so evident that one should be completely convinced by them. The philosophical form of it is still very uncertain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-453":["Every uncertainty is a ground of a rightful doubt, since I still find it possible to seek out grounds for the opposite of the cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-454":["The consciousness of the inadequacy of a ground for truth serves at least to accept as still possible that there are still grounds for the opposite of the matter. Doubt in the proper sense does not yet take place with the uncertainty of a cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-455":["But the uncertainty of one or another matter makes doubt quite possible, so that one has grounds for the opposite. Often there actually arises from the fact that one has seen and noticed that reason is very often erroneous a kind of enmity toward reason, misology, roughly in the way that misanthropy, enmity toward human beings, which springs from a suspicion against the whole human race, which one draws from the observation of how little justly human beings act.",[]],"blomberg-s08-456":["“That writer and author without doubt deserves the greatest thanks who teaches in his writings that which will hold perhaps in a hundred years,” says Terrasson. The world then still always moves by its prejudices, which the former wanted to root out, until it finally begins to reflect correctly about them,",[]],"blomberg-s08-457":["and with the sequence [685/6] of time also gradually calms down, and grants this.",[]],"blomberg-s08-458":["One also cannot always investigate the grounds of the conflict of speaking. With people who have themselves written great books and works about this or that matter, it is not good and advisable still to dispute about this matter; for one can indeed oneself conclude that these will not so easily be brought to the point of perhaps regarding so lightly, estimating as slight, their own work of many years etc., or of refuting themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s08-459":["In an inner proof one is immediately conscious of the uncertainty in a cognition. But an outer proof, that is, the consciousness of the uncertainty of a cognition, and indeed not immediately, but mediately according to the judgments of others. With many a *question* of reason a plausibility is to be met and found, so that I cannot at all withdraw my assent from it, but if I present it to others, they nevertheless often think straightway the opposite of it, and I then first become aware that according to the minds and insights of others my cognition is still not wholly and undoubtedly certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-460":["In common usage of speech the word doubt signifies every uncertainty, and in this respect and in this sense doubt is either dogmatic or skeptical. The former is a doubt of decision, but the latter is a doubt of retardation, of postponement. From the former springs certainty, but from the latter closer investigation and inquiry, in order thereby once to arrive at the right and undoubted certainty of the cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-461":["With dogmatic doubt we reject all inquiry, and do not accept something against which we have, or believe we have, a grounded doubt. We thus then decide briefly, and say: In this matter it is not at all possible to attain any certainty. Dogmatic doubt thus regards very many cognitions as if nothing at all could be established and settled about them. A *dogmaticus* consequently is called that philosopher who passes the judgment about universal truths of reason that one can bring them to complete certainty and reliability: but does not at all engage in the consideration of such truths of reason as are, or can be, still exposed to one or another doubt. The *mathematicus* e.g. is thus constituted; all his proofs are at once decisive, he presents a proposition positively, proves it immediately *decisive*, so that it is wholly impossible that the judgment cannot take place.",[]],"blomberg-s08-462":["The *methodus dogmatica philosophiae* will thus consist in this, that with all judgments we attain a completely secure certainty, are not satisfied with irresolution, and leave nothing undecided. But through this dogmatic method one will never completely settle the actual certainty of a matter, one will thereby not teach truths, but rather only very often help propagate errors, since with it one sets oneself as it were on the judgment seat of human reason, and pronounces the verdict: that of this or that cognition no certainty at all is to be attained, all inquiry thus is always only wholly vainly applied. so, and in this way, one sets as it were bounds and limitations to the human understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-463":["All reflection, all efforts to procure for oneself a true certainty of something [691a], and to arrive at conviction, is thereby wholly removed, and what else can arise from this than ignorance, indeed even error?",[]],"blomberg-s08-464":["The dogmatic spirit of philosophy is thus the proud language of the ignorant, who like to decide everything, and want to investigate nothing at all: whereas actually our understanding is very inclined to test everything beforehand, and to investigate it exactly, before it accepts and asserts it, also to look around well beforehand, without blindly rejecting something that occurs to us. The dogmatists could be opposed to the dialecticians.",[]],"blomberg-s08-465":["The cognitions are 1 *mo* such whose certainty can be cognized apodictically. These are cognitions *a priori*, which are seen through mere reason. In testimonies an apodictic certainty will never be able to be attained. Apodictic judgments are judgments *a priori*, and such cognitions are *dogmata*, such is e.g. mathematics.",[]],"blomberg-s08-466":["But with cognitions of experience no apodictic certainty at all can take place. It is no pure impossibility that all those who tell us much of the *praxi* in thinking, acting, judging, inferring, nevertheless deceive us, without nevertheless for that reason needing to be evilly disposed. All that which is apodictically certain is capable of a dogmatic, but all the rest actually only of a dialectical, way of cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-467":["The ancients distinguished their philosophers into dogmatists and skeptics, into seekers, inquirers, *speculatores* of the truth, which they always sought, but could never find.",[]],"blomberg-s08-468":["Further there were intellectual philosophers: among whom Plato said: “all that which we want to cognize as good and rightly see must happen *a priori* through reason solely and alone. The senses alone contain nothing but deception.”",[]],"blomberg-s08-469":["The sensual philosophers on the other hand, among whom one reckoned Epicurus as the foremost, said in turn:",[]],"blomberg-s08-470":["“All that which we are in a position to cognize *a priori* through reason is nothing but chimerical. The senses alone give the right true certainty.”",[]],"blomberg-s08-471":["But upon this came a third, and made peace between these two 2 heated disputing parties, by crying out: “I believe both equally little or not at all s",[]],"blomberg-s08-472":["Neither the intellectual nor the sensitive has certainty. everything in the human understanding is nothing but confusion ”. And from this then arose formal skepticism, which seems to originate from so famous and exceedingly estimable a man as Socrates is. For when the dogmatists either quarreled among one another, or also asserted one or another proposition with all *gravitaet* and certainty, he listened to them at first quite humbly, but soon he began, as it were as a teachable listener and pupil of the heated and pathetic dogmatists, to ask them for advice, while they disputed with one another, and thereby he could then so catch them with their own speeches, and knew how to confuse them in such a way, that at last they wholly contradicted themselves, and thus often had to become ridiculous to whole assemblies. This he did so often, and at the right time, that the enemies of the proud dogmatists already rejoiced in advance when Socrates visited their lecture halls, and these already feared him when he began to ask, since they indeed had to answer him, as he knew how to pose as an inquisitive pupil of theirs. From this finally arose a ruinous propensity of his successors to doubt wholly of each and every dogmatic cognition and its certainty. But Pyrrho can rightly be regarded as the actual founder and propagator of the skeptical sect. His *non liquet*, which he indifferently called out against the proud assertions of the dogmatists, and thereby at least often made them very much taken aback, as also his careful and untiring efforts to investigate carefully all propositions of the dogmatists, are known everywhere.",[]],"blomberg-s08-473":["But the *sceptici* have meanwhile, if we rightly reflect on it, procured in a certain way still far more use for philosophy than the proud dogmatists: although it is of course also true that the former at last degenerated into bitter mockeries through misuse. But skepticism, or the method of skeptical doubt, where one places a mistrust in oneself, reflects on the grounds for and against the cognition which one has, and in this way endeavors to come to complete certainty of it, this is the *katharkticon*, the best means of purification of reason. This hinders errors as much as possible, leads the human being to more inquiry, and is the way to arrive at the truth of the matter, (although of course not at once and suddenly, but slowly and gradually through a more and closer testing).",[]],"blomberg-s08-474":["The doubt of postponement is hence actually a more secure mark of the maturity of reason, and of experience in the truth of cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-475":["But if we wanted here to ask which method of philosophizing will be the most fitting and the best at *accademien*, and please most, the dogmatic or the skeptical?",[]],"blomberg-s08-476":["Then we must nevertheless necessarily answer: the dogmatic.",[]],"blomberg-s08-477":["If here a scholar steps up, and establishes something dogmatically about this or that cognition, then nothing can indeed be easier for the listener, he need indeed test nothing, investigate nothing, but only impress on his memory the little that the teacher says and presents to him. He remains in this way in complete rest and comfort, he need only memorize; whereas on the other hand doubt about cognitions is far from so *commode*, but rather far more disquieting, and requires one's own reflection and investigating.",[]],"blomberg-s08-478":["Doubt can thus be divided",[]],"blomberg-s08-479":["a) into the *accademic*",[]],"blomberg-s08-480":["B) into the skeptical.",[]],"blomberg-s08-481":["The school of Plato, the great *dogmatici*, was called the *accademie*. *Accademic* doubt is very much distinguished from skeptical.",[]],"blomberg-s08-482":["1.) “The *accademic* doubter proved and demonstrated *per definitionem* that actually nothing could be proved, nothing defined, which was nevertheless a *contradictio in adjecto*, and this was the dogmatic-seeming doubt.”",[]],"blomberg-s08-483":["*2 do* The Pyrrhonian, i.e., the skeptical doubter, on the other hand, said: to all, and each, or at the least to most of our judgments there can always be set against, and opposed, another judgment which asserts exactly the opposite of what is contained in the former judgment; this was thus actually to be regarded more as a kind of very fine and excellent observation than as a reprehensible doubt. and it would actually be to be wished that one had made this observation correctly, and carefully; very advantageous sequels would certainly have arisen from it for the whole of human cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-484":["But how? Is it not a *contradictio* to establish something in the case of an error, and to be a dogmatic doubter?",[]],"blomberg-s08-485":["Answer. Both can very well subsist with one another. Dogmatic doubt is always combined with persuasion of the certainty that of this, or that cognition absolutely nothing is to be established, or to be asserted.",[]],"blomberg-s08-486":["Dogmatic doubt thus consists in nothing other than this, that one judges that one could never, with a cognition, attain to complete certainty of it and all inquiry would thus henceforth always be employed in vain, and for nothing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-487":["Skeptical doubt, on the other hand, consists in turn in this, that one is conscious of the uncertainty in a cognition, and is therefore driven to inquire into it ever more, and more; so that one may finally nevertheless, by the help of careful investigations, attain to certainty. The former, then, the dogmatist, rejects certainty entirely all at once. The latter, the skeptic, however, seeks it little, by little. The origin of the expression skepticism is properly the following σκέπτεσθαι. This word means in Greek: to inquire, *scrutari, investigare, indagare*. The *scepticus* thus always inquires, he tests and investigates, he places a distrust in everything, but never without ground. He thus resembles in this a judge who weighs the grounds both for and against the matter, and hears both the plaintiff and also the defendant, before, and ere he decides the matter, and passes a judgment. He postpones his final judgment very long until he ventures to settle something completely. These were the old, and pure properties of *scepticismus*, and of an unadulterated skeptic.",[]],"blomberg-s08-488":["In the beginning skepticism was actually very reasonable, only the pupils spoiled it, and brought it into such ill repute.",[]],"blomberg-s08-489":["These latter were so subtle that they even went so far, and said: everything is uncertain, even this is uncertain, that everything is uncertain. That was actually a kind of purgative for human reason, which was of such constitution that, after it has wholly cleansed our understanding of all impurities, i.e., of all false delusion, prejudices, incorrect judgments, entirely, it carried itself out again.",[]],"blomberg-s08-490":["But nothing in fact is more harmful to the learned world than a universal calm and agreement, and peace; at least this always hinders an improvement, and increase of learning, it is with this about almost just the same as with the English, who, as soon as the court stand in proper agreement with the subjects, and are completely at one with each other, already hold there to be danger and believe they have lost all their freedom.",[]],"blomberg-s08-491":["A universal resolution to doubt everything is of no use whatsoever; it is entirely absurd, but there are also few, or we could even well say no human beings, who would be inclined to so childish and harmful an addiction to doubt.",[]],"blomberg-s08-492":["True skepticism, however, is certainly of great usefulness. and as such it is nothing other than a precise careful investigation of all apodictically alleged *dogmata*, which, insofar as they actually are such, and thus hold up, only after this testing shine forth in their whole *valeur*, in their whole strength, and strike the eye.",[]],"blomberg-s08-493":["All skeptical method is a true investigating of truth through postponement, in that one names, or rejects nothing at once, but first allows disputation about it. By our nature we are properly not at all inclined to postponement. in our times one calls skeptics partly such people as do not in the least deserve the name of philosophers (e.g., a Voltaire) partly such people as are not actual *academici*, but merely have a skeptical method about them, and as it were affect it, e.g., a Hume. among the new skeptics, however, one can count Bayle etc.",[]],"blomberg-s08-494":["Thus with us now the name of a skeptic is mostly regarded as something hateful, for one thinks of such a one as does nothing, applies himself to nothing other than that he endeavors to overturn the most undeniable, and most certain truths; one thus actually takes him for a dogmatic doubter, who believes he is in a position to judge about nothing, and one could attain of no matter a complete certainty that nothing can overthrow. Formerly, however, an upright skeptic signified nothing other than such a one as only postpones his judgment until he has had opportunity, and time to take this, or that matter more closely into consideration, and only then ventures to infer thereupon whether a cognition is to be taken as true, or rather to be rejected as false. To the dogmatic method the skeptical is directly opposed.",[]],"blomberg-s08-495":["But the method of judging, namely where one doubts more than one decides, where one thus believes that our cognitions can be false sooner than true, that is the *suspensio*, or that kind of distrust toward the cognition that we have, where we likewise actually settle nothing, yet nevertheless judge, and first investigate whether the grounds for, or against the cognition retain the upper hand.",[]],"blomberg-s08-496":["With this method we collect grounds of assent or of rejection, but we settle nothing at all, we do not yet decide at all. This kind of suspension is therefore directly opposed to dogmatic pride. the *dogmaticus* asks: is something true, or not? He thus decides at once, but this one, who suspends his judgment, does not trust himself very much at all, he inquires, tests, and investigates everything well beforehand always, precisely in order thereby to find out the way to truth, and to attain to genuine true insight.",[]],"blomberg-s08-497":["Human nature is actually far more inclined to decide than always to test and to settle than always to inquire. For we are not at all content when we must leave something, and something particular in our cognition unfinished, but we would gladly settle everything, so that we can recur to it, as to a completely certain, and reliable cognition, in a case of need that arises.",[]],"blomberg-s08-498":["Through the decision, and the deciding our understanding is actually more satisfied, one believes, namely, one has already acquired something thereby with which one could settle more cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-499":["The dogmatic way of philosophizing is therefore directly opposed to the problematic way of philosophizing, and contradistinguished.",[]],"blomberg-s08-500":["In the former one settles everything, but in the latter one decides nothing at all, but one postpones one's judgments until one has duly sought out the grounds, and counter-grounds, and can then pass a judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-501":["In physics one must above all philosophize problematically, and every cautious scholar will without doubt make use of this way of philosophizing. But the doubt in the problematic way of philosophizing is either a skeptical, an inquiring, investigating, testing, or else a dogmatic settling, and deciding doubt.",[]],"blomberg-s08-502":["The history of philosophy, when it comes to this point, namely to the point of the origin of the sect of the skeptics, then it always mostly appeals first to Socrates. The sophists, who in older times out of vain ambition, and imagined science to decide everything, believed they had the right, and defended a matter from whichever side it only pleased them, were hateful to Socrates, in that he held them merely for sham-wise men (which also is properly all they were), and publicly gave them out as such.",[]],"blomberg-s08-503":["He judged that it was not necessary for us to know many things, whence he also had the motto that testified sufficiently for his wisdom: “*quod supra nos, nihil ad nos*.” he thus established that our best cognition would be to come to know ourselves, the human being would be the proper object of all philosophy. Hence he accepted nothing but what he had tested. he gave his assent to no cognition merely blindly, but inquired, and investigated.",[]],"blomberg-s08-504":["When therefore someone was inclined to decide something at once, he showed him the counter-grounds, and difficulties that would necessitate him to suspend his judgment. This inclination to decide nothing without testing stemmed in him from no pride, but from a high degree of modesty, since he did not trust himself all too much, but actually placed a kind of distrust in his cognitions. and this modesty he also impressed above all on all his pupils, yet it did not remain long with them, for his immediate pupil Plato already began to become somewhat more dogmatic, and from him arose, and thus originated the sect of the academics, which was opposed to the sect of the skeptics.",[]],"blomberg-s08-505":["These endeavored to philosophize problematically, but those, the dogmatists, decided everything, their whole philosophy consisted in deciding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-506":["Pyrrho was the first who brought the sect of the skeptics into favor, and his adherents were therefore also called Pyrrhonists.",[]],"blomberg-s08-507":["This sect properly did not doubt all truths, but it prolonged the investigation, and postponed the decision. Hence it proved through the doubts that the grounds for decision were not yet complete, and thus led the dogmatists, who wanted to decide everything, onto the path of caution.",[]],"blomberg-s08-508":["The skeptics, however, are also called *zetetici*, seekers, and inquirers, and indeed because they sought, and inquired. They will thus with all propositions neither at all times decisively doubt them, nor yet decisively take something to be true, and accept it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-509":["The *zeteticus* is thus not the one who has the maxim of rejecting each and every thing, and of deciding *positive* about each and every thing without distinction, or of blindly asserting, but who reflects on cognitions, and tests them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-510":["Pyrrho had a certain motto of his sect: *non liquet*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-511":["To everyone who asserted his propositions with the greatest, and most settled gravity, he called out in reply *non liquet*, it is not yet settled, and then began to present his counter-speech.",[]],"blomberg-s08-512":["A skillful, and learned way to humble many a proud dogmatist, and to show his weak side. This is a sure motto for every pure reason. One must not accept everything at once, but test, and investigate the objects, and consider them. This sect of the Pyrrhonists, however, deviated so far from its originator that one afterwards assumed Pyrrho himself had entirely, and wholly denied the certainty of all, and every *dogmata*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-513":["That Pyrrho denied many *dogmata*, and placed a just, and well-considered distrust in them, further that he in particular rejected many rational judgments, is indisputable, and not to be denied, but that he denied each and every *dogma*, that is fundamentally false. He who accepts no *dogmata* at all can teach no morality at all. There are certainly certain, so to speak, eternal principles of our reason which cannot be contested at all. But one also blamed Pyrrho, over and above this, for having doubted the truth of all empirical judgments, and not trusted them. This, however, is nothing but an invention that has no ground at all. Pyrrho was a very wise man, but he would certainly have been a fool if he had wanted to reject all empirical judgments, whatever they might be; a Pyrrhonist thus in the beginning did nothing but moderate his judgment, but afterwards the skeptics did indeed seek to put everything in uncertainty, they endeavored to demonstrate that in all our cognitions no certainty at all could take place. and precisely from this arose *scepticismus problematicus, et dogmaticus*. The former doubted, to be sure, but it nevertheless investigated first, and thus wanted to attain to certainty by means of this investigation. The latter, on the other hand, decided, and assumed that in no matter was a complete certainty to be attained.",[]],"blomberg-s08-514":["This inclination to want to hold nothing at all to be certain arises actually from nothing other than from a desire for [724a] completion. He who saw that in very many things one could not attain to certainty, who doubted the truth of many things and *dogmata*, and had not accepted them until a testing had taken place, saw it as better to doubt everything, and to contest the truth of all, and every principle by adducing the counter-grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-515":["Of Pyrrho himself we properly have no writings left, except that Diogenes Laertius cites something of them. Meanwhile so much is certain, that one imputes to him, about as to Epicurus, and foists on him far more than he actually did. One says, e.g., of him that he denied the certainty of all experiences, that he therefore went straight onto all swamps, and morasses, that he was not afraid of the running of horses, nor of deep abysses and ditches, and yet adds to these reports so disadvantageous to him that he became 90 years old, as an evident proof that all such accusations, which one had devised about him, must be false, for with such recklessness he would certainly have come to lose his life sooner.",[]],"blomberg-s08-516":["Carneades, the originator of the last academic school, however, deviated greatly from the doctrines of Pyrrho, and actually asserted that one could attain in no matter a proper certainty, and come to certain truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-517":["Skepticism, however, is either *logicus*, the logical. To this belongs, e.g., the diallele: truth is supposed to consist in the agreement of a cognition with itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-518":["Many of the arguments of the skeptics had and receive their name often from the *terminus medius* of the *syllogismus*. Thus, e.g., one of their arguments is called the Liar, which ran thus: if you say you are lying, and in saying this, speak the truth, then you speak the truth in that you are lying, *atqui* you say you are lying, and, in saying this, so you thus speak the truth in that you are lying. The right conclusion, however, should be: *ergo* you speak the truth in saying you are lying.",[]],"blomberg-s08-519":["or skepticism is also *physicus*, the physical, where one drew into doubt the certainty of all physical laws, e.g., the possibility of motion. Thus the ancient *sceptici* had a certain argument which, on account of the *medius terminus*, they also called the Achilles. they namely inferred thus: if the running Achilles were to have a race with a tortoise, but might have a mile in advance, and Achilles also were able to run a hundred times faster than the tortoise, he would nevertheless never be able to catch up with the tortoise.",[]],"blomberg-s08-520":["The grounds he made use of in defense of this argument were: that all numbers were divisible *in infinitum*, that thus, however fast Achilles might run, there would still always take place a, though very small, *spatium* between him and the tortoise.",[]],"blomberg-s08-521":["Here they thus inferred merely through reason without recurring to experience, for then they would have found that Achilles must necessarily catch up with the tortoise in 1¼ miles, provided only he could run 1000 times faster than the tortoise. Skepticism was further *vel moralis*, and this consisted in giving out all rules of virtue for fashions, or customs, and usages in actions that human beings undertake. Thus, e.g., close marriages were not at all forbidden in Egypt, as now among us; among some Negroes, e.g., further, the greatest, most excellent, and last service of love that children who love their parents tenderly can render them is that they strangle them with advancing age. among the Spartans, e.g., further, theft, provided only it could happen that it was not publicly noticed, was not punished at all, and it was even permitted to steal, provided only it happened in secret.",[]],"blomberg-s08-522":["Skepticism was further *vel historicus*, the historical, which consisted in believing each and every history and report, also narratives of ancient times, to be uncertain, invented, and fabulous.",[]],"blomberg-s08-523":["Yet another kind of skeptic had the habit always of speaking a great deal of the uncertainty of the senses, and of conversing. they flatly denied the certainty of all and every experience. thus, e.g., there was a skeptic who, after he had often asserted with many grounds that one could not trust the senses at all, wanted to convince his friend of it quite surely and undeniably, in that he said to him: I have in my pantry many provisions daily, but when I go into it, count over the provisions, and come again the day after, this provision is nevertheless smaller than it was yesterday, a sure, and irrefutable proof that the senses must deceive. His friend, who well noticed what must be behind it, advised him to keep the key to the pantry, as much as possible, most carefully. The servant of the skeptic, however, who was very sly, soon knew how to get hold of the key, and diminished the provisions of his master under this cover, as before, until his master nevertheless once caught him in the act, and let him feel the just punishment of his theft, but also had to experience in this matter that one could well trust the senses.",[]],"blomberg-s08-524":["Such a skepticism could well be called *dogmaticus*, for the *sceptici* themselves were dogmatists, in that they doubted things etc., to be sure, but nevertheless believed they had a right to demand that every human being should give assent to their proofs, and accept their doubts. They thus contradicted themselves, for they said: everything is uncertain without distinction, and yet they asserted their propositions and attributed to them an infallible certainty. They had, e.g., the proposition: Everything is uncertain, and indeed in such a way that even it is uncertain that everything is uncertain –",[]],"blomberg-s08-525":["As regards modern times with respect to *scepticismus*, the dogmatic method was very customary at the beginning of the Reformation. At that time one held philosophy, and regarded it as a necessary support of religion, one therefore also did not raise many doubts and counter-grounds against the certainty of philosophical propositions, but taught, and established. But after a free way of thinking had afterwards crept in among many nations, one already began with more, and greater steadfastness and certainty to doubt the truth of many previously unchallenged propositions, and not to believe, and accept everything straightaway at once. one thus also soon took pains to resist the proud dogmatists somewhat, and to demonstrate the groundlessness of many of their propositions. Especially in the most recent times David Hume is known as a *scepticus*, who, however, had a preponderant, indeed a somewhat extravagant inclination to doubt. his writings, which appear before the learned world under the title: Philosophical Investigations, and are also otherwise called Miscellaneous Writings, contain political articles, treatises of literature, moral, and also metaphysical articles, but all come down to *scepticismus*. There is, however, in these writings of Hume a gentle, composed, and prejudice-free testing. He namely first of all considers therein the one side of a matter; he seeks out all possible grounds for it, and presents them with the best orator's style. Then he also takes up the other side, likewise sets it forth wholly impartially for judgment, presents all counter-grounds again with just the same eloquence, but at the end, and at the conclusion he appears in his true shape, as a proper skeptic, he complains of the uncertainty of all our cognitions in general, shows how little they are to be trusted, and at last doubts, instead of inferring, and settling, which cognition of these two is then true, and which false. He would, however, certainly be one of the best and most readable authors, if only he did not have the preponderant propensity to doubt everything, but would seek by means of the testing and investigation of cognitions to attain to a true certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-526":["Cardanus in his book *de vanitate scientiarum* also expresses *scepticismus*, and likewise asserts that everything is uncertain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-527":["Voltaire is the *scepticus* of the newer skeptics of our times. his skepticism, however, is far more harmful than it is useful. He brings forward grounds neither for, nor against the matter. he inquires, and tests nothing at all, but doubts without any proof that a cognition is to be trusted not at all. his grounds are therefore also nothing other than sham grounds, which can indeed deceive a simple-minded, but never an acute and reflective learned man. and precisely for that reason Voltaire is very dangerous for the great mass, and especially for the common man. For he suppeditates to him wholly false grounds for doubting the truth of this or that matter. But if one wants to have a closer instruction about the skepticism of modern times, one can with great use read for this purpose the work which Haller published under the title: Examination of the Sect That Doubts Everything. also the above-mentioned writings of Hume are in a certain way actually to be recommended.",[]],"blomberg-s08-528":["In this *paragraphus* the author speaks of an opinion in general. it is, however, contradistinguished from persuasion. In both there are uncertain cognitions, but in persuasion one holds them to be certain. In opinion, on the other hand, one does not hold them to be certain. we must therefore above all endeavor to distinguish persuasion well from conviction. The very same one who holds something for an opinion cannot yet hold it for a persuasion, and actually errs as soon as he does so. Persuasion is thus at all times blameworthy, in that therein one holds something to be certain from insufficient grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-529":["The difference of persuasion from conviction, however, is rather difficult, and is thus very seldom properly determined. This, however, comes from the fact that one has perhaps never yet tasted a true, but only a sham conviction.",[]],"blomberg-s08-530":["Opinion, however, is properly no error, but only an imperfect cognition, a lack, where something in our judgment has no sufficient grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-531":["We can always put forward many opinions, provided only we do not hold them for persuasions, without erring therein. The rabble does this very often. even if a cognition is wholly false, the opinion of it can nevertheless be wholly certain, and agree with the rules of the understanding, and of reason. E.g., when someone in common life chooses a servant who is of good parents, and also has a good upbringing, he opines that he is good, one could trust oneself to him. Here all reason is present, and applied, although the opposite of it can very often take place, and my cognition can be false. Wolff has a wholly reasonable opinion that there are inhabitants in the moon, for at that time discoveries were not yet so frequent as now, since one has seen that the moon has no *atmosphaera*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-532":["Opinion can often be false as to matter, although it is true as to form; and conversely it can be true as to matter, but false as to form, in the first case it is false, but in the other always true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-533":["When my cognition agrees with the constitution of the matter itself, then it is true. but when it is contrary to the constitution of the matter, then it is false. An opinion, however, can also be very true, reasonable, and well-grounded as to form, although as to matter it does not agree with the constitution of the matter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-534":["An opinion is never a certainty, the opposite of it can always take place, meanwhile it can nevertheless very often be reasonable, and right.",[]],"blomberg-s08-535":["One gets an opinion in favor of something from certain grounds, which one cognizes. But the outcome of the matter teaches us whether our opinion was true, or false as to matter. but whether the form of the opinion was right, this the *eventus* of the matter cannot properly show us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-536":["The chief error of our cognitions consists in the formal, and not in the material of cognitions. one can hold something to be true according to all the form of reason, and have a grounded opinion in favor of something, when the *data* that one now has for the matter are more preponderant than the grounds of the opposite, although the outcome of the matter first properly teaches one whether the opinion was true as to matter, and the *eventus* was otherwise than one had opined.",[]],"blomberg-s08-537":["Each and every opinion is very changeable. Today one can have more grounds for, tomorrow against the opinion, whereupon one must at once change one's opinion, and accept another, often opposed to the former, which then can in fact also be nothing disadvantageous at all to a true philosopher.",[]],"blomberg-s08-538":["Hypotheses are a quite special kind of philosophical opinions. not all philosophical opinions, however, are hypotheses, but only some. they do not serve to explain the appearances of the world, but only to explain something in general, whether it arise from reason, or from experience, or from appearance.",[]],"blomberg-s08-539":["An opinion of the truth of a ground from its sufficiency for the sequel is a hypothesis. namely when I hold something to be true *a priori*, for the sake of its grounds, then this is nothing other than an opinion. but when, on the other hand, from the *sufficience* of the sequels for a ground I cognize the ground, then this is also an opinion, but not *a priori*, but *a posteriori*, and thus a hypothesis. A *hypothesis* is as it were a presupposition. Thus a physician makes a *hypothesis* when he cures the sick person, he must subsume everything under hypotheses, and see whether from them arise the sequels that he now sees before his eyes.",[]],"blomberg-s08-540":["When all sequels that follow from an assumed *hypothesis* are true, and agree with what is given, then it is no longer an opinion, but a certainty",[]],"blomberg-s08-541":["It is further a demonstrated truth, but since we are not at all, or very seldom in a position to draw each and every sequel from it, they are nevertheless mostly only pure opinions, we must therefore say: if all sequels that we have been able to draw from the *hypothesis* are true, then I have great cause to conclude that the *hypothesis* itself is certain, and true. if a *hypothesis*, besides the sufficiency for its sequels, is also strengthened by other assurances, then it is confirmed. A *hypothesis subsidiaria*, however, is such a hypothesis as presupposes a new hypothesis. but the more I must assume in it, the less steadfast it is. the more unity there is in the hypothesis, the better it is, for otherwise invention is mostly added besides. and in this way there arises a philosophical novel. A novel of reason as, e.g., that of Cartesius etc. etc. A world-edifice out of pure figments of the brain. but let one note the rule: I can indeed invent in order to philosophize, but not invent in philosophizing itself",[]],"blomberg-s08-542":["Every presupposition, however, has infinite sequels. In relation, however, something is certain when the grounds of assent surpass all grounds of the opposite, that is then a comparative certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-543":["but something is *absolute* certain of which the opposite is impossible, or absurd. Two presuppositions, if they are so constituted that from the one one, and the same sequels flow as from the other, are one, and the same representations, and not at all different.",[]],"blomberg-s08-544":["Thus the Copernican system is a hypothesis. One can still always represent the opposite as possible, however difficult it is; and however easy this system is, and in conformity with reason, and in agreement with all *phaenomena* of the heavens. –",[]],"blomberg-s08-545":["With all hypotheses one is, in order to procure them entry, and certainty, necessitated that they can be confirmed, and derived not merely *a posteriori* through the relation to their sequels, but also through the *nexus a priori*, that is, through the relation to their grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-546":["From the sufficiency of a ground for a given sequel it can never be inferred with complete truth that this is the true ground of the sequel: for this is only an opinion. there is a ground of possibility, but not of certainty, for a sequel can often arise from several grounds, and originate from more than one ground. But the more sufficient a ground is, the more one will infer that it is the ground of the sequel. For all hypotheses are posited arbitrarily. namely I assume something, and see whether something is sufficient to derive a certain sequel from it, or not.",[]],"blomberg-s08-547":["When a ground suffices for all determinations that are contained in a certain given sequel, not more, also not fewer, when it is thus adequate, so that from it neither fewer determinations are to be inferred, and it is thus complete, nor also more, and it is thus precise, then it is the true ground, in that it is so constituted as it ought to be.",[]],"blomberg-s08-548":["Several grounds, which are supposed to be wholly adequate to one sequel, are impossible. Grounds that are different from one another also cannot agree with one another in all cases. But grounds that agree in all sequels are not different grounds, but one and the same ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-549":["When a ground suffices for some of what is contained in the sequel. then according to logical rules there takes place an opinion that the ground is the true one. If the ground suffices for more determinations of the sequel, then the opinion is all the more probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-550":["Until finally, when the ground suffices for all determinations, but also not for more determinations than are contained in the sequel; then a true ground is there, and then the hypothesis ceases. The ground becomes a theory. A certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-551":["When a sequel is given to me, and I am to find the ground for it, then this is a hypothesis. but if a ground is given to me, and I am to derive the sequels from it, then this is indeed an opinion, but no hypothesis. In the hypothesis the sequels are given, but the ground is invented.",[]],"blomberg-s08-552":["One of the most famous hypotheses is the Copernican. Copernicus assumed the sun stands still, and the planets moved around it, and saw whether from this all appearances of the heavenly bodies can be explained; but this hypothesis of his afterwards becomes a true opinion and a complete system that deserves to find assent everywhere. When it is asked: why do all bodies have precisely an inclination to fall toward the earth? then here the sequel is there, and the ground is to be found.",[]],"blomberg-s08-553":["Cartesius assumed that bodies made certain vortices, still others assumed that these vortices intersected and thus they sought from this to explain the fall of bodies, and indeed always toward the center of the earth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-554":["In *metaphysica* there are many hypotheses. The proof of the existence of God is at first a hypothesis. one assumes a being that is the most perfect among all others, and investigates then whether from it grounds of explanation of that can be derived, of which nature, experience, and the creatures that are on the earth, teach us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-555":["Plato, and Malebranche assumed it as a hypothesis that human beings stood in a wholly particular combination with the Godhead, and saw whether they would be able to explain from it the sequences that they had insight into.",[]],"blomberg-s08-556":["Hypotheses, however, actually have a very great utility, and can never be wholly banished from human cognition. all judges make hypotheses, which they often reject again upon better investigation. Hypotheses are the way to theory, and oftentimes to a truth wholly opposed to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-557":["One does not always find through hypotheses that which one wants, but oftentimes something else, one attempts, one tries, assumes something, and investigates whether one could explain from it the known sequences, or not; if the first takes place, then one accepts the *hypothesin* , if the latter takes place, then one rejects it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-558":["Since from the sufficiency of a ground for the sequence the truth of the ground can be inferred, and this ground is fabricated, it follows from this: The more there is fabricated in a ground, the more follows *licite* (legitimately) from it, and the newer is the same. and the more hypotheses must be assumed in order to explain something, the more fabrication takes place therein.",[]],"blomberg-s08-559":["With some inferences one must fabricate many *Hypotheses Subsidiarias* (subsidiary hypotheses). If we have sequences, and fabricate the ground for them, then it can oftentimes be that we have still more sequences than that the ground suffices for them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-560":["We must therefore fabricate new grounds, and these new grounds are nothing other than the *Hypotheses Subsidiariae*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-561":["These, however, are actually opposed to true philosophy, and show *effren is fingendi Licentia* (unbridled license of fabricating). If I want to fabricate everything, then I want to explain everything. Unity is therefore a chief property of hypotheses, that namely one assumes a ground which suffices to explain from it all determinations, and sequences of the thing, which we cognize, without however having need to help oneself further through *Hypotheses subsidiarias*, and their assumption.",[]],"blomberg-s08-562":["The unity of hypotheses is a great ground that the ground is the true one, and no other can take place from which one could explain just as many sequences.",[]],"blomberg-s08-563":["A chief ground of the truth of the hypothesis is also: if one shows that the ground which one has thought up for the sake of the sufficiency for the sequences deserves to be assumed also from other causes. Here namely one shows from other grounds that what is fabricated must be assumed, one thus confirms the truth of the hypothesis.",[]],"blomberg-s08-564":["All taking to be true arises from a twofold combination, namely either because the ground is sufficient to derive all sequences from it, or because it also agrees with other grounds which have a similarity with it, and is *aequivoce*, so to speak. With all hypotheses we will therefore first show the sufficiency of the *Fiction is historicae* (historical fiction) for the sequences, and also the agreement of the fiction with other grounds, so that there are still other grounds from which some sequences can be derived. Hypotheses must first be probable as inferences, but also at the same time be able to be confirmed through other grounds. e.g., whoever wants to explain the thunderstorm assumes in the air an electrical kind of matter, and shows afterwards from other grounds that a sulfurous, and electrical matter must actually be found in the air. Hypotheses have on one side something very disadvantageous to philosophy, insofar as they give permission to invent. on the other side, however, they are in turn very advantageous, namely if the *Fictiones* are heuristic, or have as end to discover something.",[]],"blomberg-s08-565":["The hypothesis is thus a method of our reason, and of our understanding, actually wholly indispensable in philosophy, all hypotheses must be assumed thoroughly problematically, consequently one must be obstinate about no *hypothesin*, and hold fast to it, such that one should not be able to let it go as soon as one has insight into its falsehood, or uncertainty. Oftentimes many attempts at fabricating are wholly in vain, since one thereby discovers no truth. Hypotheses therefore also have the harm that, when they are artfully devised, and one has noticed therein the agreement with many sequences, they take root very much in our understanding, and one, even if afterwards one has insight into their falsehood, nevertheless does not so easily let them go So e.g., if this or that hypothesis were sufficient for new, and correct sequences, and there is only one, e.g., the hundredth, which contradicts the ground, then one rejects this one, although it is evidently true, rather than that one should let go that hypothesis which seems to be so good. It is therefore very sad if in an age nothing but hypotheses rule, which however are false, and thus do not suffice to find out the true ground of the matter. Such hypotheses harm more than they are of use, for they do not suffice to explain everything, and if one moreover assumes them as true, then instead of true one gets nothing but false cognitions and representations. He who will not let go of false hypotheses because he has hatched them, and they seem to him so probable, is like a person who has raised a child with much trouble and care, which afterwards he does not like to throw away, so as not to lose all his work, trouble and expense.",[]],"blomberg-s08-566":["Nevertheless such hypotheses, in which nothing but false hypotheses are forged in, serve for this, that in the following ages",[]],"blomberg-s08-567":["true hypotheses are discovered, for it is impossible that he who knows all only possible false ways should not finally find the right way. The remaining propositions, which the author",[]],"blomberg-s08-568":["adduces, are for the most part tautological, and therefore deserve no closer investigation, and explanation (the author often proceeds thus). When in § 183 in the 7th rule he says: if one has not discovered all too many opinions, then we can remark on this: much can be a perfection according to quality, but not a perfection according to quantity. namely if it exceeds a certain degree, which would constitute the perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-569":["This degree, however, is difficult, indeed almost not at all to determine e.g., to say: One should not be too open-hearted is no art, but the art consists in this, to determine quite precisely the degree of open-heartedness and of reticence which one must not overstep, if one does not want to be too open-hearted, and too reticent.",[]],"blomberg-s08-570":["There are innumerable delusions in morals, one gives rules that say nothing more than that which one has long known already, it is thus nothing new at all for our understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-571":["Certainty is either",[]],"blomberg-s08-572":["a) comparative or also B) apodictic.",[]],"blomberg-s08-573":["Comparative certainty is the relation of the grounds of taking to be true to the grounds of the opposite.",[]],"blomberg-s08-574":["Apodictic certainty, however, is absolute, and consists in the relation of the grounds of taking to be true to the sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-575":["History is merely comparatively certain, and never apodictically. Morals, however, among which particularly the *Jus naturale* (natural right), contains many apodictic certainties in itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-576":["From apodictic certainty is further different mathematical certainty in intuition, which is evident. Mathematics presents its propositions",[]],"blomberg-s08-577":["1: discursively",[]],"blomberg-s08-578":["2: intuitively",[]],"blomberg-s08-579":["Moral, physical, even metaphysical propositions cannot possibly be mathematically certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-580":["Evidentness is certainly always lacking, although they are apodictically certain. The term ‘arbitrary truths’ is as good as nothing, for can then truth perhaps also be false? what a contradictory expression!",[]],"blomberg-s08-581":["A proposition, however, is 1: true",[]],"blomberg-s08-582":["2 false",[]],"blomberg-s08-583":["3 arbitrary and then it is not settled whether it is this, or that, that is: It can be true, but also false. arbitrarily I cannot form the object, but I can so dispose the subject, arbitrarily, as is most convenient for me, to cognize the object, and make the object graspable to me in the most commodious, and most convenient way.",[]],"blomberg-s08-584":["Everything that is mediately certain is provable. what is immediately certain is unprovably certain. i.e., it is so undeniable that it needs no means of certainty, or of proof.",[]],"blomberg-s08-585":["Proof, as a means of apodictic certainty, is demonstration. Proof as a means of comparative certainty is probation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-586":["A mathematical demonstration, however, is moreover intuitive, evident. it speaks of the universal *in individuo*. Who can comprehend that? Just as little as how we can see everything with our eyes, since they are yet so small. we continually see objects that strike our eyes, without however in the least thinking about it, how does that come about?",[]],"blomberg-s08-587":["In this *Paragrapho* the author says: one should guard against all and every false cognition, one should avoid a seeming conviction, or seeming persuasion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-588":["This, however, everyone already knows, but he would have said something excellent if he had indicated the marks by which persuasion can be distinguished from conviction.",[]],"blomberg-s08-589":["Persuasion does not consist in material falsehood, but in a false form, when one takes an opinion for a complete certainty. The matter itself can always be true, only this is not the manner, the way, to arrive at truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-590":["One need not err in persuasion according to the object, but one errs according to the form. When one takes the grounds for opinion, for taking to be true from insufficient grounds, for grounds for certainty from sufficient grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-591":["But we have in fact always only a theory of morals, a speculation about the moral ethical conduct of human beings, and no actual practical morals. Hence no one can demand either that one should indicate the *Notas* (marks) in which false persuasion is distinguished from conviction in the true sense. This is yet to be discovered. We likewise have only a theoretical and altogether no practical logic.",[]],"blomberg-s08-592":["Persuasions arise from logical grounds, others from aesthetic grounds, which do not come from the understanding, and from reason, but arise from a certain inclination. we are oftentimes not indifferent to find a thing true or false, and then there is present a great ground that we will be persuaded of a cognition. The human being is in general subject to very many illusions, they play with him as with a ball. but he can most easily be persuaded who is very easily inclined to be put into one or another affect.",[]],"blomberg-s08-593":["The great crowd, the rabble, the common man is therefore for the most part very easily, and most easily to be persuaded, by someone who understands the art of winning over. But he who can easily be persuaded can also easily be put into affect. Both are thus almost inseparably combined with one another.",[]],"blomberg-s08-594":["Science (which really does not belong here at all, and the consideration of which the author has introduced here wrongly, and in a certain way unsuitably,) one can oppose to conjecturing, and also to opinions. To believe something, or to opine, is very much different from knowing. For knowing signifies taking something to be true with sufficient certainty, and grounds, so that no doubt remains, nor can remain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-595":["As concerns the degrees of taking to be true, they are the following:",[]],"blomberg-s08-596":["1: Opining, or taking to be true from any insufficient ground, which nevertheless has more weight than the ground of the opposite, which I cognize, and have insight into.",[]],"blomberg-s08-597":["2: Believing, or taking something to be true in the degree that it is sufficient to act, and to resolve myself to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-598":["3: Knowing, or cognizing the truth sufficiently.",[]],"blomberg-s08-599":["The word ‘wissen’ (to know) is the origin, or the root word of ‘Gewissheit’ (certainty). A certainty, however, we have already explained above. A science, however, is nothing other than a cognition through which we know something. The word ‘science’ is contradistinguished from art, hence one also commonly says in common life, and conversely, that is no science, but an art. e.g., with riddles this takes place, there one exclaims, that is no science but an art (a great difference).",[]],"blomberg-s08-600":["There are actions for which a certain art, but there are also, on the other hand, in turn certain actions for which a science is required, and is needed. something, however, is called (in order to set these concepts apart) an art for which a specific perfection of practice is required. something, however, is called a science for which a specific perfection of theory is needed. Every art involves a practical skill in acting. The practical skill that one has in art, however, cannot be attained merely according to rules, and particular certain prescriptions, but one must thereby also have a particular aptitude. Further, science is opposed to critique. all theoretical cognition is either a doctrine, or a critique. Critique, however, is in fact nothing other than a species of a theoretical (thus not practical) cognition about any product that is there, which shows us the rules according to which this product is executed. Doctrine, however, is that theoretical cognition in which one meets with the grounds of how a thing can be executed, or meets with the rules according to which a good product can be brought forth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-601":["A critique is really no science, for otherwise we might be able at once ourselves to bring forth good products. this, however, happens with critique only after a long time, when one gradually learns to apply the cognized rules to oneself. In common usage of speech, however, the word ‘science’ is often falsified, and one involves with it other concepts than this word actually contains.",[]],"blomberg-s08-602":["With certainty what is needed is not the truth itself of the thing that we cognize, but the taking it to be true. The greater the probability of a thing is, the more the understanding is necessitated to take it to be true. This necessity, however, to accept the thing lies not in the *Objecto* itself, but in the subject, because the grounds of the opposite by no means come up to those which I cognize for the truth of the thing. This subjective necessity to accept something is very deceptive, and changeable, because it is not grounded in the *Objecto*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-603":["The author also speaks here of mathematical certainty, of the first, and second rank. The former rests on reason, the latter on experience: and both certainties are therefore distinguished in the *Datis*. to all mathematical certainty belongs a sufficient ground, a precision, and a certain intuiting of the cognition, i.e., the intuitive. Mathematical certainty carries with it a certain sensibility, for this takes place always only with quantities, but never with qualities.",[]],"blomberg-s08-604":["In philosophy there are no intuitive cognitions, but only in mathematics, which has to do with magnitudes. in mathematical certainty there is found, just as in philosophical, distinctness, and sufficiency, in the former, however, solely and alone precision, and evidentness are to be met with. Historical certainty can never possibly become apodictic. The grounds of the truth of a historical certainty, however, are",[]],"blomberg-s08-605":["1: The purity of insight.",[]],"blomberg-s08-606":["2, the correctness of the power of judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-607":["3 truthfulness, further",[]],"blomberg-s08-608":["4 the inclination to truth or to speak the truth, and finally",[]],"blomberg-s08-609":["5: uprightness. for the last two grounds are the causes of the third ground, namely of the truthfulness of the one who asserts it. Now, however, one cannot at all infer apodictically from the sequences, because there can be far more than one sequence of one and the very same thing. Certain historical cognitions will have such grounds of taking to be true that they are sufficient for practical, though to be sure not for theoretical certainty. Apodictic certainty is consequently not really distinguished from practical certainty *in gradu* (in degree), but only *in specie* (in species), even if some would want to assert the former.",[]],"blomberg-s08-610":["The author in this *Paragrapho* calls hypotheses arbitrary truths. an arbitrary proposition makes it that something is true, only it must not contradict itself. such an arbitrary proposition is called *positio*. arbitrarily nothing can ever be assumed that is objective, but only the subjective is fixed through choice. all arbitrary propositions concern what one ought to do, the practical, and thus never teach what the thing is. A *Positio* takes place solely in regard to practice, and through this it is distinguished from the hypothesis. arbitrary *Positiones* which concern actions occur preeminently in mathematics, e.g., with the circle, that one divides it into 360 degrees, with logarithms, the relation of the arithmetical to the geometrical number, proportions, progressions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-611":["The word ‘hypothesis’ therefore signifies no arbitrary proposition at all, but an opinion from certain grounds *a posteriori*, which are sufficient for these or those sequences.",[]],"blomberg-s08-612":["With positions I really fix nothing at all that lies in the object, but that which I arbitrarily wish in it, and want to have. A mathematical magnitude rests on the arbitrary and discretionary repetition of unity. what holds here of a particular kind of triangles, circles etc., that holds at the same time of all triangles, circles etc. of this kind. A triangle, circle, quadrangle etc. takes the place of all the remaining possible ones. every triangle contains e.g. 180 degrees in itself, this holds of all other possible triangles. Every square has 4 equal sides, and 4 right angles, this holds of all and every possible square etc. etc. Here therefore the universal is always seen, and considered, in a single case.",[]],"blomberg-s08-613":["Every certain proposition is either unprovably, or immediately, or mediately, or provably certain. That cognition which contains in itself the ground of the truth of another cognition is the *Beweisthum* (argument) and the connection of the cognitions",[]],"blomberg-s08-614":["with their truth is the proof. there are thus really 3 pieces to be met with here, namely",[]],"blomberg-s08-615":["1: the argument or the matter",[]],"blomberg-s08-616":["2 the proof, or the consequence, or the form",[]],"blomberg-s08-617":["3 and finally the conclusion, which follows from the *consequens*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-618":["With every proof one really investigates not the proposition to be proved, but the *rationem probandi* (ground of proving) The *Materia* of the proof is the *Medius Terminus* (middle term), and the form the consequence. some proofs are empirical, other proofs are rational. and these are either mathematical, and then they are called *Demonstrationes*, or they are philosophical, and incomplete *Probationes*. if convincing proofs lack evidentness, the intuitive, and precision, exactness, then they are not yet *Demonstrationes*",[]],"blomberg-s08-619":["All rational proofs, supposing that they were even sufficient, are therefore not yet *demonstrationes*, still much less, however, are empirical proofs *Demonstrationes*. Empirical proofs can oftentimes be very sufficient, and evident, notwithstanding this they are not *demonstrationes*, for they are not rational. The word ‘unprovable’ signifies here, as we have already mentioned above, such certain cognitions, of whose certainty, and truth one may not, and cannot, doubt at all.",[]],"blomberg-s08-620":["All provable cognition is not clear through itself, but is only taken to be true as a sequence by means of other cognitions. if one therefore holds a provable cognition to be unprovably certain, then one errs in the form. The proofs of the existence of God, of his properties, of his effects are not unprovable, but provable. With unprovably certain cognitions I postulate, demand as it were, assent. With provable certain cognitions, however, I beg and request as it were the concept from others. all immediate experiences are unprovably certain, and also cannot be proved at all. e.g., there is a sun in the sky, the barometer has fallen, or risen, all this is immediately certain, and therefore unprovable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-621":["If, however, I fix universal judgments from experiences, or propositions of experience, then they are always provable. all rational, and unprovable cognitions are *Axiomata*, and they are either synthetic or analytic. in every judgment one conceives the subject as a mark of a thing that can otherwise be unknown to me. one thus knows merely the mark, this is known to one. e.g., the proposition: A body is divisible, is analytic, for to the subject to which a body pertains, divisibility also pertains, and to which divisibility pertains, to that subject a body also pertains. A body, however, has an attractive force, this proposition is synthetic, for attractive force, separated from all experience, does not lie in the concept of a body.",[]],"blomberg-s08-622":["All analytic propositions, where the predicate lies in the notion of the subject, belong under the law in identity. Synthetic propositions, however, whose predicate lies not in the notion of the subject, but in a mark that is distinct from the subject, do not belong under the *Principium Identitatis*, and *Contradictionis*. This *Principium* refers only to analytic propositions ; In synthetic propositions a predicate is added to the notion of the subject that is not contained in it. and all propositions that have this constitution can be cognized only intuitively, not through reason. Through reason one can cognize no coordination at all, for reason only subordinates. all synthetic propositions are either immediate propositions of experience, or intuitive propositions which are brought forth through the understanding, and do not lie in the notion of the subject. Analytic propositions, however, can be drawn out *per analysin* from the concept of the subject. For they are contained in the notion of the subject, and subordinated to it. Here reason can occupy itself, and dissect the subordinated concepts, and lay them apart. e.g., gold does not rust. This is a synthetic proposition, this is evident merely from immediate experience, and cannot really be derived at all from the notion of gold *per analysin*, if something is to be postulated, then it must be so constituted that it absolutely convinces everyone. In a proof falsehood according to the matter is only accidental, but according to the form essential. False matter can be improved, but on the other hand false form is wholly *incorrigib ile*. Further, falsehood of form is also far more widespread, and more general, than falsehood of matter. The *Proton pseudos*, where someone infers according to absurdity, or also assumes false propositions, likewise lies in the form, for it is peculiar that the form is subject not only to many, but even to incorrigible errors.",[]],"blomberg-s08-623":["To prove something *directe* means to prove something in interconnection with the grounds. That is an ostensive proof, in which one shows that that is false which is the opposite, thus an apagogic proof.",[]],"blomberg-s08-624":["To prove something *indirecte*, on the other hand, means to prove something in interconnection with its sequences. This latter, however, can never happen otherwise than *negative* , negatively. we never cognize, however, all sequences of a thing, but many of them are still unknown to us. Hence it comes that the indirect proof can never become a demonstration, but can well become a philosophical probation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-625":["If we want to prove that a cognition is false, then this can really happen The opposite of such a false cognition drawn already proves the partial falsehood of the cognition, hence it comes that the apagogic inference is so much favored, because it is the only one that can give an apodictic certainty. Since the direct inference is able to give nothing other than a comparative certainty. The apagogic proof, however, is also that which comes nearest of all to evidentness. it is also thus far easier than the direct proof, since it brings the opposite *ad absurdum*, and it is settled that absurdity always strikes the eyes far more strongly than all and every truth just as the greatest ceased pleasure is not nearly so palpable to me as the opposite pain, and a pleasure is heightened through preceding pain. Truth, e.g., has not nearly the striking, the *frappant* about it, as the representation of the absurdity of the opposite. The fault of this proof, however, is: It can indeed prove that the opposite of the proposition is false, but it cannot yet prove it from itself. In this respect the direct, the genetic proof is far better than the former, the latter namely discloses not only the truth, but also at the same time the *Genesin*, the source of generation of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-626":["The apagogic proof, however favored it also is, one thus uses only so *ad interim* etc. The right true insight, however, one really obtains only through the direct genetic proof. *Directe* a cognition is proved if its truth can be established from its relation to the grounds. *Indirecte*, on the other hand, if its truth flows from its relation to the sequences. Direct proofs are really genetic, and are conducted *per rationes essendi, non vero Cognoscendi* (through grounds of being, not of cognizing). indirect proofs, however, are conducted *per rationes Cognoscendi, non essendi*. to infer *affirmative* from the sequences to the grounds does not work at all, but this must always happen *negative*",[]],"blomberg-s08-627":["In order that I say that something is demonstrated, the proof must be so constituted that it convinces everyone, that is, the proof must hold not for me alone, but also for others. for demonstration evidentness is required, so that the cognition, and the proof of it is so constituted that one cannot possibly suspend one's assent. From this it is thus evident that one must use the word ‘demonstration’ very sparingly. Much can be a philosophical probation, but no demonstration. Mathematical proofs, however, are of the kind that they are all *Demonstrationes*. Something can thus not be passed off as a demonstration until experience has taught that it is impossible to hold back one's assent to it, and to resist it, or, until other human beings also agree in this, that it is not possible further to doubt the cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-628":["*Demonstrationes* are thus to be met with only very seldom in philosophy. No aesthetic proof can thus be a demonstration, for an aesthetic probation arises merely from the agreement of the cognition with our feeling, and our taste, it is thus nothing other than a persuasion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-629":["Aesthetically I can prove something to someone as soon as I know his passions, and his feeling, and am able to move it. This probation, however, is not universally valid, and is therefore wrongly called a demonstration by our *Autore*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-630":["Everyone indeed has an inclination to demonstrate, evidentness pleases everyone, only it is a pity therein that it is so difficult, and very seldom to be attained.",[]],"blomberg-s08-631":["The all too great inclination to demonstrate, says our author: is the addiction to demonstration. Charlatans really have no addiction to demonstrating themselves, but rather they deceive those who lie sick with the addiction to demonstrating. The addiction to demonstrating from pedantry, however, is the true addiction to demonstration, and it really consists in this, that one has an inclination either to demonstrate everything, or to accept nothing that is not demonstrated, and established indubitably. Pedantry in demonstrating thus believes reliably that in the whole of human cognition one can get on altogether not at all without demonstration. This pedantry, however, can notwithstanding this very often be combined with a very sharp critique of cognition. it is thus really no ignorance, also no error, but one perhaps does not demonstrate at all, because something cannot be demonstrated, and would rather not accept at all such cognitions as cannot be demonstrated. One can nourish this addiction to demonstrating very much if one knows how to conceal the weak side of demonstrations. One lays it aside, however, when one knows, and has insight, that in many things nothing at all can be demonstrated; and when one thus cognizes that much is cognized as a demonstration which yet is not proved at all, or is only a probation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-632":["In our present age, however, there rules no addiction to demonstrating at all, but a certain shallowness, a kind of gallantry even in learned cognitions. the latter, however, is nevertheless always still far more tolerable than the former. For shallowness requires only an addition in order that it become thorough, it is thus fully capable of an improvement. The addiction to demonstrating, on the other hand, is *incorrigible*, one very often therein accepts false cognitions as true, and is moreover proud of this falsehood itself, and not at all eager to let oneself be taught and correctly convinced of the opposite of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-633":["Sensation is the representation of our present state, insofar as it arises from the presence of a certain object. sometimes there arise in us representations of which we ourselves are the author, whose presence, however, we derive from the existence of a thing. and then it comes about that we oftentimes confuse fictions with sensations, and in this way we commit an error. we can oftentimes fabricate something for ourselves, but this is no sensation at all. So e.g. the fear of an impending pain is distinct from the pain that is present. even the fear of pain namely is a fiction. on the other hand pain itself is a sensation. Of sensations nothing further can be said, or reasoned, i.e., rationalized, for they are the *Principium materiale* of all our representations.",[]],"blomberg-s08-634":["A representation of sensation cannot be fabricated by us at all, but must actually have been given to us by means of the senses. Experience, and sensation are distinguished according to form, and according to degree. According to matter both are the same, but with experience there is also found a form, reason. Experience is nothing other than a reflected sensation, or a sensation which is expressed through a judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-635":["Experiences namely are not mere concepts, and representations, but also judgments e.g., the representations of warmth or of cold are concepts of experience. they are universal properties of things, these, however, cannot be had insight into merely through the senses, but really through judgments. Irrational animals therefore have no experience, but only sensations. Everyone who can describe the objects of his experience has experience, for to description belongs not merely sensation, but also a judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-636":["Mere sensations, without judging about them, cannot be described. Every experience is really immediate, and mediate experiences are according to our *Autore* *Contradictiones in adjecto*. The judgment that is passed immediately on the occasion of sensations is the judgment of experience.",[]],"blomberg-s08-637":["All judgment is either empirical, or rational. much can be empirical according to matter, but rational according to form. much, however, can be rational according to matter as well as according to form. The form of all our experiences is rational. all experiences have the form of reason, and without it they will be no experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s08-638":["With experiences we must be active, through reason With sensations, however, we are merely passive. The proofs of certain cognitions are according to form always rational, according to matter, however, they can arise either from experience or from reason. A proof without the form of reason is no proof at all we must, if we want to prove something, yet infer from certain *datis*. supposing that the matter also arises from the senses, or is a sensation, and therefore every proof is rational. Notwithstanding this, however, it is called empirical if its matter is a sensation, or if the ground of proof arises from experience. If one has an empirical proof, then the ground of proof is immediately a sensation arises thus accordingly always according to experience from sensation. immediate sensations cannot be proved.",[]],"blomberg-s08-639":["We must learn to distinguish well from one another the inferences from experiences and the *judicia discursiva* of experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s08-640":["But nothing is more deceptive than that one secretly infers from certain examples, without, however, knowing that one infers, and hence passes off an inference from experience for an immediate experience. Immediate experience shows us only that which is in our sense, without our inferring thereby.",[]],"blomberg-s08-641":["The judgment which expresses the appearance is an immediate judgment of experience. Now if we mix up the judgment which expresses the appearance with those judgments which express the causes of the appearance, then we confuse the *judicium discursivum* with the *judicio intuitivo*. the *judicium discursivum* is thus that which the cause of the appearance the *intuitivum*, that which expresses the appearance itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-642":["The care not to mix up the *judicia discursiva* with the *judiciis intuitivis* is of especially great importance, and it is unavoidably necessary to apply them properly, so that one does not fall into various errors. all proofs come about from reason, and the demonstration of a cognition from an immediate experience is not a proof, but a *factum*, as it were an *inspectio ocularis*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-643":["But a proof from reason, of which the author speaks in the 204th *paragraphus*, is such a proof as arises from reason both with regard to the ground of proof, or as to matter, or with regard to the formal, and thus needs no experience. Proofs from reason do indeed have for their ground of proof such propositions as arise from reason, but in part these propositions arise, as to their first origin, from experience. A proof from reason is thus not always a proof of pure reason without application of experience. But a proof through pure reason can be conducted only of such cognitions as are not objects of the senses at all. if cognitions are even only in part *objecte* of the senses, then they must already be derived from experience as to their first *principia*. Proofs of pure reason therefore also have place only in mathematics, and metaphysics. that which can be demonstrated through pure reason not only does not need a proof from experience, but also does not permit one at all. There have been some who have striven to invent an experimental geometry, but all their efforts have become nothing but children's games.",[]],"blomberg-s08-644":["Experiences permit no universal judgments at all, except only of possibility. Experience can not at all teach me with apodictic certainty that all human beings must die e.g., but only merely that all human beings who have lived until now have died. But a proof from pure reason proves universal propositions, and therefore can no longer be supported at all by proofs from experience, for these prove no universal propositions. A demonstration from pure reason is so distinct from proof from experience that if both were to be combined with one another and joined together, a bastard would necessarily arise from it. Proofs from pure reason are like a light against which all proofs from experience vanish, just as the light of the moon is wholly dispensable, and vanishes, when the rays of the sun make the day all at once. Everything that can be proved through pure reason receives through experience no new proof, but only a mere illustration, and elucidation. To have the idea of a thing is very difficult.",[]],"blomberg-s08-645":["One can have much use from a science, indeed have solid cognitions of it, without nevertheless having an idea of it. so it obviously goes e.g. with logic, with jurisprudence.",[]],"blomberg-s08-646":["Logic is a science which states the actions which arise as to form. all *conceptus* are 1: *empirici*, which from experience 2: *intellectuales*, which from the understanding 3: *arbitrarii*, i.e., concepts arising from choice. There are intuitions in concepts, i.e., *perceptiones communes*, commonly valid, or common representations. but all *perceptiones* are",[]],"blomberg-s08-647":["1) *intuitive* intuiting",[]],"blomberg-s08-648":["2*do* *reflexive* requiring reflection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-649":["But the *reflexive perceptiones* are in turn",[]],"blomberg-s08-650":["A) *Communes*",[]],"blomberg-s08-651":["B) *Singulares*",[]],"blomberg-s08-652":["But in logic all *perceptiones* must be *communes*. if they are applied separately, then they get the names of particular concepts. Through logical separation no cognition at all can arise. Through a *conceptus communis* I can consider an object",[]],"blomberg-s08-653":["a) *in abstracto* or else also B) *in concreto*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-654":["But the word *conceptus abstractus* is a bad expression. to what end may the art of the human understanding be able to form commonly valid concepts, *conceptus communes*? They serve to assign to each concept its place, and they are as it were the representatives of a multitude of things, they thus serve especially very much for elucidation. The common concept contains many under itself, but these concepts which it contains under itself contain it in themselves, it is contained in them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-655":["A wondrous counter-relation e.g. humanity extends to all human beings, and yet in all human beings lies humanity. But the multitude of things that I can think under the *conceptus communis* make up the *sphaera conceptus*. The more things a mark belongs to, the greater a concept is according to its domain, i.e., according to its *capacitaet*, the less, on the other hand, does it contain in itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-656":["The concept of something holds of everything, and contains nothing in itself, and so it is always. But every *conceptus communis* can be considered as a *conceptus superior*, a higher concept, yet one names it so specially for this reason, insofar as it in turn contains other *conceptus communes* in itself. e.g. the human being the man. There is thus a subordination of our concepts. A *conceptus communis* is called, with regard to the concepts which are contained under it, the *genus*, the genus, but with regard to the concepts in which it is contained, the *species*. So e.g. the human being is the *genus* with regard to men etc. but with regard to a rational being the human being is in turn the *species*. But the boundary of concepts subordinated to one another is the *conceptus summus*, i.e., that *conceptus* which is indeed a *genus* but no *species*. The *conceptus infimus*, on the contrary, will be a *species* which has no further *genus*. But a *conceptus singularis* has no *sphaera*. The *genus summum*, then, and the *individuum* are the two boundaries of logically subordinated concepts. Every *conceptus* has a domain of the concepts to which it extends, the *sphaera notionis*, and the proposition is well known: *Quo major est sphaera conceptus, eo minus in illo est contentum, et vice versa*. That *conceptus* whose *sphaera* is only a part of the *sphaera* of another *conceptus* is called a *conceptus angustior*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-657":["A *conceptus singularis* has no domain at all, and thus also no *sphaera*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-658":["*Cognitio a priori* is a cognition, and *probatio a priori* is a proof from above downward. But *cognitio a posteriori* is a cognition, and *probatio a posteriori* a proof from below upward. These expressions are far more fitting than the author's expressions from behind, and from in front proofs *a posteriori* are proofs from experience. on the other hand proofs *a priori* are proofs from reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-659":["If one proves something from pure reason, then no experience takes place. but if one demonstrates something from experience, then proofs from pure reason drop away, yet some proofs can be conducted *partim a priori, partim a posteriori*, if experience is the origin of the proofs of reason. But reason and experience are connected either through coordination, or through subordination. in subordination I posit an experience as ground, and infer from it, by means of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-660":["But in coordination I prove first wholly alone from reason, and then wholly alone from experience, so that the proof from experience serves the proof from reason as a proof *ad evidentiam*. in this way the *connubium rationis et experientiae* is brought about. Experience has no need at all of the proof of reason and on the contrary the proof of reason is not in need of the proof of experience. Neither is subordinated to the other, both make up not one, but two proofs. For in the proofs there is merely a coordination. But one can be very greatly deceived in this. if the proof of reason is a demonstration, or a complete proof, then the proof of experience serves it for no greater strengthening at all, but only for illustration, and if the proof of experience is a complete proof, then the proof of reason also helps it nothing. Proofs of experience and of reason make no coordination at all, and still less, when they are already coordinated, a distinctness. many insufficient proofs coordinated with one another never yield a demonstration, but they are mere *probationes*, and can bring about no apodictic certainty at all. Proofs of experience and of reason can therefore not possibly possess their evidence if they are not subordinated and placed under one another. where we already find very many proofs of a thing, there we can also already infer that we will never attain to an apodictic certainty; a single proof from sufficient grounds is far better than 1000 insufficient proofs which are subordinated to one another, because one is after all not able to attain complete certainty through them. It does indeed often happen that both in mathematics and in the other sciences more than one sufficient proof of one and the same thing is given, but this happens for the most part more to show one's art in demonstrating than because one still demands more proofs for the evidence of the thing. But even if several insufficient proofs coordinated with one another are adduced, they serve for no greater distinctness, and certainty, but the thing remains, as before, undecided.",[]],"blomberg-s08-661":["One can opine something without believing, namely when one has more grounds for than against the cognition. here I take something to be true without its having an influence on our actions. But to know something means nothing other than to cognize something with certainty. But in belief there is a great degree of taking to be true, so that one would wager on it that something is true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-662":["Belief is either a belief in things, or in a person. One can take a thing to be true immediately, merely because one has grounds for the thing, and then it is called believing a thing. but on the contrary one can also believe a thing immediately because a person passes it off as true, which one otherwise would not in itself have taken to be true, and then one believes in a person. But this belief, which refers to a certain kind of person, is twofold. one sometimes believes everyone, be he who he will, merely because he says something, without attending to his person, or his *caractère* or station. if someone e.g. says: it has snowed in his region, or many ships had arrived, then one believes this simply, without however investigating much; one thus does not judge at all thereby about the credibility of the witnesses. but again in certain things one does not believe everyone at all, but a particular credibility of the one who passes something off as true is required by us for it, so that one accepts such, and takes it to be true. But believing toward a person is either moral, or historical. The former consists in trust in the uprightness of the other, although he has made no statement of the narration. But the other consists in taking to be true what the other states, merely because he affirms it. For historical belief no particular morality need be in the other, but one takes something to be true because he narrates something that has happened. But moral belief requires that one have a particular trust toward a person that it will be kind, and upright. e.g. if a faithful servant in the absence of his master, upon a great danger arising, leaves his own things in the lurch, and rescues the things of his master, in the hope that he will well reward him for these services, then this is a moral belief. Historical belief [n] is required alone in theology, that one takes something to be true on account of a statement. But everyone, even the most wicked, can have this belief. Without fidelity, and faith no *republique*, no commonwealth at all could subsist, also no *pacta* could take place. Fidelity is always required with regard to him who promises something, that he also keeps what is promised, but faith with regard to him to whom something is promised, namely that he accept as true that the other will keep his promise. Both must be combined with one another. For even if everyone kept faith, but no one believed, then no agreements etc. could take place, and just as little if all believe, but no one wanted to keep faith. Belief is thus very necessary in common life, if no one wished to give credit, it would really look bad very often, for one cannot always pay cash at once. It also indicates a very bad way of thinking if one trusts no one with anything but wants to see everything that is promised and pledged to one immediately present, and fulfilled. The Greeks were of such a kind, they believed no one, but wanted to have everything paid out in cash at once. Hence also the proverb arose: *graeca fide mercari*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-663":["Theological belief trusts someone merely on his statement, and takes such to be true. In historical belief the object of belief is always a thing that has happened, an experience of the other, but not a judgment of reason, hence e.g. if someone said to me with confidence: Believe me, there are inhabitants in the moon, I will naturally be able to answer him: have you been there? Have you seen them? That which you have produced merely through reason, that I cannot believe you. . consequently all judgments where the other rationalizes are not objects of historical belief. Theological belief is thus merely historical. But religious belief is not merely historical, but moral, since one takes something to be true on account of a promise, and therefore undertakes certain actions, in the hope of one day reaping good sequels from them in the future. Through this belief one must hope that there will be a future world, and future rewards, or punishments of these actions. Natural, moral belief is in all the virtuous, since one believes that good actions will be rewarded already of themselves, even though one also saw no particular promise before one thereby. Such a virtuous person is to be compared with a *creditor*, he hopes for the future good, granted that he also has as yet no trace of it. But here we do not speak at all of this moral belief, even though it is the most worthy of consideration, and most excellent, since one receives credit, and trusts not merely the present, but the future, since one wants to have not everything paid in the world, but also some things in a future life.",[]],"blomberg-s08-664":["All *dogmata* permit no other ground of taking to be true than either one's own experience, or that one sets up observations of reason about them, or rationalizes. The experience of others thus has place therein. it follows, then, that it also cannot be cognized through belief. [841a]",[]],"blomberg-s08-665":["But the subject which states something, and which I am to believe, must chiefly have three properties: 1*mo*) A capacity to set up an experience. it must thus have a rational reflection. 2*do*) it must have a capacity to retain faithfully, and to preserve, the experience set up; or have a good memory. 3*tio*) it must also be able distinctly to declare, to present, and to make known to everyone the experiences set up. But the moral constitution of the subject is that it also actually have the disposition of mind, *seu ut animum habeat*, to represent, to declare the experiences faithfully, and not otherwise, just as they were set up by it, and the pragmatic principle for believing the subject is finally: that it have no grounds at all or no advantages to expect if it states the opposite of the experience it has set up.",[]],"blomberg-s08-666":["What then can we accept on belief? on belief one cannot accept dogmatic cognitions which the other states, but merely experience. universal cognitions of reason one cannot accept on belief because one can very easily err with them. Statements of *dogmata* are thus not testimonies, but statements of empirical cognitions, of experiences, are testimonies. A witness is thus properly he who states an experience. But he who states universal judgments of reason as true is no witness, and the best historical belief thus also does not extend to *dogmata* (except insofar as they are divine *dogmata*), but merely to experiences. But the conditions under which a subject that has set up experiences is to be believed are grounded partly in its own properties, partly in its state. The properties of the subject are partly logical, since it is capable of having, setting up, knowing, and stating experiences. Partly moral, since it also has the will to state the truth, or to declare the experiences just as they have been. But in credibility with regard to the state one must also divide the state",[]],"blomberg-s08-667":["A:) into the logical state and B ) into the practical state: the most upright human being can sometimes come into such relations that his uprightness succumbs. E.g. if he is held back by threats or promises from saying the truth, hence one must also pay heed to his state.",[]],"blomberg-s08-668":["A great part of our cognitions arises through belief, and without belief we would have to do without very many historical cognitions. we would have no greater cognitions than at most of the place where we live, and of the time in which we live.",[]],"blomberg-s08-669":["A great aid of historical cognitions is thus to adopt the experiences of others, through this we can experience what happened 1000 years before us, and 1000 miles distant from us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-670":["If a very great deception of the senses took place, then we could not trust the experiences which others have set up, if further those who set up experiences had so defective a memory that they could not remember them, and fantasy brought forth other images to them, if finally our language had such faults that we could not express ourselves rightly, and communicate our experiences, then the communication of foreign experiences would be wholly impossible. but if this is also presupposed, as it also is actually true, that 1:) *mo* we always have the capacity to set up experiences, and then 2:) our memory is also faithful enough to retain, and preserve, the experiences had, without being disturbed by fantasy, 3) we are also capable in our language of making known to others all experiences set up, then for the credibility of this one, or that one, who have set up experiences, it will still be required that they are honest, and have an inclination to truth. But this inclination to truth often arises more from love of honor than from honesty. because namely the whole *commercium* of human beings would be abolished if no one stated the truth, since then no one would trust the other: because also the lie is something harmful, and the statement of the truth is the surest way to escape contempt. e.g. geography, physics, history, and other sciences always presuppose the experiences of others. in common life one must not believe the common man with regard to such cognitions as do not concern the *interesse* of all human beings, but are indifferent. For in such cases it is all the same to him whether something is constituted thus, or otherwise, since he does not have insight into the importance of the cognition. But the learned man already has much more credibility in this kind of cognitions, for to him in all cognition even the least will be of importance, also he will be more inclined out of love of honor to state everything as exactly as he has experienced it, since otherwise he would lose his credit.",[]],"blomberg-s08-671":["As for the rest, as concerns the credibility, and honesty of the witnesses who state experiences set up, everyone is held to be honest, and sincere so long until the opposite, namely that he departs from the truth etc., has been proved. According to the well-known rule of equity:",[]],"blomberg-s08-672":["*Quilibet prosumitur bonus,*",[]],"blomberg-s08-673":["*Donec probetur contrarium.*",[]],"blomberg-s08-674":["But in order to attain to the right cognition of the truth, it is certainly required that one also investigate this, whether someone speaks the truth, or lies.",[]],"blomberg-s08-675":["An eyewitness is such a one as has himself set up experiences, or seen something, thus is completely convinced of a knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-676":["But an ear-witness is one who has heard, i.e., received, something from an eyewitness, or who is a witness of the testimony of the eyewitness. It is one of the greatest human faults that they are so very inclined, when they are ear-witnesses of a thing, gladly to be eyewitnesses. they narrate a thing with such a certainty, and confidence, as if they had seen themselves, though they have only received from others. But what difference is there in the credibility of eyewitnesses and ear-witnesses? The ear-witness has as it were only half as much credibility as the eye[ n] witness, for with him I must first investigate his own, and then also the credibility of him who has seen the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-677":["In the series of witnesses subordinated to one another credibility decreases. but in the series of witnesses coordinated with one another the probability, and credibility of the testimony always increases. e.g. if someone describes the history of a learned man, or of a king, but his successor does not hand down the history itself, but clothes it in his style, and this happens so *continuo* ever further, then the credibility becomes thereby ever smaller, and smaller, but if many at the same time state one and the very same thing, then the credibility is only all the greater, especially if these are people who love honor.",[]],"blomberg-s08-678":["The series of witnesses coordinated with one another is called public rumor *sive rumor*. The series of statements subordinated to one another is called an oral tradition, *traditio oralis*. but if in the series of witnesses both coordinated and subordinated with one another the first originator, i.e., the eyewitness, is unknown, then such is a mere common saying, *sive rumor sine capite*. and such a statement is to be trusted little, or not at all, little belief is to be accorded it. But there are certain statements in which one has no need at all to investigate the credibility of the witness, but which one already rejects before all investigation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-679":["This seems indeed at first sight to be very harsh, but it is nevertheless also certain. Cognitions which are wholly, and completely, and obviously false need no investigation at all, after all, and certainly deserve complete contempt. Of this sort are the statements about the divining rod, these deserve no investigation, one can reject them at once, they deserve no refutation, for they are in, and for themselves false.",[]],"blomberg-s08-680":["There are thus grounds for rejecting a testimony which are not historical or taken from the constitution of the witness, but are rational, and lie in the constitution of the thing itself. But yet one must be very cautious in this, and not reject a cognition at once before it is evident that the thing is obviously false. Much seems at first sight to be wholly false, but upon closer investigation one finds the opposite of it. all empirical judgments of the possibility or impossibility of an *eventus* do not yet entitle us to reject the cognition at once, without investigating the credibility of the witness in it. Someone testifies to something which is very improbable, and contradicts the ordinary occurrences in nature, then he cannot yet demand that one should believe him at once on his statement, but if he wants to have this, then he must conduct the proof. so e.g. when Pontoppidan writes that in certain seas there is an exceedingly large fish which looks like a piece of land, and which he names Crac, then he must, because such an animal has never yet been seen, prove such. but if someone testifies to something which agrees evidently, and well with experience, and reason, then one believes him without proof. but if the credibility of the witness cannot be made to waver at all, but the narration or the testimony that he gives contradicts reason and experience in the highest degree, then the question is, must one in this case give assent to his statement, and as it were deny one's reason, or not?",[]],"blomberg-s08-681":["Answer: The best is then neither to reject the cognition, nor to accept it as true, but to postpone one's assent so long until one has more grounds for, or against it. There are sometimes persons who state something, of whose credibility one cannot doubt in the least, of whom one also cannot assume that they had the *animum*, or intention, to deceive others, whom one may nevertheless not trust The fault of their statement then lies for the most part only in the experience not set up, or in a *vitium* of the memory, which does not retain it faithfully enough. Hence it also happens that people who have put forward something very probably, and asserted it as true, when one asks them: whether they could well swear to this their statement? doubt, and must take all sorts of evasions. They then commonly say they could well have forgotten something since that time. Fantasy can also certainly reproduce for them other images than the experiences were which they have had.",[]],"blomberg-s08-682":["Belief can be taken in the moral sense, when one has a lack of all trust in the moral constitution of a witness. He who has such a moral unbelief holds all human beings to be liars, he trusts no human being, he believes no promises, but always doubts the sincerity of other people. A moral unbelief is contrary to the moral foundation of all human society. without fidelity and faith, as has already been indicated above, no state can subsist. But it is commonly to be found in those who themselves possess a moral *pravitaet*, so that one cannot accord them much belief.",[]],"blomberg-s08-683":["These people also judge other people according to themselves. Moral belief, on the contrary, trusts everyone, even without any promises. Of this sort is belief in a future world. But incredulity is distinct from unbelief. It consists in this, that one will not give assent to the logical grounds of a testimony. This incredulity can also be called logical belief, and is thus very distinct from moral unbelief.",[]],"blomberg-s08-684":["This logical unbelief does not lie in a certain lack of the heart, but of cognition, in a certain inexperience, since one is not acquainted with the grounds of others, and does not have insight into their importance. It is therefore found chiefly in those who do not have a proper knowledge of literature, to these everything seems paradoxical, and ridiculous, which is otherwise true, and credible. one must thus guard oneself very much against having to do with such people, because one is not at all able to convince them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-685":["A seeing belief is that which is combined with examination of the witness, and rests on it. But a blind belief is that which accepts a testimony without examination, and investigation of its credibility. The former leads to truth, but the other is the way to error, thus harmful, but the former useful.",[]],"blomberg-s08-686":["The union of all these three sources of cognition rests on this, that they have not one, but many proofs as ground. Belief always requires a *connubium* of other sources of cognition, namely of reason, and of experience, so that it becomes evident, and convincing. If one source of cognition is not able to give a sufficient proof of the truth of a thing, then one must take the remaining *fontes cognitionis* to help, and seek to bring forth a greater evidence from them. But if one source of cognition is adequate to give a complete proof of a thing, then one does not need the others. If something can be demonstrated completely and evidently through reason, then one does not need experience in order to make the proof still more complete. If experience is sufficient to give a complete proof of a thing, then one need not consult reason.",[]],"blomberg-s09-001":["If a cognition contains in itself motives to actions, then it is practical. all practical cognition is either a cognition of ends, or of means, the latter must be distinguished from the former. Practical geometry teaches no ends, no motives to actions, but only means to attain to certain ends. it shows the conditions under which an end can be reached with regard to measurement.",[]],"blomberg-s09-002":["All doctrine of skill is a practical cognition of means. but all doctrine of morality is a practical cognition of ends, as is also the doctrine of prudence. our author speaks in general in this whole section of cognition as it relates to the free will. but in logic the relation of cognition to the will is not considered at all, rather this belongs in morals. The relation of the free will is not an *objectum domesticum* of logic. The practical science of skill gives us no motives at all to certain actions. For every science which shows only means, and no ends, contains no motives all motives namely must be derived from the end. a cognition is called practical insofar as it is *contradistinguiret* from theoretical",[]],"blomberg-s09-003":["cognition, or from mere contemplation, and speculation. But a cognition is practical *vel explicite, vel implicite*. *Explicite* when it shows the rules themselves to actions. *implicite* when it offers the motives or represents such truths from which certain practical rules to actions can be derived. e.g. the proposition: you shall not lie, you shall be faithful, and honest, is *explicite* practical. But the proposition: There is a God, consequently one is obligated also to arrange one's actions in such a way as is in conformity with the holy will of this divine being, is *implicite* practical. In metaphysics there are for the most part pure, not practical, but theoretical *speculationes*, which thus have no particular influence on the free will. Such *speculationes* are neither harmful nor useful. For they contain no grounds at all to actions. Many subtleties, and errors devised by human beings are nothing other than pure speculations, we thus pass over this section, because it is placed here unfittingly, and belongs more in morals than in logic, and in logic no advantage is to be hoped for from the treatment of it, for this likewise occupies itself with speculative cognitions.",[]],"blomberg-s10-001":["Not every representation is a concept. A representation through the senses is e.g. a sensation. A representation through the understanding is an appearance. A representation through reason is a concept. The senses sense, the understanding coordinates, reason on the other hand subordinates. general representations are concepts, and concepts are general representations. Sensations on the other hand are only single representations, appearances likewise also only, concepts however are general and arise through reason. As long as I cannot yet subordinate a thing to any general representation, so long do I as yet have no concept of it. of many items we have a learned cognition, but still no proper concept.",[]],"blomberg-s10-002":["With learned and unlearned concepts there is not exactly any specific difference to be noted. Better is the difference between common and learned concepts. A concept is not exactly a simple representation. The concept, the result of many representations, can be very rich, pregnant, beautiful, and learned, and lively. A concept that possesses much liveliness is an aesthetic concept. An aesthetic concept arises through a multitude, and combination of many coordinated representations. A proper concept of reason, however, arises through a multitude of subordinated representations. The matter of an aesthetic concept consists of its relation to our taste, and feeling, the form however in a multitude of representations coordinated with one another, in order thereby to produce the requisite liveliness. Such aesthetic concepts are used especially by the poets, and the orators, when they e.g. want to describe the summer, or the spring, they do not show the causes of these, but rather they only depict the manifold alterations that go on at these seasons in the field, among the animals, in the forests, in the air, in the country, and in the city. of many things we have, to be sure, sensations, but no concepts. Concepts we encounter only in certain cases, where reason subordinates the representations to one another.",[]],"blomberg-s10-003":["This division in this *§pho* is in no way correct. Through abstraction we obtain no representations, but rather representations must already be given before abstraction, and through it only become clear. all our cognitions are either given, or made, and feigned. *omnes Conceptus Sunt vel Dati, vel facti, sive Ficti* [all concepts are either given, or made, or feigned].",[]],"blomberg-s10-004":["Through experience many cognitions are given to us, and the matter of all cognitions springs from sensation, through the senses. often, however, one feigns cognitions for oneself through fantasy, we form as it were our own world.",[]],"blomberg-s10-005":["*Conceptus dati sunt porro dati a priori* [given concepts are further given *a priori*] through pure reason, *vel dati a posteriori* [or given *a posteriori*] through experiences. all *conceptus a posteriori dati* [concepts given *a posteriori*] are drawn off *ab Experientia* [from experience]. others however do not spring from experiences, but rather are drawn off from the laws of pure reason e.g. the concept of the possible and the impossible. A *Conceptus abstractus* [abstracted concept], however, signifies especially a representation that is drawn off from experience, and not a representation drawn off from reason.",[]],"blomberg-s10-006":["The *Conceptus dati sunt* [given concepts are] thus *vel abstracti* [either abstracted] from experience, *vel rationales* [or rational].",[]],"blomberg-s10-007":["The concept of all species, and genera of things that fall into our senses is a *Conceptus abstractus* e.g. of horses sheep etc.: when I say a horse, then this is a concept given *per Experientiam* [through experience]. The generality of representations, however, has arisen through abstraction. Of all concepts through experience the generality springs through abstraction from single concepts and images the representation itself however arises through experience. The matter itself thus lies herein in experience, the form of generality however lies in abstraction. Not the least cognition springs through abstraction, but generality arises thereby. The first origin of our cognition is thus experience. Concepts of experience are thus those that are given through experience, and have become general through abstraction. Hence all concepts of experience are abstracted concepts. The pure concepts of reason, however, are not given through experience by means of isolation, but rather through pure reason, and thereby precisely they are distinguished from the concepts of experience. The concept of right, and wrong is in this way a pure concept of reason. These concepts remain constant, supposing that also no experience took place. if the world were ever so godless, everyone would still have the concept of right, and wrong. The concepts of experience, however, do not take place at all without experience. if there were no horse, then no one would be in a position to fictionalize a horse for himself.",[]],"blomberg-s10-008":["Fabricated concepts, however, are those that were made, insofar as they are not given. These fabricated concepts are called *Idea prototypa* [prototypal ideas]. The abstracted concepts, however, are εκτυπα [ectypes]. The former are the originals. These concepts, however, are the copies. He who made the fine fairy tales has made nothing but *Prototypa*, for such a thing does not exist at all.",[]],"blomberg-s10-009":["The Stoic philosopher on the other hand is such a Προτοτυπον ἀρχέτυπον [prototype, archetype]. all definitions of the mathematicians contain nothing but *Ideas Prototypas* e.g. the concept of the octagon is not borrowed from experience, but rather the mathematician thinks it for himself through his pure reason. he represents to himself a figure that has 8 lines, and is enclosed by them.",[]],"blomberg-s10-010":["Every inventor must therefore fabricate, and fictionalize, and all inventions are fictions, although not all fictions are inventions. Fiction produces archetypes abstraction copies through voluntary combination, or through voluntary separation.",[]],"blomberg-s10-011":["Through leaving out, and adding one makes *Fictiones* [fictions]. all fiction is further voluntary, and involuntary. Involuntary fiction is a *Chimera*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-012":["Hypochondriac persons are very much subject to involuntary fictions. Here however is now properly the place to speak of voluntary fictions, which are combined with consciousness, and will to have them. If the human being can subject everything to choice, then he is the most perfect among all beings, and hence voluntary fictions will also often be very useful to him.",[]],"blomberg-s10-013":["All concepts that do not spring through attention, through the senses, that also are not given, but rather voluntarily feigned, are pure concepts of reason. Of this sort are the concepts of the necessary, and contingent, of causes, and effects. Concepts however that spring through the senses, and are drawn off from experiences, are concepts of experience.",[]],"blomberg-s10-014":["We must chiefly endeavor to distinguish concepts of experience from concepts of reason. what does not fall into the senses does not come before experience. from the mixing of concepts of experience and of reason arises the *vitium Subreptionis* [vice of subreption], of which there is talk in the *Metaphysica*. Right, and wrong are not concepts of experience, but rather pure concepts of reason, they would however be concepts of experience if wrong were what is not usual, what human beings do not do.",[]],"blomberg-s10-015":["This however never takes place. much is right that no one does, and wrong that all do. With the concept of cause and effect many commit the *vitium Subreptionis*, that they want to derive it from experience, since it is after all a pure concept of reason. Concepts of experience all refer to space and time. Concepts of reason however to the foundation of cause, and effect. of possibility, and impossibility we have no experience, but rather only of actuality. also we have experiences only of affirming, but not of negating representations, that which cannot be cannot become sensation. e.g. when we have read a book, we cannot immediately experience that this, or that passage is not contained in it, but rather this we must bring out through inferences.",[]],"blomberg-s10-016":["Herein we have not exactly anything to note, metaphysics namely speaks of it more extensively. The only question still remaining to us is how does one make concepts out of many experiences? this is not so easy as it perhaps seems at first sight. one can have made many experiences, but yet have drawn off no concepts of experience from them.",[]],"blomberg-s10-017":["To attain concepts of experience requires an instruction. anyone who has good *Organa Sensoria* [sense organs] can experience, but to draw off from single experiences general concepts of experience, under which the *Experientiae Singulae* [single experiences] can be subordinated, requires a skill of having insight into what is common to many experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s10-018":["One cannot make money by robbing someone of it, and just so one can also make no concepts through isolation. Through isolation our representations are only made general, as has already been indicated above. if we have no representations of things at all, then also no abstraction will be in a position to make concepts for us. we compare in logical isolation many concepts with one another, we see what they have in common in themselves, or wherein they agree, and thereby our representations become concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s10-019":["If many concepts have a mark in common, then this mark is contained in them. The things are contained under the concept that is isolated from them. This concept is a common ground of cognizing. it is contained in all things, and all things from which it is abstracted are under it. A concept can be abstracted from experience, and also from the laws of pure reason. The latter are not isolated from things, but rather are called rational.",[]],"blomberg-s10-020":["By the isolated concepts, however, concepts of experience are always understood.",[]],"blomberg-s10-021":["We want first to speak somewhat of fabricated concepts. every concept that has been given neither through experience, i.e. the sensation of things through our senses, nor through reason, is a feigned concept. This concept, however, is either made, so that with respect to the matter an experience still lies at the ground, or that not even the matter is taken from experience, but rather both the matter as well as the form is feigned. The latter however is impossible. All fiction however arises either through voluntary combination, or through separation. Through combination, when I connect together that which in experience is never combined, and united with one another. Through separation however, when I isolate from a thing that which in experience is never separated from it. For the most part in feigned concepts voluntary combination, and voluntary separation are combined with one another. The writer of novels ascribes to his hero all the praiseworthy properties still lacking to him, in order to present a truly perfect person, and if he only notices some faults in him, then he immediately abstracts from them, he isolates them all at once from him, or if this also often hardly, or not at all [889/890] is feasible, then he is at least concerned to make him praiseworthy even in his transgressions, and to be able to give all his vices such a coating, or semblance, that they either lose the ugliness proper to them, and striking the eye, or indeed seem to be just so many virtues.",[]],"blomberg-s10-022":["The Arcadian shepherd's life, so famous among the ancients, and so much painted up by the poets of that time, is of this kind, it is one of the most agreeable representations that one can ever have, or represent to oneself. namely everything beautiful, charming, and attractive is thought of it, the greatest abundance of everything that can attract combined with it, and on the other hand all, and every adversity, displeasure, all lack, which after all also very often has been found, and is usually found, in the shepherd's life, isolated from it, and removed, so that only thereby a wholly perfect ideal of happiness might be sketched.",[]],"blomberg-s10-023":["all *Conceptus* however are either",[]],"blomberg-s10-024":["a) *conceptus singulares* [singular concepts] or b) *conceptus communes* [common concepts].",[]],"blomberg-s10-025":["With the former I think to myself only one thing, through the latter however I already go further, I think to myself namely there already that which is common to many things.",[]],"blomberg-s10-026":["The former concepts thus consider something individual. so e.g. *Roma*, *Bu cephalus* etc. This is a *Conceptus Singularis*. on the other hand the latter go to a *Complexum* [complex] of many individual things, so a city e.g. in general, a 4-footed animal etc. A human being, that is a *Conceptus communis*. The representations of immediate experience are all *Conceptus Singulares*, for they represent single things. Mediate concepts of experience, however, which are abstracted from many experiences, are *conceptus communes*, so also all our concepts of reason. The concept of God belongs, to be sure, only to one being, to which alone it is to be ascribed, yet one can, without settling whether more, or only one God is possible, make out more concepts of God. Afterwards, however, a proof must be added that the concept of God is a *Conceptus Singularis*. every *conceptus communis* contains under itself all concepts to which it is common. E.g. the concept of mortality holds under itself all human beings, and animals, because it is common to them. Yet such a *Conceptus Communis* is contained in all those things that it contains under itself. The *Conceptus Communis* is the ground of cognition, or the mark of all those that are under them. it is however also at the same time a partial concept of the things that it contains under itself, for it is abstracted from the things that are contained under it.",[]],"blomberg-s10-027":["The marks are always coordinated to a thing, and as parts together make up the whole concept of the thing, the thing however is subordinated to the marks that have been given of it, and contained in them. The *Sphaera notionis* [sphere of the notion] properly signifies the multitude of things that are comprehended under a concept, as a *Nota Communis* [common mark]. Through a *Notam Communem* I can thus think to myself many things. The *Sphaera Conceptus Communis* [sphere of the common concept], however, can be large, and can be small: the very same concept under which many things are comprehended is contained in the things that are under it.",[]],"blomberg-s10-028":["The larger *Sphaeram* a *Conceptus Communis* has, and the more it holds under itself, the less is contained in it. A concept that contains much under itself arises in that that has been abstracted which is common to many things. it is thus a mark of a mark, has thus, to be sure, much under itself, but little in itself. For a mark of a mark indeed contains still much less than the first mark, of which it is a mark. If I think to myself something of many things, then this, which I think of these many things, is very little. The less, however, a concept contains under itself, the more it holds in itself, and a single concept contains the most in itself, for here I cognize, to be sure, only one object, but very much in it. The more I think in my concept, the less do I think through the concept. A *Conceptus Communis* arises only in that that is considered which is common to many objects. That however which they have that is different is left out, and not considered. This *Conceptus* contains, to be sure, little in itself, but is a concept of a large *Sphaera* e.g. The concept of body has a large *Sphaeram*, but contains little in itself. A concept can never become of greater domain, so that it contains more under itself, than in that it contains less in itself. This consideration teaches us especially to estimate the worth of our cognition. Through reason we cognize nothing but *Notas Communes*, thus little of many things, there is much under, but little in the cognitions of reason contained. Through experience we cognize few objects, but very much in them. The concepts of experience, however, are *minores Sphaerae* [of smaller spheres] but they contain much in themselves. A mark of a mark always has a larger *Sphaeram* than a nearer mark, or always contains more under itself than a *Nota proprior* [nearer mark]. The *Nota* of a thing in general is the *Nota Remotißima* [most remote mark] of a thing. Afterwards comes the *Nota remotior*, *remota* [more remote, remote mark], and then only come the nearer marks. The *Nota remotissima*, however, contains the most under itself. If several concepts stand in such a relation that one is contained under the other, then that which contains the other under itself is a *Conceptus Superior* [higher concept], and the concept that is contained under the other the *Conceptus inferior* [lower concept]. One, and the very same concept, however, can according to different relations, according namely as it contains other concepts under itself, and also stands under another, be now a *Conceptus Superior*, now a *Conceptus inferior*. E.g. I say human beings. then I compare with the irrational animals the human being, and say animals so the concept of the human being already contains very much under itself. e.g. the white human beings, and the Negroes, and all sorts of nations etc. and is thus in this respect a *Conceptus Superior*. it is however a *Conceptus inferior* insofar as it is contained under the concept animals. The *Conceptus nullo inferior* [concept lower than none], however, is the *Conceptus Summus* [highest concept]. To this belongs the concept of a thing, or of a possible. This *Conceptus Summus* is not merely *relative*, but rather *Absolute Superior* [absolutely higher]. The *Conceptus infimus* [lowest concept], however, is that which is not only *relative*, but rather *absolute inferior*. The *Conceptus Singulares* are *infimi*. The *conceptus universales* [universal concepts], however, are *Summi*, and between these *conceptibus infimis, et Summis* there is a *Longam Seriem* [long series] of *conceptibus inferioribus*, and *Superioribus*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-029":["Every mark is a ground of cognition of things, we cognize all things through reason only by means of marks. The more a concept is removed from the immediate mark of a thing, the higher it is, and the more it contains under itself. The very same *conceptus communis* is called, with respect to the *Conceptus Communes* that it contains under itself, relatively a *Genus*, but with respect to the *Conceptus Communes* under which it is contained, it is called relatively a *Species*. E.g. unlearned are a *Genus* with respect to the particular species of learned, the philosophers, physicians, etc. jurists. they are however a *Species* with respect to the concept of a human being in general. A *conceptus communis* is called *Species* because it *1 mo* is contained under another *Communi Superiori conceptu* [common higher concept], but also 2: because it is itself a *con ceptus communis*. For a *Conceptus Singularis*, even if it is subordinated to another *Conceptui Communi*, is not a *Species* e.g. when I think to myself among the learned Herr von Wolff, this is no longer a *Species*, but rather an *Individuum*. Every *Species* is *infima* [lowest] insofar as under it no *conceptus communes*, but rather merely *Singulares* are contained. *Species infima est, quae tantum individua sub se continet* [the lowest species is that which contains only individuals under itself]. otherwise one can always think a *Species* again as a *Genus*, and subordinate other concepts to it. E.g. a philosopher is a *Species* of the learned. this however becomes a *Genus* when I think to myself under it especially the Wolffians, Crusians etc. all *Genera* that are contained under higher *Generibus* are, with respect to these *generum*, *Specierum*, to be regarded as *Species*. The *Genus Summum* [highest genus], however, is that which can no longer be a *Species*. The *Species infima*, however, can never any longer be a *genus*. in the first case it is subordinated to another, in the second case however others are subordinated to it. Just so the very same concept can be both *Genus*, and also *Species* in different respects. The subordination of concepts, however, can happen both *Logice* [logically], and *realiter* [really]. The logical subordination consists in this, that I take that which is common to many concepts with one another, and thereby form for myself a general concept, under which I can subordinate the single representations. in this way I make for myself various *Genera*, and subordinate to them the *Species*, and *Individua*. The real subordination, however, consists in this, that I actually combine the concepts with one another, so that not only is one contained under the other, but rather they also *cohaeriren* [cohere] as causes, and effects.",[]],"blomberg-s10-030":["Now we come further to the *Sphaeram Notionum* [sphere of notions]. the more isolated a concept is, the larger is its *Sphaera*. the larger the *Sphaera Notionis* is, of so many more things can I judge, but of so many fewer things can I cognize. The senses cognize in few things much, reason however in many things little. Reason, and experience must thus be united as much as possible, in order to be able to replace through the other ground of cognition that which is lacking to the one ground of cognition.",[]],"blomberg-s10-031":["The concept that contains another under itself is a *Conceptus Latior* [wider concept], the concept however that is contained under another is a *Conceptus angustior* [narrower concept]. So the concept of the human being is *Conceptus Latior* with respect to the learned. The concept of the jurist, etc. philosopher however a *Conceptus angustior* in regard to the concept of the learned. The first concept is thus that which contains only a single thing under itself, and through which I can cognize only a single one.",[]],"blomberg-s10-032":["The *Conceptus Latissimus* [widest concept], however, is that through which I can cognize the most things, or to which the most things are subordinated, and contained under it.",[]],"blomberg-s10-033":["A reciprocal concept is that which contains just as much under and in itself as another concept which thus can fittingly be interchanged with it, and of which one can completely take the place of the other. A general, or common concept is that under which many are contained. each and every *Conceptus Communis* is also at the same time, with respect to those concepts that are contained under it, a *Conceptus universalis*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-034":["A concept can be contained only in part under another concept. *Quare Conceptus inferior Semper est particularis, ratione conceptus Superioris, quare Conceptus Superior vero Semper est universalis, ratione conceptus inferioris* [Hence the lower concept is always particular in relation to the higher concept, hence the higher concept however is always universal in relation to the lower concept] All abstract cognition namely with respect to the concepts that are contained under them is the most general cognition, that is, that which contains the *conceptus Summos* under itself, and descends from these to all *Conceptibus inferioribus* that are contained under them. Metaphysics is a science that contains such *Conceptus Summos* under itself, it hence also cognizes the most general constitution of things.",[]],"blomberg-s10-035":["This *Paragraphum* we can fittingly pass over, for here there is not talk of inferences, but rather only of learned concepts. in the doctrine of inferences will be the place to speak of it, *quare hoc non tractare volumus anticipando* [hence we do not wish to treat this by anticipating].",[]],"blomberg-s10-036":["Here the author speaks of the use of abstract cognition. It is very great, yet it is not very necessary to cite it particularly, and go through it, because the greatest part of all our cognitions rests on it.",[]],"blomberg-s10-037":["The author speaks here now of concepts through voluntary combination. all *conceptus ficti* [feigned concepts] however are made *vel per Combinationem, vel per Separationem arbitrariam* [either through combination, or through voluntary separation]. yet it is to be noted in general that in all our feigned concepts only the form is, and becomes, feigned.",[]],"blomberg-s10-038":["The matter will never be able to be feigned, he who has no sight cannot feign for himself any color, he who has no hearing cannot feign for himself any tone. all form however is feigned according to the relation of space, and of time, and according to the general nature of our reason. All concepts are either empirical, or transcendental concepts of reason. To the latter concepts belong the representations of the possible and impossible, of the necessary, and contingent, these by no means spring from experience, and are not abstracted from it, but rather they are produced through pure reason. Concepts are thus either given, or made. given they are either through experience, or through pure reason. Made they are when they have been given neither through experience, nor through pure reason. These made concepts, however, are always feigned only as to form. Through isolation I think of a part of the concept, through separation however I negate something of my concept. Isolation, abstraction, separation, *Separation* are thus distinguished from one another. Through separation alone spring feigned concepts, through isolation however never. To a *Fictione Separando facta* [fiction made by separating] belongs a *remotion* [removal] of that which is otherwise usually combined with a concept. one feigns much *Separando* [by separating], all novels spring through this kind of fiction. in just this way one must also fictionalize for oneself the idea of God, in that one isolates all imperfection from it, and thus arrives at the concept of the highest being. Through combination however one fictionalizes something when one puts together much that is never connected with one another in experience. In this way, as has already been indicated above, the novels arise. The Stoic sage arises through this fiction, one gives him more power than is proper to the human being. Fictions are either *Fictiones aestheticae*, or *heuristicae* [aesthetic, or heuristic fictions]. The former serve for gratification, the latter for inventing.",[]],"blomberg-s10-039":["Mathematics, and physics have many *Fictiones heuristicas*, of this sort are the hypotheses, the *Fictiones* happen further either according to *Qualitaet* [quality], or according to *Quantitaet* [quantity]. The *Fictiones Aestheticae* according to quality are the best, those according to quantity however are not exactly the best. For to them not a great art is required, except merely in the description, in order thereby to obtain a lively concept. If I only first have an object, then I can soon attribute to it an enormous magnitude, which can, to be sure, cause admiration, but no being moved. There are however also involuntary fictions, or involuntary",[]],"blomberg-s10-040":["feigned concepts of reason. e.g. of spirits. These concepts have not sprung, or been given, through experience nor through reason. To these involuntary concepts one comes through an unnoticed fiction, and through secret inferences which one often does not become aware of at all.",[]],"blomberg-s10-041":["The distinctness of a concept is a property of a logical perfection. Making distinct happens through analysis. but when I make distinct a concept that e.g. contains many marks, then I shall have to seek to avoid tautology among these marks, I shall thus promote the *Praecision* [precision] of the marks. i.e. I shall restrict the quantity of the marks, without however being detrimental to their quality. also the *Totalitaet* [totality] of the distinct representation of a thing is asked about, and this totality I often need very much. in all distinctness of concepts I can be content with *Partialitaet* [partiality], but if my marks are sufficient for the whole concept of the thing, then I have a totality. but if the precision is also asked for, then I have a complete, precise concept of a thing, and that is a definition. The definition, and the action of our soul therein can be perfectly compared with the reduction of fractions *ad minimos Terminos* [to lowest terms] occurring in arithmetic, e.g. 4/2 3/6 2/3 1/3 = 4/12. and this is also the aim of defining. we want to have 1) a distinct. 2) a complete and then 3) a precise concept of a thing. in order thus to seek whether a definition is correct, we thus always ask, 1) whether this alleged definition is true as a proposition, and so we can use many *definitiones* as true propositions, in which many otherwise unnoticeable marks occur to us. False *definitiones* can sometimes even be of use to us as true propositions, indeed one can even often quite well do without the whole definition on account of the usability of such a true proposition. supposing it does not fulfill what it promised, it can still be so useful, it can always produce use, although it does not attain its aim.",[]],"blomberg-s10-042":["2:) Let one ask whether the definition as a concept is distinct, whether I can cognize it clearly. for every distinctness, however, it is required that a mark be a clear ground of cognition of the thing. all tautology in definitions is thus a lack of distinctness, and such a definition is not only no definition, but rather also no distinct concept. all empty propositions are tautological. The soul e.g. is in an animal a ruling monad, this proposition is not empty, but in it a *comparative obscurum per aeque obscurum* [comparatively obscure through the equally obscure] is explained. The uselessness of this proposition, however, can here still be removed. With tautology, however, this is not feasible at all. 3:) Let one ask whether the definition as a distinct concept is exhaustive, that is: distinct. *nam definire est fines conceptus alicuius ponere* [for to define is to posit the bounds of some concept]. I.e. the definition is a determination of all that which only belongs to the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s10-043":["*4 to* it is asked whether this distinct and exhaustive concept is also measured, that is precise, namely that the marks of the definition do not flow out of one another, or are subordinated to one another, but rather are coordinated to one another. The marks must stand together as *compartes* [co-parts] of a whole, but not next to one another as members in a series. If everything were left out without which a concept nevertheless remains distinct, and complete, then such a one is called: *conceptus rem adaequans* [concept adequate to the thing]. *Completudo* [completeness] thus, and the precision of a concept together make up its *adaequation* [adequacy]. An *Adaequatum* [adequate] thus admits of no *Comparativum* [comparative]. Wolff says: A concept is adequate when the marks of a concept, and these marks of the marks in turn are distinct. *Definitio autem adaequat Conceptum* [The definition, however, is adequate to the concept].",[]],"blomberg-s10-044":["There belongs finally to this precision, this consists namely in this, that one mark in the definition is not perchance already contained already in another, or what is the same, that no *repetition* is to take place in it. e.g. if I were to say: Body is an extended divisible matter, then this would be acted against precision, for the mark of divisibility already lies within the mark of matter, and consequently the mark of divisibility is only merely *repetirt* [repeated], for it already lies in the one mark of body namely in the concept of matter. In short the most correct, and best definition of precision is this:",[]],"blomberg-s10-045":["*Praecisio* is a *reductio* of a definition *ad minimos Terminos*. just as one is accustomed e.g. in *Arithmetica* to bring fractions *ad minimos Terminos*. Accordingly then the definition is nothing other than the relation of equality of two concepts, namely so that one can always be *Substituirt* [substituted] for the other.",[]],"blomberg-s10-046":["Now however we ask: is then the concept in the definition perhaps fully equal to the concept of the *Definiti* [thing defined]? and there we must note: *materialiter id est quoad objectum* [materially, that is, as regards the object] these concepts are indeed always equal to one another, but as regards the form, they are not, and must also not be, fully equal to one another. as regards the matter, I think to myself, to be sure, always the same object, but not in the same, but rather in another way, what namely I previously represented to myself *confuse* [confusedly] of the *Definito*, that I represent to myself distinctly in the definition, and accordingly every definition must be distinct. for distinctness in general, however, and thus also in the distinctness of definition, marks are required, these marks however must first be clear concepts, they must also be still clearer than the concept of the *Definiti*, and from this flows this canon: *ne definiatur obscurum per aequ e obscurum* [let the obscure not be defined through the equally obscure]. e.g. if I were to say to someone who does not know what *Monas* is: the soul is a monad thinking in the body, then this would be offending against this rule. Secondly, however, these marks must also be clear grounds of cognition. For as a ground of cognition it is after all to be distinguished from the sequence, consequently it must be thought otherwise, and far more clearly than in the *Definito*, for he names me no ground at all who only merely says the same. and from this then flows this rule: *ne Notae in definitione Sint Tavtologicae* [let the marks in a definition not be tautological]. otherwise one also calls this defining *per circulum* [through a circle]. The *Circulus vitiosus in definiendo* [vicious circle in defining] is either *implicitus* (hidden) or *Explicitus*, namely when even according to the expressions the marks are the same. of the first an example: e.g. the definition, the ground is that why something is cognized, there the why signifies just the same as *quâ ratione* [by what reason], and consequently herein a *Tavtologia implicita* is hidden, and hence also the form in the definition must always be other than the form of the *Definiti*. This was thus the first *requisitum* of a good definition, namely that it be distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s10-047":["The 2nd *requisitum* of a correct definition, now, is this, that it be adequate, or that it contain in itself neither more nor less than is contained in the *definitum*. one can also properly call it the *completudo*, or exhaustiveness and sufficiency. to the *completudo* of a definition, now, belongs that 1. the marks are sufficient of the *definitum* from all others. 2.) also be sufficient for the cognition of the identity of the same with others, or, in short, that the definition be neither *latior* nor *angustior*, but what does this mean? in comparison of the thing with others every ground of cognition is to be used in a twofold way.",[]],"blomberg-s10-048":["1*mo* in order from it the identity or",[]],"blomberg-s10-049":["2*do* the diversity of the same from all others to distinguish. thus the definition is complete when the marks are sufficient",[]],"blomberg-s10-050":["1*mo* the difference of the concept from all others that do not stand under the universal concept of the same, and",[]],"blomberg-s10-051":["2.) also the sameness of the same with other concepts, which stand with one another under a universal concept, to cognize from them, the former is the sufficiency for distinction, but the latter the sufficiency for agnition",[]],"blomberg-s10-052":["Now we can note: The more marks a concept has in itself, the less it is the same with others, thus if there are too many marks in the definition, this can indeed contribute to distinction, but not at all to the agnition of the concept, we can thus also say: the *definitio* is *angustior* if it does not suffice for the cognition of identity, and *latior* if it does not suffice for the cognition of diversity. or if there are too few marks there, then the definition is *latior*, but if there are too many marks there, then the definition is *angustior*. It is certainly curious, the means to cognize identity are exactly opposed to those which I make use of in order to have insight into their diversity from others. For the more I want to cognize the difference, the more I must heap up the marks, but the more I now want to have insight into the identity, the more I must diminish the number of marks. Now we can note this too: *Quo major conceptus determinatus est, eo angustior est quoque definitio.* otherwise, as concerns also the persons, we can say: People who have much wit commonly make *definitiones latiores*. they will e.g. quickly say: living beings are, which grow and generate. but e.g. one experience also generates the other, but for that reason it still does not generate a living being. This definition is thus *latior*. it serves not for the investigation of all things, and distinction of the thing from others, but rather for the cognition of the sameness of the thing with others, and from this we thus also see that the *latitudo* of a definition belongs to the cognition of identity. but as concerns the brooder, this one often makes *definitiones angustiores*. he seeks to find differences there where none are to be encountered, and that is the so-called *argutatio inanis*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-053":["From all this, now, that has been said, follows the logical rule, and the 3rd canon: *ne Definitio nec latior nec angustior su o Definito sit*, or with one expression: *Definitio et Definitum sint conceptus reciproci*",[]],"blomberg-s10-054":["That is the 3rd *requisitum* of a good definition, and it seems as if with this demand of definition all remaining precautions have an end. but since in different expressions yet an identical mark, which has already once occurred in the definition, can take place once more in the same, then in order to prevent this, yet a certain precision in the definition is necessary, and according to this it is precisely required that a concept or a mark must not already be located in another.",[]],"blomberg-s10-055":["As concerns tautology, we have already grasped it above as a great fault, for in this the marks given are not grounds of cognition at all, and the definition is in it mixed up with the *conceptus definiti*. but as concerns this. namely when the *notae* in a definition are the same, and differ from one another merely according to expression, then this is not exactly so great a fault, and distinctness can still take place with it. it is merely at fault only in this, that one expresses one's own thoughts all too luxuriantly. in order now to remove this in the definition, a certain precision is very necessary. This serves, however, as said, more for neatness than for the essence of the definition itself, but one still always gladly desires it in the definition. It is constituted with this just as with a garment, to be sure it renders me just the same services if it is made wide as if it is made even, but for the sake of neatness we reject the former, and choose the other.",[]],"blomberg-s10-056":["As concerns precision, then, the familiar and customary definition of the circle line, e.g., offends against it. one says namely: The circle line is a curved line whose individual points stand equally far from the *centro*. But the word curved line is here entirely unnecessary, and lies already within the concept of what follows. For every line whose parts stand equally far from the *centro* must necessarily be curved. one must thus not coordinate in a definition that which is already once contained under the same concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s10-057":["From definition, now, description is distinguished, for this is a distinct concept which, however, is only comparatively complete. Its exhaustiveness is sufficient only merely for my end, although it may be entirely contrary to the ends of all others. Description is thus not, like definition, absolutely complete, i.e., it does not serve for every aim, but only for my private aim, but is for that reason still not at all to be rejected.",[]],"blomberg-s10-058":["Now we can note: [934a] All *definitiones* are either *nominales* or *reales definitiones*. *Nominales definitiones* are those that contain in themselves all that which is equal to the whole concept, namely that which we make for ourselves of the thing, but *reales definitiones* are those that contain in themselves everything that pertains to the thing in and for itself.",[]],"blomberg-s10-059":["Or",[]],"blomberg-s10-060":["Nominal are those *definitiones* whose marks, taken together, are adequate to the whole concept that we think with the expression of the *definitum*. e.g. when I think and say: Salt is that which is dissolved in water. This I think, to be sure, with the concept of salt, but it is still not all possible characteristics of salt itself. [934b] Real *definitiones*, however, are those whose marks make up the whole similar concept of the thing. now",[]],"blomberg-s10-061":["1): all *definitiones* of arbitrary concepts,",[]],"blomberg-s10-062":["2.) all *definitiones* of concepts of reason, and 3:) all *definitiones* of arbitrary inventions are real *definitiones*. but why, we ask, are the *definitiones* of arbitrary concepts always real *definitiones*? Because it lies solely and only with me to fabricate the concept and to fix it as I please, and the whole concept thus has no other reality than merely as my fabrication wills, consequently I can always posit all pieces in a thing that I name, and these must then also make up the whole [934c] possible concept of the thing, for the whole thing is actual only through my will.",[]],"blomberg-s10-063":["Secondly, however, the *definitiones* of such things as are arbitrary inventions, consequently also the *definitiones* of all works of art, are *reales definitiones*, for they are still always first fixed through an arbitrary concept.",[]],"blomberg-s10-064":["Now we want to note this: That all mathematical *definitiones* are *definitiones* of arbitrary concepts. The mathematicians also have some given concepts, which, however, they cannot define, and this the mathematician also must not do, for otherwise he philosophizes, and that is then a gross fault for a mathematician, as into which fault Wolffius himself has fallen. The mathematician does not concern himself at all with what others think under the concept, but he says: this I want to call so, and so it shall also be called. As soon, therefore, as the mathematician represents to himself the concept in the definition as given, he philosophizes, and then only can one dispute with him, but not before, and then he encroaches upon the philosopher, who can then lay claim to him, and for this reason, therefore, a mathematician can define no place, no direction, no straight line, etc., for these are all already given concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s10-065":["Otherwise, as concerns this too, [936-939] there are indeed many arbitrary concepts, and also heuristic *definitiones*, to be encountered in philosophy, but they are not at all philosophical *definitiones*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-066":["Now, therefore, we can say: All *definitiones* of arbitrary concepts are",[]],"blomberg-s10-067":["1.) real 2 each of them is without any error 3): each of them is a complete definition. The arbitrary *definitiones* cannot be false at all, for in that he says: I want this to be called so, then it is also called so, and he can commit no error at all, even if he wanted to commit it by force. Only in regard to precision, there he can go wrong, but this is no error, but only merely a fault. and from this we see again what a great advantage a mathematician has. all his concepts namely are true, and he can only go wrong in regard to precision, otherwise one can well say that he can offend against the usage of speech. E.g. if he calls the circumference of a sphere, or of a polygon, periphery, since it should yet be called perimeter. Further, the names of the things that occur in art are also always true, the inventor of them is always right, for they are indeed arbitrary concepts. but if we want to define the works of art of others, then we must hear what the others want to understand by them. These *definitiones* of arbitrary concepts are all synthetic, and arise only *per synthesin*, for *per analysin* no concept can arise, but only distinctness can be given to it thereby. but with arbitrary concepts the concept is to arise, and hence all arbitrary definitions are made only *per synthesin*. *per analysin* a definition arises only insofar as the *definitum* is confusedly",[]],"blomberg-s10-068":["given to us, and there I can then make it distinct *per analysin*. *per synthesin*, however, I make the concept together with the distinctness, we can hence also note with a view to given concepts: all given concepts are given either through reason or through experience. Those, now, that are given through reason are made *per analysin*, and those that are given through experience are made *realiter per synthesin*. Now the concepts of reason can all be defined through the *definitiones reales*. for since the concept is already given to me through reason, I need only think in it that which reason shows me in the thing, and in such a way I then have a real definition, and hence we can also say, all metaphysical and moral *definitiones* can all be real, and occur only *per analysin*, for the concept is already given through reason, and I am only merely to make it distinct, which, however, occurs precisely only *per analysin*. Thus e.g. the concept of honesty is constituted, etc. but how is it, then, 2ndly, constituted with the concepts of experience with a view to their definition? There we must note: The *definitiones* of concepts of experience are never nominal otherwise than *per analysin*, secondly the concepts of experience are never real except only *per synthesin*. With the empirical concept I always ask what is given? and there I can then make a definition of it in a twofold way, *nempe vel definio conceptum datum. vel definiendum est definitum ipsum*, the first is always with all concepts a nominal one, and the other a real definition. For my concept contains only what I think in the thing, but not what pertains to it according to nature, but if I want to attain the latter, then I must make a real definition. and these occur with empirical concepts solely *per synthesin*, as I on the other side in turn get the nominal definition of them out in no other way than merely *per analysin*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-069":["Now we ask: Is a definition indeed possible with empirical concepts? O no, with all empirical concepts only a description takes place. At most it only seems that nominal definitions would be possible. For to define *realiter* means to indicate all marks which taken together make up the whole. and a real definition is thus the *completudo* of the marks that pertain only to the thing itself. but with empirical concepts I do not define the object, but only the concept that one thinks with the thing. The marks of experience are thus alterable, and serve only merely for nominal definition.",[]],"blomberg-s10-070":["Since, therefore, the concept is already given through experience, and I am to make it distinct, I need only enumerate all marks that I think with the expression of the *definitum*, and thus it seems in fact as if a nominal definition yet took place with it. but even this is not once possible with it. For with the term of empirical concepts themselves we do not even all have the same opinions. for the one has an entirely different experience of the thing than the other, and hence with empirical concepts only a description can be thought, which I can consider merely in relation with my, but not with every aim.",[]],"blomberg-s10-071":["But what aim, we ask, do we then have in this?",[]],"blomberg-s10-072":["The aim in this is not to get out the cognition of the *definitum* through its dissection, but only merely to give the *definitum* a significance. It is thus in this only merely the prior significance that is the final end, namely so that this not be entirely lost from the language. For e.g. in the Harz mountains one already calls the spruces firs, and in turn the firs spruces. I define an empirical concept in order afterwards always to add something *per synthesin*. but never do I define a concept of experience so that I come to know the object through dissection. I define no concept of experience, e.g. gold, in order to infer something from it, and to draw consequences out of this definition. but only merely in order thereby to fix the verbal significance of the *definitum*, here we note this: analytic are the *definitiones* of morals, and serve for this, that I draw from this concept all possible sequences. The *definitiones* of morals are all real, and I hence need only analyze them, so it is thus actually curious that one can infer from the use of definitions to their particular kind. The empirical definitions go only to the understanding, but not at all to reason (for only merely through the understanding do I have insight into the significance) they thus also have no philosophical use at all. From this it follows, therefore, that the *definitiones* of empirical concepts are entirely unnecessary, for even if there actually could be some, they would still be merely only nominal *definitiones*. For I do not say with them: all this taken together makes up the whole concept of the thing, but only this is all that I think to myself with the concept of the thing. all empirical concepts are capable only of description, all arbitrary concepts are synthetic, and their definition real. Finally all concepts of reason are, because they are given, analytic, and their *definitiones* likewise real. now we make these remarks: With all given concepts of reason the definition is not the first, but the confused concept of the *definitum*. For with the given concepts of reason I am only to make the confused concept distinct through the definition, and thus the confused concept of the *definitum* must still already precede all definition, but only with arbitrary concepts does the definition always precede, for this indeed gives the concepts then their *esse*. now all distinctness of definitions can be attained only through reason *per analysin*, and the concept of reason becomes complete only successively. namely when I weigh one clear mark after the other, and since now every cognition, if it has clear marks, is a judgment, all *definitiones* are at the same time judgments. before all analytic *definitiones* many *judicia elementaria* go ahead, which expound the marks that contain in themselves the concept of the *definitum*. It is sometimes agreeable for my aim to prove something where merely one mark is necessary, and the whole definition is often superfluous, e.g. when I say: the soul of the human being is incorporeal, then I do not need the whole definition, but I take out only the one mark, namely divisibility, and say: every body is divisible, but the soul is simple, consequently it cannot be corporeal.",[]],"blomberg-s10-073":["Never must one close the definition too early in philosophy, unless it be that I can cognize the whole thing from one mark, for this obstructs the way to cognition of the thing. before I close the definition, I must still long analyze the concept, and still postpone my judgment, so that in the meantime I perhaps attain a yet more exhaustive cognition of the object.",[]],"blomberg-s10-074":["As concerns Wolff's fault, it consists in this, that Wolff wanted to apply the mathematical procedure in philosophy, all concepts in *mathesis* are arbitrary concepts, and must not be given at all.",[]],"blomberg-s10-075":["From the universal the particular is cognized. With universal concepts I have",[]],"blomberg-s10-076":["1:) to look to the lower concepts which are contained in the universal,",[]],"blomberg-s10-077":["2:) but also to the difference of these lower concepts, and all this occurs in logical division. This synthetic method consists namely precisely in this, when I compare the *sph aeram* of the concept with all its lower concepts, yet in such a way that I also attend to their correct difference.",[]],"blomberg-s10-078":["To a correct division, now, belongs",[]],"blomberg-s10-079":["1: that it be complete. i.e. that the *membra dividentia* (these are precisely the lower concepts) taken together make up exactly the universal concept.",[]],"blomberg-s10-080":["2:) but the *membra dividentia* must also be properly opposed to one another, so that I can say either, or with truth. there must thus also always be an actual difference there, e.g. I will not divide thus: all human beings are either virtuous, or vicious, or philosophers. This concept lies indeed in the human being, but it is not at all represented *in oppositione*. The division can now be a codivision as well as a subdivision. but a subdivision is that where the *membrum dividens* is again subordinated.",[]],"blomberg-s10-081":["The rules which the author adduces herein serve us for nothing at all, through them I merely learn to express through formulas that which I already know entirely, to represent it to myself *in abstracto*, which I, if I only follow my healthy reason, already actually exercise *in concreto*.",[]],"blomberg-s11-001":["Properly we already know judgments from the previous section, because we have treated of distinct concepts, which, however, can arise only through a judgment, for to cognize distinctly means to cognize everything through one clear mark. But to cognize something through a clear mark means precisely also to judge. We can thus also say: distinct concepts are those which are cognized through a judgment. now: the 2 concepts that are compared with one another, and indeed in such a way that the one contains the marks in itself, and the other the clarity, make up the matter of the judgment. That concept, now, which is to be made distinct through comparison with its mark is called the subject. on the other hand that concept which is added to the mark as a ground of distinctness is called the predicate.",[]],"blomberg-s11-002":["Comparison is twofold, for either I can compare 2 concepts through the relation of connection, or through the relation of conflict with one another. A judgment, now, in which the predicate is thought in connection to the subject is called an affirmative judgment. But a judgment where the predicate is thought in conflict to the subject is called a negative judgment. The form of the judgment is the relation. the sign which indicates this relation or the form is the copula *est*. and since all relation is either affirmative or negative, the copula *est*, if it is simply affirmative, is accompanied with yes (*id est* simply with *est*), but if it is negative, with an *id est non*. The negation no, or the *non*, does not affect the matter at all, but only the form of the judgment, it must therefore be posited not with the subject or with the predicate, but with the copula, e.g. *anima non est mortalis*, but not *anima est non mortalis*, for otherwise the negation of the judgment of reason affects not the form but the matter of the judgment, and consequently it is then also not a negative but a so-called *judicium infinitum*.",[]],"blomberg-s11-003":["In every judgment there occurs",[]],"blomberg-s11-004":["1): the matter of the judgment, that is, subject and predicate. The matter of the judgment, now, is called the *termini* of the judgment, and indeed for reasons we will hear afterwards we call the subject the *terminus minor*, and the predicate is called with us *terminus major*, and is always regarded as a universal concept under which the *conceptus subjecti* is contained. all judgments have the end of distinctness, but one always represents the predicate as a ground of the distinctness of the subject in a judgment. Since now, however, in a mark there is also always the ground of distinctness, we have insight that the subject must always be contained under the predicate as under a mark. The",[]],"blomberg-s11-005":["2nd piece of every judgment is the form of it. This the copula *est* always expresses this copula is now posited *simpliciter* when it indicates the relation of two concepts in their connection but if it expresses the relation in their conflict, then it is further accompanied by the word *non*. This *non* now expresses the negation of the judgment, but NB: only then when it affects the form of the judgment, i.e. the copula.",[]],"blomberg-s11-006":["Where the author says thus: all true judgments have a ground, the ground of truth now is the condition of judgments, all indemonstrable judgments have no particular ground of cognition, but they are the grounds of cognition themselves, and consequently they can contain no grounds of truth at all. as, however, the author means here, thus e.g. the judgment is constituted: Every body is extended, for this judgment cannot be derived anywhere further. but if now the condition of truth is only added to a judgment, then the judgment is determined. every judgment is now determined, and I cannot represent to myself at all how there should yet be such people who say: certain judgments are undetermined, as to whom our author too belongs, properly one should say unlimited, for all that which is not determined is also never a judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s11-007":["All judgments are either universal or particular. it is universal when the *nota* of the subject is either entirely contained in the *sphaera* of the predicate or not contained at all, and indeed in the first case it is a universally affirmative, but in the other case, namely when the *nota* of the subject is located entirely outside the *sphaera* of the predicate, a universally negative judgment. A particular judgment, however, is one where the *nota* of the subject is contained only in part under the *sphaera* of the predicate, or also is not contained, in the former case it is then a particularly affirmative, and in the other a particularly negative judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s11-008":["More judgments *quoad quantitatem* there are not, for as concerns singular judgments (*judicia singularia*), or those where the subject is an individual, these belong already to universal judgments. yet one can still note this in this: a singular judgment must be either wholly affirmative or wholly negative. more does not take place with it, for it belongs to the universal",[]],"blomberg-s11-009":["judgment, and thus every singular judgment is universal. This was now the division of judgments *quoad quantitatem*, namely into universal judgments and into particular. in regard to quality now all judgments are either affirmative or negative, and indeed either universally affirmative or universally negative or particularly affirmative or particularly negative. The first the logicians express by A, the second by E, the 3rd by I and the 4th by 0. this whole designation is a mere pedantry (the *Barbara Celarent*), and otherwise the whole syllogistic is also mere pedantry, and I will afterwards show that every syllogism outside the first figure is not simple, as one however falsely holds, but is composed of the *consequentia immediata* and a *ratiocinium*. although now syllogistic is unnecessary, one must still know it.",[]],"blomberg-s11-010":["The following remark is well to be annotated: *Omnia judicia sunt vel problematica, vel assertoria* (deciding). A problematic judgment is one in which I only consider the relation of two concepts undeterminedly, but do not posit it, I weigh something in it as given, for investigation, i.e. I fix no relation in it, but I only see what, if it were actual, would follow from it. they have notwithstanding a great utility. For through the relation of two such judgments one can nonetheless learn to use a certain caution in judging, and to make our judgments advantageous.",[]],"blomberg-s11-011":["All relation in judgments is either a relation of connection or a relation of conflict. That judgment, now, through which the relation of connection of two judgments with one another is indicated is called a hypothetical judgment. But that judgment in which the relation of conflict of two judgments is indicated is called a disjunctive judgment. one calls namely those judgments where one thinks the relation of two judgments with one another relation judgments (*relative*), these judgments now consider the relation of one judgment to the other either according to connection, and then they are hypothetical judgments, or according to conflict, and they are then disjunctive judgments. with the former one always finds the relation of the ground to the sequences. in conditioned judgments now that which contains the ground in itself is called *antecedens* or also *prius*. But that which contains the sequences in itself is called in them *consequens* or *posterius*. but as concerns disjunctive judgments, in them the relation of conflict is again of two kinds, either it is a relation of two or more judgments which contradict one another, but we will show that a true disjunctive judgment takes place only with two, and not at all with more judgments, for no true and pure contradiction can take place otherwise than merely with two concepts which stand exactly against one another in opposition. It is then also called *bimembris propositio* and since now disjunctive judgments contain in themselves the relation of a true contradiction, they can comprise only 2 judgments. if we judge merely rationally, then the *propositio disjunctiva* in the judgment must be *bimembris*, but all those disjunctive judgments whose *propositio disjunctiva* is not *bimembris* have arisen from induction, for how else do I know that I have also correctly enumerated the cases than only from experience, and thus *per inductionem*. A disjunctive judgment, if it is to become certain without induction, must be *bimembris*. A disjunctive judgment is nothing more than a representation of a logical division, or *repraesentatio divisionis logicae*.",[]],"blomberg-s11-012":["An exponible judgment is one which properly has 2 judgments in a hidden way, not such that it perhaps has 2 subjects or predicates, but the judgments in it are of two kinds according to quantity, also according to quality, for they are affirmative, also negative. Logic now has the duty, it must express the exponible judgments. what do the questions signify: *Quae? Qualis? Quanta?* 1. the question *Qualis est propositio?* aims at quality, namely whether the judgment was affirmative or negative. 2. *Quanta est propositio?* would contain the question whether it was a universal or a particular judgment? 3. *quae est propositio?* is the question whether the *judicium* be *purum* or *modale*. But modal is the name of a judgment which carries a condition of judgment with the predicate to these questions now an answer must be given according to the constitution of the judgment. now when an empty judgment came up, one was hence accustomed to say: *Quae? qualis? Quanta?* i.e. there is not even the least in the judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s11-013":["A judgment is practically expressed if it enunciates a possibly necessary action. this seems indeed to be contradictory, something is possibly necessary. but it is entirely correct here, for the action is indeed always necessary, namely if I want to bring the thing about, but the case is not necessary, but merely possible. It is very good that with this kind of judgments one does not posit the *imperativus*, [965a] but only the *infinitivus*, and expresses them with it, as when etc.",[]],"blomberg-s11-014":["Demonstrably certain signifies nothing other than a mediately certain judgment. But a judgment is on the other side absolutely indemonstrable if it is immediately certain. and such judgments must also be encountered in the human understanding, for even if we have ever so many consequences, and can cognize these from their grounds as from their marks, we must still finally come to a ground which is no further sequence of another ground, but is itself the very first mark of all sequences, and this is then immediately certain, or an indemonstrably certain judgment. But whether there be only one or many such absolutely first grounds is treated in metaphysics.",[]],"blomberg-s11-015":["All indemonstrable judgments, insofar as they are the ground of all judgments, are called principles and they are either theoretical or practical. a practically indemonstrable proposition is called a *postulatum*. all these *principia* are either material, insofar namely as they have a *medium terminum*, or formal, which have no *medium terminum*. These have no determinate predicate at all, but go only merely to the relation of predicates to subjects in general. to these now belong the *principium contradictionis* and the *principium identitatis*. to these there is namely no determinate subject and predicate, or no determinate case, but it can be applied to every subject and predicate. we now have in our judgments a twofold form, namely the synthetic and analytic form, the *principium contradictionis* now is only analytic.",[]],"blomberg-s11-016":["Here the author gives an instruction for demonstration. one has, just as tautological *definitiones*, so also tautological *resolutiones*, where namely one answers just the same thing as one asked. and so our author also does it here. when a *quaestio* is at hand, then one should attend very well whether the given answer and *resolutio* of the *quaestio* is not merely an analysis of the *quaestio* itself. For then I have by this not taught the other how to resolve the *quaestio*, but only merely made known to him through the analysis that which was in it. I have thus done nothing more than made him understand better that which he asked me. so the author also does it many times. It is curious, he merely shows me that I am to do that about which I ask him, and does not at all show me how I should do it. I have in my last disputation *de mundi sensibilis atque intelligibilis forma et principiis* also had to resolve various *quaestiones*, but I have not merely analyzed the *quaestiones*, but in fact also indicated entirely correct and complete marks of the things. e.g. p. 30 § 25 (see *Dict. Metaph.*, where this disputation is bound in) There namely this infallible mark of sensibility is adduced *Si de conceptu quocunque intellectuali generaliter quicquam praedicatur, quod pertinet ad respectus spatii atque temporis: objective non est enuntiandum et non denotat nisi conditionem, sine qua conceptus datus sensitive cognoscibilis non est.* see ibid.",[]],"blomberg-s11-017":["Where the author speaks of intuitive judgments. An intuitive judgment is properly a singular judgment, insofar as it is cognized immediately, a *judicium discursivum*, however, is an inferred judgment. all immediate judgments, however, are intuitive merely because they are cognized immediately, insofar as the form of them is sensitive, for otherwise, if the form is rational, then the immediate judgments are nevertheless not at all intuitive judgments, to the intuitive judgments only the discursive could be contradistinguished, and the ancients called the universal",[]],"blomberg-s11-018":["judgments *discursiva*, the singular ones, however, *intuitiva*, and so we too will take it here. This, now, was about the intuitive or experience-judgments. whether now a judgment is an experience-judgment or not, that belongs to metaphysics to investigate. Logic does not consider judgments at all insofar as they are given, as metaphysics does, but insofar as it is to compare them. Logic shows only whether judgments are universal or particular, and so it occupies itself only with opposition and identity. All propositions are either theoretical or practical. The first are such as merely show the relation of concepts to other concepts, the others, however, indicate the relation of action to concepts. Both propositions, however, are demonstrable or indemonstrable. The theoretically demonstrable propositions, when they arise through a *ratiocinium polysyllogisticum*, are called *theoremata*. Every demonstrable proposition, however, which is only monosyllogistic, is called a *corollarium*. a practically demonstrable proposition is a *problema*, a practically indemonstrable proposition, however, a *postulatum*. In all theoretically demonstrable propositions the proposition itself is the *theorema*, the proof of the proposition, however, the demonstration, the action which is to be done therein is called the *quaestio*, The propositions according to which it is cognized, the *resolutio* of it. a *lemma* is a proposition which is indeed demonstrable, but not in this, but in another discipline, from which it is taken. with this one must take great care, so that one does not assume something and pass it off as already proven in another science, when it is yet not at all proven in it yet indeed one often finds something in the authors which they assume at the beginning, which they promise to prove at the end, but the proof is forgotten. *Scholia* are propositions which are added to a doctrinal system not at all as *essentialia*, but only as *accidentalia*.",[]],"blomberg-s11-019":["Every inference is in general a judgment, only with the difference: if the judgment is mediate, then it is an inference of reason, or a mediate inference. if, however, the judgment is immediate, then the inference is only an inference of the understanding one thing namely is judged from the other *absque medio termino*, then the judgment, or also the *conclusio*, is *immediata*, or one thing is inferred from the other *per medium terminum*, and then the inference is *mediate*, or also an inference of reason. we will now consider solely mediate inferences. as regards their manner of arising, this is of 5 kinds, for they can arise either",[]],"blomberg-s11-020":["1) *per judicia aequipollentia* or",[]],"blomberg-s11-021":["2) *per judicia subalternata*, or",[]],"blomberg-s11-022":["3) *per judicia opposita* or",[]],"blomberg-s11-023":["4) *per conversionem logicam* or",[]],"blomberg-s11-024":["5) *per contrapositionem*. as regards now the *consequentia per judicia aequipollentia*, this is, because both judgments in it are entirely one and the same with each other, properly no consequence at all, and thus the so-called inference *per judicia aequipollentia* is no inference at all. The *consequentia per judicia subalternata* takes place without any alteration of the place of the *termini*, the quality also remains the same, only the quantity is altered therein. In the *consequentia per conversionem logicam* likewise the quality must never be altered, but only the quantity. In the *conversio logica*, however, both the position of the *termini* and the quality must be altered in it. With the *oppositio judiciorum* the logicians have made the following division:",[]],"blomberg-s11-025":["1: *Contradictorie* 2 judgments are opposed to each other when to an affirmative judgment a particular negative judgment (or conversely) is opposed. e.g. all human beings are mortal, but some are not mortal.",[]],"blomberg-s11-026":["2 *Subcontraria* are judgments when in 2 particular judgments which have the same subject and predicate, the one is affirmative, and the other negative.",[]],"blomberg-s11-027":["3 there is also a *contrarietas judicii*, this takes place when in 2 judgments the one is universally affirmative, and the other universally negative. The *judicia subcontraria* contain in themselves less, or not enough, than belongs to correct opposition, the *judicia contraria* contain more, and the *judicia contradictoria* just as much as is enough for right opposition, in themselves. and we can therefore say: *judicia subcontraria*, because they do not contain enough, can 1) both be true, but they cannot 2) both be false.",[]],"blomberg-s11-028":["Second, *judicia contraria* can both be false, for in both there is too much, and they can be false precisely in their too much. of 2 pure oppositions this must always be able to hold: *posito uno tollitur alterum. et vice versa* In all logical conversion (see above § 346) of judgments the judgment which is to be converted is called *conversum*, the judgment which is to arise from this conversion, *convertens*. A *conversio* in which the quality of the judgment is not hindered is called *simplex*, where it is hindered, however, *conversio per accidens*. universally affirmative judgments can be converted *per accidens*, all negative judgments, however, can be converted *simpliciter*, and finally the particular ones can all be converted *simpliciter*, that is the only rule of logical conversion. The rule of contraposition is this: universally affirmative judgments can only be contraposed.",[]],"blomberg-s12-001":["Inferences of reason are judgments which take place mediately by means of a *medius terminus*. There are namely also, as said before, certain judgments and consequences, which we have called *immediatas*, these were indemonstrable. we also called these sequences of a judgment from other judgments which are indemonstrable not an inference of reason, but an inference of the understanding. but on the other side there are yet certain consequences from other mediate judgments, and these are always demonstrable. There are uncertain illusory inferences, which are not inferences of reason, but only mere *themata* for them, and of this sort are the so-called *ratiocinia disjunctiva* and *hypothetica*, or also *speciosa*. of these inferences, which are only *themata* for inferences of reason, we will not treat here at all, but solely of the inferences of reason themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s12-002":["When two concepts are put together, the one is the mark of the other, then a certain relation is posited in these concepts. This relation can now be represented immediately, and then it is a *judicium*. if, however, it takes place *per notam intermediam*, then it is a *ratiocinium*. or the inference of reason is nothing other than the comparison of a *nota remota* with the thing itself, by means of a *nota intermedia*. The mediate relation of a *nota remota* is always *remota* with regard to the thing, but also *nota proxima* of the *nota intermedia*, and thus a *nota rei ipsius*, with the given concept, that is a *judicium medium*, or a *ratiocinium*.",[]],"blomberg-s12-003":["An adequate inference consists in an inference of reason whose rule is this: *nota notae rei est nota rei ipsius* and this is also the fundamental rule of all syllogistic, and at the same time the whole difference of a judgment from an inference of reason. to a *judicium*, namely, belongs an immediate clear mark, to a *ratiocinium*, however, also a mediate clear mark. Consequently the *ratiocinium* is the relation of the *nota remota* through a *nota mediata, vel intermedia* to a certain given concept of a thing. in a *ratiocinium* there thus occur these 3 actions: 1*mo* the *nota proxima* or *intermedia* is compared with the *nota remota*, 2 the *nota proxima* is compared with the thing, *et* 3*tio* the *nota remota* is compared with the thing itself. The *nota intermedia* is also called the *medius terminus*. Since now in every judgment the predicate constitutes the *major terminus*, we can say: in every inference of reason there are 3 *termini*. 1. the proposition which I am to cognize by means of the *nota intermedia*, 2: the *nota remota* and 3*tens* the *terminus medius*, or the *nota intermedia*, which contains in itself the ground of the relation of the thing to the *nota remota*. and these make up the 3 *termini*, namely *majorem, minorem et conclusionem*. in every inference of reason 1. the *terminus major* in the *terminus medius*, or the *nota proxima* with the *nota remota*, 2 the *terminus medius* with the *minor*, or the subject, and finally 3: the *minor* with the *major*, or the predicate, is compared.",[]],"blomberg-s12-004":["Thus to infer requires only the synthetic way of reason, this now requires always that one should descend from the universal to the particular, if one reverses this, then it is against reason, and a leap is then at once committed. and therefore also the *major propositio* must always, or the predicate of the conclusion, come first, or first I must go *per notam remotam* to the *nota proxima*, then I must go again from the *nota proxima* to the thing, and finally from the thing to the *nota remota*. That is the synthesis of reason, and if one loses sight of this, then one also does not observe the order. in every inference of reason there are thus certain *termini*, of which now no more than 3 must be present, and also in every right inference of reason no more than 3 *judicia* take place. The 2 first are called the premises, the 3rd judgment, however, is called the conclusion. The premises now make up the matter, the conclusion, however, the form of inferences of reason, hence the correct form of an inference of reason will rest solely and alone on the correct sequence of the conclusion. in both inferences now there always occurs the *medius terminus* or the *nota intermedia* (but not at all in the *consequens* itself). Every inference of reason is nothing other than a representation of a mark by means of a *terminus medius*. To an inference of reason it belongs that a mark be subordinated to another mark of the thing as a *notae nota*. I thus compare in an inference of reason the *nota remota* with the *nota proxima*, then the thing with the *nota proxima*, and finally also the *nota remota* with the thing itself. All form of inferences of reason is only twofold, and more also do not take place, for either the judgment is immediate, or mediate",[]],"blomberg-s12-005":["The fundamental rule of all affirmative inferences are these:",[]],"blomberg-s12-006":["1: *Nota notae rei est nota rei ipsius*",[]],"blomberg-s12-007":["The sole fundamental rule, however,",[]],"blomberg-s12-008":["2:) of all negative inferences will thus be this:",[]],"blomberg-s12-009":["*Repugnans notae notae rei, repugnat rei ipsi* This now is the fundamental rule of all inferences of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s12-010":["The logicians have a certain *dictum de omni et nullo* which they hold to be the very first ground of all *ratiocinia*. But these adduced fundamental rules themselves still precede this.",[]],"blomberg-s12-011":["The *dictum de omni et nullo* runs among the logicians thus: what belongs to a universal concept also belongs to all concepts which are contained under it.",[]],"blomberg-s12-012":["When in an inference of reason the relation of two judgments (not of two concepts) is considered, then that is called a *ratiocinium extraordinarium* or when an inference of reason is assumed where the *major propositio* is not a *judicium ordinarium* but a *judicium extraordinarium*, then it is then also called *ratiocinium extraordinarium*. all *ratiocinia extraordinaria* are either *hypothetica* or *disjunctiva*. For in them either the relation of connection or the relation of conflict is indicated; if it is the first, then it is called a hypothetical, in the other case, however, a disjunctive inference of reason. The *judicium hypotheticum* consists only of 2 propositions, and thus no *judicium hypotheticum* can produce a *ratiocinium*. in the *judicium hypotheticum* only the *consequens* need be proven, for in it nothing is enunciated and asserted. but with *judicia categorica* not only the *consequens* but also the assertion itself must be proven from correct grounds. The conditioned inference is no inference of reason, but only the substratum for the inference of reason, for it must first still be proven *per ratiocinium*. it is first the *quaestio* itself, and the *datum*, which is still to be proven, thus properly nothing is yet proven by it, but only a ground stated, of which one at the same time affirms that its sequence is correct, but how it is correct, that I do not at all see by it, it is only merely, so to speak, the ground of proof, not the proof proper that is: by it merely the matter but not the form of the proof is given. this analysis is certainly not at all unnecessary, and entirely correct. For in the *judicium conditionale* no subject at all is connected with a predicate through a mediate mark, and in such an inference there is no *major propositio* at all in it, and thus they are also not at all orderly inferences of reason, but only first substrata for them, one does not actually infer according to them, but one merely says there what one wants to infer. all proof goes either to the truth of the *antecedens*, or of the *consequens*. every *syllogismus hypotheticus* has 2 *modi*, namely a *modus ponens*, where an affirmation is inferred in the conclusion, and the *modus tollens*, where namely a negation is inferred. An affirmation is here inferred according to the rule: *atqui verum est prius, ergo quoque posterius*. The negation, however, from the falsity of the *consequens* to the falsity of the *antecedens*, and indeed according to the rule: *atqui falsum est prius, ergo quoque posterius remoto antecedente non tollitur consequens sed sublata consequentia tollitur antecedens*. I can thus here never infer *a negatione antecedentis* to the *negationem consequentis*, but indeed conversely.",[]],"blomberg-s12-013":["As regards now the disjunctive inferences, these are such inferences in which the *major propositio* is disjunctive. a disjunctive inference is a logical division, I represent to myself in it a divided concept, and all *membra* which are equal to the *divisum*. The subject of the *major propositio* in it is the *divisum*, and the predicate are all *membra dividentia*. Consequently the correctness of a disjunctive inference rests on the correctness of a logical division, everything namely must stand in true disjunction and opposition to one another, and moreover everything must also be complete.",[]],"blomberg-s12-014":["A *dilemma* is properly a *ratiocinium hypotheticum* whose consequence is disjunctive. one should thus proceed in it according to the rules both of the hypothetical and of the disjunctive kind of inference. *atqui falsum est prius, ergo etiam posterius*. One never has a *dilemma* as *modus ponendi*, but always as *modus tollendi*. Through a *dilemma* we do not seek properly to prove a proposition with it, but only to convince someone of the falsity. The *dilemma* is otherwise also called *cornutum*, because by it one refutes the other, and at the same time also cuts off all ways for him, he may namely go whichever he will, then he is caught. and therefore this *dilemma* is also used very much in dialectic For this reason the *sceptici* also took it very gladly.",[]],"blomberg-s12-015":["Here the author speaks of immediate consequences, it is not at all fitting, however, for they are indeed not inferences of reason at all but only inferences of the understanding. For they have no *medius terminus*.",[]],"blomberg-s12-016":["Are either formal inferences of reason, or *ratiocinia cryptica*. *Ratiocinia cryptica* are such where I enunciate only 2 judgments, but think the 3rd along with them. One always says in such a *syllogismus* less than one voluntarily thinks, and that is the so-called dissimulating, which is very often salutary and fair. but simulating is called when one speaks otherwise than one thinks. and is of course to be entirely rejected as unfair.",[]],"blomberg-s12-017":["*Enthymema* this is such an inference in which only 2 judgments, namely one *praemissa* and the *conclusio*, are posited. To these also belong the *ratiocinia contracta* These are such *syllogismi* where not 2 propositions at all, but only one proposition and the *medius terminus* are present. Further there also belongs to this induction, which is especially noteworthy. The rule of reason in apodictic certainty in inferences always goes from the universal to the particular. there are nevertheless still inferences where we infer from the particular to the universal. the former is the *ratiocinatio rigida*. The latter, however, the *ratiocinatio laxa*. to the last now belong 1.) *inductio*. 2. analogy. *Inductio* is the inference where I take that which belongs to many under a universal inference and concept to be true as if it belonged to all. I infer thus: what belongs to so many things as I have ever cognized must also belong to all which are of this species and genus. This kind of inference is indeed entirely contrary to the logical rule, but one cannot at all do without it, and with it at the same time most of our cognitions would also have to be abolished.",[]],"blomberg-s12-018":["As regards 2*ndly* the inference according to an analogy, this is nothing other than an induction, but only an induction with respect to the predicate. if namely 2 things have agreed with respect to all properties which I have been able to cognize in them, then they will also agree with each other in the remaining properties which I have not cognized in them, and so runs the inference with respect to analogy. Analogy and induction are merely the crutches for our understanding. For in universal propositions of truth we will indeed always be able to proceed according to logical strictness, but in the use of our cognition (*a posteriori*) there we must also often make do with the probable.",[]],"blomberg-s12-019":["There is a complete, also an incomplete induction. Complete, when its *membra* are equal to the given concept, or when everything can be derived from a particular concept.",[]],"blomberg-s12-020":["Every inference false in form is a *paralogismus*. every false inference, however, which is to be a means of producing an error in the other, is called a *sophisma*. there are various *sophismata*, which could be given many names, which, however, is not at all necessary. otherwise there are indeed still some *sophismata* which deserve a place in logic, for some of them can be truer than the others. otherwise the author here also mentions the *sophisma ignorationis elenchi*. for us, however, only this is to be remarked about it: He who in a dispute forgets the question in dispute commits a *sophisma ignorationis elenchi*.",[]],"blomberg-s12-021":["When inferences of reason are coordinated with one another, then there arises from this a *ratiocinium compositum*, also *polysyllogisticum*. A *ratiocinium* is called *polysyllogisticus* when the *conclusio* of the one inference of reason is the *praemissa* of the other. That *syllogismus* now whose *conclusio* is the premise of another is called *prosyllogismus*. That *syllogismus*, however, whose *praemissa* is a conclusion of the previous one is called *episyllogismus*. or more briefly: the *prosyllogismus* contains in itself the ground, the *episyllogismus*, however, the sequence of an inference of reason. see auct.: § 406: § 408: There are *sorites per syllogismos hypoth.*: and then it is also called *sorites hypotheticus*, or the *sorites* can arise *per syllogismos categoricos*, and then it is also called *categoricus*.",[]],"blomberg-s12-022":["The *formalia ratiocinia* are used only in disputing, but not in books and the like, for otherwise this would become too disgusting. and as regards otherwise the many inferences of reason, these make things more indistinct than distinct. they are, however, expressed only for this reason, how reason actually thinks.",[]],"blomberg-s12-023":["Where the author speaks of the leap in proof, of the legitimate as well as of the illegitimate, we remark here only this: *nullus saltus in intellectu est possibilis, et non aliter saltus possibilis est, quam in enunciando*. The immediate connection of a remote ground namely with the sequence is a leap. in thoughts now such a leap is entirely impossible, but in enunciation it can well take place, and in this case it can indeed also sometimes be *legitimus*, when namely I believe the other will unfailingly know what has been left out, and thus supply it in thoughts.",[]],"blomberg-s12-024":["The actual omission of a proposition which deserves a proof is called *petitio principii*. this comes from *peto*, I beg. I must namely in such a kind of conviction as it were beg the other for his assent, and from this we thus see that this can be called no proof at all.",[]],"blomberg-s12-025":["The *circulus in demonstrando* is that error when one assumes the concluding proposition in a proof as its own antecedent proposition. This happens",[]],"blomberg-s12-026":["1:) when one wants to prove that which is indemonstrably true, or immediately certain.",[]],"blomberg-s12-027":["2:) when one wants to prove indemonstrably false propositions, in these two cases the circle in proving is always necessary. chiefly, however, it arises",[]],"blomberg-s12-028":["3:) when namely one is already certain, before all proof, of the truth of a proposition which is demonstrable, then one already grants the other the proof, and therefore a circle in proving can always creep in.",[]],"blomberg-s12-029":["A proof proves too little when it proves only a part of the proposition. It proves too much when it proves more than can be proven. A proof which proves too little can be true, but a proof which proves too much is always false. For when it is said: The proof proves too much, is as much as it proves more than is true, this, however, is nothing other than that it is false.",[]],"blomberg-s12-030":["The author speaks at last of something about the utility of inferences of reason, which seems almost unnecessary, for who indeed will not hold the use of reason to be useful.",[]],"blomberg-s13-001":["The method of teaching is nothing other than the form of a whole of cognitions, insofar as it is",[]],"blomberg-s13-002":["arranged according to the rules of logical perfection. Since now, however, logical perfection is twofold, namely either the logical perfection according to healthy reason, or the logical perfection according to erudition and science, the method of teaching will in the same way be able to be divided. For the rules of healthy reason are distinct from the rules of science. indeed in all sciences and erudition the method of healthy reason must rule, but conversely not everything which is found in erudition need always also be in healthy reason. In all sciences I do not look to how something appears in practice, but how it can also be judged before all practice. I look to how something can also be thought *in abstracto*, if I go, however, on the contrary according to the rules of healthy reason, then I must always show everything *in concreto*. The other kind is a cognition which carries a certain life with it. the first, however, serves merely for speculation and curiosity. The methods of the learned now are distinct:",[]],"blomberg-s13-003":["1.) *quoad objectum*. i.e. according to the matter which they comprehend under themselves, or to which they are directed. For the object can be either",[]],"blomberg-s13-004":["a:) historical cognition, or B ) rational cognition. to the latter belong e.g. mathematics and philosophy, to the former, however, geography and the history proper of *historia*",[]],"blomberg-s13-005":["2: also according to the subject. for there there is",[]],"blomberg-s13-006":["a) A method of instruction for womenfolk, and a separate method for menfolk.",[]],"blomberg-s13-007":["B) a method for the instruction of children, and another for grown-up people.",[]],"blomberg-s13-008":["C) An entirely separate method of instruction for people who are destined to a low station, and again another for people who are destined to the higher station. etc. entirely otherwise namely must our method of teaching be arranged for a peasant than if it is applied even only to a person of middling station.",[]],"blomberg-s13-009":["3 these differences can also concern the cognitive powers. for there can be either",[]],"blomberg-s13-010":["1: a method merely of healthy reason, or",[]],"blomberg-s13-011":["2) the method can be scientific, or there can also",[]],"blomberg-s13-012":["3) a difference of the method of teaching take place even in its subject,",[]],"blomberg-s13-013":["4) the difference, however, with regard to the 2nd method is indeed this: the method can be either",[]],"blomberg-s13-014":["a:) analytic",[]],"blomberg-s13-015":["B ) synthetic",[]],"blomberg-s13-016":["Analysis and synthesis are either according to coordination or according to subordination",[]],"blomberg-s13-017":["α) the synthesis of coordination is the combination of the parts with the whole.",[]],"blomberg-s13-018":["β) the analysis of coordination, however, is the resolution of the whole into its parts.",[]],"blomberg-s13-019":["γ) the synthesis of subordination is the combination of the grounds with the sequences",[]],"blomberg-s13-020":["δ) the analysis of subordination, however, is the derivation of the sequence from its grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s13-021":["In all historical cognitions the connection is that of coordination, in all rational cognitions, however, only the connection of subordination takes place. in *historia* I namely count one thing after another, be it now according to space or according to time. but in rational cognitions and sciences we always derive one thing from another. in analytic coordination I go namely from the whole to its parts, in synthetic coordination, however, from the parts to the whole.",[]],"blomberg-s13-022":["The analytic method is most advisable in historical cognitions. first I must namely form for myself an idea of the whole, and then I make an idea and concepts of the parts contained under it. In all rational cognitions there is the form of subordination, and this is now either synthetic or analytic. in the former I go from the ground to the sequence, in the latter from the sequence to the ground.",[]],"blomberg-s13-023":["The artificial method of teaching is contradistinguished from the natural, just as a distinction is to be made between a rational cognition, i.e. such a one as does not contradict reason, and a rational cognition, when namely one understands by it such a one as arises from reason. so too a cognition is called natural in a twofold sense, namely 1: when it agrees with the nature of the object and of the subject, and then 2:*ndly* when it springs from nature. The method of teaching must be natural not so much subjectively as objectively, i.e. appropriate to the object.",[]],"blomberg-s13-024":["Cf. Logik Philippi: xxviv:482.",[]],"blomberg-s13-025":["*Methodus tabellaris*, i.e. to understand and to sketch a whole science in tables is very fine. but insight belongs to it so as not to make altogether too many and not useless divisions. The method of teaching according to the dissection of a whole doctrinal system is no method of teaching, the way of composition of the parts makes up the doctrinal system.",[]],"blomberg-s13-026":["*Affectatio* is called an unnatural art, affected is called everything whereby one wants to seem artful. So it is affected when one gives not one's art, but traces of an artful investigation. The affected is commonly combined with pedantry , it shines forth especially when one applies a single method to everything, whether it fits or not.",[]],"blomberg-s13-027":["The Socratic method of teaching takes place through conversations, where through well-arranged questions one brings everything out of the pupil, and leads him to answers. it has great utility. It is a genus of catechetical method, but it is very tiring.",[]],"blomberg-s13-028":["The Platonic method of teaching consisted in a free discourse, where one becomes aware of a system.",[]],"blomberg-s13-029":["In historical science s one has 2 methods, the chronological and geographical. Both are to be united together. The last is preferable to the first. in all interconnected cognitions one must first take into consideration the whole, as its parts, and of the parts the large sooner than the small, the upper sooner than the lower division.",[]],"blomberg-s13-030":["Of *historia* the author says that it belongs to no doctrine, but it is just as much a doctrine as dogmatic truths. One is to distinguish:",[]],"blomberg-s13-031":["a) Doctrine i.e. an interconnection of various cognitions and teachings.",[]],"blomberg-s13-032":["B:) Discipline when this interconnection is brought into a method.",[]],"blomberg-s13-033":["C:) Science, when the cognition is completely worked out according to such a method.",[]],"blomberg-s13-034":["Science is a complete discipline. In doctrine I direct my attention only to that which is taught, in discipline I look to the method. Doctrine and discipline thus take place just as well in historical as in dogmatic cognitions. In sciences there must at all times be doctrine.",[]],"blomberg-s13-035":["One thinks tumultuously when one thinks without method.",[]],"blomberg-s13-036":["To meditate means not to recall cognitions which one has had, but to produce new ones which one has not yet had. Methodical meditating hinders one from procuring for oneself enough of one's materials for meditating. at the beginning one meditates tumultuously, one must write down what occurs to us, as thoughts sometimes come into our mind which we otherwise do not have. one should first write down all thoughts as they come, without order, afterwards one should begin to coordinate, and then to subordinate. In every working-out 3 labors must precede",[]],"blomberg-s13-037":["1) One writes down all thoughts without order",[]],"blomberg-s13-038":["2) one makes a general plan.",[]],"blomberg-s13-039":["3) One works out all parts",[]],"blomberg-s13-040":["Doubting exercises one in reflecting , but also the endeavor to see rightly into each person's opinion, and to be able to put oneself into it. it is good if one alternates meditating with other sciences which are refreshing.",[]],"blomberg-s14-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 484ff.",[]],"blomberg-s14-002":["The constitution of words is a worthy object of the considerations of the philosopher. The right use is this, that they designate a thought exactly.",[]],"blomberg-s14-003":["*Terminus inanis* is that whose meaning we believe we understand, but in fact do not understand. an expression is deceptive (*deceptrix*) where we believe something is possible, and it is yet impossible.",[]],"blomberg-s14-004":["*Terminus familiaris* is the expression whose sense has never been investigated, because it has never been heeded. Every language has such. I say e.g. *süchtig* [addicted; sickly] such *termini* are, on closer investigation, also often deceptive.",[]],"blomberg-s14-005":["The words: charlatanry of the learned is a deceptive trade, where one passes off words for things, what is more common than this expression, and deception! The expression is called intelligible which can be understood. Something can be intelligible without its being distinct. If 2 expressions have one meaning, or where at least the meanings are not very different, then they are called synonymous expressions. If an expression has more than one meaning, then it is called *terminus vagus ambiguus anceps*.",[]],"blomberg-s15-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 484ff.",[]],"blomberg-s15-002":["Style properly signifies the tool of a sculptor. Here it signifies the character of expressing one's thoughts. Everyone has his particular style, just as everyone writes his particular hand. Style is to be considered 1) insofar as it is appropriate to its object. 2) to the person (subject) and his station. 3) to those for whom one writes. For each a particular style is fitting, and not everyone can bring his thoughts into the form of another.",[]],"blomberg-s15-003":["The suitability of the manner of writing consists in its being so constituted that it is not too grand, but fitting for the thing. The euphony of the manner of writing is something that belongs more among chimeras than to the true. The English say that Virgil's poems sound well, but if one hears an Englishman recite the verses, then this would be unbearable to our ears. The *sonorum* consists in the measure of the syllables. Congruence consists in the style's being in conformity with the thing. This can be learned only through experience.",[]],"blomberg-s16-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 488ff.",[]],"blomberg-s16-002":["On the *donum didacticum* and the requisites of a teacher. In general 2 things must be found in a teacher. He must possess 1) a condescension to the poorer, and 2) a coascension to the higher capacities",[]],"blomberg-s16-003":["1. Every teacher must know how to put himself in the place of his listeners, I can teach another only through his own understanding, consequently I have a matter to work on which already lies in the head of the other. This is called condescension it presupposes that one know how to put oneself in the place of those whom one teaches. Very few teachers of religion, and others, can do this.",[]],"blomberg-s16-004":["2): the 2nd *requisitum* of a teacher is coascension, namely that one climb up to the talents which are raised above the middle level. He who knows little certainly cannot do it. It is the best case that one choose a middle degree. But how must one conduct oneself in public lectures, since one does not cognize the capacity of the *auditorum*.",[]],"blomberg-s16-005":["It is to be distinguished from one another: to impart an opinion to someone, and to refute someone's opinion. In the first case the subject is empty, but in the other I must empty it. Before all teaching I must first have the *data* in which the other errs. Every error is to be regarded as a *phaenomenon* which is worthy of an explanation. I must first gain insight into how he could have come to the error. Then I must also track down the part of truth in his error.",[]],"blomberg-s16-006":["Verbal dispute is when different persons believe they are contesting different opinions, and yet are at odds only in the words, but in the thing itself agreed. Concerning predestination some have believed that it is more a verbal dispute than a dispute about things. *Argumentum ad hominem* is such an argument as is indeed not true for everyone, but yet serves to bring someone to silence. When I e.g. fetch something *ex concessis* and from the particular propositions that another holds. These are good means to keep someone off one's neck, and to end the dispute, but not to find the truth. *Retorsiones* and *reprehensiones* are personal disputes, distinctions serve very well to pull oneself out of a tight spot. *Instantia* is when something is used universally, and I show that it is not universal. A defense which concerns the person is called apology, if it concerns the judgments of others, then it is called *refutatio*.",[]],"blomberg-s16-007":["A formal disputation is the action where in the presence of learned people one exposes some propositions to defend them against all objections. Disputing could produce great utility if it were not a *certamen personarum*. and if one wanted to seek out the truth not through dispute, but through agreement.",[]],"blomberg-s17-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 488ff.",[]],"blomberg-s17-002":["All learned writings are either historical or dogmatic. A writing can be historical both according to matter, if it is something actually happened or cognition of the *individuum*, and also according to form. The form is called historical which is not rational, where I do not derive through universal cognition of the grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s17-003":["A cognition is called historical-dogmatic according to form if the *data* are *dogmata*, but are presented historically. But according to matter if some is historical, but other is dogmatic. Everything that happens is considered in combination with space and time. If one considers that which happens insofar as it is at different times, then that is called history, but insofar as it is at the same time, then it belongs in the field of geography. There are different kinds of geography and history.",[]],"blomberg-s17-004":["The distinction which the author makes, in dividing history into political, church, learned, and private history, is determined by different objects of historical cognitions. There could also be a moral history, which considers the morals of common life, and also the moral *data*. Biography is the life of a single subject. All history, of whatever kind it may be, should have the end of extending reason, it should provide the material for the use of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s17-005":["That history is called pragmatic which becomes useful through universal rules; this can have a relation either to speculation or to practical reason. If one merely learns the names of the rulers, and knows their reign, then this gives little material for the use of reason i.e. for speculation, or for practical rules. Meanwhile one must at the beginning already abstract from the use of reason and on good hope provide oneself with many historical cognitions. Learned history becomes pragmatic in that one considers erudition merely in relation to human reason, looks to its growth, or to the causes through which it has been held back. There are 2 methods of composing dogmatic writings either tumultuary, or methodical. The latter method brings about a system. A system is any whole whatever. Systematic writings are distinguished from tumultuary writings in that they constitute an interconnected whole. Extracts,",[]],"blomberg-s17-006":["epitomes, are actual systems. In dogmatic writings the *principia* of the dogmatists must be presented, then drawn together *in concreto* and weighed. and thereupon one must strew in something historical, in order to give evidence.",[]],"blomberg-s17-007":["The method where one does not draw a cognition from one's own sources, but takes it from those who have drawn from the source is called compiling. History is compiled if I have not written it together from the first sources, but from later ones. One can also compile philosophical propositions, where one gathers together what others have thought through reason. One nevertheless proves skill if from the authors who possess great acuity, from the most thorough histories one gathers together the most important with prudent choice. From the compiler the plagiarist is distinguished. The latter affects that he has drawn from the first learned sources himself, although he only exhibits a product of another.",[]],"blomberg-s17-008":["The French are especially of the *caractère* that among them the sciences grow more *extensive* than *intensive*. For epitomes much understanding is required, in order to be able to choose the most important and most graspable.",[]],"blomberg-s18-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 493ff.",[]],"blomberg-s18-002":["The character of a learned man is twofold, the character of the head, and of the heart. The first concerns cognition, the latter inclination. The character of the heart has great combination with the character of the head, if erudition is to be furthered. The constitution of the heart has a great influence on the direction of the understanding, and it is therefore not indifferent even in merely speculative sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s18-003":["The character of the head concerns understanding, reason, and taste. The faculty of imitation is just opposed to the faculty of genius. Those who possess the capacity of genius sometimes do not have the spirit of imitation, which on the other hand is proper in great degree to most who show no genius. The Russians are said to have the failing that they lack genius, and for just that reason cannot teach it. Hence science always dies out among them. For philosophy genius is required, for the beautiful sciences likewise, but for useful sciences the spirit of imitation. Mathematics needs only imitation. Erudition and good taste are distinguished from one another.",[]],"blomberg-s18-004":["Mother wit is the natural capacity of the healthy understanding, and of healthy reason. This cannot be learned, but what is learned afterwards is called school wit. All teaching already presupposes certain cognitions, or at least the capacity for them. Thus school wit supposes mother wit. It is more bearable to lack school wit than to have it without mother wit. What is learned is beautiful in the human being, he himself absurd in the application. Hence comes pedantry, and one becomes through school wit still more absurd than otherwise because one then has more materials. School wit is a completion of mother wit.",[]],"blomberg-s18-005":["To the particular learned exercises belongs",[]],"blomberg-s18-006":["1: learning. This happens through oral lecture or through reading of books. One learns in that one either produces a cognition, or reproduces cognitions had. To teach properly means to let cognitions be produced through others. The learner is in this respect purely passive, but he must also be active. He must have *receptivitaet* i.e. capacity of understanding, and also apply it. Oral lecture has a great advantage over the written, for the oral is not so artificial as the written. It is also more graspable, for there is one action of the mind less than in reading. In reading I must namely also hear in thoughts.",[]],"blomberg-s18-007":["2: The reading of learned writings. It depends first very much on the order in which one reads. and on the choice of the writing where one begins. The question is: should compendia or great works be read first? One should not begin with the compendia which contain many thoughts in little space, but with those which contain few thoughts in a wide space. In mathematics, and in history one must first make the universal known to oneself. One must first proceed in the main. Historical books which one chooses for reading must at first concern not so much the exact occurrences but the universal ones. One must go according to a certain order, and in the morning, and evening read books of speculation, at midday historical books. One must read much and so to speak digest what is read, and the field must not be changed very much. Every book one must read at least 2 times, once quickly, where one marks for oneself the place which needs an explanation. Some books are of great importance, and require much investigation, these one must read often. E.g. Hume, Rousseau, Locke, who can be regarded as a grammar for the understanding, and Montesquieu on the spirit of the laws",[]],"blomberg-s18-008":["All too frequent repetition is just as harmful as all too long suspension. § 546: Imitation in matters of style is not advisable. § 548: of the invention of new truths no rule is to be given. § 549 *Avtodidactus* is he who makes himself learned through meditating, and reading. This is always a great genius. An oral lecture is meanwhile always to be preferred. One can make more *reflexiones*.",[]],"blomberg-s18-009":["Learned diligence signifies the strength of the exertion which one applies to learn something. The human being is by nature sluggish, and laziness is itself the final end of his diligence. Future rest is the spur which sets human beings into *activitaet*. The magnitude, and worthiness of the objects can yet greatly exercise diligence. Learned diligence, if it continues, is called a steady diligence. From this is distinguished vehement diligence, which consists in an all too strong exertion.",[]],"blomberg-s18-010":["The mind suffers with vehement diligence, the representations soon die out.",[]],"blomberg-s18-011":["One who studies must first well weigh the end, and the domain of his powers it is bad that others prescribe the end to us, and that one does not yet know oneself when one begins to study. Never must one study with vehement diligence, but never must one cease to learn. One knows how to judge what one has to learn only when one is near the end. It belongs also to the character of a learned man that he be a person without title, station and civil rank. Chiefly that he be a man of all stations, and that his cognition be universally usable, and not be fitting merely for the form of the school The final end of erudition should be to give the human race the true form, to free it from prejudices, to refine morals, and to elevate the powers of the soul; then it is a benefaction for the human race: but if it is used merely for *operation*, then it is to be regarded only as a kind of luxury",[]],"blomberg-s18-012":["End of the Logic",[]]};
